                                text "Revalidated against make.narration v1.\n\nWhy the rule moved: cde7c91 (make-recipe-command-narration) added one sentence — an ordinary inline recipe that supplies no description compiles to a rule described by the expanded recipe text, rather than a synthesized generic `build` description — so that the shared reporter names the action that actually runs. It is the resolution of this node's own two errata: the first observed that both graph-construction paths synthesized `description = build $out`, the second corrected the proposed fix, because dropping the description entirely exposed the `env ... /bin/sh -c` executor wrapper instead.\n\nThe implementation already satisfies it, and needed no change. GraphSink::executor_rule_bindings takes rule.description.or(the inline script) and binds a description only when one of the two exists, so the compact oversized response-file path is the only one left with no description and the reporter's own fallback is what covers it. The bumping commit carried all five impl sites (sink.rs, cli.rs twice, report.rs twice) and all eight verify sites in tests/cli.rs to +1 as it landed, so this node had no annotation left at v0.\n\nVerified: make_mode_narrates_recipe_command asserts the progress lines for a two-edge build name the printf and the cp rather than `build out`; the seven other narration cases still hold, covering Ninja-shaped recipe failure, io diagnostics, per-target output sync, composed-child narration with no directory banners, and the accepted narration no-ops. The equivalence corpus reporting 0 disagreements is what says kati's manifest writer makes the same choice as the direct sink; declare_rule in kati/src-rs/ninja.rs is the site that makes it there and does not claim the rule, which is a site-level gap rather than a coverage one.\n\nEvidence: 716 tests across ronin and kati, build-intent oracle 348 cases, equivalence corpus 297 makefiles / 276 graphs / 0 disagreements."
