                    {
                        id make-pattern-variable-assignment-scope
                        title "Expand pattern-specific assignment values in ambient scope"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-target-specific-separator-provenance)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-10T11:05:45.875216748Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Inherited reproducer confirmed during separator-provenance review: `%.o: N=P` followed by `%.o: X := $(N)` and a pattern recipe prints X. GNU Make 4.4.1 expands the pattern-specific value in ambient scope and prints empty; Kati sets current_scope to the pattern rule_vars scope before eval_rhs and prints P. Base 8d4ecbd already has this loop behavior. The provenance correction keeps pattern-specific name expansion ambient, as GNU does, and changes only concrete target names to expand per file scope; RHS scope remains this separate defect."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:01.434956041Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:00:21.08929308Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:31:08.587926471Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 RHS expansion timing and scope, probed against the oracle and confirmed in reference/gnumake (src/read.c record_target_var).\n\nTiming. The right-hand side is processed while the makefile is read, once per entry in the assignment's target list, left to right, whether or not any target ever matches: `%.neverusedext: S := $(shell ...)` still runs the shell. A two-pattern head `%.a %.b: SIDE := $(shell ...)` runs it twice — once per pattern, not once per matched target — matching the earlier finding that a side-effecting NAME also expands once per pattern.\n\nScope, and the asymmetry that was the defect. Only immediate flavours expand at read time, and where they expand depends on what the entry names:\n  - a PATTERN entry expands in the ambient/global scope. record_target_var calls allocated_variable_expand with no file scope pushed, so the pattern's own set is invisible — even a value the same pattern assigned on the line above. `N = global` + `%.o: N = from-pattern` + `%.o: X := $(N)` gives X=global.\n  - a NAMED-TARGET entry expands with that target's own variable set innermost, because record_target_var enters the file and pushes its set. `c.o: N = P` + `c.o: X := $(N)` gives X=P. Other targets' sets are not visible.\n\nRecursive `=` and `+=` values are not expanded at read time at all; they are stored as text and resolved against the scope chain when the target's variables are initialised.\n\nKati set current_scope to the pattern's Vars before eval_rhs, so pattern values read the scope being built. Fixed by separating the two roles the scope was serving: expansion reads the ambient chain, definition still lands in the pattern's set. `+=`/`?=` still read the rule scope for the value they build on, because a second assignment to one pattern accumulates onto the first — that is a lookup, not the value expansion."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:32:07.39527794Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-upstream-residue-triage
                        title "Classify the bounded GNU Make upstream residue into intent, interface, or narration"
                        kind @test
                        deps (make-upstream-suite make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:27.79263368Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Bounded discovery scope is the versioned 2026-08-11 upstream inventory: resolve all 513 unclassified observations and revalidate the 68 compiler-labelled candidates against graph shape, selected work, filesystem effects, and accepted interface. Work category by category, starting with the largest clusters (variables/MAKEFLAGS 65, implicit_search 25, patternrules 24, include 21, targetvars 17, bs-nl 17, word 16, shell 13, EXTRA_PREREQS 13, define 13). Reclassify intentional Ninja narration and runner choreography without implementation nodes; when a focused GNU Make reproducer proves a shared compiler/interface defect, add one grouped implementation leaf and an owned build-intent case. Completion is finite: no unclassified rows remain in this inventory snapshot and every surviving compiler candidate is either linked to such a leaf or downgraded with evidence."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:01.603393077Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T11:54:35.048437734Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "The bounded upstream snapshot advances after make-output-sync-feature-advertisement makes GNU Make's 17-case features/output-sync category reachable. Supersede the earlier counts with the versioned 1,368-row inventory: 526 unclassified, 69 compiler candidates, 171 interface observations, and 602 narration observations. Triage owns the ten new unclassified rows and the new no-rule-to-make compiler candidate; the six newly explicit narration rows require no implementation leaf under make.narration."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T21:13:23.79749243Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Re-ran the pinned GNU Make 4.4.1 discovery suite after inline recipes became their own descriptions. The versioned inventory remains 1,306 rows, but three targets/WAIT rows (diff, diff.1, diff.2) are now provably narration-only instead of unclassified evaluation/failure candidates: the visible recipe establishes that their selected work agrees. Counts move from 522 unclassified / 68 compiler / 103 interface / 613 narration to 519 / 68 / 103 / 616. options/dash-B.diff.7 and variables/automatic.diff.7 remain unclassified; only their runner facet changed from ninja-progress to no-work-line. No new compiler or interface leaf is warranted."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T06:05:35.904533992Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Triage pass of 2026-08-14, against the same 1,287-row snapshot. Counts move 475/36/38/738 (unclassified/compiler/interface/narration) to 215/50/45/977. Two full runs from fresh disposable worktrees; the second exited 0 against the first's recording, and the three watched flapper rows held their class.\n\nThe classification is produced by the classifier, not edited into the tsv, so every move below is a recogniser in examples/make_upstream.rs with a unit test.\n\nWHAT WAS DRAINED (260 rows out of unclassified)\n\nThe largest single cause was a regression this backlog introduced. Ninja's failure block echoes the command it ran, and `make-makeflags-in-recipe-environment` put `env 'MAKEFLAGS=…' 'MAKELEVEL=…' 'MFLAGS=…'` in front of `/bin/sh -c \"…\"`, which the `ninja-failure-block` recogniser matched by prefix. 87 cases had been quietly falling out of narration ever since. The recogniser now reads through the assignments.\n\nThe most useful new evidence is that Ronin's progress counter carries the command it is about to run, which is precisely what Make says by echoing the recipe line. The counter's payload now cancels Make's echo. This is better evidence than the existing `recipe-echo` path, which reads the makefile off disk: one suite script writes `errors.mk` several times over and only the last version survives to be read, so the older sub-tests could never match. Cancellation is applied only to lines that do not already cancel against our own output, which is what stopped it stealing 19 cases that were narration for a different reason.\n\nMake wraps a fatal diagnostic in `*** ` and `  Stop.` and Ronin states the same thing plainly. The decoration now comes off before the two sides are compared, on the same principle the product name already came off — what a tool said is the thing worth comparing, not how it dressed it.\n\nTwo fatal refusals are one decision worded twice. Where both sides left nothing but diagnostics and one of Make's wore the fatal wrapper, the case is `shared-refusal`. This deliberately narrows the older rule that two tools saying different things must not cancel, and the narrowing is argued in the test that replaced it: each tool decided to build nothing, which is the same decision, and `make.narration` puts the sentence outside the contract. It is only available because Make refused too — the opposite case became a compiler family, below.\n\nSmaller recognisers: GNU's `--debug` trace, gated on the run actually asking for debug output because the suite writes those expectations as bare regexes; Make's keep-going summary `Target 'x' not remade because of errors.`; a missing command reported by Make's exec and by our shell, matched on the command rather than either wording; and a MAKEFLAGS value quoted inside a sentence a makefile printed around it.\n\nWHAT WAS FOUND (23 rows that were hiding, not resting)\n\nThe `refusal-not-made` family is new and is the point of the exercise. Make refused the run outright and Ronin went on to build: the refusal was the whole of Make's output, so anything Ronin ran is work Make never authorised. These had been sitting in `unclassified` looking like output noise. Every one is a real divergence and every one is now owned.\n\nCOMPILER BUCKET: 50 rows, all owned or reclassified\n\nReclassified with evidence — 9 rows. All nine `parse-failure` rows were shared refusals: both tools reject the same makefile, at the same line in six of them. `parse-failure` means \"Ronin could not read what Make read\", which is false when Make refused too, so the marker no longer fires on a shared refusal. misc/failure.diff.3 and .6 refuse at different lines for different reasons — GNU calls `ifeq(foo,bar)` a missing separator, Ronin calls the `endif` below it extraneous — and no makefile could be constructed where one builds and the other does not.\n\nNewly owned — 41 rows across eleven new nodes filed under make-subsumption, each with a reproducer minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n  make-every-makefile-argument-is-read (19) — only the last -f is read; the default goal must come from the first\n  make-match-anything-rule-chaining (7) — the implicit search will not chain through a `%:` rule\n  make-builtin-rule-catalogue (4) — the fork bootstraps `.c.o` and `.cc.o` and nothing else; the link rules are missing entirely\n  make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem (4) — a directory stem must be prefixed, not substituted in place\n  make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites (3) — and it fails in both directions at once\n  make-link-library-prerequisites (3) — `-lNAME` and .LIBPATTERNS\n  make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent (2) — the stem goes in the first `%` only\n  make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules (2) — `-r` from a makefile's MAKEFLAGS arrives after the rules are installed\n  make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcards (1), make-extra-prereqs-are-real-prerequisites (1), make-word-index-out-of-range (1), make-jobserver-style-is-checked (1)\n\nAttributed to an existing open node — 1 row. features/double_colon.diff.16 is make-terminal-pattern-rule-needs-an-existing-prerequisite, noted there.\n\nWHAT REMAINS: 215 rows, and why\n\nThe remainder is a long tail rather than a bucket: 59 test scripts, largest group 13. The family-shaped work is done, and draining further needs per-case reproducers rather than recognisers. Five themes are identified, with the question each is blocked on:\n\n  22 rows — Make announces removing an intermediate (`rm test.int`) and Ronin removes it silently. Probed: the filesystem effect agrees, both tools delete the file, so this is narration. Blocked on recognising it safely — Make's announcement is `rm` followed by bare names, which is also exactly what an echoed recipe running `rm` looks like, and the payload cancellation does not help because Make prints this one itself.\n\n  17 rows — MAKEFLAGS content the shape recogniser will not read, mostly `-Ilocaltmp` and `--warn-undefined-variables` missing from ours. Blocked on whether their absence is deliberate; if it is, these are interface observations and not residue.\n\n  13 rows — Make remade a makefile and Ronin did not. Overlaps make-every-makefile-argument-is-read; blocked on that node landing, after which they should be re-read rather than classified now.\n\n  8 rows — a suite regex expectation with no counterpart, outside the debug-trace gate.\n\n  7 rows — GNU-only warnings (jobserver, pattern peer, suffix-rule prerequisites). Narration in effect, but each is evidence for a specific open node and worth leaving visible until those land.\n\n  148 rows — everything else, led by features/include (12), misc/bs-nl (11), features/suffixrules (8) and variables/automatic (7). misc/bs-nl belongs with make-recipe-continuation-bytes, make-posix-continuation-blanks and make-recipe-expanded-newline, all open.\n\nThis node's definition of done requires no unclassified rows to remain, so it stays open. What it now owns is a bounded and themed 215 rather than an undifferentiated 475."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-delete-on-error-cleanup
                        title "Remove failed outputs under .DELETE_ON_ERROR"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.000472387Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns the five .DELETE_ON_ERROR markers covering a direct failed output, a dependency-chain output, a secondary output, and keep-going, plus recorded corpus case delete_on_error.mk#test. Preserve .PRECIOUS where it independently forbids deletion and verify filesystem effects rather than GNU Make's failure wording."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:01.799537993Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T17:23:18.587869831Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T17:23:39.059742123Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "How the delete-on-error intent rides the graph, and what it costs the equivalence gate.\n\nThe intent crosses the sink as `SinkEdge::delete_on_error_outputs`: the names of this edge's outputs a failed recipe must not leave behind, not a flag. Names rather than a flag because GNU Make's exclusions are per output and not per edge — probed and confirmed: `.DELETE_ON_ERROR: / .PRECIOUS: b / a b &: ; echo A > a; echo B > b; exit 1` deletes `a` and keeps `b`. A boolean on the edge could not say that.\n\nKati decides who is eligible, because that is Make semantics: the global `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` switch, minus `.PHONY` names (which stand for no file) and `.PRECIOUS` names. The executor decides who actually goes, because that is a fact about the run: an output is removed only when its timestamp is no longer the one the build read before the recipe ran, which is GNU's own test in `delete_target`.\n\nDirect graph: a side table on `Graph` keyed by edge, not a field on `Edge`. Same reasoning the comment on `validation_uses` already gives — `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` is something a Makefile either says or never mentions, and an inline list would charge every edge in every manifest for a Make feature almost none of them use.\n\nEmitted manifest: a documented bounded divergence, not an encoding in the generated command. Ninja has no notion of an output the build withdraws when the command fails, so the writer ignores the field exactly as it already ignores `intermediate` and `disposable` — the precedent is in `build_sink.rs`, which says of those two that \"a sink that runs the build is the one that answers for it\". Wrapping the recipe in failure-path `rm` was the alternative and was rejected on two grounds: it cannot carry the timestamp guard (a recipe that rewrote its target and restored the mtime must keep it — probed, and Ronin now agrees), and it would make the manifest's `command` text differ, buying a `destination_specific_binding` exemption for a channel that is not on the path from Makefile to build.\n\nEquivalence consequence: none. `make.manifest-equivalence` enumerates what must match — edges, input partitions, outputs, validations, commands, pool, depfile, restat, generator, default targets — and this is not among them, exactly as `intermediate` and `disposable` are not. No binding was added to `BINDINGS`, so no rule bump and no rule-version bookkeeping commit. The divergence is asserted rather than assumed: `only_the_direct_graph_withdraws_a_failed_recipe_s_outputs` compares both graphs, asserts the direct one names `gone` and not the `.PRECIOUS` `kept` or the `.PHONY` `all`, asserts the parsed manifest names none, and asserts the two still agree under `differences`.\n\nNarration: nothing is printed. `make.narration+1` puts Make mode's reporting in the manifest front end's shape, so GNU's `*** Deleting file 'x'` is not owed, and withdrawing a half-written output is the same act the interrupt path in `finish_edge` has always performed silently. The two paths now share one `withdraw_outputs` helper differing only in which outputs are eligible."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T17:27:12.828743271Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T17:29:25.159510492Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @errata
                                text "The decision entry above names the equivalence test `only_the_direct_graph_withdraws_a_failed_recipe_s_outputs`. It landed as `withdrawal_reaches_only_the_direct_graph` in `src/make/equivalence.rs`: the first name read as a sentence and the commit's function-name gate refused it at ten words and fifty-seven characters against caps of six and forty-five.\n\nThe same gate run refused `src/graph.rs` at fifty-two functions against a cap of fifty, which the two side-table accessors this node added had pushed it over. They now live in `src/graph/withdrawal.rs`, beside the module doc explaining why the property is a list of names rather than a switch — which is where a reader looking for it would have gone first anyway. Neither gate was bypassed."
                                after_done true
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-double-colon-rule-freshness
                        title "Test ordinary double-colon rules against their own prerequisites"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-grouped-double-colon-actions make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.179574377Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns feature-double-colon-only-outdated-runs, target-double-colon-rules-are-independent, target-double-colon-up-to-date-rules-do-not-run, and recorded multi_explicit_output_patterns_double_colon.mk#test. Ordinary :: records remain separate actions: each record tests only its own prerequisites and static-pattern form retains its own stem and recipe."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.021659578Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T17:54:49.1586711Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T18:00:15.388952092Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 matrix, probed against the pristine oracle before implementing and confirmed against reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:\n\nEach `::` record is an independent rule. A record runs iff the target is out of date against the prerequisites that record declared, never the union: with two records whose prerequisites are `a b` and `c d`, making only `b` newer runs the first alone. `$?`, `$^`, `$<` and `$|` are per-record. A record with no prerequisites at all always runs (`update_file_1`: `if (file->double_colon && file->deps == 0) must_make = 1`), and that verdict is its own — the record beside it is still weighed. Order-only prerequisites do not exempt a record from that test, because GNU asks whether the entry declared any dependency rather than any it would compare timestamps against, so `out:: | oo` is not unconditional. A recipe-less record still has its own prerequisites brought up to date and contributes nothing to run, and its being out of date does not lend that verdict to a sibling that has a recipe. `.PHONY` on the target runs every record. Mixing `:` and `::` for one target is fatal.\n\nTwo cells the design turns on. Post-run mtime: a record that updates the target does not settle later records in the same run. `notice_finished_file` unifies the chain's timestamps to `max_mtime` only once every entry is updated, precisely so that entries are \"separate rules with separate timestamps while they are treated as targets and then as one rule with the unified timestamp when they are considered as a prerequisite of some target\". Probed: a first record pushing the target to 2030 still leaves a second record whose prerequisite is 2021 with work to do. Parallelism: GNU serialises the chain. `update_file` returns as soon as an entry is running — \"Don't run other :: rules for this target until this rule is finished\" — so under -j10 the entries run one at a time in written order. The upstream suite's \"All double-colon rules should be run in parallel build\" (sv 44742) asserts that none is skipped in a parallel build, not that they overlap.\n\nImplementation reuses the make-grouped-double-colon-actions machinery rather than adding a second one. `build_double_action` and `build_grouped_double_member` already compiled one private action node per exact record plus a public completion join, and `DoubleActionId` already carried `trigger: Some(member)` for non-grouped records; only the dispatch in `build_plan` withheld ordinary records from that path. It now admits a merger holding more than one record. A lone `::` record keeps its single-node shape, which confines the graph change to the targets whose semantics actually require it. The order-only chain that serialises records sharing a member gives the written-order execution for free, since every record of one target shares that target. Freshness needs no new mechanism: the deferred baseline is captured by the dirty walk before it descends into prerequisites, which is the same snapshot GNU caches in each entry's `last_mtime`.\n\nTwo corrections the work required. A prerequisite-free record is classified in `build_double_action` after second expansion, which is where GNU reaches the same test; this also repairs grouped `&::` records with no prerequisites, which were never classified unconditional at all and so never re-ran — the same GNU predicate, in the function both forms share. And a completion join must not publish `deferred_freshness_always_dirty`: it owns no members, and letting the chain's unconditional flag reach that field made the direct and manifest sinks disagree over a value neither path reads."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T18:02:22.001936382Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-default-goal-variable-semantics
                        title "Select and expose goals through .DEFAULT_GOAL"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.390047357Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns variable-default-goal, variable-default-goal-reset, variable-default-goal-reassigned, and variable-default-goal-unknown. The selected graph root and the Makefile-visible .DEFAULT_GOAL value must track assignment, reset, and reassignment, while an invalid multi-target value is rejected during evaluation."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.20931783Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:12:18.981999052Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Correction to the preceding scope note: the unknown-goal fixture assigns .DEFAULT_GOAL = nosuchtarget. GNU Make honors that name as the graph root and then reports that no rule can make it; it is not the separate multi-target-value validation case."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T18:06:42.702330094Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T18:45:01.707545027Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Characterised against the 4.4.1 oracle before writing anything; thirty probes, each run through both binaries and compared. What GNU Make does:\n\nReading. `.DEFAULT_GOAL` exists before the first line of the first Makefile — `$(origin)` is `file`, `$(flavor)` is `simple`, value empty. It reads as the current choice at every point of the read, so the same `$(info)` before and after a rule answers `[]` then `[foo]`.\n\nChoosing. `check_specials` (read.c) offers each recorded target while the variable's *text* is empty. A `%` in the name ends the offer for that rule; a leading dot with no `/` after it is passed over and the next target of the same rule is still considered (`.foo bar:` chooses `bar`, `..dots:` is passed over, `sub/.deps:` is chosen). Included files record targets like any other and choose the goal; a target-specific assignment line records nothing and chooses nothing; pattern rules choose nothing; grouped `&:` and `::` rules choose their first target; a static pattern rule chooses its concrete target.\n\nAssigning. Assignment disarms selection for every later target; assigning empty re-arms it for the next one; the last assignment before the read ends is what counts. Armed on the text, not the expansion: `.DEFAULT_GOAL = $(EMPTY)` stays disarmed and then dies `*** No targets.` A recursive value is expanded at goal-resolution time, so `.DEFAULT_GOAL = $(CHOSEN)` follows a `CHOSEN` assigned anywhere in the read.\n\nResolving. Command-line goals bypass it entirely and `MAKECMDGOALS` never sees it. A command-line `.DEFAULT_GOAL=x` outranks the Makefile; an environment one is discarded, because the empty binding is installed at file origin over it — unless `-e`, where the environment outranks file and the environment value chooses. A value naming no rule dies `No rule to make target`; a value naming an existing file with no rule is `Nothing to be done`; two names die `.DEFAULT_GOAL contains more than one target` — but the whole value is looked up as a target first, so `a\\ xb` (one target, escaped space) resolves to that target.\n\nTwo behaviours deliberately not reproduced. `undefine .DEFAULT_GOAL` followed by any target segfaults GNU Make 4.4.1 (`default_goal_var->value` is freed and the selection check dereferences it); Ronin carries on with selection re-armed. And the suffix half of the eligibility check is not implemented — see make-default-goal-suffix-rejection, which owns tests/make/variable-default-goal-suffix-name.\n\nDelivered in kati 6efb7e2 on branch nplan/make-default-goal-variable-semantics: the variable is bound before the read (evaluate.rs), offered each rule's targets during it (eval.rs), and read back by dependency analysis (dep.rs), which leaves the resolved goals on the evaluator for the manifest's `default` line (ninja.rs). `first_rule` survives only for `--gen_all_targets`.\n\nTwo regressions caught by the gates and fixed before landing, both worth recording because they are the shape of this change's risk. The Makefiles a front end has to remake are appended to dependency analysis's roots, so reading the goals off the roots at emit time made them goals — caught by feature-optional-include-*. And splitting the value into words before looking it up as a name broke a target whose name holds an escaped space — caught by features/double_colon in the upstream suite.\n\nGates: build-intent corpus 383 cases green, ten new (origin/flavor, include, pattern, reserved names, recursive value, recursive-empty, two targets, command line, spaced name, and the suffix divergence); four owned divergence markers deleted; workspace 655 + kati 112 = 767 green; conformance 387 runs, 302 normalised identical / 85 differing, unmoved; equivalence 297/276/0; upstream inventory 1287 rows, three moved, all in variables/DEFAULT_GOAL and all improvements (test 1 and test 2 leave the unclassified class, test 5 stops building a goal GNU refuses); ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery clean, kati `-D warnings` clean."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T18:45:17.264520233Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-pattern-specific-variable-application
                        title "Apply, inherit, and order pattern-specific variables"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-target-variable-order make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.599485364Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns feature-pattern-specific-variable, feature-pattern-specific-variable-is-inherited, and feature-pattern-specific-shortest-stem, together with the focused share of upstream features/patspecific_vars. Apply pattern-specific bindings to explicit targets, inherit them into prerequisites, and choose matching bindings using GNU Make's shortest-stem precedence. RHS ambient-scope expansion remains separately owned by make-pattern-variable-assignment-scope."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.377907457Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:00:16.756957072Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:30:55.428197982Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 application matrix, probed against the oracle and confirmed in reference/gnumake (src/variable.c: create_pattern_var, lookup_pattern_var, initialize_file_variables).\n\nOrder. For a target T the scopes apply weakest first: every pattern P in the pattern-variable list that matches T, then T's own named settings last. GNU keeps pattern_vars sorted by strlen(P) ascending with ties in definition order, walks it head to tail, and each entry overwrites the last into one accumulated set; that set is spliced between the file's own set and global. So the longest matching pattern wins, which for a fixed target is the same as the shortest stem, and equal-length patterns are decided by which was written first. Probed: `%-mt.x` vs `%.x` on foo-mt.x gives `shortpat longer`; `a%c` vs `%bc` on abc gives written order both ways round.\n\nStem. lookup_pattern_var skips any pattern with p->len > targlen, so a pattern variable needs a non-empty stem: `%.z` reaches `a.z` and not `.z`, and `foo%bar` does not reach `foobar`. Pattern *rules* do match the empty stem, so Pattern::matches alone was the wrong test.\n\n`+=`. Each scope's `+=` appends to what the scopes before it left, falling through to the global/inherited value when none did. `%.x: which += a` then `foo.x: which += b` gives `a b`; with a global it starts from the global.\n\nInheritance and private. Pattern values reach the target's prerequisites exactly as named target-specific ones do (they are on the same chain), and `private` still stops at the target's own recipe — probed, not regressed.\n\nFlavour and override. `:=` keeps its snapshot and `=` re-expands at use, through the pattern scope. `override` in a pattern or target assignment does not reorder anything; precedence stays positional.\n\nAdjacent defect found and fixed here because it is the same machinery: `+=` in a rule's own scope was double-counted, appending both at parse time to the scope-local value and again at build time to the ambient one. It is settled only when it built on a value the scope had already set outright; a run of `+=` with only each other to build on stays pending, which is what upstream features/targetvars TEST #9/#10 needs.\n\nAlso fixed: scope bindings were released front to back, which only became observable once two scopes could bind one name — the outer binding was restored first and the inner one left installed, leaking a pattern's value into the next sibling target built."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T19:32:03.836210004Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-modern-function-evaluation
                        title "Evaluate let, intcmp, and builtin names reached through call"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.817043523Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-let, function-let-recursive, function-intcmp, and function-call-builtin. Implement GNU Make 4.4.1 let/intcmp semantics and dispatch a builtin function named through call, including recursive let bindings; the upstream intcmp unsupported-feature row is a confirmed member rather than generic residue."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.535492661Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:02.389849611Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:33.6523695Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. Three functions, and the surprising cells are what decided the shape.\n\n`$(let names,list,body)` (function.c func_let, FT_ENTRY 3/3/EXP=0). Each name but the last takes one word; the last takes *the remainder of the string*, not a rejoined word list. `$(let a b,  1   2   3  ,+$(a)+$(b)+)` is `+1+2   3  +`: leading whitespace comes off the remainder and everything inside and after it stays. Names past the end of the list bind to the empty string rather than going unbound. An empty name list is not an error — `$(let ,1 2,body)` expands the body. Bindings are `o_automatic` (`$(origin)` says `automatic`, `$(flavor)` says `simple`) and unwind when the body finishes, so an outer `a` means what it meant again.\n\n`$(intcmp lhs,rhs[,lt[,eq[,gt]]])` (2/5/EXP=0). Parsing is `parse_textint`: optional whitespace, one optional sign, digits, optional whitespace. Leading zeros are dropped and a zero has no sign, so `$(intcmp 007,7)` is `7` and `$(intcmp -0,0)` is `0` — the two-argument form answers with the canonical spelling of the value, not with what was written. The comparison is the surprising one: sign, then digit count, then memcmp of the digits, with no negation for a negative pair. `$(intcmp -5,-6,lt,eq,gt)` is `lt` and `$(intcmp -10,-9,lt,eq,gt)` is `gt` — GNU orders two negatives by magnitude. It is also arbitrary precision by construction: 23-digit values compare correctly. Replicated exactly, quirk included, because the oracle is the specification. Arm defaulting: less takes lt; equal takes eq; greater takes gt if gt was given *and* eq was given, else eq — so `$(intcmp 3,2,lt,eq)` is `eq` and `$(intcmp 3,2,lt)` is empty. Only the chosen arm expands.\n\n`$(call NAME,...)` where NAME is a builtin. `func_call` calls `lookup_function` *before* `lookup_variable`, so a builtin always wins: a Makefile that defines `notdir = SHADOW` cannot reach its own through `$(call)`. Calling nothing is a no-op; `$(call notdir)` is empty rather than an error (`expand_builtin_function` returns early on argc 0), while too few arguments for the function is the same `insufficient number of arguments` it would have refused directly. No maximum-argument clamp applies, because the commas were split by `call`'s unlimited arity — `$(call subst,a,b,banana,ignored)` ignores the fourth. The cell that decided the implementation: `call` expands its arguments first, so a builtin GNU would have handed unexpanded text expands it a *second* time. `$(call foreach,v,1 2,x$(v))` is `x x` (the `$(v)` was consumed before anything was bound) while `$(call foreach,v,1 2,$$(v)y)` is `1y 2y`. `$(call notdir,$$(V))` is the literal `$(V)`.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati/src-rs/func.rs. `FuncInfo` gained `pre_expanded_args`, which is GNU's EXP column and nothing else: every function here expands its own arguments, so it changes nothing about a direct call and decides only what `$(call)` hands over — literal text for an eager function, a re-parsed expression for a lazy one. False for `if`, `and`, `or`, `foreach`, `let`, `intcmp`, and `KATI_foreach_sep` because it is `foreach`. `let_func` binds through the existing `with_bounds`, so a body that fails restores every name at once. `intcmp_func` carries a `MakeInt` that is the parse and the comparison together. `call_func` grew the builtin lookup ahead of the variable lookup, and the GNU no-op for an empty name.\n\nFixtures. The four owned markers are gone: function-let, function-let-recursive, function-intcmp, function-call-builtin all match. Six new cases recorded from the oracle: function-let-binds-the-rest-to-the-last-name, function-let-unwinds-what-it-bound, function-intcmp-reads-sign-and-leading-zeros, function-intcmp-falls-back-to-the-equal-arm (whose lt arm is a `$(shell touch)` that leaves no file, so the laziness is observable as an absence), function-call-hands-a-builtin-expanded-text, function-call-builtin-outranks-a-variable. Three kati unit tests: let_restores_bindings_when_body_fails, intcmp_refuses_a_non_numeric_argument, call_refuses_too_few_builtin_arguments.\n\nUpstream inventory. functions/call.diff, functions/let.diff, .1 and .3 moved unclassified → narration: GNU's own expectations now match line for line and only Ronin's `[N/M]` progress line differs. functions/intcmp.diff.1-4 and functions/let.diff.5-6 stayed unclassified but changed family, ninja-progress+evaluation → product-name+evaluation: those are the error cases, and they now die where they used to evaluate, so what is left is our name and our quoting (`` `intcmp' `` for `'intcmp'`, no `  Stop.` suffix) rather than a different answer. functions/intcmp.diff did not move: it fails on `$(shell echo lt)` inside an arm, which is make-shell-function-evaluation's refusal and not this node's.\n\nTwo defects found and left for follow-ups rather than folded in, both filed under make-subsumption with reproducers: the recursion guard is by name where GNU's is per variable object (reproduces on `foreach` alone, so it predates `let`), and `-e` makes an environment variable's origin `environment override` where GNU says `environment`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:38.150028004Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-filter-percent-escaping
                        title "Preserve escaped percent signs in filter patterns"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:29.042838804Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-filter-escaped-percent. A backslash-escaped percent is a literal percent in filter and filter-out patterns, with the remaining wildcard and backslash behavior matching GNU Make."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.700515235Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:42.808745711Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:03.639086166Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU 4.4.1's rule is `find_percent` (read.c:2528), which is `find_char_unquote(pattern, '%')`, and it is a parity rule rather than a \"strip the backslash\" rule. Characterised against the oracle before anything moved.\n\nA run of N backslashes immediately before a `%` collapses to floor(N/2) of them, and the `%` is the wildcard only when N is even. So `foo\\%bar` is the literal name `foo%bar` with no backslash and no wildcard; `foo\\\\%bar` is a literal backslash then the wildcard, matching `foo\\%bar` and `foo\\Xbar`; `foo\\\\\\%bar` is a literal backslash then a literal percent. Verified all three against the oracle.\n\nThe cell that decides where the code goes: GNU stops rewriting the moment it has its answer. `find_char_unquote` returns at the first unescaped `%`, so escaping *after* it is never compressed. `$(filter %x\\%y,ax\\%y aXy ax%y)` is `ax\\%y` — the pattern's suffix is the four characters `x\\%y`, backslash included, and the word that matches carries one. A pattern with several percents keeps only the first as the wildcard: `a%b%c` is prefix `a`, suffix `b%c`.\n\nThe same function runs on the replacement. `$(patsubst abc,\\%,abc def)` is `% def` — the replacement is unescaped even when the pattern has no wildcard at all, because `patsubst_expand` calls `find_percent` on both unconditionally. A substitution reference is the one place it does not: `variable_expand_string` calls `find_percent(replace)` only when the pattern had a percent, so `$(V:abc=\\%)` keeps its backslash (`\\% def`) while `$(V:%c=\\%)` does not (`% def`). And the pattern half is compressed either way — `$(V:\\%=x)` is a suffix substitution on the single character `%`.\n\nWhat changed. `strutil::find_percent` is the rewrite-and-locate pair, and `Pattern::new` is now its only caller, which is where GNU puts it too — `filter`, `filter-out`, `patsubst` and substitution references all construct patterns through it, so one place covers every function that has this question. `append_subst` resolves the replacement through the same function instead of scanning for a bare `%`; `append_subst_ref` still uses the raw replacement on its no-percent branch, which is the asymmetry the oracle showed. The fast path is a `memchr` and returns the original `Bytes` untouched, so a pattern with no escaping copies nothing.\n\nNot done here, deliberately: GNU compresses a *rule's* target and prerequisite names at read time (`find_percent_cached` in read.c), where kati stores them as written. `Pattern::new` now resolves the pattern half of that at use, but a rule target spelled with `\\%` would still have an uncompressed name to match against. No marker owns it and nothing in the corpora reaches it; it is a parser-layer question rather than an evaluator one.\n\nFixtures. function-filter-escaped-percent matches and its divergence file is gone. Two new cases recorded from the oracle: function-filter-halves-backslashes-at-a-percent (the three parities, filter-out, the stops-at-the-wildcard cell, and a two-percent pattern) and function-patsubst-escaped-percent (pattern and replacement escaping, and the substitution-reference asymmetry). Two kati unit tests: escaped_percent_is_a_literal_percent and escaping_stops_at_the_wildcard.\n\nUpstream inventory: functions/filter-out.diff.3, .4 and .5 moved unclassified → narration. Those are GNU's own escaped-percent cases, and the output now matches but for Ronin's progress line."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:08.557718165Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

