                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:36.848995249Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Both halves characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything moved.\n\n`$(suffix)` (function.c func_notdir_suffix). GNU scans back from the end of each word for whichever comes first, a dot or a directory separator, and answers only when it found a dot. So a dot inside a directory name names no suffix — `/tmp.c/bar` and `dir.d/file` have none — and the boundary is the same one `$(basename)` takes the stem from. The surprising cells: a name that is nothing but a dotted tail keeps all of it, so `$(suffix .hidden)` is `.hidden` and `$(suffix dir/.hidden)` is `.hidden`; a trailing dot is a one-character suffix, so `$(suffix a.)` is `.` and `$(suffix ..)` is `.`; `./x` has none and `./.x` has `.x`. A word with no suffix contributes nothing at all rather than an empty word, while `$(basename .hidden)` does contribute one.\n\n`$(realpath)` (func_realpath). realpath(3) then stat(2), so resolution is the filesystem's: symlinks followed, `..` and doubled separators collapsed, `//` answered as `/`. A word that names nothing — a missing path, or a symlink with no target — leaves the output entirely rather than appearing as an empty word, so `$(words $(realpath d/f nosuch d/sub))` is 2. `$(abspath)` was probed alongside and already agrees on every cell tried (`//`, `///a`, trailing slashes, `.`, a nonexistent path), so nothing was changed there and no marker asked for it.\n\nWhat changed. `strutil::get_ext` now stops at a directory separator, which makes it the exact complement of `strip_ext` (that one already stopped there, so the pair had disagreed about what a name's suffix was). `$(suffix)` reads it, and so does dep.rs's suffix-rule lookup, where the old answer for `dir.d/file` was the nonsense suffix `.d/file`; GNU converts suffix rules to pattern rules at read time and would never have matched that either, so the fix is in the same direction on both paths.\n\n`realpath_func` no longer checks `avoid_io`. Upstream kati deferred `$(realpath)` reached from a recipe into `$(<self> --realpath ... 2>/dev/null)` for the recipe's shell to run — a different value at a different time, and in Ronin it did not even run, because the compiled command carried the text inside single quotes and the literal `$( ... )` landed in the output. GNU resolves where it reads, and `$(wildcard)` in this same file already answers during evaluation for exactly that reason; realpath now follows the precedent. `rkati --realpath` is left in place: nothing emits that command line any more, but it is a working mode of the binary and removing a CLI surface is a different question from this one.\n\nFixtures. function-suffix and function-realpath both match and their divergence files are gone. Two new cases recorded from the oracle: function-suffix-reads-the-last-component-only (the dotted-tail, trailing-dot and directory-dot cells, with `$(basename)` beside them for the shared boundary) and function-realpath-resolves-links-and-drops-strays. The realpath case is written against `$(CURDIR)` through `$(filter)` rather than emitting absolute paths, because the harness runs each case in a scratch directory whose path is not stable; its dangling symlink is named `.dangling` so the harness's directory listing skips it, since `fs::read` of one is an error rather than a file. One kati unit test: suffix_stops_at_a_directory_separator.\n\nUpstream inventory: functions/suffix.diff, functions/realpath.diff.1 and .2 moved unclassified → narration. GNU's own suffix expectations now match line for line and only Ronin's `[N/M]` progress line differs."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:41.838244462Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-shell-function-evaluation
                        title "Compile shell function values in the evaluation environment"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:29.496249638Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-shell and variable-curdir. Shell-backed evaluation must produce its value before graph construction, with the evaluation working directory represented correctly so CURDIR comparisons and recipe-context shell expansion do not retain literal command substitution."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:03.038813395Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:43:34.849576515Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:45:44.617495297Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved, then the refusal it was owning was traced to a decision that belongs to the destination rather than to kati.\n\nWhat kati was doing. `avoid_io` is set for the whole of command evaluation, and under it `$(shell)` never runs: at `eval_depth <= 1` the call is written back out as `$(cmd)`, a shell command substitution for the generated command's own shell to answer, and at `eval_depth > 1` — anywhere the value would reach another Make function — it is a hard error, `doesn't support passing results of $(shell) to other make constructs`. The one exception is `has_no_io_in_shell_script`: an empty command, or `echo $((...))`, which Android needs arithmetic from. Outside a recipe nothing defers. So the map was: read time runs, recipe top level defers, recipe nested refuses. `function-shell` was the first (`printf '[%s]' '$(shell echo there)' > out` gave `[$(echo there)]`, because a command substitution inside single quotes is not one), `variable-curdir` and `functions/intcmp.diff` were the second.\n\nThe deferral is not a defect. It is correct when the destination is a build.ninja another program will run later, which is what kati was built for. It is wrong when the destination runs the build itself. So it became `BuildSink::shell_evaluation`, beside the `new_inputs_timing` that already splits the same way for `$?`: a manifest writer keeps `RecipeShell`, Ronin's `GraphSink` asks for `Expansion`, and `NinjaWriter` and the equivalence `Tee` keep the default, so the manifest and the graph it is compared against still agree.\n\nThe GNU matrix, all verified rather than assumed. `func_shell_base` hands `construct_command_argv` a zero for cmd_flags, so `$(shell)` runs `$(SHELL)` with `.SHELLFLAGS` — `SHELL := /bin/echo` makes `$(shell hello world)` answer `-c hello world`, which shows both at once — and `.POSIX:` keeps its `-e` because no `COMMANDS_NOERROR` ever arrives. Environment: `target_environment(NULL, 0)`, whose NULL means `current_variable_set_list` rather than no scope at all. The brief's expectation was that target-specific exports would not be visible; the oracle says they are. `all: export TS = t` reaches a `$(shell)` in `all`'s recipe, and reaches one in a prerequisite's recipe too, because recipe expansion happens with the file's variable set installed. MAKELEVEL arrives incremented, SHELL is the environment's rather than Make's, and MAKEFLAGS is the run's switches (` -j4` under `-j4`) with the jobserver auth invalidated. Output: interior newlines become spaces, `\\r` is dropped only when a `\\n` follows it, the whole trailing run goes for `$(shell)` and all but one of it for `!=`, and a NUL ends the output because GNU Make reads it as a C string. `.SHELLSTATUS` is an `o_override` variable, unset before the first call, 127 for a command that was not there, and 128 plus the signal for one that was killed; a failing `$(shell)` is not a Make error and the run still exits 0. Timing: `:=` runs it once at read, `=` at every expansion, a function arm only when the arm is taken, and a recipe line's during expansion — so its side effect lands before any of the commands that line became. `$(shell $(MAKE) ...)` sees MAKELEVEL 1 and no usable jobserver; Ronin's child sees MAKELEVEL 0, which is the environment gap that belongs to make-export-scope-semantics rather than here.\n\nThree cells of that matrix Ronin also got wrong, all in what comes back rather than in who runs it, and all fixed here because they are one GNU function: `fold_newlines` is now translated exactly, including the CRLF rule and the NUL, and a signalled command reports 128 plus the signal where it used to report 1.\n\nMarkers. function-shell and variable-curdir are gone. function-shell-sees-exported-variable stays: the environment a `$(shell)` child gets is make-export-scope-semantics's, and this node did not touch it.\n\nSeven fixtures recorded from the oracle: function-shell-composes-inside-a-recipe, function-shell-runs-before-the-recipe-line, function-shell-folds-line-endings, function-shell-output-stops-at-a-nul, function-shell-status-after-a-signal, function-shell-uses-shell-and-shellflags, function-shell-runs-once-for-a-simple-value. Four kati unit tests: the two halves of the fold, and one test per side of the sink's choice.\n\nUpstream inventory, 1287 rows, five moved and none the wrong way. `functions/intcmp.diff` compiler → narration: the `$(shell echo lt)` arm was this node's refusal, as the previous dispatch said, and only the progress line is left. `functions/shell.diff.4` and `.16` unclassified/interface → narration, an exported variable read through a recursive value in a recipe and `.SHELLSTATUS` after a SIGINT, both now right. `functions/shell.diff.14` lost `ninja-failure-block`: a `$(shell)` naming a missing program used to be written into the recipe and fail the build, and now it runs, reports 127 and lets the build finish. `features/reinvoke.diff.2` is the known flapper and contains no `$(shell)` at all, so its move cannot be this change.\n\nThree defects found and left as follow-ups under make-subsumption rather than folded in: make-lazy-recipe-expansion (Ronin expands every reachable recipe at graph construction, so an up-to-date target's `$(shell)` side effect now fires anyway — the eagerness predates this and reaches `$(wildcard)` too, but answering `$(shell)` during expansion is what made it observable), make-oracle-recording-trailing-newline (the build-intent recording writes a signal for \"no trailing newline\" that its own reader ignores, so such a case can never match), and make-simple-command-without-a-shell (GNU Make execs a command with no shell syntax directly, so it reports a missing program itself where Ronin reports what /bin/sh said)."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:46:50.919257137Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

