                    {
                        id make-wildcard-cache-coherence
                        title "Refresh wildcard results after evaluation-time filesystem changes"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (kati-caches-session make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:30.822944791Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns feature-wildcard-sees-a-file-made-while-reading and recorded wildcard_cache.mk#test. Evaluation-time shell effects can change directory contents, so wildcard results must obey GNU Make's observation points instead of a session-long stale cache."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:04.13014625Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T23:24:37.117921048Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:00:39.518785508Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved, and the mechanism read out of `src/dir.c` rather than guessed at.\n\nThe mechanism. GNU does cache directory contents, and the cache is not keyed on time or on mtime: `find_directory` returns what it read only while the entry's `counter` still equals the global `command_count`, and otherwise calls `clear_directory_contents` and reads the directory again. `command_count` starts at 1 and is incremented in exactly three places: `job.c` when a child is reaped, `function.c` in `func_file` when it opens a file for writing (with a comment saying it is doing so because it may have changed a directory), and `function.c` for a user-defined function loaded through `load` that declared `adds_command`. Nothing else moves it. So the whole rule is: a command ran, therefore what was read before it is hearsay — and it ages every directory at once, not the one the command is guessed to have touched.\n\nThe consequence for a makefile, which is the whole finding: a makefile can only change the filesystem by running a command. `$(shell)` forks and is reaped; `$(file >)` and `$(file >>)` bump the counter themselves; a recipe is a child that is waited for. Every one of those bumps the counter, so the cache is never observably stale from inside a makefile. GNU's directory cache is memoisation within a quiescent period, not a semantic. \"Match GNU exactly\" and \"always fresh after anything that could have changed the filesystem\" are the same rule here, and there is no wart left to record.\n\nProbed cells, all against `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make`, all fresh:\n  - `$(wildcard *.p)` before and after `$(shell ... > new.p)`: second sees it.\n  - the same by literal name, `$(wildcard new.p)`: sees it.\n  - `$(file > new.p,x)` between two wildcards: second sees it (no shell involved).\n  - deletion, `$(shell rm)`, by pattern and by name: second loses it.\n  - `$(shell mkdir sub && ... > sub/a)` then `$(wildcard sub)` and `$(wildcard sub/*)`: both see it; a directory removed with `rm -rf` and made again is read again too.\n  - two wildcards and the shell call between them inside one variable's value: the second reads again.\n  - recipe launch time, both directions: a recipe expanded after another recipe made or removed a file sees or does not see it, even when the same pattern was already answered while the makefile was read.\n  - across a makefile-remaking restart: read again, trivially — GNU re-execs.\n  - `.SECONDEXPANSION` prerequisite globbing after a `$(shell)`: fresh.\n\nThe cache is real and was measured, not assumed. Three identical `$(wildcard *.p)` with nothing between them cost one directory read (2 `getdents64`); the same three with `$(shell true)` between them cost three (6). `--debug=v` prints one \"Directory . cache invalidated (count 1 != command 2)\" for the third. One asymmetry exists at the syscall level and is not observable from a makefile: a pattern with no metacharacters is answered by glibc's `glob` through `gl_lstat`, which dir.c leaves as the real `lstat`, so a literal `$(wildcard f)` stats every time and never consults the cache at all. Since nothing can change the filesystem without bumping the counter, caching literals as patterns are cached is indistinguishable, and it is what kati now does — the reads are worth keeping.\n\nWhere the fix went. `GlobCache` in kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs is now a struct with an epoch, which is `command_count` under its own name: an entry carries the epoch it was read at, and a lookup at a later epoch reads again. `Session::note_command_ran` bumps it, and is called from `shell_func_impl` (covering `$(shell)`, `KATI_shell_no_rerun`, and the shell assignment operator, including the find-emulator path, which stands in for a command GNU would have run), from `file_write_func` for both `>` and `>>`, from `DeferredRecipes::expand` at recipe launch, and from kati's own `--exec` runner after each command it waits for.\n\nThe regeneration stamp had to be separated from the cache, which had been serving as both. An entry now keeps `first` alongside `current`, and `GlobCache::recorded` gives the stamp the first answer rather than the last: the regeneration check runs before any of the makefile's commands do, so the answer it can meaningfully compare a later filesystem against is the one the read started from. For every makefile that does not change the filesystem mid-read the two are the same value, which is what the stamp recorded before.\n\nNegative control: with `invalidate` stubbed out, seven of the eight corpus cases fail with exactly the stale answers. The eighth is the restart case, which passes either way because `prepare_graph` builds a fresh session per pass — worth keeping as a lock on that."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:02:17.239295061Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-grouped-pattern-targets
                        title "Compile grouped pattern rules as one multi-output action"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-grouped-double-colon-actions make-pattern-rule-candidate-identity)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:31.031498576Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns target-grouped-pattern-with-intermediates and the remaining grouped_targets upstream residue. Extend the already-correct exact grouped action model to pattern rules: one recipe instance produces all peers, automatic variables name the triggering output correctly, and intermediate handling does not split the action."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:04.341788496Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:39:27.306599434Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:40:56.823180797Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle first, and the matrix says the grouping was never the missing piece — telling the group's members apart was.\n\n  cell                                            GNU 4.4.1\n  `%.tab.c %.tab.h: %.y`, both asked for          one run, `$@` = the first\n  `%.tab.c %.tab.h &: %.y`, both asked for        identical in every respect\n  `%.c %.h:: %.in` (terminal), `&::`              identical too\n  `$@`                                            the name whose own state\n                                                  reached the rule\n  `$*`                                            the stem, whichever name it was\n  peer absent, nobody asked for it                nothing to be done\n  peer absent, asked for                          the recipe runs\n  peer stale, asked for, target current           the recipe runs\n  peer asked for by a later rule                  a target of its own from then on\n  `-j4`                                           one job, never two\n  recipe writes only one of the names             success, plus a warning\n  the same shape on an explicit `&:` rule         no warning, and the whole group\n                                                  is one decision\n  peer of an invented intermediate                kept; only the invented name goes\n  `.PRECIOUS: %.w` on the peer                    changes nothing — already kept\n\nSo `&:` on a pattern rule asks for what several target patterns already meant, exactly as the manual says, and the two forms are indistinguishable through the oracle. The real division is between pattern grouping and explicit `&:` grouping: an explicit group is one decision over all its members, and a pattern rule is one recipe over targets that each decide for themselves. `implicit.c` is where that is written down — the peers go into `also_make` and each gets `is_target = 1`, \"so that this file is not treated as intermediate by the pattern rule search algorithm\".\n\nRonin had the recipe right and the identity wrong. kati recorded every peer as an ordinary implicit output, so a name nobody asked for was indistinguishable from one that was: absent, it made the edge dirty forever; present, it was swept up beside the intermediate the search had invented. Both owned symptoms fall out of that one confusion, and so do the two adjacent nodes.\n\nThe change is a list, not a flag, for the reason `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` already carries one: the distinction is per output. kati's DepNode gains `peer_outputs`, a name reached in its own right is retained out of it while the graph is walked, and the sink publishes it. Ronin keeps it beside the edge arena in `src/graph/peer.rs` and reads it in two places: the freshness comparison skips peers, and `Plan::disposable_outputs` spares them. Peers stay outputs — the edge is still what writes them, and a failed recipe still withdraws them.\n\nMarkers. All three owned or adjacent markers are gone: target-grouped-pattern-with-intermediates (this node), multi-target-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate and include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule (the two adjacent nodes, both closed with this).\n\nFixtures, eleven recorded from the oracle. Ten gate: multi-target-pattern-rule-runs-once-for-both, -grouped-rule-runs-once-for-both, -double-colon-groups-the-same-way, -grouped-double-colon-groups-the-same-way, -absent-peer-is-not-asked-about, -absent-peer-that-was-asked-for-is-made, -stale-peer-that-was-asked-for-is-made, -peer-reached-later-is-a-target-of-its-own, -rule-is-one-job-in-parallel, -recipe-may-leave-a-peer-unmade. The eleventh, multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date, is the reproducer for the one cell that did not close and carries a divergence marker. Two Ronin tests assert the compiled graph directly — which output is the peer, and that being asked for by name takes it off the list — and say that a parsed manifest carries neither.\n\nWhat did not close, and why it is a node rather than a silence. `$@` still names the first output the walk reached where GNU names the one that was out of date. Everything else in the automatic-variable cell agrees, and from scratch the two answers coincide, which is why the other ten fixtures gate. It is a run-time binding question rather than a compilation one — `$@` is substituted when kati compiles the recipe — so it is filed as make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity with the reproducer above. GNU's peer warning is filed beside it as make-pattern-peer-warning: its build intent gates, its text cannot, and adding a line to stderr moves upstream inventory rows, which is a decision worth taking on its own.\n"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:41:13.172823307Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Gates, all run under scripts/sandboxed.\n\n  cargo fmt --all --check                        clean\n  cargo check --workspace --all-targets          clean\n  cargo test --workspace                         750 passed, 1 ignored, 0 failed\n  cargo test --workspace --all-targets           790 passed, 787 at baseline;\n                                                 the three added are this\n                                                 node's own — one in\n                                                 src/graph/peer.rs and two in\n                                                 src/make/equivalence.rs\n  clippy, ronin lib+bins --no-deps, pedantic\n    and nursery                                  clean\n  clippy -p kati --all-targets -D warnings       clean\n  Make build-intent oracle                       441 cases run, 430 at\n                                                 baseline; 430 must match and\n                                                 do, 11 are marked as diverging\n                                                 and do. Eleven fixtures added,\n                                                 three markers removed, one\n                                                 added for the follow-up\n  scripts/check-make-conformance.sh              387 runs, 309 identical /\n                                                 78 differing — the baseline\n                                                 exactly\n  scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh              297 makefiles, 276 graphs\n                                                 compared, 0 disagreements\n  scripts/check-make-upstream.sh                 1287 rows, unclassified 504 /\n                                                 compiler 37 / interface 44 /\n                                                 narration 702 — every count\n                                                 the baseline's, exit 0, run\n                                                 twice\n\nNot one upstream inventory row moved, which is worth saying because movement was expected: GNU's own features/patternrules cases are where the grouped shapes live. They are there — 72 rows for that file alone — but the rows Ronin differs on differ over narration, and the change is to which files a build leaves behind rather than to what it prints while leaving them.\n\nThe known flapper features/patternrules.diff.34 is the upstream face of make-pattern-peer-restart-convergence, which this change closes. It held its recorded value, ninja-progress+no-work-line+up-to-date-line+evaluation, across two full gate runs — the `no work to do` face, which is the converged one. Dispatch #14 recorded four focused runs splitting two-and-two between that and `dirty after 100 tries`; the reproducer that made the second face is now deterministic and green in the build-intent corpus. The other two flappers, features/reinvoke.diff.2 and features/parallelism.diff.1, did not flap in these runs and were not touched.\n\nThe combined Clippy line in scripts/check-release.sh is red as it was before, on examples and tests this change does not touch.\n"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T00:41:45.341629084Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-static-pattern-nonmatch-semantics
                        title "Warn and continue for a static-pattern target that does not match"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:31.22865597Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns target-static-pattern-target-must-match. GNU Make warns when a listed static-pattern target misses the target pattern but continues to build applicable work; Kati currently turns that condition into a fatal refusal."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:04.54917Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:04:06.821386619Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:04:18.599914457Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU 4.4.1 matrix, probed cell by cell against the pinned oracle. A static-pattern target that misses the target pattern is diagnosed by `record_files` through `error`, not `fatal`: the message goes to stderr, the read continues, and make still exits 0. What the target keeps and loses is decided by one `else` in read.c — the prerequisite chain is copied only in the branch the mismatch diagnostic skipped, while the stem is set unconditionally a few lines later by substituting the name into a bare `%`. So the nonmatching target keeps its recipe, keeps whatever prerequisites other rules gave it, takes NONE of the static pattern's prerequisites (normal, order-only, and the deferred chain under .SECONDEXPANSION alike), and reads `$*` as its own whole name rather than as the empty string a failed match would give. Its matching siblings in the same target list are unaffected and get ordinary stems. Errors, by contrast, are reserved for a malformed rule rather than a mismatched target: two patterns in the target-pattern position and a target pattern with no `%` are both `*** ... Stop.` at exit 2, and GNU reaches neither after a match test — it settles the pattern while parsing the line and only tests targets later, so a two-pattern rule dies on that alone with no mismatch warning first."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:04:31.950292517Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Ronin's divergence was narrower than the marker read, and worse where it landed. The parse-time warning was already right; the failure was downstream. `apply_output_pattern` substituted the target into the prerequisite pattern through `Pattern::append_subst`, whose non-match branch returns the input unchanged — so `mismatch: %.o: %.c` gave `mismatch` its own name as a prerequisite and the build died on `dependency cycle: mismatch -> mismatch`. Under .SECONDEXPANSION the same target instead got a prerequisite built from an empty stem. Both are now decided by one predicate, `prerequisites_reach`, applied at `apply_output_pattern` and at each of the three deferred-prerequisite sites plus `declared_by`, which is the same question GNU's `else` asks. `$*` moved from `Pattern::stem` to substitution into a bare `%`, which is how GNU derives the stem and is the only spelling that answers both cells: identical for a target that matched, the whole name for one that did not.\n\nProbing the empty-stem cell found a live panic underneath. `Pattern::match_impl` tested prefix and suffix without checking they fit side by side, so `%.o` \"matched\" `.o` and `Pattern::stem` then computed `str.len() - pat.len()` and overflowed, and `a%a` \"matched\" `a` and produced a reversed slice. Neither needs a static pattern to reach: `$(patsubst a%a,X%X,a)` panicked outright. Fixed with GNU's own length test in `pattern_matches` and by taking the subtraction last, and pinned by `pattern-halves-cannot-overlap`, which records the plain patsubst answers."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:06:27.893713693Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-pattern-rule-cancellation
                        title "Cancel a pattern rule on recipe-less redeclaration"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-pattern-rule-candidate-identity make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:31.463403661Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns feature-pattern-rule-is-cancelled. A recipe-less redeclaration cancels the matching pattern rule in GNU Make's rule table rather than leaving the old candidate selectable."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:04.771571317Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:39:46.150040013Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:40:07.649948586Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU 4.4.1 probed before anything moved. Cancellation is two mechanisms, and Ronin already had one of them.\n\nThe first is `new_pattern_rule` (rule.c:383), which read.c:2137 calls with override on: a pattern rule whose targets and prerequisites match one already in the list removes that one and reinstalls itself at the end. `replaces_pattern_rule` in dep.rs has done that since make-pattern-rule-candidate-identity, and every probe of it held — `%.gen: %.src` withdraws `%.gen: %.src` but not `%.gen: %.alt`; `%.gen: %.src %.alt` withdraws `%.gen: %.src | %.alt`, because `dep_name` reads one chain and the bar is not in it; a later two-target rule withdraws a one-target rule it covers and the reverse does not; the double colon is not part of the name either; a third rule of the same name takes it back; and the reinstall at the end is observable — with `%.gen: %.src`, `%.gen: %.alt`, `%.gen: %.src` again, GNU runs the `%.alt` recipe, because the rewritten rule is now last.\n\nThe second is implicit.c:320, `if (rule->deps != 0 && rule->cmds == 0) continue;`, and Ronin did not have it. A recipe-less rule is never collected as a candidate, so cancelling means the target ends with no rule at all rather than a rule that makes it out of nothing. That is the whole of the divergence feature-pattern-rule-is-cancelled recorded: Ronin picked the recipe-less rule, wrote nothing, and exited 0 where GNU says `No rule to make target`.\n\nThe prerequisite question, which the dispatch asked to settle definitively: a recipe-less pattern rule adds NO prerequisites to anything. `%.gen: extra.h` beside `%.gen: %.src ; …` leaves `$^` as `foo.src` alone, written on either side of the working rule; and `foo.gen: ; …` beside `%.gen: %.src` leaves `$^` empty. A rule the search never collects contributes neither recipe nor prerequisite. Section 10.5.6 is the whole story; there is no pattern form that adds prerequisites without a recipe.\n\nThe `deps == 0 && cmds == 0` form is separate and also never used (implicit.c:409): `%.gen:` alone withdraws nothing, because it does not match the identity of a rule that has prerequisites. What it does do is set `specific_rule_matched`, which retroactively rejects non-terminal match-anything rules — that half is NOT implemented here and is filed as make-pattern-search-specific-rule-match.\n\nSuffix rules turned out to be part of the same question. GNU converts them in `snap_deps`, after the last makefile, and rule.c:344 passes over a suffix pair whose file has no recipe, so `.src.gen:` alone is not a rule and writing it beside a `.src.gen:` that has one withdraws nothing. `convert_suffix_rule` then installs through `new_pattern_rule` with override OFF (rule.c:284), so a written pattern rule that already holds the identity keeps it and the suffix-derived rule is destroyed — which is how a recipe-less `%.gen: %.src` cancels `.src.gen:`, in either written order. Built-in rules are cancelled by exactly that same override-off path (main.c installs them last); Ronin installs no built-in rules and reaches the same answer by the shorter road, which the recorded case says out loud."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:40:59.189909566Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "What changed, all in kati/src-rs/dep.rs.\n\n`ordered_candidates` no longer collects a rule with no recipe — implicit.c:320 and :409 in one line, since Ronin does not yet keep `specific_rule_matched` and both GNU conditions come to the same refusal without it. `implicit_chain_exists` had been making that test for itself; it now reads only its own step 6a.\n\n`populate_suffix_rule` returns early on a rule with no recipe (rule.c:344), so `.src.gen:` alone never becomes anything and writing it beside a `.src.gen:` that has a recipe withdraws nothing.\n\n`discard_suffix_rules_a_pattern_rule_holds` runs once the last rule is populated and throws away every suffix rule whose identity a written pattern rule already holds — `new_pattern_rule` with override off, the other direction of the comparison `replaces_pattern_rule` already makes, so the target half of it is now `pattern_rule_targets_match` and both callers use it. Without this the first change would have been a regression: `%.gen: %.src` with no recipe stopped cancelling `.src.gen:` and let the suffix rule through instead.\n\nMarker resolved: tests/make/feature-pattern-rule-is-cancelled/divergence deleted; the case matches. Nine markers remain, none of them this node's.\n\nFourteen cases recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle, all passing against Ronin on the first run after the change: feature-pattern-rule-cancel-needs-the-same-prerequisites, feature-pattern-rule-cancel-ignores-the-order-only-bar, feature-pattern-rule-cancelled-by-a-longer-target-list, feature-pattern-rule-cancel-passes-over-a-longer-target-list, feature-pattern-rule-cancelled-from-an-included-makefile, feature-pattern-rule-redefinition-brings-it-back, feature-pattern-rule-redefinition-moves-it-last, feature-double-colon-pattern-rule-is-cancelled, feature-recipe-less-pattern-rule-adds-no-prerequisite, feature-recipe-less-pattern-rule-leaves-an-explicit-rule-alone, feature-recipe-less-pattern-rule-cancels-a-built-in-rule, feature-pattern-rule-cancels-a-suffix-rule, feature-recipe-less-suffix-rule-is-not-a-rule, feature-recipe-less-suffix-redeclaration-keeps-the-recipe. They use .src/.gen/.alt rather than .w/.tex so that no built-in rule can decide a case by accident; the one case that is about built-in cancellation says so and uses .o/.c deliberately.\n\nGates. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 750 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is also what 97847a4 gives in a clean worktree — the dispatch's 790 was stale, no test was added or lost. Build-intent corpus 466 cases, all passing (452 + 14). Make conformance 387 runs, 309 normalised identical / 78 differing, unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 276 compared / 0 disagreements. Ninja conformance 425 upstream + 10 differential + 86 build-outcome + 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log content. Real-project gate builds ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 21s at -j8. Clippy: ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery clean, kati `-D warnings` clean.\n\nUpstream inventory, 1287 rows, five moved and every one audited against a base binary built from 97847a4 in a separate worktree. Class counts unclassified 504 → 502, narration 702 → 704; compiler 37 and interface 169... (interface 44) unchanged.\n\n  features/implicit_search.diff.77 unclassified → narration, product-name+refusal-attribution. `all: hello.o` / `unrelated: hello.c` / `%.o: %.f`. Before, Ronin took the recipe-less rule and said `no work to do`; now it says `'hello.o', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`. GNU refuses one link further in — `No rule to make target 'hello.c', needed by 'hello.o'` — because its built-in `%.o: %.c` matched first. Both refuse; they name different links.\n\n  features/patternrules.diff.8 unclassified → narration, product-name+shared-refusal. The case is GNU's own `# Disable builtin rules` idiom: `%.tex : %.w` then `%.tex : %.w %.ch`, both recipe-less. Before `no work to do`; now `'foo.tex' missing and no known rule to make it` against GNU's `No rule to make target 'foo.tex'`. Same refusal, different words.\n\n  features/vpathplus.diff.1, .2 and .3 stay unclassified; their family moves from no-work-line to ninja-progress. The makefile opens with `%.a:` `%.b:` `%.c:` `%.d:` — four bare rules that in GNU exist only to set `specific_rule_matched`. Ronin had been treating them as rules, so all three cases did nothing at all. They now run the chain: .2 runs GNU's three commands exactly, .1 runs them and fails as GNU does, and .3 differs on where the intermediate is written (work/inter.c against GNU's inter.c), which is make-vpath-gpath-output-location's territory and was simply invisible while nothing ran.\n\n  variables/undefine.diff.4 stays narration; family ninja-progress+no-work-line → ninja-progress. The row dispatch #5 predicted. `%:undefine` gave the Makefile itself a rule, which made it a regeneration root; with the recipe-less rule passed over, the Makefile has no rule, there is no restart, and the trailing `ronin: no work to do.` is gone. Ronin's `[1/1]` progress line is all that is left.\n\n  The three known flappers — features/reinvoke.diff.2, features/patternrules.diff.34, features/parallelism.diff.1 — did not move.\n\nAdjacent defect filed rather than fixed here: make-pattern-search-specific-rule-match, with its reproducer."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T01:42:21.998182785Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-continuation-expansion
                        title "Preserve dollar and recipe semantics across backslash-newline continuation"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-parser-parity make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:31.685819199Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns variable-line-continuation-dollar-backslash and recorded multiline_recipe.mk#test6. Preserve GNU Make's dollar/backslash treatment across logical-line folding and recipe continuation so the shell receives the same variable syntax and command text."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:04.965110281Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T02:02:04.495392235Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T02:22:12.682540694Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 matrix, probed cell by cell against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before any change.\n\nVariable, define, conditional, include and rule lines all fold the same way, because GNU folds the whole logical line once (misc.c collapse_continuations) before it reads any of it. A run of backslashes ending a line continues it only when the run is odd; half the run survives (`a\\\\\\` folds to `a\\`, `a\\\\\\\\\\` to `a\\\\`), and the newline together with the blanks on both sides of it becomes exactly one space. An even run does not continue the line at all, which is why `A = a\\\\` followed by `  b` is a missing separator in both tools.\n\nBecause the fold happens first, a `$` written against the continuation takes as its name whatever the fold left beside it: a space, so `x$\\<nl>  y` is `x` then the (unset) variable named \" \" then `y`, which is `xy`; or, when the run leaves backslashes, the first of them, so `x$\\\\\\<nl>  y` is `x y`. The same rule is what makes `$(FO$\\<nl>  O)` read `FOO` and `$(subst $\\<nl>  ,X,a b)` substitute an empty string.\n\nA recipe is the exception in both halves. GNU does not fold there — the backslash and the newline both go to the shell — and the only thing it removes is one recipe prefix character following the newline (job.c start_job_command), which is `.RECIPEPREFIX` and nothing else: a second tab stays, spaces stay, and under `.RECIPEPREFIX = >` a tab is ordinary text. A recipe written after a literal `;` is continued the same way. `.POSIX:` changes nothing about a recipe continuation in 4.4.1 — recorded byte for byte identical — but it does change a variable's: the blanks before the fold are then kept.\n\nDelivered in kati: expr.rs now folds an odd backslash run by halving it and reads a `$` against a continuation as GNU does; strutil.rs gained strip_recipe_prefix_continuations, applied to a recipe line in parser.rs and to a `;` recipe in eval.rs through a recipe_prefix recorded on RuleStmt at read time; ninja.rs translate_command now lets a comment end its line rather than carrying the comment's last byte into the text after it.\n\nFour adjacent defects filed rather than fixed here: make-recipe-expanded-newline, make-recipe-continuation-bytes, make-posix-continuation-blanks, make-trailing-dollar-in-a-value."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T02:28:31.087189546Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-vpath-gpath-output-location
                        title "Rebuild VPATH-found targets in their GPATH directory"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-vpath-search make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:31.897791417Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Follow-up to completed make-vpath-search. Owns feature-gpath-keeps-the-found-directory: when GPATH retains a VPATH-found name, rebuild the target at that found path instead of relocating the output to the current directory."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:05.133302043Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T02:32:12.036909572Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T03:10:25.741148099Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The GNU matrix, probed against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written. A target that does not exist here and that the directory search finds at D/T:\n\n  D in GPATH, stale → remade at D/T. $@ is D/T and every dependent reads D/T.\n  D in GPATH, current → remade nowhere; dependents read D/T.\n  D not in GPATH, stale → the found name is thrown away and the target is remade here as T; dependents read T.\n  D not in GPATH, current → remade nowhere; dependents read D/T.\n\nThe first two are one answer, not two: GPATH renames the file object (remake.c f_mtime → gpath_search → rename_file) whatever the target's state, so where it goes is settled before anything asks whether it has to go anywhere. That makes it a compile-time decision and it is the half this node delivered. The last two differ only in whether the target has to be remade, which update_file_1 settles after the prerequisites are updated, and a single static output name cannot carry both — that is make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target.\n\nTwo further cells the probe pinned down. The rename happens before the implicit rule search, so `%.o: %.c` under GPATH searches build/out.o, wants build/out.c, finds none and does nothing — kati already reached this shape because at_vpath rewrites a rule-less prerequisite first, so build_plan is handed the found name and pattern-matches it. And GPATH is compared against the search entry rather than the whole prefix of the found path: `GPATH = build` answers a target named sub/out.o found at build/sub/out.o, and `GPATH = build/sub` does not.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati/src-rs/dep.rs. at_vpath returns whether GPATH is what kept the answer and no longer declines a name that has a rule of its own when it did; the rename is recorded in gpath_origin, and lookup_rule_merger and lookup_rule_vars read through it, which is kati's way of doing what rename_file does by moving one object. Goals go through at_gpath in build() before ev.goals is read, because ev.goals is what the manifest is aimed at and a goal renamed in the graph but not there is asked for under a name nothing produces. gpath_directories reads $(GPATH) once DepBuilder::new has finished the read, where build_vpath_lists reads it; search_path and search_directory are free functions with unit tests, which is why they are free functions.\n\nMarker resolved: tests/make/feature-gpath-keeps-the-found-directory/divergence deleted. Six markers remain, two of them newly this node's recording of what it did not do.\n\nFourteen cases recorded from the oracle, twelve of them passing against Ronin: feature-gpath-leaves-a-current-target-where-it-found-it, feature-vpath-remakes-a-stale-target-in-the-current-directory, feature-gpath-passes-over-a-directory-it-does-not-name, feature-gpath-names-one-of-several-search-directories, feature-gpath-answers-a-vpath-directive, feature-gpath-reads-past-a-trailing-slash, feature-gpath-names-the-search-directory-alone, feature-gpath-does-not-name-the-whole-found-prefix, feature-gpath-renames-before-the-implicit-rule-search, feature-gpath-reaches-a-goal-that-was-named, feature-gpath-says-nothing-without-a-search, feature-gpath-is-read-once-the-read-has-finished. The remaining two — feature-vpath-keeps-a-current-target-that-has-a-rule and feature-vpath-relocates-a-target-an-implicit-rule-remakes — carry divergence markers naming the follow-up.\n\nGates. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace 758 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, 756 baseline plus the two new unit tests. Build-intent corpus 493 cases, all matching or explained (479 + 14). Make conformance 387 runs, 310 normalised identical / 77 differing, unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 276 compared / 0 disagreements. Ninja conformance 425 upstream + 10 differential + 86 build-outcome + 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log content. Real-project gate builds ninja 1.14.0.git in 21s at -j8. Clippy: ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery clean, kati -D warnings clean.\n\nUpstream inventory 1287 rows, zero drift: unclassified 491, compiler 37, interface 44, narration 715, every family count unchanged and the three known flappers unmoved. Nothing moved and nothing was expected to. The suite's one GPATH case, features/vpathgpath.diff, is a build where nothing is out of date, so both tools do nothing and it was already narration/no-work-line before this and after it. features/vpathplus does not set GPATH at all: its .diff.3 is the non-GPATH stale cell one chain deeper, and it stays where dispatch #18 left it until the follow-up lands. The pre-existing targets/clean abort is still there.\n\nAdjacent thing seen and not chased: a static pattern rule's prerequisites are recorded per target at read time in GNU Make, so a GPATH rename carries them along, where kati re-applies the target pattern to the renamed name and would derive build/out.c from build/out.o. No marker; noted here."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T03:11:20.970686924Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-export-scope-semantics
                        title "Complete export, unexport, and target-specific export scope"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-export-to-recipes make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:32.104453259Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Follow-up to completed make-export-to-recipes. Owns feature-target-specific-export, feature-unexport-outranks-export-all, function-shell-sees-exported-variable, and recorded export_export.mk#test. Resolve bare export, later named unexport, target-specific export, and the environment seen by evaluation-time shell consistently."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:05.311962264Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T04:28:32.473762779Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T04:29:12.266389178Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle across the whole matrix before anything moved, and the defect turned out to be one missing structure rather than a list of missing features: kati recorded exports in a side map keyed by name (`Evaluator::exports: HashMap<Symbol, bool>`), and GNU records them on the variable (`v->export`). Everything this node owned falls out of that difference, so the fix was to put the attribute where GNU keeps it and translate `target_environment` beside it as `kati::export`.\n\nThe matrix, all verified rather than assumed.\n\nDirective timing. Named `export V` / `unexport V` set an attribute at read time and the last directive to name a variable wins, wherever it sits — including after the rule whose recipe reads the name, because the set is consulted when a child is started and not where it is written. The value is likewise the value at spawn time: `export V` then `V = two` hands out `two`. Bare `export` and bare `unexport` are one switch, `export_all_variables`, thrown at read time; `.EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES:` is a target and its targets are read after the whole makefile, so it outranks a bare `unexport` written anywhere while a *named* `unexport one` still outranks it. Bareness is decided on the unexpanded text: `export $(EMPTY)` names an empty list and exports nothing.\n\nWhat a bare export reaches. Origin decides: `o_default` and `o_automatic` never (`CC`, `MAKE`, `$@` stay out), `o_file` and `o_override` only under the switch, `o_command` and `o_env` always and without being asked. Then a name filter — leading letter or underscore, then letters, digits, underscores — which is why `A.B` and `1AB` are skipped. The filter is only on the unnamed path: `export A.B = x` puts `A.B` in the child's envp, which is observable only there, since the shell a recipe runs under purges such a name while it starts. That cell is a kati unit test for exactly that reason.\n\nTarget-specific export. `target: export V = x` reaches that target's recipe and every recipe below it, because it is the ordinary target-specific inheritance carrying one more attribute; `private` stops it at the target, and a pattern-specific `%.x: export V` reaches what the pattern matched. `unexport` is a modifier in that position too and withholds a name the global set exported. The merge runs the other way when the target-specific binding says nothing: `all: V = local` beside a global `export V` is exported, with the target's value. A `private` global lends nothing — it is invisible from a target's scope rather than merely unexported, so a target-specific binding beside one answers for itself and answers no. There is no `all: unexport V` directive form: that parses as a prerequisite named `unexport`.\n\nCommand-line variables reach a recipe's environment directly as well as through MAKEFLAGS. Environment-origin variables pass through byte-identically and unexpanded, and keep the export attribute the import gave them across a reassignment, because GNU redefines a variable in place and `export` is not one of the fields a redefinition overwrites — `undefine` is what forgets. `SHELL` is marked withheld at import so a makefile's SHELL never reaches a child, and the invocation's own value is re-emitted; `export SHELL` overrides that with Make's. MAKELEVEL is set in every environment from the invocation's depth rather than from the variable, so assigning it changes what the makefile reads and not what its children are told. `$(origin)` is unaffected by export status throughout.\n\nWhat changed. In kati: `var::VarExport` and `Variable::export` with `is_exportable_name`; the attribute carried across redefinition in both `VarSet::assign` and `Vars::assign`; `AssignDirective::export` widened from a bool to the polarity, so `export`/`unexport` in front of a definition is a modifier of that definition as GNU's `parse_var_assignment` treats it — which is what makes `unexport V = x` define `V`, `export define` export, and `export V ?= x` decide `?=` against a name nothing has created yet; the bare form of the directive setting `flags.export_all_variables`; and a new `kati::export` holding `should_export` and `exported_environment`, which is `target_environment` and now answers for recipes, `$(shell)` and semantic children alike. `$(shell)` runs its child under it (`ChildKind::Expansion`, GNU's `target_environment(NULL)`), which needed GNU's `env_recursion` guard: an exported variable holding a `$(shell)` asks for its own value to answer the call that produces it, and GNU answers that from the invocation's environment rather than refusing the makefile. Per-target exports cross the sink as `SinkRule::recipe_environment` and `ExpandedRecipe::recipe_environment`, so the manifest writer and the graph it is compared against agree. In Ronin: `exported_environment` in `src/make.rs` is now the translation of kati's answer rather than a second implementation of it, and `CommandLayout::prefix` takes the target's overlay.\n\nMarkers. All three this node owned are gone: feature-target-specific-export, feature-unexport-outranks-export-all, function-shell-sees-exported-variable. `export_export.mk#test` is byte-identical and its inventory row is removed.\n\nTwenty-two fixtures recorded from the oracle and six kati unit tests, the latter for the three cells no build-intent case can hold: the envp-level name filter, an untouched environment variable not being rewritten, and the `private` global that a `$(shell)` reaches and a recipe does not.\n\nGates. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 764 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, up from 758 by the six new kati tests; Clippy pedantic+nursery on the Ronin lib and bins and plain `-D warnings` on kati both clean; build-intent oracle 515 cases green, up from 493; conformance 311 identical / 76 differing, up from 310/77 by export_export.mk; equivalence 297 makefiles / 276 compared / 0 disagreements.\n\nUpstream inventory, 1287 rows, twenty-five moved and none the wrong way. Twenty-three reached narration: features/export.diff.2 and .13, features/targetvars.diff.4, .5, .29, .30, .34-.38, .40, .41, .44 and .46, features/patspecific_vars.diff.4, variables/private.diff.4, .5, .6 and .14, variables/SHELL.diff.4, and functions/shell.diff.5 and .6 — every one of them a case whose behaviour is now GNU's with only the progress line left. The other two are the pair of `PATH` target-variable tests and they swapped: features/targetvars.diff.25 lost `ninja-failure-block` because `all: PATH := sd` now reaches the recipe and the helper is found, and .26 gained it because `all: PATH := ..` now reaches the recipe and the helper is correctly not found — GNU fails that one with 127 too, so both moved toward it. None of the three known flappers moved.\n\nOne defect found and left as a follow-up rather than folded in: make-makefiles-variable. GNU defines `MAKEFILES` as an `o_default` variable with the export-if-set attribute — `$(origin MAKEFILES)` is `default` where Ronin says `undefined` — and reads the makefiles it names. The `VarExport::IfSet` variant that cell needs was written and then removed here rather than left unconstructible."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T04:31:25.119006382Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

