                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T15:18:25.26886915Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The GNU restart matrix, probed against 4.4.1 before writing anything, and what each cell landed as.\n\nRemade and restarted: an included Makefile older than its prerequisite (MAKE_RESTARTS=1, second read sees the new text); a missing -include with a rule; a `::` rule that names a prerequisite; the Makefile named by -f remaking itself from a Makefile.in; two generated includes in one read costing one restart between them; a generated include that itself includes a generated include costing two. Not remade: an include already newer than its prerequisite; a missing -include with no rule; a Makefile declared .PHONY; a `::` recipe with no prerequisites. Fatal: a required include with no rule, unchanged.\n\nWhat decides a restart is the Makefile's timestamp, not that its rule ran. A recipe that fires and puts the file back untouched settles the read (probe: `touch -d 2020-01-01 $@`, one run, MAKE_RESTARTS empty); a recipe that writes back byte-identical text restarts anyway. There is no cap and no cycle detection beyond the two refused shapes: a `gen.mk: force` rule that really rewrites the file restarts GNU Make forever, which a 5s probe confirms by timing out. Ronin stops at the existing hundred-pass ManifestRetryLimit instead, which is not fixture-able either way.\n\nMAKE_RESTARTS is undefined on the first read and `1` after one restart, with origin `environment` and recursive flavour, and it is not in a recipe's environment — GNU sets v_noexport on it precisely because it arrives through the exec. Ronin reads again in place rather than re-executing, so the count is recorded into the session's invocation environment, which lands on the same origin and flavour and never reaches a recipe. Probed byte for byte.\n\nImplementation. kati 74ebff8 records every Makefile the read reached and plans a regeneration root for each one that has a rule and is neither of the two refused shapes. Ronin's prepare_graph keeps the passes it already had for generated includes and now decides between them the way GNU Make does: build what the provisional graph offers, and go around again only if a Makefile's timestamp moved. A pass in which nothing moved is the final compilation, so it returns its own graph and the logs it already opened rather than costing an extra evaluation — Persistence is documented as open-once-per-graph and that is now honoured across both phases. Loaded::remake_targets separates the Makefiles from the prerequisites staged for a recursive unit, so recursive composition still drives its own loop and never counts as a restart.\n\nSixteen recorded cases carry the matrix. The marker this node owned, feature-restart-count, is resolved and deleted.\n\nThree cells stay divergent and are now nodes with recorded reproducers rather than scope creep: make-remaking-ignores-goal-switches (-n and -q must not stop a Makefile being made), make-optional-include-rule-failure (a failed optional include is not a failed build), make-remake-work-not-repeated (an always-out-of-date remake rule runs once per phase because the two phases are two scheduler runs).\n\nThe pcre2 no-regression replay could not be run: /data/pkg-build/work/pcre2*/build.overlay/0/upper is an overlay upper layer whose lower is gone, so Makefile.in and the sources are absent and GNU Make 4.4.1 fails on it too. Replayed instead against a complete configured Automake tree — reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1, which is GNU Make's own autotools build — where `make -n` under both tools compiles the same single all-recursive step, neither runs config.status, and nothing in the tree is written. The five cases c6c544d left behind still pass."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T15:22:21.975530177Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-posix-recipe-semantics
                        title "Honor POSIX shell flags and ignored-failure continuation"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-ignore-errors-status make-recipe-one-shell-per-line)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:32.55377803Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Follow-up to completed make-ignore-errors-status and one-shell support. Owns target-posix-shellflags-take-precedence and target-posix-spares-an-ignored-failure: an explicit .SHELLFLAGS assignment outranks the .POSIX default, and a prefixed ignored failure does not prevent later recipe work."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:05.630970647Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T13:36:23.329973065Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T14:00:31.981623109Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Characterized GNU Make 4.4.1's shell-flag matrix against the oracle and reference/gnumake/src before implementing. The mechanism is two-part and both parts were missing from kati.\n\n(1) `.SHELLFLAGS` is a real variable. GNU binds it in main.c at `-c`/origin default (NOT in default.c's catalogue, so `-R` leaves it), and `.POSIX:` rebinds it to `-ec` at origin default via check_specials (read.c:1905). Any stronger origin — a Makefile assignment before or after `.POSIX:`, a target-specific assignment, the command line — outranks the default and is used verbatim. kati had no such variable at all: `get_shell_flag()` was a hardcoded `is_posix ? \"-ec\" : \"-c\"` with a standing TODO.\n\n(2) The `-e` is dropped per recipe LINE, from the `-` prefix alone. job.c:1280 hands `construct_command_argv` only `cmds->lines_flags[command_line - 1]`, not the combined `flags` that also carries `.IGNORE`; job.c:3784 then reads `posix_pedantic && origin == o_default` and answers `-c` when COMMANDS_NOERROR is set on that line. So `-i` and `.IGNORE` (global or targeted) ignore the failure but leave `-e` in place — under `.POSIX:` such a recipe still stops at its first failing command and reports \"Error N (ignored)\". Only the `-` prefix relaxes the shell. Verified empirically: `.POSIX:` + `.IGNORE:` + `false ; echo body > out` succeeds without producing `out`, while the same line prefixed `-` produces it.\n\nImplemented in kati (no Ronin change needed). `.SHELLFLAGS` is bound in `GlobalVars::with_builtins` beside `.RECIPEPREFIX` — chosen over a separate installer in builtins.rs so every Session has it, matching GNU's \"defined before anything runs\"; `-R` does not reach it because `undefine_default_variables` only withdraws names in DEFAULT_VARIABLES. `.POSIX:` substitution rides the existing POSIX_VARIABLES table and its `claimable` origin gate, which is what makes an explicit assignment win for free. `get_shell_flag(dash_prefixed)` mirrors job.c's three branches; it is called from command.rs per line while `current_scope` still holds the rule's variables, so a target-specific `.SHELLFLAGS` is honoured without new plumbing. `Command` gained `shell_flag` and `dash_prefixed` — the latter because kati's existing `ignore_error` conflates the prefix with `-i`/`.IGNORE` and the shell-flag decision must not.\n\nThe manifest path needed a decision the direct path did not. kati fuses a whole recipe into ONE `sh <flags> \"script\"`, so per-line flags cannot be expressed directly. Rather than emit nested shell invocations (which would rewrite every recipe's command text), the strict flags govern the script and a line that asked for relaxed flags takes `-e` off inside its own subshell with `set +e`. This is exact: within one recipe the only possible divergence is `-c` vs `-ec` from the POSIX default, prefixed lines always already have a subshell (the prefix forces `ignore_error`, which `force_no_subshell` never coexists with), and scripts with no divergence are byte-identical to before. Under `.ONESHELL` there is genuinely one invocation and GNU reads the first line's prefix for the whole script, so no compensation is applied there — confirmed against the oracle in both directions."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T14:12:26.740234136Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-recipe-eval-mutation-refusal
                        title "Reject recipe-time eval that mutates the dependency graph"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-refusal-reaches-further make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:32.775935015Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Follow-up to completed refusal-boundary work. Owns function-eval-prerequisite-in-recipe: recipe-time eval may not mutate the already-compiled dependency graph, and the front end must refuse the construct during compilation rather than silently accepting a different graph."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:05.784986845Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T04:51:44.538331009Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:02:23.434122809Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Probed GNU Make 4.4.1 before implementing, and the node's premise was wrong in the way that matters: GNU refuses this itself, so the deliverable is compatibility rather than a recorded divergence. The `divergence` marker on function-eval-prerequisite-in-recipe is deleted, not replaced.\n\nGNU's boundary is `snapped_deps` (read.c:2041, the first act of `record_files`; Savannah bug #12124). Once `snap_deps` has resolved the rules into files, any statement that would record a rule is fatal: `Makefile:N: *** prerequisites cannot be defined in recipes.  Stop.`, exit 2. The matrix, all recorded as fixtures:\n\n  REFUSED — reaches `record_files`\n    $(eval all: extra)                  a prerequisite for the target being built\n    $(eval later: ; @true)              a rule for a target not yet considered\n    $(eval b: ; @echo)                  a rule redefining a target still to come\n    $(eval %.o: %.c ; ...)              a pattern rule\n    $(eval .PHONY: fake)                a special target — it is a rule like any other\n    the same reached through a second expansion, not only through a recipe\n\n  ACCEPTED — never reaches it\n    $(eval V = x)                       and every flavour; read by the rest of THIS\n                                        recipe and by every later one\n    $(eval undefine X)                  origin goes to `undefined`\n    $(eval define D ... endef)\n    $(eval include other.mk)            the file is read and its variables land\n    $(eval vpath %.c src)\n    $(eval export V)                    accepted, and GNU's recipe sees it\n    $(eval tgt: VAR = value)            `record_target_var`, not `record_files`\n\nLine attribution: GNU names the recipe's FIRST line whatever line of it the eval is on, because `recursively_expand_for_file` sets `reading_file = &file->cmds->fileinfo` and `readstring` never advances the line counter inside an eval buffer. Ronin names the recipe line the eval is actually on. Kept deliberately: it is strictly more precise, stderr is not compared by any gate, and refusal wording is already recorded as narration rather than semantics (make-refusal-wording-is-narration).\n\nWhat Ronin did BEFORE, established by probe: it silently accepted every graph mutation and dropped it. `$(eval all: extra)` built `all` and never built `extra`; `$(eval b: ; @echo b-from-eval)` ran the original `b`. Worse, in one case it corrupted session state: `eval_rule_specific_assign` finished by setting `current_scope = None`, and at recipe-expansion time that scope is the recipe's own target scope — so `$(eval other: LOCAL = x)` in the middle of a recipe made every line after it read globals where GNU still read the target's variables.\n\nFixed in kati, three places, no Ronin change:\n\n  `Evaluator::rules_snapped` is GNU's `snapped_deps`. `DepBuilder::new` raises it after `populate_rules` has taken the rules and the special targets have been handled — the same point `snap_deps` raises GNU's, which is why the refusal reaches second expansion (it runs during `build`, after `new`) as GNU's does.\n\n  `eval_rule` refuses on it in `record_files`' position: after `parse_rule_assignment` has dispatched a target-specific assignment and before anything is recorded. That placement is the whole reason `$(eval all: LOCAL = x)` stays accepted while `$(eval all: extra)` does not.\n\n  `eval_rule_specific_assign` now saves and restores `current_scope` around the write instead of clearing it, so the recipe it interrupted goes on reading its own variables. Gated by function-eval-in-a-recipe-keeps-the-target-scope.\n\nDiagnostic shape follows make-fatal-diagnostic-shape: `ronin: Makefile:3: prerequisites cannot be defined in recipes.`, exit 2 — Ronin's identity and location prefix, GNU's sentence, without Make's `***` and `Stop.` runner wording.\n\nEleven fixtures recorded from the oracle. Two cells GNU accepts and Ronin still gets wrong are split out with their own recorded divergence markers rather than folded in: make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-the-scope and make-recipe-time-export-reaches-the-environment. Upstream movement: GNU's own negative test for this (features/se_explicit.diff.6, \"catch eval in a prereq list trying to create new target/prereq relationships\") left the `compiler` class — it was a compiler candidate only because Ronin accepted the eval and then found no rule for the target it had silently dropped — and functions/eval.diff.8, GNU's test for the refusal itself, moved from `no-work-line` (Ronin succeeded, doing nothing) to `product-name` (Ronin refuses, under its own name)."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:06:01.690436468Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-local-binding-assignment-scope
                        title "Keep eval assignments inside foreach and call bindings semantically scoped"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (kati-scoped-binding make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:32.997500581Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns recorded autovar_assign.mk#default and param.mk#test. Assignments performed through eval inside foreach and call must update the GNU Make binding that those constructs designate without leaking or overwriting their temporary loop/parameter bindings."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:05.94196303Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T04:51:48.420786064Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:02:50.211037233Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "One rule decides every cell, and GNU states it in a comment in `do_variable_definition` (variable.c): \"If we are defining variables inside an $(eval ...), we might have a different variable context pushed, not the global context (maybe we're inside a $(call ...) or something. ... make sure we define this variable in the global set.\" `foreach`, `call` and `let` each `push_new_variable_scope` and bind their names `o_automatic` there. Reads walk the chain and find the binding. Writes do not: `define_variable_in_set` is handed a null set — the global one — for everything except a target-specific assignment, and `undefine` is `undefine_variable_global`.\n\nSo: the binding shadows every read for its whole extent, and an assignment or undefine written inside it is aimed past it, taking effect when it unwinds. Probed and recorded as fixtures:\n\n  $(foreach v,a b,$(eval seen_$(v) = yes))     globals; `v` itself stays undefined\n  $(eval v = hijack) naming the loop variable  `$(v)` reads `a`, then `b`; `$(origin v)`\n                                               is `automatic` throughout; after the loop\n                                               `v` is `hijack`, recursive, file\n  the same where `v` was unbound before        identical; after the loop it is `file`\n  $(eval x += $(x)) — kati's autovar_assign.mk `+=` reads THROUGH the binding, so it\n                                               pastes onto the loop word, and the answer\n                                               is defined outside it: after `FAIL PASS`,\n                                               x is `PASS PASS`, simple, file\n  $(eval 1:=bar) in a $(call) — param.mk       `$(1)` stays `baz` for the whole body and\n                                               for the nested $(call); `1` is `bar` after\n  $(let y z,1 2,$(eval y = hijack))            same as foreach; `let` bindings are the\n                                               same kind of binding\n  nested foreach both binding `n`              the write reaches past BOTH; `deep` survives\n  $(eval undefine z) inside foreach            withdraws the global; loop word unaffected;\n                                               `z` undefined after\n  := / ?= / override inside foreach            landing does not depend on the spelling.\n                                               `:=` expands its value against the loop word\n                                               (`simple-a`); `?=` finds the binding defined\n                                               and does nothing, so the global survives;\n                                               `override` lands with origin override\n  $(eval define d ... endef) over the name     `deep`, recursive, file\n\nRonin BEFORE: a hard stop. Every one of these but the first died with `ronin: overriding automatic variable is not implemented yet`, because kati binds loop and parameter names by replacing the entry in the one global table and saving the old value, so an assignment to the name was an assignment over an `o_automatic` variable. `+=` had a written-down workaround for the same reason — it appended in place and skipped the assignment, with a comment saying there was \"no way to spell\" GNU's landing while the binding is a replaced global.\n\nThere is now. `GlobalVars` grew a `bindings` stack: `bind`/`unbind` push and pop a `Binding { sym, shadowed }`, and `writable_slot` sends every write for a bound name to the `shadowed` field of the OUTERMOST binding of that name — which is exactly the slot the global value went into, since every inner binding saved another automatic value. `assign` and `undefine` both go through it, so precedence, readonly and export-attribute carrying are all decided against the global value as GNU decides them against the global set. `Evaluator::with_bound`/`with_bounds` (foreach, foreach_sep, call, let) now bind and unbind instead of replacing and restoring, so nothing else had to change; the `+=` workaround is deleted and its path is now the ordinary one.\n\nThe \"overriding automatic variable is not implemented yet\" refusal stays for what it was actually about: a Makefile assigning over `$@` and its kin, which are globals with `o_automatic` origin and no binding standing in front of them.\n\nTen fixtures recorded from the oracle, plus three kati unit tests over the landing rule directly (single binding, nested bindings, undefine). The two cases the vendored kati corpus wrote this defect down in — autovar_assign.mk#default and param.mk#test, each headed by the corpus's own fix-me annotation — are now byte-identical to GNU Make and their inventory rows are removed; the corpus difference count moves 76 → 74, `recorded` 8 → 6. param.mk also stops dying during compilation, so graph equivalence compares 277 graphs where it compared 276."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:06:05.92501955Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-automatic-variable-value-function
                        title "Expose automatic variables correctly through the value function"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:33.224187652Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns recorded value_at.mk#test. The value function must expose the unexpanded values of @, @D, and @F in recipe context according to GNU Make's automatic-variable rules instead of losing or deferring them."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:06.095918528Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:26:39.760933044Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:27:26.615522653Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. The matrix has one shape, and the D/F question the node was written around has a definite answer.\n\nTwo kinds of automatic variable, built differently, and `value` is where the difference is visible.\n\nThe base forms — @ < ^ + * ? % | — are set per file in `set_file_variables` as SIMPLE variables whose value is the name just computed. A simple variable has no unexpanded text behind it, so `$(value @)` is exactly `$@`. Verified in an explicit rule (`at=[all] lt=[a.in] hat=[a.in b.in] plus=[a.in b.in] star=[] q=[a.in b.in] bar=[ord]`), in a pattern rule where `$(value *)` reads back the stem, and with an order-only prerequisite where `$(value |)` reads back the order-only list.\n\nThe D and F forms are NOT computed per file. `define_automatic_variables` (reference/gnumake/src/variable.c:991-1005) defines them ONCE, at startup, as RECURSIVE variables whose text is a literal dir/notdir expression over the base form. So `$(value @D)` returns the LITERAL text `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $@))` and `$(value @F)` returns `$(notdir $@)` — whatever the current target is, in a pattern rule as much as an explicit one. That is the definitive answer to the question the dispatch flagged: the D/F forms return literal text, not the computed path. The same holds for every derived form: `$(value <D)` is `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $<))`, `$(value ^F)` is `$(notdir $^)`, `$(value *D)` is `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $*))`.\n\nFlavor and origin tell the same story, and had to be checked because kati was self-contradictory here. Every automatic in scope has origin `automatic`. Flavor is what separates the kinds: base forms `simple`, D/F forms `recursive`. `$|` is the one with no D/F form at all, so `|D` reads as undefined/undefined rather than as an automatic — kati already had that right.\n\nThe binding forms were probed and already agree with GNU, which is dispatch #22's bindings-stack work holding: `$(value 1)` inside `$(call)` is the expanded argument text, flavor simple, origin automatic; a `$(foreach)` loop variable and a `$(let)` variable are the same. `$(value MAKEFILE_LIST)` is simple/file for contrast. Nested `$(value $(value X))` composes with no special case.\n\nkati's answers before the change: `$(value @)` and every other automatic errored outright with \"$(value @) is not implemented yet\". `$(flavor @)` and `$(flavor @D)` both said `undefined` while `$(origin)` was already saying `automatic` — incoherent, and the same \"what kind of variable is this\" question `value` asks, so it moved with it.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati. `AutoCommandVar::definition` returns the expression a D or F form was defined from and `None` for a base form, leaving the caller to evaluate; `AutoCommandVar::flavor` follows the same split. `value_func` routes an automatic through those instead of through the stored-text path that had nothing to return. `Variable::flavor` delegates. `Variable::string` keeps a diagnostic for the base forms, since reading one genuinely needs the rule in hand, but it now says that rather than claiming the function is unimplemented.\n\nScope held to the value function. Two adjacent defects found and filed rather than fixed: make-notdir-strips-a-root-directory (kati's basename leaves `/x.o` whole where GNU's notdir gives `x.o`, which also reaches `$(@F)`), and make-path-automatic-variables-defined-at-read-time (GNU has the D/F forms defined before any rule is chosen; kati registers every automatic from CommandEvaluator::new, so outside a recipe they read as undefined). `$%` remains unsupported in kati — pre-existing and untouched, so `$(value %)` now reports the same \"isn't supported yet\" that `$%` itself does rather than a different message.\n\nOwned markers: none. No divergence file under tests/make/*/divergence names the value function or an automatic variable; the node's concrete scope was the recorded corpus case value_at.mk#test, which now matches GNU byte for byte and has lost its fix-me annotation and its inventory row."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-14T05:29:25.036933669Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

