            {
                id make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcards
                title "Expand a wildcard in a pattern rule's prerequisite"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:18.732445457Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: features/rule_glob.diff.1.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle, with a.two and a.three on disk:\n\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: a.four\n  %.four : %.t* ; @echo $@: $(sort $^)\n\nGNU prints `a.four: a.three a.two`. Ronin refuses: `'a.four', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\nA pattern rule's prerequisite may hold shell wildcards, which are expanded after the stem is substituted — here `%.t*` becomes `a.t*` and then globs to the two files. Ronin substitutes the stem and then looks for a file literally named `a.t*`.\n\n`make-prerequisite-globbing` already delivered this for an ordinary rule's prerequisite list; this is the same expansion on the implicit-rule path, where it has to happen after substitution rather than at read time."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:27:19.078270819Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:27:40.888636253Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "When GNU Make globs a pattern rule's prerequisite, and the one part of that Ronin cannot follow.\n\nWHERE IT HAPPENS. `pattern_search` hands each substituted prerequisite to `PARSE_FILE_SEQ` without `PARSEFS_NOGLOB`, so the glob runs after the stem goes in and not before. Rule definition is too early to glob at — the text is still `%.t*` — and the source confirms it with the comment \"Parse the expanded string. It might have wildcards.\" on the non-deferred branch.\n\nWHAT IT DOES.\n\n  a wildcard is only looked for in `?*[`; `parse_file_seq` skips `glob()` entirely when `strpbrk (name, \"?*[\")` finds nothing, which is the same three bytes `glob_word` already tests\n  no match leaves the literal name standing: `PARSEFS_EXISTS` is not set here, so `GLOB_NOMATCH` falls through to keeping the name as written\n  one prerequisite may become several; `PARSEFS_ONEWORD` stops it splitting on blanks but not on glob results\n\nProbed and agreed already on the deferred path, which has run through `glob_word` since second expansion landed: `%.t?`, `%.t[xy]` and no-match-keeps-literal all match GNU. Only the immediate path was reading the substituted name as a literal file.\n\nTHE ORDER THE DIRECTORY GOES ON. The two paths differ and GNU is the reason. On the immediate path the held-aside directory is written into the buffer before `PARSE_FILE_SEQ` sees it, so the glob matches `lib/6bye*`. On the deferred path the directory is handed over as `parse_file_seq`'s `prefix` argument, which is applied to each name the sequence yields — after the glob. Probed, and the two really do disagree:\n\n  immediate, `%.x: 6%*` against `lib/bye.x`, with lib/6bye1 and lib/6bye2 → lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2\n  deferred, same rule, same files → no rule to make it; the glob looked for `6bye*` in the working directory, found nothing, kept the literal, and `lib/6bye*` is not a file\n  deferred, same rule, with 6bye1 and 6bye2 in the working directory → lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2\n\nRonin already reproduces the deferred order, because `make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem` put the prefix on after `glob_word` for exactly this reason. This node adds the immediate one, where the directory is already part of the name by the time the glob runs.\n\nWHAT RONIN CANNOT FOLLOW, AND WHY IT IS A FOLLOW-UP RATHER THAN THIS NODE. GNU globs during the build, so a file an earlier recipe created is visible to a later pattern rule's wildcard. Probed with nothing on disk at all:\n\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: mk a.four\n  mk: ; @touch a.three\n  %.four : %.t* ; @echo \"GOT [$@: $(sort $^)]\"\n\nGNU prints `a.four: a.three`. Ronin compiles the whole graph before any recipe runs, so its glob necessarily reads the filesystem as it stands at compile time and can only see `a.three` if something at evaluation time made it. That is the compiler boundary the whole design rests on, not a defect in this substitution, and it is filed separately."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:36:34.183058506Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivery, fixtures, and the inventory audit for the whole dispatch.\n\nCHANGE. `resolved_prerequisites` builds a matched rule's prerequisite names in one place: substitute under the directory split, then hand the result to `glob_word`, which already tests for the same `?*[` GNU Make's `parse_file_seq` tests for and already keeps the name when nothing matched. Ordinary and order-only prerequisites both go through it, because GNU walks one `rule->deps` chain and globs every entry of it — probed with `%.x: dep | oo*`, which now agrees.\n\nFIXTURES\n\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-expands-after-substitution — the node's own reproducer; log `a.three a.two`.\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-with-no-match-keeps-the-name — log `a.t*`, pinning that a wildcard matching nothing is a name and not a dropped prerequisite.\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-globs-below-the-stems-directory — `%.x: 6%*` against `lib/bye.x`, log `lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2`, pinning that on this path the directory is part of the name before the glob runs.\n\nRecording moved no existing case's expectation.\n\nUPSTREAM INVENTORY, audited row by row. 1287 cases before and after. Eleven rows changed and every one of them improved; nothing regressed.\n\n  compiler     18 → 11\n  unclassified 194 → 190\n  narration    1030 → 1041\n  interface    45 → 45\n\nThe seven owned rows all left the compiler class for narration:\n\n  features/patternrules.diff.46 .47 .64 .65 — make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem\n  features/patternrules.diff.58 .70 — make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent\n  features/rule_glob.diff.1 — this node\n\nFour more moved unclassified → narration without being owned, and both groups are accounted for by this dispatch rather than being unexplained drift:\n\n  features/se_implicit.diff.3 — second-expansion implicit rules, which is where the first-percent law and the newly bound `$(*F)` both land\n  variables/automatic.diff.1 .2 .3 — the automatic variables, which is where `DepNode::stem` and the D and F bindings land\n\nThe three known flappers did not flap and needed no restoring: features/reinvoke.diff.2, features/patternrules.diff.34 and features/parallelism.diff.1 are all byte-identical to their recorded rows. targets/clean still dies in the driver with signal 6, as it did before."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:37:56.076447388Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites
                title "Read a suffix-named rule that carries prerequisites as an ordinary rule"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:24.706802135Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns three rows, and they point in opposite directions, which is what identifies the mechanism: features/suffixrules.diff.4 and .5 (`no-rule-to-make`) and features/suffixrules.diff.6 (`refusal-not-made`). All three run the same makefile.\n\n  .SUFFIXES:\n  .SUFFIXES: .biz .baz\n  .baz.biz: foo.bar ; @echo make $@ from $<\n\nGNU reads a suffix-named rule that carries prerequisites as an ordinary rule for a file called `.baz.biz`, warns `ignoring prerequisites on suffix rule definition`, and installs no `.baz`-to-`.biz` suffix rule at all. So:\n\n  goal `.baz.biz` → GNU warns and builds it; Ronin refuses, `'.baz.biz' missing and no known rule to make it`.\n  goal `foo.biz`  → GNU refuses, `No rule to make target 'foo.biz'`; Ronin builds it from foo.baz.\n\nRonin has made exactly the opposite choice on both counts: it installs the suffix rule and loses the file target. One decision fixes both directions — a suffix-named rule with prerequisites is an ordinary rule — so the two row groups are one node rather than two.\n\nThe warning is narration and not owed. What is owed is which of the two targets exists."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:52:38.961128912Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:58:56.774553214Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written, and the triage note this node was filed from is half wrong — usefully so, because what is wrong about it is what identifies the mechanism.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Every cell probed through both binaries.\n\n  The shape alone decides nothing. `.baz.biz:` with neither half on `.SUFFIXES` is an ordinary file target and nothing else; with one half declared, likewise. Both halves have to be declared for the pair to be converted, which `keep_only_declared_suffix_rules` already had.\n\n  The file target always survives. `convert_to_pattern` looks the pair's name up in the file database and leaves the entry where it found it, so the conversion adds a pattern rule and takes nothing away. `make .c.o` runs the `.c.o:` recipe. `make .baz.biz` runs the `.baz.biz: foo.bar` recipe with `$<` = `foo.bar`. `.PHONY: .c.o` beside a `.c.o:` rule makes the file target phony and leaves the suffix rule working. This is the whole of what Ronin was getting wrong in the `no-rule-to-make` direction, and it was getting it wrong for every suffix-shaped name rather than only for one with prerequisites.\n\n  Prerequisites, outside POSIX. The pair is still converted; the written prerequisites are dropped on the way; a warning says so, at the recipe's line. Upstream test #8 is the observable consequence and it now agrees: a `foo.biz` newer than `foo.baz` is current however new `foo.bar` is, because `foo.bar` is not one of the converted rule's prerequisites.\n\n  Prerequisites, under POSIX. `rule.c:352` — `if (posix_pedantic) continue;` — passes the pair over entirely, so no `%.biz: %.baz` exists and `foo.biz` cannot be made though `foo.baz` is right there. This is the whole of the `refusal-not-made` direction. `.POSIX:` is latched wherever it stands, before or after the rule, and with prerequisites of its own; the conversion runs after the last makefile is closed, so ordering never matters. A pair with no prerequisites is converted under POSIX as always: the rejection is about prerequisites, not about `.POSIX:`.\n\n  `f->deps` is the whole chain. An order-only prerequisite disqualifies a pair under POSIX and is dropped by the ordinary conversion, exactly as a normal one is.\n\nWHAT THE TRIAGE NOTE HAD WRONG, AND WHY IT MATTERS. It read the three rows as one decision failing in both directions — Ronin installing the suffix rule where GNU installs none, and losing the file target. Only the second half is true. GNU does install the suffix rule; features/suffixrules tests #4 through #7 turn POSIX mode on and off through `$X.POSIX:`, and the note's `foo.biz` cell was GNU's POSIX cell read as its ordinary one. So this is two decisions rather than one direction of a single one, and the second decision — POSIX — had no representation in the fork at all.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT. `is_buildable_target` in dep.rs excluded every suffix-shaped name from the manifest unconditionally. It now takes `has_rule` and asks about it: the exclusion is only ever needed for a suffix-shaped name nothing declared, where emission hands `.c.o` to the built-in `%.o: %.c` with an empty stem and runs `cc -c -o .c.o` against no input. That cell is unmoved and stays a deliberate divergence — GNU runs the cc and fails, Ronin refuses, and no file is written either way.\n\n`populate_suffix_rule` gained the POSIX branch and the warning, and `r.order_only_inputs.clear()` beside the `r.inputs.clear()` that was already there — the dropping was half done, so `.baz.biz: | foo.bar` reached the converted rule and had the stem substituted into it, looking for `foo.foo.bar`. `.POSIX:` is read through a new `Evaluator::is_posix`, because the switch is latched during the read and the conversion happens after it.\n\nFIXTURES. Eight, recorded from the oracle: feature-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites-is-a-file, feature-suffix-shaped-target-is-a-file-when-no-suffix-is-declared, feature-suffix-rule-target-is-a-file-of-that-name, feature-suffix-rule-conversion-drops-the-written-prerequisites, feature-suffix-rule-conversion-drops-an-order-only-prerequisite, feature-posix-suffix-rule-with-prerequisites-makes-no-pattern-rule, feature-posix-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites-is-still-a-file, feature-posix-keeps-a-suffix-rule-without-prerequisites.\n\nDelivered in kati d619930 on branch nplan/make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:49:35.484938391Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Row audit, against a rebuilt base binary and a re-recorded inventory (the tsv landed in 0af6325, the third commit of this dispatch, because that is the run that re-recorded it).\n\nThe three owned rows all left the compiler bucket:\n\n  features/suffixrules.diff.4  compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.5  compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.6  compiler ninja-progress+fatal-decoration+refusal-not-made → narration product-name+fatal-decoration+shared-refusal\n\n.4 and .5 are the file target, in both POSIX and non-POSIX mode: Ronin builds `.baz.biz` now and the only difference left is Ninja's progress line. .6 is the POSIX half, and `shared-refusal` is the right shape for it — both tools now refuse `foo.biz` because neither has a pattern rule to make it from `foo.baz`, and they word the refusal differently.\n\nTwo more rows came with them, out of unclassified rather than out of compiler, and they are the cells the node's own matrix predicted:\n\n  features/suffixrules.diff.7  unclassified ninja-progress+evaluation → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.8  unclassified no-work-line+up-to-date-line+evaluation → narration no-work-line+up-to-date-line\n\n.7 is upstream test #7, the non-POSIX conversion happening at all. .8 is upstream test #8, the written prerequisites being dropped from the converted rule — the cell that `r.order_only_inputs.clear()` and its normal-prerequisite sibling are for.\n\nNo row moved the wrong way. The three watched flappers held their class."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:51:59.228730605Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-vpath-prerequisite-resolves-to-a-target
                title "Find a prerequisite through vpath when the found name has a rule"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-vpath-search)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:35.113011836Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: features/vpath.diff.1.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle, with an empty vpath-d/ directory:\n\n  vpath %.te vpath-d/\n  .SECONDARY:\n  default: vpath-d/a vpath-d/b\n  vpath-d/a: fail.te\n  vpath-d/b : fail.te\n  vpath-d/fail.te:\n\nGNU says `Nothing to be done for 'default'`. Ronin refuses: `'fail.te', needed by 'vpath-d/a', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\n`fail.te` exists nowhere on disk, so the vpath search cannot succeed on a file test alone. GNU's search also accepts a name it can reach — `vpath-d/fail.te` is a target the makefile declares, with an empty recipe — and that is the candidate it takes. Ronin's vpath search looks only for files that already exist, so it never offers the directory that would have named a known target.\n\n`make-vpath-search` delivered the file-existence half. This is the half where the found name is a target rather than a file, and it should be read alongside `make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target`, which is about discarding a found name again."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:17:42.808834655Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:35:23.966292548Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written. Every cell below was run through both binaries; the two that decided the shape of the code are the ones a \"accept anything the makefile said\" reading gets wrong.\n\nWHAT THE SEARCH ACCEPTS. `selective_vpath_search` (src/vpath.c:307) asks two questions of each directory and Ronin only ever asked the second. The first is `lookup_file` on the joined path, and the function's own comment says what it buys: \"Makefile-mentioned file need not exist\" — no stat is done and none is wanted. The condition is one line, `exists = not_target || f->is_target`, with `not_target` settled once before the search starts, off the name being searched FOR:\n\n  searched name is not a target    any name the makefile knows at all answers\n  searched name IS a target        only another target answers\n\nThe matrix, `VPATH = d` or `vpath %.te d/`, nothing on disk anywhere:\n\n  d/fail.te has a rule with no recipe        found. Nothing to be done, exit 0\n  d/fail.te has a real recipe                found, MADE there, dependent's $< reads d/fail.te\n  d/fail.te only some other rule's prereq    found, then refused as `No rule to make target 'd/fail.te'`\n  d/fail.te only an order-only prereq        same\n  d/fail.te carries only `d/fail.te: V = x`  same — a target-specific binding reaches the database\n  d/fail.te is only `.PHONY`                 found; GNU sets is_target for every name .PHONY lists\n  d/%.te is the only mention                 NOT found. A pattern is not a name\n  fail.te has a rule here, d/fail.te merely mentioned   NOT found. The rule here runs\n  d1/fail.te mentioned, d2/fail.te on disk, `vpath %.te d1/ d2/`   d1 wins\n\nThat last cell is the one that says the two questions are asked per directory rather than in two passes: a mentioned name in an earlier directory beats a file that is really there in a later one. And the `fail.te has a rule here` cell is the one that stops this from being a blanket acceptance — it is the 1990 restriction GNU's comment admits to not understanding, kept because the probe says it is still there in 4.4.1.\n\nTHE LIBRARY INTERPLAY, which the dispatch flagged and which turns out to be half true. `library_search` (remake.c:1700) has three tiers and asks the filesystem itself for two of them — the working directory and the compiled-in system directories. Its middle tier is a call to `vpath_search`, so it gets whatever the ordinary search gives, target acceptance included. Probed: `.LIBPATTERNS = made_%.a` with `vpath %.a d/` beside a `d/made_foo.a:` rule resolves to `d/made_foo.a` and RUNS the rule. The same rule with no vpath written at all is still refused, which is what the suffix/library dispatch established and what its thirteen fixtures pin — none of them writes a vpath, so none of them is touched. The new cell is fixtured beside them rather than left as a claim.\n\nWHAT WAS NOT DONE, and why it is not this node's sentence. With `fail.te` carrying a recipe here AND `d/fail.te` carrying one there, GNU finds the second, merges the two file records with `rehash_file` (file.c:208), says `Recipe was specified for file 'fail.te' ... but 'fail.te' is now considered the same file as 'd/fail.te'. Recipe for 'fail.te' will be ignored in favor of the one for 'd/fail.te'`, and runs the found one. That is a file-identity merge — two declared targets becoming one object with one recipe and concatenated prerequisites — and kati has no such concept. Ronin runs the rule written here. Filed as make-vpath-found-name-merges-two-declared-targets with the reproducer. It is reached only when both names carry recipes, which is why none of the nine fixtures runs into it.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, in kati edbf935 on branch nplan/make-vpath-prerequisite-resolves-to-a-target. `first_directory_holding` became `first_directory_offering`, a method rather than an associated function because the question needs the rule tables. `makefile_offers` is the acceptance, `declares_a_target` is GNU's `is_target` (a rule with or without a recipe, or a `.PHONY` member), and `names_a_target` is the same question over bytes that may never have been interned — the library search reaches it with a `.LIBPATTERNS` candidate the search invented, and asking about a name must not mint it, so `peek_symbol` and not `intern`. `is_written_down` was already there and is already GNU's `lookup_file`; it did not need writing, only calling.\n\n`at_vpath`'s existing guards are untouched, deliberately. It still returns None for a name that exists here, for a phony one, and for one that has a rule of its own — the last being the compile-time stand-in for `update_file_1`'s throwing-away, which make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target owns and which this node was told not to attempt. The GPATH branch sits ahead of that guard and is unchanged.\n\nFIXTURES, nine, all recorded from the oracle: feature-vpath-finds-a-name-only-a-rule-offers (the owned row's own reproducer), -makes-the-name-it-found, -takes-a-name-only-a-prerequisite-mentions, -a-mention-beats-a-file-in-a-later-directory, -a-target-is-answered-only-by-a-target, -finds-a-phony-target, -a-pattern-rule-target-is-not-a-name, -a-name-with-target-variables-is-written-down, and feature-link-library-through-vpath-finds-a-makeable-target. The three negative cells were rewritten so the harness can see them: a case that only ever fails is not a gate, so each was given a route that succeeds when the name is NOT taken, and the files on disk afterwards say which way it went.\n\nROW AUDIT, and the headline. features/vpath.diff.1 moved compiler -> narration, family `no-work-line` alone: Ronin now builds it and the whole remaining difference is that it says `no work to do` where Make says `Nothing to be done for 'default'`. THE COMPILER BUCKET IS ZERO — 1287 rows at 228 unclassified / 0 compiler / 41 interface / 1018 narration.\n\nThe other 57 moved rows are not this change's and were checked rather than assumed. The recorded tsv was last written at c5c9eb0 and six commits have landed since; a run built at HEAD with this change reverted gives 228/1/41/1017, so the 57 narration -> unclassified moves were already there before this dispatch started. All 57 carry `ninja-failure-block` and all gained `evaluation`. The cause is 9294140, which stopped interposing a launcher: the FAILED block's command line now reads `exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=' ... /bin/sh -c \"...\"` and the classifier's narration recognisers were not taught the new shape. Filed as make-upstream-failure-block-narration-unrecognised. Re-recorded here because the tsv has to be a recording of what the tools do now, and diffing this change against a run of HEAD is what separates the one row from the 57.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 803 passed; clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic/nursery --no-deps clean and kati -D warnings clean; make_port 4/4 over 727 cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles, 278 graphs, 0 disagreements, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 22s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:36:02.255582719Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-link-library-prerequisites
                title "Resolve a -lNAME prerequisite through .LIBPATTERNS"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-vpath-search)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:40.907653859Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns three rows: variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff, variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff.1 and features/vpath.diff.3.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle, with mtest_foo.a on disk:\n\n  .LIBPATTERNS = mtest_%.a\n  all: -lfoo ; @echo \"build $@ from $<\"\n\nGNU prints `build all from mtest_foo.a`. Ronin refuses: `'-lfoo', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\nA prerequisite spelled `-lNAME` is not a file name: Make substitutes NAME into each whitespace-separated pattern of `.LIBPATTERNS` and searches for the results, through vpath as well as in the working directory, taking the first that exists or can be made. `.LIBPATTERNS` itself is not defined in the fork either, so both the variable and the prerequisite rewriting are missing.\n\nvpath.diff.3 is the same feature reached through the search path rather than the current directory — `all: -l1 -lc -l2 -l3` over several `vpath` lines — so it belongs here and not with the vpath nodes.\n\nLIBPATTERNS.diff.1 additionally expects `\\.LIBPATTERNS element 'mtest_foo.a' is not a pattern` for an element with no `%` in it. That diagnostic is narration; what it is evidence of is that a non-pattern element is skipped rather than taken literally."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:59:38.117984186Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:29:11.153078901Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written; every cell below was run through both binaries rather than read off `remake.c`, and one of them contradicts what this node was filed expecting.\n\nTHE SEARCH ORDER. `library_search` is reached from `f_mtime`, as the last resort after the ordinary directory search has failed — so `-lfoo` is a file name whenever a file of that name is there, and a linker-style reference only when it is not. `.LIBPATTERNS` is expanded at that moment rather than read as text, so a recursive value follows an assignment made anywhere in the read, including below the rule that names the library. An element with no `%` is warned about and passed over rather than taken literally, and the search goes on to the next one. An empty `.LIBPATTERNS` finds nothing.\n\nEvery element is tried rather than the first that hits, because the answer is the *earliest* one any of them reaches — the linker-compatible behaviour `remake.c` asks for in as many words. Earliest resolves in three tiers:\n\n  1. The working directory. A hit here ends the search where it stands, so a cwd hit on a LATER element beats a vpath hit an earlier one already recorded.\n  2. The vpath search, ranked by (which `vpath` entry answered, which of its directories). The earliest entry wins whichever element reached it — element order gets no say — and this was probed both ways round, swapping the two directives and watching the answer follow them.\n  3. The compiled-in system directories, `/lib`, `/usr/lib` and `LIBDIR`, ranked behind every vpath entry and in their own order. GNU arranges that by starting their indices above every vpath index there could be; here it is the `LibraryRank` enum's declaration order, with a unit test on it because nothing else in the file would notice it going wrong. `LIBDIR` is `--libdir` at configure time, `/usr/local/lib` for a default prefix, and is compiled in here for the same reason it is there.\n\nTies go to the earlier element: GNU's two comparisons are strict `<`, and there is a unit test on that too.\n\nWHAT THE DISPATCH HAD WRONG. It expected \"first pattern that yields an existing file OR a makeable target\". There is no makeable half. `library_search` asks the filesystem and nothing else, and the probe is unambiguous: `-lfoo` under `.LIBPATTERNS = made_%.a` beside a `made_foo.a:` rule is refused outright, and with an `exists_foo.b` reachable through a LATER element the existing file wins and the rule is never consulted. Both cells are fixtures.\n\nTHE CELL THAT SHAPED THE CODE. A `-lfoo:` rule written beside a `libfoo.a` on disk does not stop the search. `f_mtime` asks about the file before anything asks about the rule; GNU renames the file to what it found and finds it current there, and the recipe never runs. Moving the prerequisite to the found name is the same answer for the same reason, so the library search deliberately does *not* inherit `at_vpath`'s \"a name with a rule of its own is left alone\" guard. The other half — search finds nothing, name stays as written, rule makes it — falls out. Both are fixtures.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, in kati bba80a4 on branch nplan/make-link-library-prerequisites. `at_found_name` gained `at_library` behind the vpath answer. `vpath_of` grew a byte-taking half, `vpath_search`, reporting GNU's `vpath_index`/`path_index`: a library candidate is a name the search invented rather than a target, and the answers have to be comparable. `Pattern::substitute` is the other direction from `Pattern::stem` — the pattern with text where its wildcard is — and its `None` is exactly the element GNU warns about. `resolve_vpaths`'s fast path out had to learn to ask about `-l` as well as about vpath, since the library search runs whether or not anything wrote a `vpath`.\n\nTHE STRONGEST EVIDENCE is GNU's own features/vpath cell, recorded whole as feature-link-library-through-vpath: `all: -l1 -lc -l2 -l3` over five vpath directives resolves to `a1/lib1.a a1/libc.a a2/lib2.a lib3.a`, byte for byte what the suite expects. `-l2` is the cell that pins the tie-break — `lib2.so` in b2 and `lib2.a` in a2 are both reached by the same `vpath % a2 b2` entry, and a2 comes first within it.\n\nFIXTURES. Thirteen: feature-link-library-prerequisite, -non-pattern-element-is-skipped, -earliest-pattern-wins, -existing-file-beats-a-makeable-target, -makeable-target-is-not-found, -through-vpath, -working-directory-beats-vpath, -vpath-entry-order-decides, -vpath-entry-order-decides-the-other-way, -patterns-expand-at-search-time, -reference-that-is-a-file-is-a-file, -search-outranks-a-rule-for-the-reference, -rule-for-the-reference-runs-when-nothing-is-found.\n\nNOT FIXTURED, DELIBERATELY. The system-directory tier is implemented and was probed against `/usr/lib/libsupp.a` on this host, resolving to `/lib/libsupp.a` — the first of the three — with cwd and vpath both beating it. It is not in the corpus because a case that depends on a particular host's `/usr/lib` is a case that fails on the next host, and the corpus is meant to travel."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:49:46.279719775Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Row audit, against a rebuilt base binary and a re-recorded inventory (the tsv landed in 0af6325, the third commit of this dispatch, because that is the run that re-recorded it).\n\n  features/vpath.diff.3         compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff    compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff.1  compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → unclassified product-name+ninja-progress+evaluation\n\nThe first two are clean: Ronin resolves the reference and builds, and Ninja's progress line is the whole of what is left.\n\nThe third moved out of the compiler bucket but not all the way to narration, and the reason is worth writing down rather than leaving as residue. The build now happens and is correct; the remaining difference is that GNU says `.LIBPATTERNS element 'mtest_foo.a' is not a pattern` and Ronin says the same sentence quoted `mtest_foo.a'. That is the fork's inherited GNU Make 3.8x quote style, which the Make conformance gate has owned as a family of 21 cases for some time and which the upstream classifier has no recogniser for. Filed as make-diagnostic-quote-style under make-subsumption with this reproducer, because changing the style is one decision across every diagnostic in the fork and is not this node's sentence.\n\nNo row moved the wrong way. The three watched flappers held their class."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:52:03.489264705Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-every-makefile-argument-is-read
                title "Read every makefile the command line names, not only the last"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-interface-surface)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:54.617123299Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns nineteen rows — the largest single group the triage found — all in options/dash-f: .1, .3, .4, and .5 through .20.\n\nRonin keeps only the last `-f` and forgets the ones before it. Reproduced against the pristine oracle with three makefiles, one target each:\n\n  make -f one.mk -f two.mk -f three.mk TWO\n      GNU `from-two`; Ronin `'TWO' missing and no known rule to make it`.\n  make -f one.mk -f two.mk -f three.mk\n      GNU `from-one`; Ronin `from-three`.\n\nTwo claims, and the second is easy to lose: every named makefile is read, and the default goal comes from the first of them rather than the last.\n\nSixteen of the nineteen rows (.5 through .20) are the same defect wearing a different face. The suite spells stdin twice — `-fbye.mk -f- -f-`, and every mixture of `-f-`, `-f -`, `--file -` and `--makefile -` — and expects `Makefile from standard input specified twice.  Stop.` Ronin reads only the last of them, so it never has two stdin makefiles to object to and builds `hello, world` instead. The duplicate-stdin refusal falls out of reading them all; it is a facet of this node rather than a node of its own.\n\noptions/dash-f.diff.4 is the third face: `-fbye.mk -fR -` where R does not exist. GNU reports `R: No such file or directory`, then remakes bye.mk from bye.mk.src — real filesystem work, `touch bye.mk.src` and `touch bye.mk` — before refusing with `No rule to make target 'R'`. Ronin answers `makefile not found` and does nothing. Both refuse in the end, so the divergence that matters is the work GNU did on the way: a makefile named on the command line is a target Make tries to remake before giving up on it.\n\n`make-included-makefile-restarts` already delivered remaking for included makefiles; this is the same obligation for the ones `-f` names."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:55:55.652445107Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T07:23:01.997359248Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "What GNU Make does with more than one `-f`, established against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written, and where the fix had to go.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Every cell probed rather than assumed.\n\n  Ordering. `make -f one.mk -f two.mk -f three.mk TWO` builds two.mk's rule: every file is read, in the order written, into one evaluation. An assignment in an earlier file is in scope while a later one is read (`-f a.mk -f b.mk` with `V := from-a` in a.mk answers `from-a`). A target declared in both is the ordinary re-declaration — GNU warns `overriding recipe` and the later recipe wins — and prerequisites declared across the files accumulate on the one target.\n\n  Default goal. It comes from the FIRST file that declares an eligible target, not the last: `-f one.mk -f two.mk -f three.mk` with no goal builds one.mk's. A first file that declares only variables, or only a pattern rule, or only `.PHONY`, passes the goal on to the next file that declares a real target. This is set-once semantics across the whole sequence, which is what kati's `record_default_goal` already had for one file.\n\n  MAKEFILE_LIST. Every name, in order, appended as each file opens and before its statements run — so a file reading the variable sees itself last and the ones after it not at all. A file named twice is listed twice: the variable records reads, not a set. A file that would not open is not listed at all.\n\n  `-f- -f-`. Refused: `Makefile from standard input specified twice`, before a byte of standard input is read, for all sixteen spellings the suite writes (`-f-`, `-f -`, `--file=-`, `--file -`, `--makefile=-`, `--makefile -`, in every mixture). GNU refuses because it slurps standard input into a temporary file so a restart can re-read it, and there is only one standard input to slurp. One `-f-` among other files is read in its turn and keeps its place in the ordering: `-f bye.mk -f-` takes its default goal from bye.mk, `-f- -f bye.mk` from standard input.\n\n  A missing `-f` file. Reported where the read failed — `make: R: No such file or directory`, under Make's own name because no makefile line asked for it — and then treated as a target that must be reached, so the run dies `No rule to make target 'R'`. The reading does not stop: the files after it are still read, and a later one is still allowed to supply the rule.\n\n  Neighbours. `-C sub -f a.mk -f b.mk` resolves both against the entered directory. `--file=`/`--makefile=` are the same option. `-f` on a directory is fatal (`Is a directory`) on both tools already. A `-f` arriving through MAKEFLAGS sorts before the command line's, so `MAKEFLAGS='-f two.mk' make -f one.mk` takes its default goal from two.mk — which Ronin now agrees with and did not before. `MAKEFILES` is deliberately untouched; GNU reads it first and with the default goal suppressed, which the list shape leaves room for and `make-makefiles-variable` still owns.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT. A scalar became a list at every layer it was a scalar in, which is the whole change: `kati::flags::Flags::makefile: Option<OsString>` is now `makefiles: Vec<OsString>`, and Ronin's `Invocation::makefile` is now `makefiles: Vec<PathBuf>`. `-f` pushes instead of overwriting, in all five spellings and both option streams. Kati's evaluation loops over the list where it read one file, and `read_invocation_state` now binds `MAKEFILE_LIST` empty and lets each file append its own name as it opens — the mechanism `do_include` already had, extracted into `note_makefile_list` and shared. That extraction is what makes the ordering fall out rather than be arranged: the default goal, the variable scoping and the re-declaration rules are all just \"read them in order into one session\", and kati already had every one of them for `include`.\n\nThe missing-file case reuses the same machinery for the same reason. A `-f` file that will not open is now `note_missing_include(name, required, loc: None)`, which is exactly what a required `include` of a missing file already was, and `MissingInclude::loc` became an `Option` to say the thing that is actually different: no makefile line asked for this one. That absence is load-bearing in two places — `plan_regeneration` skips the \"No such file or directory\" warning for it because the read has already said so where it happened, and `color_warn_log` with no location now leads with the program name, which is the same rule `color_error_log` was already following two functions above it.\n\nThe duplicate-standard-input refusal is a front-end decision and stays in the front end: standard input is drained once in `run` and replayed into every pass, so two of them cannot be told apart afterwards and the refusal has to come before the draining rather than after it. It is `report::duplicate_standard_input`, an ordinary diagnostic with no usage text, because it is a fatal and not a misspelled option.\n\nWHAT MOVED. All nineteen owned rows left the compiler bucket. Eighteen are now narration: options/dash-f.diff.1 and .3 are `ninja-progress` alone, and .5 through .20 are `shared-refusal` — both tools now refuse the duplicate standard input with the same sentence differently dressed. Nine more rows came with them out of unclassified (options/dash-f.diff and .24 through .31, the stdin-and-restart group), plus features/temp_stdin.diff.3. The nineteenth, options/dash-f.diff.4, is now unclassified and is handed to `make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile` with its reproducer: Ronin says what GNU says, in GNU's order, and refuses with the same status, but GNU remakes bye.mk on the way and Ronin does not. That is a defect in when a refusal fires relative to the restart machinery, it reproduces with no `-f` at all, and it is not this node's sentence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T07:29:11.835734675Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules
                title "Withdraw the built-in rules when a makefile's MAKEFLAGS asks"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-makefile-set-makeflags)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:59.838611626Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns two rows: options/dash-r.diff.1 and options/dash-r.diff.4.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle, with xxx.c on disk and the makefile holding only:\n\n  MAKEFLAGS += -r\n\n  make xxx.o → GNU refuses, `No rule to make target 'xxx.o'`; Ronin runs `cc -c -o xxx.o xxx.c`.\n\n`-r` on the command line already works — `make -r xxx.o` refuses on both tools — so this is `-r` arriving from a MAKEFLAGS the makefile sets, after the built-in rules have already been installed. `kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs` writes the `.c.o` and `.cc.o` bootstrap rules under `if !session.flags.no_builtin_rules`, which is read once before the makefile is parsed and never revisited.\n\n`make-makefile-set-makeflags` delivered the decoding of a makefile-assigned MAKEFLAGS into the switches it names. This is the one switch whose effect is already spent by the time the decoding happens, so it needs the built-in rules withdrawn rather than merely the flag recorded."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:41:30.545753311Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by make-builtin-rule-catalogue (2026-08-14), and by placement rather than by a withdrawal.\n\nThis node was written expecting the variables' shape: install, then take back when a makefile's MAKEFLAGS turns out to have said `-r`. GNU Make does not need that for rules and neither does Ronin now. The rules are derived from `.SUFFIXES` after the last makefile closes — install_default_suffix_rules, convert_to_pattern, install_default_implicit_rules all run after read_all_makefiles — so a switch the makefile set is already in hand when the question is asked. `DepBuilder::install_builtin_rules` reads `flags.no_builtin_rules` at that point and installs nothing.\n\nThe list itself does need taking back, and that is the one place the node's original reading was right. GNU clears `.SUFFIXES` at main.c:2129 when `-r` newly arrives, but only `if (suffix_file->builtin)` — and enter_file clears that flag the moment a makefile names `.SUFFIXES` at all. `read_suffix_list` mirrors both halves: the list goes only while every `.SUFFIXES` rule came from the bootstrap. `SUFFIXES`, the readable half, is re-bound empty at the same point evaluate.rs withdraws the variable catalogue.\n\nBoth owned rows resolved, and are now `narration shared-refusal` in the inventory where they were `compiler refusal-not-made`:\n\n  options/dash-r.diff.1 and .4 — `MAKEFLAGS += -r` with xxx.c on disk, `make xxx.o`. GNU refuses; Ronin refuses. Recorded as tests/make/builtin-rules-withdrawn-by-makeflags.\n\nThe corner worth keeping: `MAKEFLAGS += -r` beside a makefile's own `.SUFFIXES: .c` keeps `.c`, so `foo.c` still has a disqualifier and a match-anything rule still cannot claim it. Recorded as tests/make/builtin-suffix-list-survives-a-makefile-that-wrote-one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:42:56.631551918Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-extra-prereqs-are-real-prerequisites
                title "Require the prerequisites .EXTRA_PREREQS adds"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:09.147964985Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: variables/EXTRA_PREREQS.diff.7.\n\nThe makefile is the whole reproducer:\n\n  .EXTRA_PREREQS = tick tack\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: ; @echo ${.EXTRA_PREREQS} $^\n\nNothing makes `tick` or `tack`. GNU refuses: `No rule to make target 'tick', needed by 'all'`. Ronin prints `tick tack` and exits 0 — it read the variable back but never made its contents prerequisites of the goal.\n\n`.EXTRA_PREREQS` adds prerequisites that are ordered and required like any other, and are only left out of `$^` and `$?`. Ronin appears to have the second half without the first, which is the safe-looking direction and the wrong one: a build that should have stopped runs instead."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:14:52.045500152Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:27:43.763379473Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:28:11.386837982Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in ronin 7500bd3, kati f90f42b.\n\nvariables/EXTRA_PREREQS.diff.7 moves compiler/refusal-not-made to narration/shared-refusal, and the whole suite file moves with it: all 13 rows are narration now, 6 of them plain `ninja-progress` (the content agrees and only the progress line differs). .8 and .9 became shared-refusal, .10-.12 ninja-progress.\n\nImplemented in kati/src-rs/dep.rs as a port of GNU's `snap_file`. The extras join the existing hidden-prerequisite pass — the one grouped `&:` peers already use — so they reach the graph, the freshness test, VPATH and `.WAIT`, and never `actual_inputs`, which is what the automatic variables read.\n\nGNU 4.4.1 matrix, probed cell by cell against reference/make-oracle:\n\n  - global form applies to every name the read made a target; a file that is only somebody's prerequisite gets none\n  - target-specific form REPLACES the global rather than adding to it\n  - the lookup does not fall back, so a target carrying any OTHER target-specific variable is left with no extras at all. `all: FOO = bar` beside a global `.EXTRA_PREREQS = glob` builds without glob. That is the implementation (`if (f->variables) … else if (f->is_target)`) rather than the documentation, and it is ported because a makefile written against 4.4.1 depends on it\n  - a list naming its own target drops WHOLE, not just the offending entry\n  - `|` in the value splits compared from order-only, as `split_prereqs` does\n  - excluded from $^, $?, $<, $+ AND $| (ignore_automatic_vars, not ignore_mtime) — so an extra newer than the target DOES force a rebuild\n  - expanded once the last makefile is closed; no second expansion (`$$(EP)` stays literal)\n  - reaches a target made by a pattern rule (GNU splices it in `snap_implicit_rules`); a pattern-target-specific `.EXTRA_PREREQS` is a no-op\n  - takes no part in choosing an implicit rule; never becomes the default goal\n  - applies to `::` records, each on its own\n  - crosses the recursive fence: a sub-make's own value applies in its own unit\n\nSeven oracle-recorded cases under tests/make/extra-prereqs-*.\n\nOne thing worth recording: the order-only `|` split was a regression this node introduced and then fixed before landing. Before the change `.EXTRA_PREREQS` was ignored entirely, so `= | tick` agreed with GNU by accident; implementing the feature without the split made Ronin refuse a literal `|` as a prerequisite name. Caught by probing the cell rather than by any gate — nothing in the corpus writes it."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-word-index-out-of-range
                title "Refuse a word index too large to be one"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:13.111626097Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: functions/word.diff.4.\n\n  word-e4: ; @echo $(word 9999999999999999999,$(FOO))\n\nGNU refuses at read time: `invalid first argument to 'word' function: '9999999999999999999' out of range.  Stop.` Ronin returns the empty string and the recipe runs.\n\nThe neighbouring cases already agree — `$(word abc,…)` and `$(word 1a,…)` are refused by both tools as non-numeric, and the wording difference there is narration this triage now records as `shared-refusal`. What is missing is the range check on a value that is all digits but does not fit, which is a different rejection from a non-numeric one and is the only one of the group Ronin lets through.\n\nSmall, but it is the compiler accepting a makefile GNU rejects, so it belongs on the intent side rather than the wording side."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:14:58.257495271Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:27:48.497715545Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:28:28.292278467Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in ronin 7500bd3, kati fa699e4.\n\nfunctions/word.diff.4 moves compiler/refusal-not-made to narration/shared-refusal; functions/word.diff moves unclassified to narration/ninja-progress as well.\n\nThe triage's reading was right and the cause was narrower than \"no range check\". kati read the index with `s.parse::<usize>()`, so every value up to 2^64 parsed successfully and became an index no list could have — the scan simply ran off the end and answered empty. GNU reads it with `strtoll` and reports the ERANGE as its own diagnostic. (The C++ `src/func.cc` still carries the older `long`/`LONG_MAX` shape, but `src-rs` is the live code.)\n\nReplaced `get_numeric_value_for_func` with a port of GNU's `parse_numeric`, which settles four cells rather than the one reported:\n\n  - whitespace off BOTH ends: `$(word  3 ,…)` is 3. We trimmed only the left, so we were refusing a makefile GNU accepts — a divergence in the opposite direction, found while probing\n  - overflow either way is `'…' out of range`; accumulation is signed and checked so i64::MIN is not itself an overflow\n  - an empty value says `empty value` rather than quoting nothing\n  - a negative index reaches word's own \"must be greater than 0\" (GNU checks `i < 1` after parsing) instead of being called non-numeric\n\n`$(wordlist)` takes the same parser plus its two boundaries: start < 1 and stop < 0 are fatal and name the number PARSED, not the text written — so `$(wordlist 000,…)` refuses '0'. A stop of zero and a stop below the start stay empty. Also fixed the malformed trailing backtick in kati's old wordlist message.\n\nWording follows GNU 4.4.1 as `$(intcmp)` already does: GNU's words, kati's backtick quoting. GNU renamed these from \"non-numeric\" to \"invalid\" when it added the range check.\n\nAll 24 word/wordlist boundary cells now agree with the oracle. Three fixtures: word-index-out-of-range, wordlist-index-out-of-range, word-index-boundaries (the six accepted cells).\n\nCorpus side effect: this retires the last `message-text` difference. err_word_non_numeric.mk now says what GNU says and differs only in quote style — its digest is identical to err_word_zero.mk's, and both are exit-status+stop-suffix+quote-style. The empty family record was removed from tests/make_corpus_inventory.tsv."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-jobserver-style-is-checked
                title "Refuse a jobserver style that names nothing"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-interface-surface)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:22.36803552Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: features/jobserver.diff.8.\n\n  make --jobserver-style=foo -j8\n\nGNU refuses: `unknown jobserver auth style 'foo'.  Stop.` Ronin accepts the argument and builds.\n\n`make-single-ninja-scheduler` settled that Ronin replaces recursive Make jobservers with one Ninja scheduler, so `--jobserver-style` has nothing to select and is rightly a no-op. Accepting a value that names nothing is a separate matter: an argument Make would have rejected should not be read as one it would have taken, or a makefile guarding on the refusal takes the wrong branch.\n\nThe narrow fix is to check the value against the styles Make knows and refuse the rest, while continuing to do nothing with the ones it accepts."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:15:03.772177746Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:27:53.013431388Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:28:43.63876285Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in ronin 7500bd3. No kati change — this is Ronin's CLI, as the node predicted.\n\nfeatures/jobserver.diff.8 moves compiler/refusal-not-made to narration/shared-refusal.\n\nGNU 4.4.1 matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle:\n\n  - accepts exactly `fifo` and `pipe`, case-sensitive. `FIFO`, `Pipe`, `sem` are all refused\n  - `unknown jobserver auth style 'X'.  Stop.`, exit 2, and NO usage block after it (unlike a malformed option, which prints usage)\n  - an empty value is refused earlier and differently: `the '--jobserver-style' option requires a non-empty string argument`, WITH usage\n  - last one wins: `--jobserver-style=pipe --jobserver-style=foo` refuses, the reverse order builds\n  - MAKEFLAGS carries `--jobserver-auth=…` (fifo:/path or 3,4), never `--jobserver-style` itself\n\nThe cell that matters and is easy to miss: GNU checks the value inside `jobserver_setup`, which it only reaches when `job_slots > 1`. So `--jobserver-style=nonsense` alone, with `-j1`, or with a bare `-j` (infinite) is never looked at and never refused. The check belongs to the jobserver, not to the option parser. Replicated: `src/make/cli.rs` records the style during parsing and checks it in `parse` once both option streams have been read, gated on the effective job count being a fixed count above one.\n\nBoth streams the CLI reads are covered — argv and MAKEFLAGS from the environment, in either combination. What is NOT covered is a style a Makefile's own MAKEFLAGS appends, because the CLI has finished by then; filed as make-makefile-set-jobserver-style, which is the same question as make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules.\n\nThree fixtures: jobserver-style-unknown (refuses), jobserver-style-known (fifo builds), jobserver-style-unchecked-without-a-jobserver (`-j1` builds despite a nonsense style — the gating cell).\n\nNote on shape: `parse_arguments` crossed clippy's 100-line limit, so the option's handling was extracted to `read_jobserver_style`; that then put cli.rs one over the 50-function density gate, so the usage-less refusal was inlined at its single call site rather than kept as a `fatal` helper. Both fixed structurally, no bypasses."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-corpus-host-make-shellout
                title "Take the host's Make out of the vendored corpus's own gates"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:48:29.219879149Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while pointing the vendored-corpus gate at the pristine oracle (make-conformance-oracle-identity). Twelve makefiles under kati/testcase open with `MAKEVER:=$(shell make --version | grep \"Make [0-9]\" | sed -E 's/.*Make ([0-9]).*/\\1/')` and branch on it: comment_in_command, include_glob_order, posix_var, multi_implicit_output_patterns, multiline_recipe, implicit_pattern_rule_prefix, var_with_space, wildcard, shell_var_with_args, and — until this pass removed their rows — err_export_override, err_override_export and wildcard_cache. That `$(shell make ...)` is the host's Make on PATH whatever `--make` names, so the corpus still asks a program the gate does not identify which branch to take.\n\nIt is symmetric today and therefore not a defect: both tools evaluate the same `$(shell ...)` and land in the same branch, so it moves the corpus's own gates together rather than tilting the comparison, and this host's `make` is 4.4.1 like the oracle. It stops being symmetric the moment a host has 3.81, or no make at all, on PATH — the corpus would then take its 3.x branch or its empty-MAKEVER branch for both tools and the inventory would drift for a reason about the host rather than about either tool.\n\nThe obvious fix is for `observe` in examples/make_conformance.rs to prepend a directory holding a `make` link to the pinned oracle onto PATH for both runs, so the corpus's own shell-out reaches the oracle. Two things to check before doing it: the three `ifeq ($(MAKE)$(MAKEVER),make4)` cases are insensitive now only because `$(MAKE)` is an absolute path, and a PATH-injected `make` must not change what `$(MAKE)` expands to; and the corpus's recursive `submake` cases must keep reaching the tool under test rather than the injected link. Re-record and audit any row that moves, as this pass did."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:25:14.106921968Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:25:42.825518964Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The vendored corpus now asks the pinned oracle which Make it is written against, and no row moved on this host.\n\nWHAT WAS ASKED, AND OF WHOM. Twelve makefiles under kati/testcase open with `MAKEVER := $(shell make --version | grep \"Make [0-9]\" | sed -E 's/.*Make ([0-9]).*/\\1/')` and branch on the answer: comment_in_command, include_glob_order, posix_var, multi_implicit_output_patterns, multiline_recipe, implicit_pattern_rule_prefix, var_with_space, wildcard, shell_var_with_args, err_export_override, err_override_export, wildcard_cache. `make` there is a bare name, so it is whatever is on PATH — the host's Make, whichever build `--make` names. Symmetric, so it tilted nothing; what it did was let a program the gate does not identify decide what twelve cases test.\n\nTHE FIX is four lines and a link. `prepare_work` creates `<work>/bin/make` pointing at `config.make`, once, before any worker starts — every thread reads it and none writes it, and a link replaced under a running case is a flake with nothing in the output to explain it. `observe` puts that directory in front of the inherited PATH for both runs, beside the `LC_ALL` it already pins and for the same reason: an input to the comparison that is not either tool's answer should be the same known thing on both sides. The vendored makefiles are not touched — they are upstream kati's corpus and the harness had somewhere better to put the answer.\n\nTHE TWO THINGS THE NODE SAID TO CHECK, both checked rather than assumed.\n\n`$(MAKE)` does not move. It is argv[0], and both tools are spawned by absolute path — `absolute_program` has always made sure of that — so nothing on PATH reaches it. The three `ifeq ($(MAKE)$(MAKEVER),make4)` cases stay insensitive, and the seven scripts that test `grep -q \"^make\"` on the name they were handed still see a path. Demonstrated by the run: those ten rows are exactly where they were.\n\nThe recursive case still recurses into the tool under test. `submake_basic.mk` is the corpus's only recursion and it spells it `$(MAKE) -f submake/basic.mk`, which is the same argv[0]. No corpus makefile invokes a bare `make` in a recipe at all; the only other bare `make` in the tree is a target NAME in comment_in_command.mk (`test2: make$(MAKEVER)`).\n\nMEASURED BOTH WAYS, which is what makes this a fix rather than a tidy-up. With a `make` on PATH that answers `GNU Make 3.81` and nothing else changed:\n\n  before   raw 274/113, normalised 317 identical / 70 differing, and the gate\n           REFUSED: unclassified difference in shell_var_with_args.mk#test\n  after    raw 275/112, normalised 318 identical / 69 differing, exit 0 —\n           identical to a run on a host whose own make is 4.4.1\n\nSo the failure the node predicted is real, reproduces on demand, and is closed. On this host, where `/usr/bin/make` is 4.4.1 like the oracle, nothing moves at all: three consecutive runs give 318 identical / 69 differing and defect 33 / recorded 4 / extension 31 / artefact 1 every time, tests/make_corpus_inventory.tsv unchanged and its pinning test green.\n\ndocs/make-oracle-divergences.md carried this as the one remaining host dependency and now records it as closed, with the twelve cases named and the 3.81 measurement written down."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:32:59.906819821Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
