            }

            {
                id make-release-clippy-runnable
                title "Make the release script's clippy step runnable again"
                kind @chore
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:48:45.631578515Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Measured 2026-08-14 while running the gates for make-conformance-oracle-identity; both findings pre-date that work and neither was introduced by it.\n\n`scripts/check-make-conformance.sh` is fine. The two clippy lines in scripts/check-release.sh are not:\n\n  cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery\n  cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings\n\nThe first fails with 638 errors in `kati` and never reaches Ronin's own findings. The lint flags reach every crate cargo builds, and kati is a path dependency, so the pedantic and nursery groups are applied to exactly the vendored fork the second line exists to spare — the comment above it says so in as many words. `--no-deps` is what the flags need; the dispatches have been running `cargo clippy --lib --bins --no-deps -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery` for some time, and it is clean.\n\nThe second fails on one finding, in the fork and pre-existing: `unused import: crate::symtab::Interner` at kati/src-rs/command.rs:883, inside a `#[cfg(test)]` module, so it only fires on the `--all-targets` build. Deleting the line is the whole of the fix; it wants a kati commit and a submodule pin, which is why it has not been taken in passing by a node that had no other reason to touch kati.\n\nWorth noting for whoever takes this: with `--no-deps`, examples are still outside the lib+bins scope the dispatches use, and `tests/support/oracle.rs` carries four `assigning_clones` and one `struct_field_names` nursery finding that `examples/make_conformance.rs` now compiles. They are the shared module's, not the harness's. `rest.clone_into(&mut into.host)` for `into.host = rest.to_owned()` in a parser that runs three times is not an improvement, so the right answer is probably to scope the group rather than to take the suggestion."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:26:07.525127021Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:31:51.739148587Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered for the clippy step, which is the node's subject. The script now lints Ronin for the first time in weeks and reaches every step after it. One step further down is red for a reason that has nothing to do with lints and is filed rather than taken; see the last section.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, and it is one word further than the node said. The single line\n\n  cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery\n\nwas trying to say three things at once about three bodies of code and therefore said none of them. The node had the `--no-deps` half: the groups reach every crate cargo builds, kati is a path dependency, and the run died on the fork's inherited findings before it looked at Ronin. The half the node did not have is that Ronin's Cargo.toml ALREADY declares `nursery` and `pedantic` at warn in `[lints.clippy]`, with `redundant_pub_crate = \"allow\"` beside them. So the command-line `-W` flags were not what applied the standard to Ronin at all — they were re-enabling, on the command line, a lint the package had deliberately allowed, which is where `examples/support/workloads.rs`'s seventeen `pub(crate) constant inside private module` findings came from. They are not findings; they are a decision being overridden by a flag.\n\nTHE THREE PASSES, and what each is for:\n\n  cargo clippy --lib --bins --no-deps -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery\n  cargo clippy --all-targets --no-deps -- -D warnings -A clippy::pedantic -A clippy::nursery\n  cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings\n\nThe first is Ronin's product code at the house standard, which is the split every dispatch has been running by hand. The second is new and is what keeps this from being a narrowing: scoping to lib+bins alone would have taken tests, examples and benches out of clippy's reach entirely, where before they at least had a line pointed at them. They keep everything about correctness — style, complexity, perf, suspicious — and lose only the two groups they cannot pass. The third is unchanged and its comment is unchanged apart from the count.\n\nAll three are green. The second one found one real thing on the way: a `collapsible_if` in examples/make_upstream.rs, `style` rather than pedantic, pre-existing at 676dd32 and fixed here as a let-chain rather than allowed.\n\nTHE SIX FINDINGS the node asked about — scoped, and here is the arithmetic that decides it. At the declared standard `--all-targets --no-deps` leaves fifteen findings, not six:\n\n  tests/support/oracle.rs      1 struct_field_names, 4 assigning_clones\n  tests/make_port.rs           1 option_if_let_else\n  examples/make_conformance.rs the same five, through its `#[path]` include of oracle.rs\n  src/make/equivalence.rs      1 option_if_let_else, 1 `Err(_)` matches all errors\n\nThe last two are the ones that settle it. They are inside a `#[cfg(test)]` module in src/, so they are reached by the lib TEST target and not by `--lib`; fixing them means editing src/, which this dispatch is explicitly not to do. So `--all-targets` at the pedantic standard cannot be green whatever is done to tests/, and fixing the six would buy nothing today. The node's own reading of `assigning_clones` — `rest.clone_into(&mut into.host)` for `into.host = rest.to_owned()` in a parser that runs three times is not an improvement — is written into the script's comment as the reason the groups come off there.\n\nFiled as make-release-pedantic-in-the-test-scope: clear the fifteen and the second pass can be raised to the first's standard.\n\nEND TO END, measured rather than claimed. `scripts/check-release.sh` from a clean tree now clears, in order: fmt; `cargo check --all-targets`; all three clippy passes; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps`; `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast`; the Make equivalence gate at 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Then it stops at `nplan port check --wave 4`.\n\nThat step is not this node's and not this dispatch's. It reports `Wave 1 · 170 symbols · markup 0/170` and refuses. The 170 symbols are the retired C samurai — `plan/.port-manifest.styx` still lists `build.c`, `deps.c`, `htab.c` and the rest, and `.config/nspec/config.styx` says in as many words that \"the C corpus it was ported from has been retired from this repository, so the source impl that tracked those symbols is gone with it\". `nplan port status` shows the same shape for every one of the 170: rule ✓, SOURCE ANNOTATION ·, target ✓, verification ✓. The one column that cannot be filled is the one whose file no longer exists. Established as pre-existing rather than assumed: a detached worktree at 676dd32, this dispatch's baseline, gives the identical output.\n\nEverything after it was run by hand and is green: pinned Ninja conformance 425/425 upstream tests, 10 differential cases, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; the performance gate, all seven workloads, Ronin ahead of pinned Ninja on six of them (large-manifest-parse 156.6 ms against 313.2, clean-tree-noop 8.2 against 12.6, manifest-command-evaluation 6.6 against 12.3) and behind on scheduler-barrier as it has been; `nplan spec uncovered` no uncovered rules; `nplan spec stale` no stale references; `nplan lint` clean, 0 violations; `nplan audit` no transitions proposed.\n\nSo the script is green from its first line to its last except for one step in the middle whose subject was retired from the repository. Filed as make-release-port-gate-has-no-source."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:33:01.675429641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile
                title "Bring the readable makefiles up to date before refusing the missing one"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-included-makefile-restarts)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T07:22:28.374905344Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: options/dash-f.diff.4, handed over by make-every-makefile-argument-is-read, which fixed the other eighteen of its nineteen and left this one because it is a different mechanism wearing a `-f` costume.\n\nThe defect has nothing to do with `-f`. It reproduces against the pristine oracle with one default Makefile and an ordinary `include`:\n\n  Makefile (mtime backdated so its own rule fires):\n    include nope.mk\n    all: ; @echo hi\n    Makefile: Makefile.src ; @echo REMAKE; touch Makefile\n    Makefile.src: ; @echo MKSRC; touch Makefile.src\n\n  GNU:   MKSRC / REMAKE / `Makefile:1: nope.mk: No such file or directory` / `*** No rule to make target 'nope.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2, and Makefile.src on disk.\n  Ronin: the two diagnostics alone, exit 2, and nothing on disk.\n\nGNU brings every makefile it can reach up to date first and refuses afterwards; Ronin refuses during dependency analysis, so the remaking never runs. Probed and confirmed: GNU does not restart after the successful remake either — `$(info $(MAKE_RESTARTS))` prints once. The refusal wins over the restart, which is `update_goal_chain` dying inside `complain()` before main.c reaches its `any_remade → re_exec` test.\n\nThe `-f` face is the same sequence: with `bye.mk` holding `bye.mk: bye.mk.src; touch $@` and `bye.mk.src:; touch $@`, and bye.mk backdated,\n\n  echo 'all:; $(info hello, world)' | make -fbye.mk -fR - all\n\nGNU says `R: No such file or directory`, runs `touch bye.mk.src` and `touch bye.mk`, then `*** No rule to make target 'R'.  Stop.`. Ronin now emits both diagnostics in GNU's order and refuses with the same status, but does no work in between.\n\nWhere it lives: `kati/src-rs/dep.rs`, `plan_regeneration`, which raises `error_loc!(... No rule to make target ...)` the moment it reaches a required makefile with no rule — discarding the regeneration roots it had already collected for the makefiles ahead of it. The fix is to carry the refusal out of dependency analysis rather than raise it there: `Evaluated` and Ronin's `Loaded` gain a pending refusal, `prepare_graph` builds the regeneration roots as it does today and raises the refusal instead of settling or restarting. Raising it on Restart as well as on Settled is what matches GNU, and the probe above is the evidence that it should.\n\nNot folded into make-every-makefile-argument-is-read because it changes when a refusal fires relative to the restart machinery `make-included-makefile-restarts` delivered, which is a contract of its own and deserves its own conformance and build-intent run."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:04:27.820388959Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the ordering the node asked about turned out to be positional rather than all-or-nothing.\n\nProbed against the oracle. GNU walks `read_files` in the order it read them and brings each one up to date in turn, so what precedes the refusal is decided by where the unmakeable makefile sits in that order:\n\n  include nope.mk / include gen.mk (gen.mk backdated, has a rule)\n    -> refuses immediately; gen.mk is NOT remade, gen.src is not made\n  include gen.mk / include nope.mk\n    -> `GENSRC`, `GEN`, then `Makefile:2: nope.mk: No such file or directory` and `*** No rule to make target 'nope.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n  two missing required includes\n    -> the first one named, and only that one\n  the node's own reproducer (Makefile remakes itself, `include nope.mk`)\n    -> MKSRC, REMAKE, then the refusal, exit 2, Makefile.src on disk\n  the `-f` face (`make -fbye.mk -fR -`)\n    -> `R: No such file or directory`, `touch bye.mk.src`, `touch bye.mk`, then the refusal\n\nSo `complain()` ends the run from where the update reaches it — which also confirms the node's probe that there is no restart: the refusal wins over `main.c`'s `any_remade -> re_exec` test even when the makefile ahead of it really was remade.\n\nShape delivered: `plan_regeneration` no longer raises. It returns `(roots, Option<anyhow::Error>)` and stops collecting at the first unmakeable required makefile — the `break` is what reproduces the positional cells above, because the roots collected before it are exactly the ones GNU remakes. kati's `Evaluated` and Ronin's `Loaded` gained a `refusal`, `CompilationState::admit` keeps the first, and `prepare_graph` raises it: before the build when there is nothing to build, and after `build_compiler_inputs` on both `Settled` and `Restart`. A goal-analysis failure that arrives while a refusal is pending loses to the refusal, because GNU refuses from inside the makefile update and never looks at a goal.\n\nkati's own binary has no makefile-remaking phase, so `rkati` raises the refusal the moment `evaluate` returns — which is what it did before and is why err_include.mk#default is still exit 2. (It briefly was not; caught by the Make-conformance gate.)\n\nRows: options/dash-f.diff.4 moved unclassified -> narration, now differing only in `ronin:` for `make:`, the progress lines, and the `*** `/`Stop.` decoration. features/include.diff.17 moved with it unasked — GNU echoes `echo > inc1` and then refuses over inc2, and Ronin now does the same work before the same refusal.\n\nThree fixtures: makefiles-are-remade-before-a-missing-one-is-refused, a-makefile-ahead-of-a-refused-one-is-remade, a-makefile-behind-a-refused-one-is-not-remade.\n\nNot matched, and filed: for a makefile reached through an `include` line GNU holds the `No such file or directory` complaint back and prints it with the refusal, after the remaking; Ronin still prints it during the read. The `-f` face already matches, which is why the owned row does. See make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:23:33.956459652Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-circular-dependency-is-dropped
                title "Drop a circular dependency and carry on rather than refusing the build"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:07:23.904704606Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-match-anything-rule-chaining, and pre-existing rather than caused by it: the widened search simply reaches far enough to expose it.\n\nOwns two rows of the upstream inventory: features/implicit_search.diff.48 and .49, which that node held and could not resolve. Both moved from `compiler no-rule-to-make` to `unclassified evaluation` with the compatibility pass in place — Ronin now finds the same rules GNU does and then dies where GNU carries on.\n\nGNU Make does not treat a cycle as fatal. `update_file_1` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:606 and :1165) tests `is_updating (d->file)` while walking a target's prerequisites, and when the prerequisite is already being updated it says `Circular %s <- %s dependency dropped.` on stderr, unlinks that one edge from the list, and keeps going. The build then succeeds, with exit status 0.\n\nSimplest reproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n  all: a\n  a: b ; @echo made a\n  b: a ; @echo made b\n\nGNU prints `make: Circular b <- a dependency dropped.`, then `made b`, `made a`, and exits 0. Ronin prints `ronin: dependency cycle: a -> b -> a` and exits 2. So this is general, not an implicit-search question — the two owned rows only reach it through the pattern search.\n\nThe rows' own shape, with hello.f on disk and -r:\n\n  all: hello.tsk\n  %.tsk: %.o; $(info hello.tsk)\n  %.o: %.c; $(info hello.c)\n  %.o: %.f %.tsk; $(info hello.f)\n\n`%.o: %.f %.tsk` is chosen because hello.f exists, and its `hello.tsk` prerequisite is taken by the compatibility pass — it is written down on `all`. That closes the loop hello.tsk -> hello.o -> hello.tsk. GNU drops it, runs both recipes and reports `Nothing to be done for 'all'.`; Ronin refuses the whole build. .49 is the same makefile with `%.o::` in place of `%.o:`.\n\nTwo questions the fix has to settle before it is written. Which edge goes: GNU drops the one it is standing on when it notices, so the answer depends on the order prerequisites are walked, and Ronin's graph is built rather than walked. And where it lives: Ronin's refusal is `src/error.rs:756`, raised out of the graph, so this is a Ronin-layer question and not a kati one unless the drop has to happen while the plan is being built. The announcement itself is narration and is not owed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:03:42.810230596Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:04:07.464778341Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed the whole matrix against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, then delivered the drop in kati. Every cell below is now byte-identical in effect; the residue is Ronin's `[N/M]` progress lines and `ronin:` for `make:`.\n\nThe matrix, and what GNU does:\n\n  a: b / b: a          `make: Circular b <- a dependency dropped.`, made b, made a, exit 0\n  a: a (self)          `make: Circular a <- a dependency dropped.` — NOT silent, exit 0\n  a->b->c->a           `Circular c <- a dependency dropped.`, c b a, exit 0\n  b: | a (order-only)  identical drop and wording; order-only is on the same list, marked rather than held apart\n  reached twice        one warning only: the edge is unlinked from the list, so the second reader finds it already gone\n  two back edges       both dropped, each announced, in prerequisite order\n  -j4                  identical to -j1; the drop serialises what is left\n  diamond              no drop at all: an already-finished target is not an ancestor\n  invented intermediate (the two owned rows) `Circular hello.o <- hello.tsk dependency dropped.`, both recipes run\n\n`$^` is the load-bearing cell. The drop is `file->deps` surgery (remake.c:608 and :1167 unlink the entry), and that list is what the automatic variables read, so the *dependent* loses the prerequisite for good and the target it pointed at keeps all of its own: for `a: b c` / `b: a`, GNU gives `b hat=[]` and `a hat=[b c] q=[b c] plus=[b c]`. Ronin matches exactly. That is also the answer to the note's \"where does it live\" question: `$^` is bound from the DepNode's `actual_inputs` at command-expansion time, so the drop has to be a kati-layer edit and not a Ronin graph-layer one.\n\nDelivered in kati/src-rs/dep.rs as `DepBuilder::drop_circular_dependencies`, run at the end of `build` over the regeneration roots in read order and then the goals in goal order, each target's `deps` then `order_onlys` in written order — an iterative DFS with GNU's own `updating`/`updated` pair, keyed on the planned record's identity, which is what makes a `::` target's separate actions one target the way `is_updating`'s double-colon base does. A back edge is announced with `warn_loc!(.., None, ..)`, which renders as `ronin: Circular X <- Y dependency dropped.` — GNU's `error(NILF, ..)` shape — and removed from both the edge list and the name list beside it.\n\nNinja-native graphs keep refusing: `src/graph.rs` is untouched, `build a: cp b` / `build b: cp a` still says `dependency cycle: a -> b -> a` and exits 1, and the graph-equivalence gate stays at 0 disagreements.\n\nRows: features/implicit_search.diff.48 and .49 both moved unclassified -> narration. Two more moved with them, unowned and unasked for: features/double_colon.diff.8 (`Circular d <- d`) and, from the same work, the vendored corpus's circular_dep.mk#test became byte-identical and left the Make-conformance inventory.\n\nSix fixtures under tests/make/: circular-dependency-is-dropped, -leaves-the-prerequisite-list, circular-self-dependency-is-dropped, -through-an-order-only-edge, -is-dropped-once-for-two-readers, -through-an-invented-intermediate.\n\nOne cell does not match and is filed rather than fixed: options/shuffle.diff.9 stayed unclassified. GNU walks the *shuffled* prerequisite list when it drops (`d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du`, remake.c:596), so `--shuffle=reverse` changes which edge goes; Ronin's shuffle is applied in src/make.rs after `evaluate` returns, which is after the drop. See make-shuffle-reaches-the-circular-drop."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:23:29.813756622Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-scheduler-completion-test-is-timing-fragile
                title "Assert completion-driven scheduling without a wall-clock race"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:42:25.856355185Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-builtin-rule-catalogue, which raised its flake rate rather than caused it. Two defects in one test, and the second is what makes the first look like a regression.\n\nsrc/build/tests.rs, `ronin_scheduler_releases_dependents_on_each_completion`. The graph is a 0.03s `fast`, a 0.30s `slow`, and an `after` that depends on `fast` and asserts `test ! -e $dir/slow`, run at -j2 and repeated eight times. What it means to assert is that a dependent is released when its own prerequisite finishes rather than when the whole wave does — a real property, and one worth a test. What it actually asserts is that the scheduler releases `after` within 0.27s of `fast` finishing, measured against a real clock while 500 other tests share the machine.\n\nMeasured on this host, `cargo test --lib` under scripts/sandboxed: six runs green before this dispatch's change, five of six after. The change added no scheduler work at all — it is entirely inside the Make front end — but the 53 `make::` lib tests it shares the run with went from 0.11s to 0.27s of CPU, and that is enough. Any future change that makes any concurrent test heavier will do the same, so the number is not the point; the margin is.\n\nThe second defect is why one flake reads as a permanent failure. `build_fixture` names its directory `ronin-build-<label>-<pid>-<counter>`, and a failing test panics before `fs::remove_dir_all`, leaving `slow` on disk. Under `scripts/sandboxed` the run is in its own pid namespace, so `std::process::id()` is small and repeats across runs — pid 14 here, every time. The next run reuses the directory, finds `slow` already there, and fails before it schedules anything. Three consecutive runs looked deterministic until the leftovers were removed by hand.\n\nWhat a fix has to settle: what evidence of \"released on completion\" is available that is not a stopwatch. The scheduler already records `commands_ran` in order, and a rule that blocks on a fifo or a lock file would let `after` prove `slow` had not finished without either of them racing. The fixture path wants entropy that survives a pid namespace, and the directory wants removing on the failing path too."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:40:42.549144028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Second sighting, with numbers, and a new cost: this test now gates scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh, whose first phase is `cargo test --all-targets`, so on a busy host the Ninja conformance gate cannot be run at all without retrying it.\n\nMeasured while checking whether make-remade-target-is-re-observed had worsened it, since that work touches `finish_command_edge` beside the scheduler loop. Under full-suite load on the same host, same session: 4 flaps in 6 runs on the fixed tree against 5 in 6 on 825c9f0 — no worse, and both far above the 1-in-6 this node was opened with. In isolation on the fixed tree, 0 failures in 20 runs. The 0.03s/0.30s margin the test is built on is simply narrower than this host's scheduling noise when 500 tests run in parallel beside it; when it flapped, eight consecutive attempts at the conformance script flapped with it, and the phases behind it passed on the first run made with the script's `conformance` example invoked directly (425 upstream tests, 10 differential, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence, log content).\n\nNothing about the change could reach it: the fixture is a parsed manifest, where `outputs_reobserved` is false on every edge and no rule sets `restat`, so neither branch the change added is live."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:44:25.808658618Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:49:25.880768524Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed, and the stopwatch is gone rather than widened.\n\nWHAT THE TEST NOW PROVES, and how. The claim is unchanged: a dependent is released when its own prerequisite finishes, not when the wave it started with does. The two are now told apart by a handshake instead of a margin. `fast` is a bare `touch`; `slow` waits for `$dir/after` to appear and only then touches its own output; `after` depends on `fast` and runs `test ! -e $dir/slow && touch $out`. Release-on-completion makes the three interlock: `fast` finishes, `after` is released while `slow` is still waiting, `after` sees no `slow` and touches itself, and `slow` sees `after` and finishes. Release-with-the-wave leaves both stuck, so `slow` gives up after 1000 sleeps of 0.01s and touches its output anyway — and then `after` runs, finds `slow` on disk, and fails the build. The assertion is the build's outcome, and `builder.build()` now carries an `expect` that says which of the two happened. The bound exists so a regression reports rather than hangs; it is not a margin, because nothing has to happen within it for the test to pass.\n\nTeeth, proved rather than argued. A temporary copy of the test with one character of difference — `build $dir/after: after $dir/fast $dir/slow`, which is exactly \"released with the wave\" — fails in 12.3s with `'after' was held until 'slow' had finished: dependents were released with the wave rather than on their own prerequisite's completion: Stopped { reason: SubcommandFailed }`. Removed again after measuring.\n\nTHE FIXTURE PATH, which is why one flake read as a permanent failure. `build_fixture` now goes through `fixture_directory`, which puts `SystemTime::now()` nanos in the name beside the pid and the counter, and creates it with `fs::create_dir` rather than `create_dir_all` — a name that already exists is a name to walk away from and retry, never one to build in. That closes the pid-namespace reuse directly: no run can ever inherit another run's leftovers, whatever the pid namespace hands out. `plan_graph`'s temp file is named the same way for the same reason. Leftovers are now inert rather than poisonous, so removing them is housekeeping; `sweep_stale_fixtures` does it once per process for `ronin-build-*` and `ronin-plan-test-*` entries untouched for an hour, which cannot reach a fixture another process is using.\n\nNUMBERS, both trees measured in the same session on the same host, `cargo test --lib` under scripts/sandboxed, six runs each.\n  Base (fff05e4 + the stdin fix): 6 failures in 6 runs, every one `ronin_scheduler_releases_dependents_on_each_completion`. Run 1 flaked on the 0.27s margin and runs 2 to 6 then failed on run 1's leftovers under the reused pid — the node's second defect, reproduced exactly, and the reason three consecutive runs once looked deterministic.\n  Fixed: 0 failures in 6 runs. The lib suite also runs faster, because the test no longer sleeps 0.30s to prove anything: 0.69s for 501 tests.\n\nFOUND WHILE MEASURING, not this node's and not fixed here: `cli::tests::runner_resolves_the_last_change_without_mutating_process_cwd` failed once in those six baseline runs with `left: \"/tmp/.tmpAgBgEC\"`, which is a `tempfile::tempdir()` belonging to `src/make/equivalence.rs`. The process working directory is a process-global that `src/make.rs`'s `in_directory` moves during Make compilation, and one test asserts it while another moves it, in the same binary, in parallel. Filed as make-process-cwd-tests-race-each-other."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:49:56.164506702Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T16:15:37.038813964Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The second cost this node recorded is settled: `scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh` runs again, first try.\n\nIts first phase is `cargo test --all-targets`, which is why eight consecutive attempts flapped when this test did. On the fixed tree the whole script passed on the first run made of it — phase 1 clean (681 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored for the ronin package; 845 passed under `--workspace --all-targets`), then the Windows type check, then 425 pinned upstream tests, 10 differential, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log content. 85 seconds end to end. Nobody has to invoke the conformance example directly to get past the suite any more.\n\nFull workspace suite, three consecutive sandboxed runs on the fixed tree: 794 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored each time, 54s, 60s, 57s. 794 is the 793 baseline plus make_port's new round-trip test.\n\nOne thing found afterwards and fixed in the same file: `sweep_stale_fixtures` wrote `Duration::from_secs(60 * 60)`, which clippy's pedantic `duration_suboptimal_units` reads as a smaller unit than it needs — `Duration::from_hours(1)` says the same thing. It was the only new clippy finding anywhere in the dispatch; ronin lib+bins are back to zero under pedantic+nursery, and kati to zero under `-D warnings`."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-member-targets
                title "Read a target named as an archive member as one"
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:42:46.676442218Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-builtin-rule-catalogue. The catalogue's first pattern rule is `(%): %` and it is now installed, so the rule that files an object into an archive is present and can never fire: nothing in Ronin reads `lib.a(foo.o)` as anything but a filename with parentheses in it.\n\nReproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, with foo.o on disk:\n\n  all: libx.a(foo.o)\n\nGNU runs `ar -rv libx.a foo.o` — `(%)` matched, the stem is `foo.o`, and the prerequisite `%` is the member. Ronin refuses: `'libx.a(foo.o)', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\nThe archive form is a name shape rather than a rule: GNU's ar_name() splits `lib(member)` wherever a target or prerequisite is written, and the whole of dir.c/arscan.c answers whether a member is present and how old it is. `(%)` matching is one line of that; the freshness question is the rest, and it is what decides whether the rule runs at all.\n\nThe suffix half is missing with it. convert_to_pattern turns a `.X.a:` rule into `(%.o): %.X` as well as `%.a: %.X` — rule.c:363 — and Ronin's suffix conversion makes only the second. There are no built-in `.X.a` entries, so this is only reachable from a makefile that writes one.\n\nNot urgent and not free: `.SUFFIXES` carries `.a`, so `%.a` disqualifies match-anything rules for a name ending in it, and that half already agrees. What is absent is building the archive, which is a shape of Makefile Ronin cannot compile at all rather than one it compiles differently."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:59.495737936Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Core delivered: `lib.a(member.o)` is now a member of an archive everywhere a target or prerequisite is written.\n\nThree pieces. kati `src-rs/archive.rs` splits the name shape — including the doubled `lib.a(lib.a(m.o))` form and the `-lfoo` search name — so `$@` is the archive, `$%` the member, and `$*` the stem GNU Make gives. `src/os/archive.rs` dates a member by scanning the archive index, following `f_mtime`'s three-part rule rather than a lookup: no archive means no member; a zero date or an absent entry means no member; and a member file on disk newer than the indexed date also means no member, because it was rebuilt and not yet filed. `src/os.rs` routes `stat` through it, gated to Unix — Make mode is a Unix front end, so there is no host where the flag is set and the branch is missing.\n\nWith that, the built-in `(%): %` rule installed by make-builtin-rule-catalogue can finally fire, which was the observation that filed this node.\n\nThe middle rule earns its own line. `ar` on this host defaults to deterministic mode and writes every member's date as zero, so `ar_member_date` answers \"absent\" for every member of an archive built by plain `ar -rv`, and both tools re-run `$(AR) $(ARFLAGS)` every invocation. That is agreement about a rule, not a shortcut that happens to match.\n\nFive corpus cases, all byte-identical to GNU Make 4.4.1: the built-in rule filing a member, a member made from its source, the automatic variables over a member, a member of an archive written with real dates being up to date, and a member named through a directory.\n\nNot claimed, and split out rather than swept under the node: make-archive-member-globbing, make-archive-suffix-rule-conversion, make-archive-member-list-in-one-name, make-archive-member-touch. GNU Make's own `features/archives` script is the measure and still shows eight compiler-class differences, which are what those four are worth; `archives` stays off the supported-feature list in kati `src-rs/evaluate.rs` until they are gone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:10:23.980933389Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:21:21.979014348Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "One upstream inventory case moved the wrong way and it is worth knowing why, because it is not a behaviour regression.\n\n`features/se_explicit.diff` went from `narration / ninja-progress` to `unclassified / ninja-progress+evaluation`. The suite's first test branches on the tool's own declaration:\n\n    my $prereq = exists $FEATURES{'archives'} ? '$' : '$(PRE)';\n\nand then writes `bar$$baz: $$(PRE)`, whose prerequisite reads after `$$`-collapsing as the six characters `$(PRE)`. A make with archives splits that into archive `$` and member `PRE`, so `.DEFAULT`'s `@echo '$@'` prints `$`; a make without them prints the whole name.\n\nRonin now prints `$` — verified against GNU Make 4.4.1 directly, which prints `$` and `$%` of `PRE` for the same makefile — while `.FEATURES` still omits `archives`, deliberately, because ar_glob, the `.X.a` conversion, the multi-member name and `-t` are outstanding. The suite therefore picks the no-archives expectation and measures it against archives behaviour.\n\nSo the case is the declaration disagreeing with the conduct, not the conduct being wrong, and it resolves itself: when make-archive-member-globbing, make-archive-suffix-rule-conversion, make-archive-member-list-in-one-name and make-archive-member-touch land and `archives` joins `.FEATURES`, this case flips to the `$` branch and the difference goes. That makes it a usable completion signal for those four rather than debt.\n\nTotals are unchanged either way — 1283 recorded, 1283 now, with the compiler-class count still zero."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-remade-target-is-re-observed
                title "Re-observe a remade Make target instead of assuming it changed"
                kind @fix
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.remade-target-re-observed])
                }
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T10:10:17.71903201Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T10:10:31.815162022Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 edge cells probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before writing any code, three invocations each, so the fix is written to a matrix rather than to the one reproducer.\n\nThe decisive finding is that GNU does not carry \"the recipe ran\" forward as \"the target changed\" at all: after a prerequisite is remade it re-stats the file and compares the timestamp it then has against the dependent's, and nothing else. Ninja's `restat` is narrower — it prunes only on an exactly equal mtime — so translating Make onto `restat` alone would have diverged on two of the ten cells.\n\n  no-op recipe, output mtime unmoved      dependent NOT remade, on every run\n  recipe never creates the output         dependent remade, on every run\n  output mtime moved backward             dependent NOT remade (still older)\n  output rewritten to an identical mtime  dependent NOT remade\n  output moved forward, still older       dependent NOT remade  <- not ninja restat\n  output moved forward, now newer         dependent remade\n  dependent older than an unmoved output  dependent remade (plain comparison)\n  .PHONY prerequisite, no file            dependent remade, on every run\n  .PHONY prerequisite, file present       dependent remade, on every run  <- phony is never re-observed\n  grouped `a b &:`, no member moved       dependent NOT remade\n  grouped `a b &:`, one member moved      dependent remade\n  double-colon with a no-op recipe        dependent NOT remade\n  absent prerequisite with a recipe       dependent remade, and it is in `$?`\n\nRead as one rule: a remade target is re-observed on disk, and a target GNU never observes on disk — a phony one, or one the recipe left absent — counts as changed. That is the rule to implement, and the missing-output guard the dispatch flagged is the second half of it rather than an exception to it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:03:47.272842053Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Implemented as a graph property the engine reads, not a Make branch in the engine and not a `restat` binding.\n\n`Edge::outputs_reobserved` is set by the direct Make sink on every edge that has a recipe (src/make/sink.rs, both the ordinary path and the recursive wrapper), beside the existing `set_filesystem_only_freshness` call. A recipe-less edge runs nothing and is skipped, so a `.PHONY` alias does not acquire it. The manifest writer is untouched: `restat` is still emitted for `.KATI_RESTAT` and nothing else, so `build.ninja` does not diverge from kati's. The equivalence comparison stays silent about the new property, as it already does about peers and `.DELETE_ON_ERROR`, and `re_observation_reaches_only_the_direct_graph` asserts both halves — the direct graph marks all three recipe edges including the `.KATI_RESTAT` one, the parsed manifest marks none, and `differences` reports nothing.\n\nIn `finish_command_edge` the prune decision widened from `command.restat` to `restat || reobserved`, with `reobserved` guarded three ways: the edge carries the flag, it is not `always_dirty` (a phony target is not a file, so there is nothing to observe and GNU always remakes its readers — probed), and every output exists after the command (GNU reads an absent target as infinitely new — probed). `graph.rs:458`'s `input_dirty` is untouched, as the dispatch said: what a recipe will do to a timestamp is not knowable before it runs.\n\nTWO DEVIATIONS from the sketch, both forced by measurement.\n\n1. A fourth guard, for wall time. Firing `recompute_consumers_after_restat` on every completed edge cost the real-project gate 22s → 25/30/27s, because each fired scan re-evaluates the whole cone behind every consumer. The guard is that an output no older than `command_start_mtime` is one the command really wrote, and nothing that reads it can have been made since — a consumer waits for all of its inputs, so it was made before this command or not at all, and either way it is older than the output. Re-observing such an output can only reach the answer the build already holds, so the scan is skipped. It is one-sided: a coarse filesystem clock resolves into doing the work, never into skipping it. The gate went back to 21s/22s against a 22s/22s baseline measured on the same host in the same session.\n\n2. A defect in the absent-intermediate substitution, which the fix exposed. An invented file that is not there stands in for the newest thing behind it, and `recompute_edge_dirty_with` assigned that stand-in only when the output's mtime was missing — so once assigned it froze. Nothing noticed while an ordinary Make edge could never prune, because the substitution was only ever computed once. With pruning it is computed again after the thing behind it was remade, and the frozen value made the chain in GNU's own suite (features/parallelism, Savannah bug #30653) stop one edge in: `file2` was remade, `file3` kept standing in for `file2`'s pre-build timestamp, and `file4` was never made where GNU makes it. The substitution is now redone on every scan, and `finish_command_edge` clears the recorded flag once the edge's command has run and its outputs have been stat'd, so a real mtime can never be substituted over. Verified against the oracle: both tools now leave the same three files with `touch file3` the only echoed line."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:04:17.261916625Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Evidence.\n\npcre2 10.47, reconstructed from /data/pcre2.tar.bz2 and configured as the Necessary OS recipe does (shared, static, pcre2-8, unicode, jit). From-scratch build: 45 edges, unchanged from the previous binary's 45. Second invocation: 1 edge, the `src/config.h: src/stamp-h1` no-op; third invocation: 1 edge, the same one. GNU Make 4.4.1 `--trace` on that tree says exactly the same thing — `update target 'src/config.h' due to: src/stamp-h1` and its two `test -f` lines, nothing else — and its `-q` answers 1 for the same reason. `make -j8 install`: 12 edges, being the sub-make's config.h edge and 11 install recipes, with no compile or link edge among them; the only `CCLD`-shaped line is libtool's own relink inside `install-libLTLIBRARIES`, which is libtool's business and not a graph edge. The previous binary on the same tree: 45 edges from scratch, 42 on the second invocation (the entire tree recompiled and relinked), and 91 on the install pass — which is the graph in which `pcre2test` was linking while libtool had the `.so` moved aside.\n\nEight build-intent cases recorded from the pristine oracle: target-remade-without-moving-leaves-readers-alone, target-remade-but-absent-remakes-readers, target-remade-without-moving-through-a-chain, target-phony-prerequisite-remakes-a-newer-reader, target-grouped-remade-without-moving-leaves-readers-alone, target-grouped-moved-member-spares-the-other-readers, target-remade-to-a-time-older-than-its-reader, target-double-colon-remade-without-moving-spares-readers. Against the previous binary six of the eight fail and two pass: the two that pass are the guards — the absent target and the phony prerequisite — which is what a guard is for, and the six that fail are the defect. `a_stamp_recipe_that_moves_nothing_rebuilds_nothing` in tests/make_regressions.rs carries the autoconf shape across three invocations, because one invocation is a state and three is the claim that the state is a fixed point, and then moves the header to assert the other half.\n\nGates. cargo fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 777 (775 baseline plus the equivalence test and the regression test); Clippy zero findings for ronin lib+bins under pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` and zero for kati under `-D warnings`; Make build-intent oracle 578 cases (570 baseline plus the eight); Make conformance 387 runs, 317 identical / 70 differing, unchanged; direct-vs-manifest equivalence 297 makefiles, 278 graphs, 0 disagreements, 1 both-refused (the known kati defect); Ninja conformance all 425 pinned upstream tests, 10 differential, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log content; real-project gate 21s and 22s against 22s and 22s for the previous binary on the same host.\n\nUpstream inventory: 1287 rows, one moved, class counts 195/18/45/1029 → 194/18/45/1030. `features/reinvoke.diff.2` unclassified (ninja-progress+product-name+recipe-echo+evaluation) → narration (ninja-progress+recipe-echo). It is on the list of known flappers, and this time it is not flapping — it is the node's own subject. The case is the suite's comment \"we create an included file that's out-of-date, but then the rule doesn't update it. Make shouldn't re-exec\": `b : c ; test -f $@ || echo >> $@` with `a : b` included. Run by hand against all three binaries, GNU prints `test -f b || echo >> b` then `hello`; the previous binary rebuilt the included makefile every pass, re-read it, and died with `ronin: manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries`; this binary prints GNU's two lines. The move was recorded rather than reverted because it is deterministic across four runs and is a correction, and the `product-name` component of the old classification was Ronin's own fatal diagnostic disappearing. An intermediate state of this work also moved `features/parallelism.diff.10` the wrong way, narration → unclassified; that was the frozen absent-intermediate substitution described in the decision note above, and with it fixed the row holds its recorded value.\n\nThe flaky test. `build::tests::ronin_scheduler_releases_dependents_on_each_completion` failed in some full-suite runs. Measured rather than assumed, because this change touches `finish_command_edge` beside it: 0 failures in 20 isolated runs on this tree, and under identical full-suite load 4 flaps in 6 runs here against 5 in 6 for the previous binary's tree. The test builds a parsed-manifest graph, where `outputs_reobserved` is false on every edge and `restat` is unset, so no line of this change is reachable from it. Not touched; it belongs to make-scheduler-completion-test-is-timing-fragile. The host is busier than when the 1-in-6 figure was recorded, which is the whole of the difference."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-stdin-refusal-test-races-its-write
                title "Stop the standard-input refusal test racing its own write"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:40:29.473454593Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "`make_refuses_standard_input_twice` fails intermittently, and the failure is the test's own harness rather than the product. `piped_make` (tests/make_regressions.rs) spawns the tool with a piped stdin and then `write_all(...).unwrap()`s the makefile into it. What the test asserts is that Ronin refuses an invocation naming standard input twice *before it reads a byte of it* — so when Ronin wins that race, it has already exited, the pipe has no reader, and the write takes EPIPE and panics at the unwrap.\n\nMeasured on a loaded host, 15 runs of the suite each: 3 failures on this branch and 4 on the tree at 825c9f0, always this test, always the same unwrap. So it is neither new nor mine — it was found while measuring whether make-remade-target-is-re-observed had worsened the scheduler flapper beside it.\n\nThe fix is in the harness: a write to a pipe whose reader has gone is one of the outcomes this test is about, so `piped_make` should treat a broken pipe as a successful delivery of nothing rather than as a panic, and the refusal assertions should stand as they are. Both spellings of the race must keep failing the test if Ronin ever reads the makefile before refusing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:40:12.718459052Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:42:43.566248192Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probe note: checking lint acceptance."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:43:12.031931541Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "(The one-line entry above it is a stray probe of the note tool — an `errata` entry is refused by lint here, and the write had to be retried as a `note`. It carries no content; this entry is the record.)\n\n\"Race\" understates it, and the node's title and its first note should be read with this correction. Measured at fff05e4 before touching anything: 4 failures in 5 sandboxed runs of the test alone, on an otherwise quiet host, always `Os { code: 32, kind: BrokenPipe }` at the `write_all(...).unwrap()`. That is not an intermittent scheduling race, it is the ordinary outcome. The behaviour under test is Ronin refusing `-f- -f-` BEFORE reading a byte of standard input, so when the product is correct the child has already exited and the pipe has no reader by the time the parent reaches its write. The test failed because the product worked. The earlier 3-in-15 figure was measured under full-suite load, which slows Ronin's exit enough for the parent to win more often — load made the test look better, not worse, which is the opposite of the scheduler flapper beside it.\n\nTwo things follow, and both are in the fix.\n\nFirst, `piped_make` treats `ErrorKind::BrokenPipe` as delivery of nothing, panics on any other write error, and drops the write handle before waiting. The previous spelling dropped a temporary at the end of the statement; a named handle has to be dropped explicitly, and the ordering test needs the EOF that drop provides.\n\nSecond, tolerating EPIPE alone would leave this node's own requirement unasserted. With the old piped source (`all: ; @printf hello > out`) a Ronin that read standard input before refusing would still refuse, still build nothing, and still pass, so nothing would fail. Standard input now announces itself if it is ever read: the piped source is `$(error standard input was read before the invocation was refused)` above that rule. A run that reads it refuses for the wrong reason and fails the `contains(\"Makefile from standard input specified twice\")` assertion; a run that never reads it never sees it, whether the write landed in the pipe's buffer or took EPIPE. Delivery and detection cannot disagree — EPIPE only happens once the read end is gone, and a child still able to read gets the whole 100-odd bytes into an empty pipe buffer. Verified from the other side by hand: `-fbye.mk -f-` prints `ronin: -:1: standard input was read before the invocation was refused.` and exits 2, while `-fbye.mk -f- -f-` prints `ronin: Makefile from standard input specified twice`. The assertion discriminates them.\n\nStability: 20 sandboxed runs of `make_refuses_standard_input_twice` and `make_orders_standard_input_among_files` together, 0 failures, against 4-in-5 for the refusal test alone at the base. No product code was touched."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:43:32.952292673Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-sees-the-build-as-it-runs
                title "Expand a pattern prerequisite's wildcard against the files the build has made"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcards)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:32:11.986802525Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcards, which fixed the substitution but not the moment.\n\nGNU Make expands a pattern rule's prerequisite wildcard inside `pattern_search`, which runs while the build runs. A file an earlier recipe created is therefore visible to a later rule's wildcard. Reproducer against the pristine oracle, with nothing at all on disk beforehand:\n\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: mk a.four\n  mk: ; @touch a.three\n  %.four : %.t* ; @echo \"GOT [$@: $(sort $^)]\"\n\nGNU prints `GOT [a.four: a.three]`. Ronin refuses: `'a.four', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`. Its glob ran while the graph was being compiled, before any recipe had run, and `a.t*` matched nothing.\n\nTake care reproducing this by hand: running the two tools in one directory hides it, because GNU's run leaves `a.three` behind for Ronin's. The `rm` between them is the whole test.\n\nThis is the compiler boundary rather than a defect in the substitution. Ronin compiles a Makefile into a graph and then executes it, so nothing read at compile time can depend on what execution will produce; GNU interleaves the two. The same question would be asked of `$(wildcard)` in a prerequisite list, and `make-wildcard-cache-coherence` already settled the evaluation-time half of it — this is the execution-time half, which is a different and much larger claim.\n\nWhat it would take is a decision rather than a patch: either a prerequisite whose wildcard is unresolved at compile time becomes a graph edge that re-globs when its dependents are ready, or Ronin records the divergence as a bounded one the way `.DELETE_ON_ERROR`'s manifest encoding is recorded. Worth deciding rather than drifting into."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-diagnostic-quote-style
                title "Quote a name in a diagnostic the way GNU Make 4.4.1 does"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:47:52.934088477Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from the make-link-library-prerequisites audit (2026-08-14), which is where it stopped being a cosmetic observation and started holding a row.\n\nRonin quotes a name in a diagnostic as `x' — the GNU Make 3.8x style kati was written against. GNU Make 4.4.1 quotes 'x'. The Make conformance gate has owned this as a family of 21 cases for some time under the name `quote-style`, and has been content to classify it rather than fix it.\n\nWhat is new is variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff.1. The library-search work moved it out of the compiler bucket, and it landed in `unclassified` rather than `narration` because the quote style is the entire remaining difference:\n\n  .LIBPATTERNS = mtest_foo.a mtest_%.a\n  all: -lfoo ; @echo \"build $@ from $<\"\n\nwith mtest_foo.a on disk. GNU says `.LIBPATTERNS element 'mtest_foo.a' is not a pattern`, Ronin says the same sentence with the same words in the same place, quoted `mtest_foo.a'. The product name, the progress line and the build itself are all already recognised; nothing else is left.\n\nTwo ways to close it and they are not equivalent, which is why this is a node rather than a patch. Changing the style product-wide is one decision affecting every diagnostic that names anything — the 21 conformance cases would move with it, and so would every kati-inherited message this fork has been keeping stable. Teaching the upstream classifier a `quote-style` recogniser is the other, and it makes the row narration without claiming the wording is right.\n\nThe first is the honest one if the answer is that Ronin is implementing 4.4.1. It is deliberately not being taken inside a node about `-lNAME`, because a sweep across every diagnostic in the fork is not that node's sentence and would make its own audit unreadable."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:02:03.769033689Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-17, explicit: take the first of the two options this node laid out — change the style product-wide — rather than teaching the upstream classifier a `quote-style` recogniser. The node said the first is the honest one if the answer is that Ronin is implementing 4.4.1. It is.\n\nWhat moved. The sweep is mechanical and confined to string literals: inside a literal, `` `x' `` becomes `'x'`. Comments, raw strings, character literals and the shell metacharacter sets that legitimately hold a grave accent are untouched — `ninja.rs`'s quoting scanner, `strutil.rs`'s escaper and `archive.rs`'s ar header magic are all unchanged. 40 sites in kati's Rust port (which Ronin links) and 31 in the C++ ckati. Both, deliberately: leaving one behind would make the fork's two implementations disagree about their own messages.\n\nTwo sites were not mechanical and are recorded because they are behaviour, not style:\n  parser: `extraneous text after `endif` directive` quoted with two grave\n  accents where its three siblings used the 3.8x style. GNU 4.4.1 says\n  `extraneous text after 'endif' directive`, measured against the oracle.\n  func.cc: `*** invalid first argument to `wordlist' function: %s`` put its\n  closing quote after the value and left the value bare. GNU says\n  `*** invalid first argument to 'wordlist' function: '0'.` — which is what the\n  Rust port already said, so this was the C++ side catching up.\n\nRonin's own `Entering directory `x'` (src/cli.rs) is deliberately NOT swept. That is upstream Ninja's wording, not GNU Make's — `reference/ninja/src/ninja.cc:1853` writes it with the grave accent — and changing it would break the Ninja compatibility oracle to satisfy a rule about GNU Make's style.\n\nThe row audit, both gates.\n\nMake conformance: 318 identical / 69 differing → 334 / 53. Sixteen cases became byte-identical, all of them cases whose entire residue was the quote: `call_with_whitespace.mk#test`, `define_with_comments.mk#test`, `err_both_colon.mk#test`, `err_extra_else.mk#default`, `err_extra_endif.mk#default`, `err_invalid_else.mk#default`, `err_invalid_ifeq3.mk#test`, `err_missing_endef.mk#default`, `err_missing_endif.mk#default`, `err_two_else.mk#default`, `err_unmatched_endef.mk#default`, `err_word_non_numeric.mk#test`, `err_word_zero.mk#test`, `func_backslash.mk#test`, `nothing_to_do.mk#default`, `warn_output_pattern_mismatch.mk#test`. The `quote-style` family went with them, being the only cases that named it — its declaration is removed rather than left to describe nothing. Five rows re-hashed without changing class. Four rows had carried `quote-style` beside another family and each was checked individually rather than re-tagged in bulk: `err_override.mk#test` and `override.mk#test` still say `commands` where 4.4.1 says `recipe` (`recipe-wording`); `file_func.sh#script` has no quoting in it at all and its residue is the `$(filter 4.2%,$(MAKE_VERSION))` gate writing an extra `testwrite` (`corpus-version-gate`); `werror_overriding_commands.sh#script` is a kati extension whose GNU side the case writes by hand as `\"foo\"` — the corpus's fabrication, not 4.4.1's style (`kati-extension`). All four lost the tag.\n\nUpstream inventory: 1299 rows, unchanged in count. Two rows moved, both `unclassified` → `narration`, and both are the sweep:\n  variables/LIBPATTERNS.diff.1 — the row this node was filed over. Its residue\n    was the quote style and nothing else; what is left is `product-name` and\n    `ninja-progress`, which is narration, and it now says so.\n  variables/define.diff.8 — the same shape, reached from `*** missing 'endef',\n    unterminated 'define'.`\nClass totals: 163/0/37/1099 → 161/0/37/1101. The compiler class stayed at ZERO.\n\nNo fixture under tests/make/ records diagnostic text, so nothing there was re-recorded; the corpus's `expected` files hold only `oracle`, `outcome`, `file` and `status`. kati's own `run_test.go` normalises grave accent, apostrophe and quotation mark to one character before comparing, so kati's vendored suite is quote-insensitive and neither detected nor blocked this."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:05:26.545122802Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-file-function-in-recipes
                title "Perform a recipe's $(file ...) where the destination runs the build"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-lazy-recipe-expansion)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T14:12:06.573362558Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T14:13:22.95502391Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered. `$(file ...)` is served in a recipe wherever the destination runs the build; BUGS.md's entry is resolved, and its regression claim was wrong.\n\nWHAT THE REFUSAL WAS. Not Ronin's, and not new. Upstream kati's src/func.cc:934 opens FileFunc_ with `if (ev->avoid_io()) ev->Error(\"*** $(file ...) is not supported in rules.\")`, and that came across into the Rust port as `if ev.avoid_io` in file_func_impl and was never touched. Checked rather than assumed: the bare guard is present at 8c269f6, 1cd853c, 10f2e0c and a7b9da0 — every revision the entry could mean, including the pre-delivery-series one it named as working. MAKE_VERSION answers 4.4.1 at all of them, and scripts/Kbuild.include gates read-file on `ifneq ($(filter-out 4.0 4.1,$(MAKE_VERSION)),)`, so Kbuild took the `$(file <)` branch and never the `$(shell cat)` fallback. Nothing in the evaluator-scope, argument or rule-catalogue work could have caused it.\n\nThe guard states a true thing about a destination as though it were true of the function. `avoid_io` means a recipe is being compiled into text another program will execute on another day: a write would land while the manifest is written, a read would answer from a tree the build has not made. kati had one destination, so the distinction never had to be drawn.\n\nTHE SHAPE, mirroring ShellEvaluation exactly. `FileEvaluation { Refused, Expansion }` on BuildSink, defaulting to Refused; `Evaluator::refuses_file_operations` is `avoid_io && file_evaluation == Refused`, and file_func_impl asks that instead of `avoid_io`. kati's ninja.rs answers Refused and still refuses — verified, `rkati --ninja` on a rule carrying `$(file < src)` prints the same sentence. kati's exec.rs answers Expansion. Ronin's GraphSink answers Expansion. DeferredRecipes carries the answer forward, because a launch expansion happens long after the sink that chose it has been put down.\n\nWHY IT COULD NOT BE DEFERRED THE WAY $(shell) IS. A read has to hand its result back to the expansion that asked for it. The kernel's read-file is `$(subst $(newline),$(space),$(file < $1))` — the subst is around the read — so writing the operation into the recipe for its shell to perform is not available. That is why the whole function was refused rather than deferred, and why the fix had to be about who expands rather than about what the recipe carries.\n\nTHE PORT. file_read_func now follows GNU's func_file rather than approximating it. Opening and reading are separate calls because GNU reports them separately, and a directory is the case where that shows: opening succeeds, reading does not, so `$(file < adir)` fails as `read:` and not `open:`. The trailing terminator is `if (n && o[-1] == '\\n') o -= 1 + (n > 1 && o[-2] == '\\r')` — one newline and no more, so `a\\n\\n` reads as `a\\n`, with the carriage return taken too so a CRLF file reads back as its last line. Absent is empty and not an error, which is what the kernel depends on: read-file is called for include/config/kernel.release whether or not a configuration was made.\n\nEVIDENCE. Six oracle-recorded fixtures: -read-of-an-absent-file-in-a-recipe-is-empty, -read-reaches-a-recipe-through-a-recursive-variable, -read-removes-one-trailing-newline, -write-in-a-recipe-is-seen-by-a-wildcard, -write-in-one-recipe-is-read-by-the-next, -write-in-a-recipe-that-does-not-run. The second is the kernel's own chain reduced — read-file, a recursive KERNELRELEASE, a filechk recipe interpolating it — and it is byte-identical to GNU Make 4.4.1. Six kati unit tests carry what the harness cannot see: the CRLF half, which the recorder's `str::lines` drops, and the directory's `read:`.\n\nGNU's suite agrees from the other side. functions/file case 21 is `all:;$(info $(file <  out1  ))`, and it is the one row that moved in the upstream inventory: unclassified product-name+up-to-date-line+evaluation → narration ninja-progress+up-to-date-line. The evaluation difference was the refusal; what is left is Ninja's progress line.\n\nWHAT IS NOT FIXED, and is filed rather than left. Recipes the compiler cannot hold unexpanded — `$?`, depfile, recursive child, grouped — now perform the operation at construction time instead of refusing, so an up-to-date target that never runs gets a write it should not, carrying the unresolved ${KATI_NEW_INPUTS} marker. Better than the hard stop, worse than silence. make-recipe-file-operation-at-launch-only owns it, with the divergence sidecar on the fixture pointing there.\n\nSeparately open, and NOT answered here: why include/generated/utsrelease.h was expanded for a headers_install goal at all. In the kernel tree that rule hangs off archprepare, while the goal's chain is `headers_install: headers` and `headers: $(version_h) scripts_unifdef uapi-asm-generic archheaders` — `$(version_h)` being a different file with a different recipe. Either Ronin compiles recipes its goal does not reach, or the kernel gets there by a route the top-level Makefile does not show. Recorded as its own BUGS.md entry; it is also the likelier explanation for the earlier revision that finished this build than any change to $(file ...), since the refusal was present there too.\n\nGATES, on the landed tree. fmt clean. `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean. Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery --no-deps clean; `cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean. `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean. Windows type check clean. Workspace tests 788 passed / 2 failed, both the known flappers, both green in isolation after clearing the pid-namespace leftovers make-scheduler-completion-test-is-timing-fragile describes; --all-targets 840 passed. make_port 3/3 carrying 602 cases. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Make conformance 387 runs, 317 identical / 70 differing — the baseline unmoved, every family unchanged. Upstream inventory 1287 rows, 188/5/45/1049 against a 189/5/45/1048 baseline: one row, functions/file.diff.21, audited above; the three watched flappers held. Ninja conformance 425 upstream / 10 differential / 86 build-outcome / 3 invocation-boundary, plus persistence and log content — run through the conformance example directly, because the script's first phase is the full test suite and the scheduler flapper fires there under load. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own generated Makefile in 32s at -j8.\n\nFix in kati c6434fa on branch nplan/make-file-function-in-recipes."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T14:13:28.351380619Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-file-operation-at-launch-only
                title "Perform a recipe's $(file ...) only when the recipe runs"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-file-function-in-recipes)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T14:12:46.349656672Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-file-function-in-recipes, which made this observable rather than created it. The fixture feature-file-write-in-a-recipe-that-does-not-run carries a `divergence` sidecar naming this node.\n\nA recipe's `$(file ...)` is now performed where GNU Make performs it — while the recipe is expanded — and make-lazy-recipe-expansion moved that expansion to launch, so for an ordinary recipe the two agree. The agreement holds only for recipes Ronin can hold unexpanded. Four kinds it cannot: one naming `$?`, one feeding a depfile, a recursive child's, and a grouped target's. Those are read while the graph is compiled, because the compiler needs their text before the graph is complete, and the file operation goes with them.\n\nWhat it costs, and the fixture is the shape:\n\n    all: target\n    target: dep\n    \t$(file > written,$?)\n    \t@printf 'ran\\n' >> log\n\nwith `target` newer than `dep`. GNU expands nothing, so there is no `written` and no `log`. Ronin writes `written` while it builds the graph — for a target that is up to date and never runs — and what it writes is the unresolved `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` marker rather than a value, because `$?` is not known until the scheduler has settled.\n\nThat is two departures in one: an operation performed for a recipe that does not run, and the wrong content when it is. It is strictly better than what it replaces, which was refusing the whole function and stopping the build, but it is a refusal traded for a silent early write, and a silent wrong write is the worse failure mode of the two if the file is consumed.\n\nA recursive child's recipes carry the same trade `$(shell)` already carries there, for the same reason and with the same fix: make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch has to lift the child unit's session and directory before a child's `$(file ...)` can wait for launch either.\n\nWhat a fix has to settle for the other three: `$?` is already carried through expansion as a marker that the scheduler resolves — NewInputsTiming::Launch — so the question is whether a file operation can be held the same way. It cannot be written into the recipe text the way `$(shell)` can, because a read composes with the Make functions around it; so holding it means deferring the whole expansion, which is what the compiler could not do for these four kinds in the first place. Either those four gain a way to be compiled without their text, or the operation needs a deferral of its own that the scheduler performs before the recipe's first line."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:11:07.587720591Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Narrowed from four kinds to three by make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch, which lifted the child unit's session and directory as this node's own text said it would have to.\n\nA recursive child's recipes are no longer read while the graph is compiled. GraphSink::recipe_expansion answers Launch for every unit, each unit's evaluator is retained beside the root's in PendingRecipes, and the child's directory is entered again for the length of the expansion — so a file operation in a child's recipe is now performed where GNU Make performs it, and not at all for a target that turns out to be up to date.\n\nProbed and recorded, tests/make/recursive-child-file-write-in-a-recipe-that-does-not-run: a root that runs a child with -C sub, and in the child a target newer than its prerequisite whose recipe writes a file during expansion. Before the change Ronin wrote sub/written while it compiled the graph, for a target that never ran; it now writes nothing, which is what GNU Make 4.4.1 does. The case failed the build-intent gate before and passes after.\n\nThe three that remain are the three the compiler still needs the text of before the graph is complete: a recipe naming the new-prerequisites variable, one feeding a depfile, and a grouped double-colon action. The fixture carrying this node's divergence marker, feature-file-write-in-a-recipe-that-does-not-run, is the first of those and still diverges, so the marker stays. What a fix has to settle for those three is unchanged and is stated above."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makefile-set-jobserver-style
                title "Check a jobserver style a Makefile's MAKEFLAGS names"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:14:46.47499275Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by `make-jobserver-style-is-checked`, which landed the check for every stream the CLI reads and left this one.\n\nReproducer:\n\n  # Makefile\n  MAKEFLAGS += --jobserver-style=foo\n  all: ; @echo ran\n\n  make -j8\n\nGNU refuses: `unknown jobserver auth style 'foo'.  Stop.` Ronin builds.\n\nThe check itself is settled — `src/make/cli.rs` refuses an unknown style once both option streams have been read, and it fires for the command line and for `MAKEFLAGS` inherited from the environment. What it cannot see is a style the Makefile itself appends, because the CLI has finished by the time the Makefile is parsed. GNU reaches `jobserver_setup` after the read and so sees all three.\n\nThis is the same shape as `make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules` — `-r` from a Makefile's MAKEFLAGS arriving after the rules are installed — and the two are one question about when a Makefile-set MAKEFLAGS is allowed to change an invocation. Worth landing together rather than threading a second late re-read.\n\nNote GNU only looks at the style when it would build a jobserver: `job_slots > 1`. `-j1`, a bare `-j` and no `-j` at all never check it, whatever the value."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:29:05.916750528Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:29:38.30540007Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. The filing had the cell right and understated the shape: the check is not merely late, it is deferred past the whole read, and the value has to survive every MAKEFLAGS assignment in between without being published by any of them.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. GNU checks the style inside `jobserver_setup`, which `main` reaches after `read_all_makefiles` and after the second `decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_env)` that lets a makefile's own writes have their say (main.c:2080-2091). Two consequences, both load-bearing:\n\n  the job count that gates the check is the one the READ finally settles on, not the one argv gave\n  the style and the job count may arrive from different streams and in either order\n\nand one more that decides the design: `define_makeflags` never writes `--jobserver-style` into MAKEFLAGS. A child is told the jobserver's address (`--jobserver-auth=`), not the style it was built in. So the value lives in a variable of its own for the whole run, and cannot be carried from one assignment to the next in the published string.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Sixteen cells probed, GNU and Ronin now agreeing on all of them. The style is `foo` unless named otherwise:\n\n  makefile style, argv -j8            refused: `unknown jobserver auth style 'foo'.  Stop.`, rc 2\n  makefile style, argv -j1            builds — slots not above one, never looked at\n  makefile style, no -j               builds\n  makefile style, argv bare -j        builds — infinite is not a fixed count\n  makefile style=pipe, argv -j4       builds\n  makefile style=fifo, argv -j4       builds\n  makefile `-j4 --jobserver-style=foo` on one line     refused\n  makefile `-j4 --jobserver-style=pipe`                builds\n  makefile `-j1 --jobserver-style=foo`                 builds\n  makefile style, then `-j4` on a LATER line           refused\n  makefile `-j4`, then style on a later line           refused\n  makefile style, then `-j1` on a later line           builds\n  makefile `--jobserver-style=pipe --jobserver-style=foo`, -j8    refused — last one wins\n  makefile `--jobserver-style=foo --jobserver-style=pipe`, -j8    builds — last one wins\n  argv style=foo, makefile `-j4`      refused — the count arrives after the style\n  argv style=pipe, makefile `-j4`     builds\n\nThe two split-across-lines cells and the last pair are the ones that rule out checking at the assignment that names the style: at that moment the job count is still one, and at the moment the count arrives the style is no longer in the text being decoded.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. The switch table and the published string became two things, which is what GNU has and Ronin did not.\n\nkati's `DecodedMakeflags` gained `carried` beside `makeflags`: what the next assignment is decoded over, as against what the variable shows. `normalize_makeflags_assignment` stores `carried` into `MakeflagsAssignment::effective`, and `install_compiler_invocation_variables` seeds that field from `protected` rather than from the published `MAKEFLAGS`, so the distinction holds from before the first makefile line. kati does not know which switches belong to which — the frontend supplies both strings, as it already supplied the grammar.\n\nIn Ronin, `carried_switches` is that second spelling: the canonical base plus `--jobserver-style=` when one is in force. `session_for` seeds both `protected` and `effective` with it, which is what lets a style named on argv be judged against a `-j` a makefile adds later. `decode_makefile_makeflags` asks the check once per assignment, over an invocation built from previous + assigned + protected, so every later assignment re-asks it with the style still in force.\n\nThe check itself is now one predicate, `unknown_jobserver_style`, used by both the argv path in `parse` and the makefile path in `decode_makefile_makeflags` — the gating rule (a fixed count above one) was already right and is no longer written twice.\n\nVerified that the carried style does NOT leak into what a makefile reads: `$(findstring jobserver-style,$(MAKEFLAGS))` is empty with `--jobserver-style=pipe -j4` in force, and MAKEFLAGS is ` -j4` exactly as before. That is its own fixture, because it is the property the whole design rests on.\n\nFour fixtures: jobserver-style-a-makefile-names (refused at -j8), jobserver-style-a-makefile-names-without-a-jobserver (-j1 builds, the gating cell), jobserver-style-a-makefile-names-before-its-job-count (style then -j4 on separate lines — the deferral cell), jobserver-style-a-makefile-names-is-not-published (the style stays out of MAKEFLAGS). The thirteen existing jobserver tests are green.\n\nNOT DONE, and filed rather than folded in: `MAKEFLAGS += --jobserver-style=` with an empty value. GNU prints `the '--jobserver-style' option requires a non-empty string argument` and BUILDS ANYWAY, rc 0 — the message is unconditional but the death is not, since it is the `bad` flag and the origin is `o_env`. Ronin refuses. That is the same machinery as `MAKEFLAGS += -W` and belongs with it, in make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags, which carries both reproducers.\n\nThe filing suggested landing this with make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules as one question about when a Makefile-set MAKEFLAGS may change an invocation. It did not need to be: the re-decode hook from make-makefile-set-makeflags was already the right seam, and what was missing was only that the table forgot a switch it was never supposed to publish. That node is untouched and still stands on its own."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:29:42.877513083Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-process-cwd-tests-race-each-other
                title "Stop two tests racing over the process working directory"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:49:45.338885756Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while measuring make-scheduler-completion-test-is-timing-fragile's baseline: one failure in six `cargo test --lib` runs at e788030, unrelated to the scheduler test that was being measured beside it.\n\n  ---- cli::tests::runner_resolves_the_last_change_without_mutating_process_cwd stdout ----\n  panicked at src/cli.rs:1983:9: assertion `left == right` failed\n    left: \"/tmp/.tmpAgBgEC\"\n   right: \"/home/brendan/work/samurai\"\n\nThe name on the left is a `tempfile::tempdir()`, and the one place in this workspace that enters one is `src/make/equivalence.rs:1243` — `std::env::set_current_dir(directory.path())` around each testcase's `compare`, restored after. The working directory is a process-global and the test binary is multithreaded, so the cli test's `assert_eq!(std::env::current_dir().unwrap(), original_directory)` is asserting a global that another test on another thread is legitimately moving. Either can be scheduled inside the other's window.\n\nIt is not only a test-side problem: `src/make.rs`'s `in_directory` moves the process working directory during Make compilation and restores it, so anything that compiles a Makefile from a directory other than the invocation's is briefly in the same position. In a single-threaded process that is fine and is what GNU Make does; in a test binary it is shared mutable state with no lock on it.\n\nWhat a fix has to settle: whether the cwd-sensitive tests take a shared lock (a `static Mutex` both sides acquire, which orders them but does not make the product's use of the global safe), or whether the tests that need a directory stop moving the process into it — `Runner::new(&base)` already takes a base path, and the equivalence harness's `compare` already receives `directory.path()` beside the argv, so the `set_current_dir` there may be removable outright. Measure the rate before and after: 1 in 6 is high enough to poison a gate run and low enough to look like something else."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:46:15.734575636Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:00:29.575307638Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed on the reading side, with the lock the product already takes to read the same global — and the filing's named cause turned out not to be the one that can fire.\n\nWHAT THE FILING SAID, AND WHAT IS ACTUALLY THERE. The note pointed at `src/make/equivalence.rs`'s `set_current_dir` around each corpus testcase. That code is inside `the_direct_graph_matches_the_manifest_over_the_corpus`, which has carried `#[ignore = \"changes the process working directory; the release gate runs it alone\"]` since 4143717 on 2026-08-09 — five days before the failure was seen. It cannot have been the mover in an ordinary `cargo test --lib` run, and it is not the mover now.\n\nWHAT CAN MOVE IT, enumerated rather than assumed — every `set_current_dir` in the workspace, kati included:\n  src/make.rs `in_directory`        a Make compilation entering a unit's own directory, under the exclusive guard\n  src/make/recipe.rs `expanded_in`  a deferred recipe expanding in its unit's directory, under the exclusive guard\n  src/make/cli.rs `enter_directories`  Make mode's own `-C`, which moves the process and does not come back\n  src/make/equivalence.rs           the ignored corpus test\nkati moves nothing; its `chdir` hits are the find emulator parsing `find`'s argument. So the two live movers both hold `COMPILATION_DIRECTORY` exclusively across the move and restore what they found — the lock `make-process-directory-isolation` built precisely so a reader can ask them to hold still.\n\nThe defect is therefore the reader. `cli::tests::runner_resolves_the_last_change_without_mutating_process_cwd` asserted `std::env::current_dir()` three times with no guard at all, which is reading a value another thread is entitled to be halfway through changing. `Runner::from_process` — twenty lines up the same file — already reads that global under `stable_process_directory_guard`, so the product had the discipline and the test did not.\n\nMEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER, as the node asked.\n  As it stands today: 0 failures in 20 `cargo test --lib` runs. Instrumenting both guarded movers to print when they actually chdir shows neither fires anywhere in a full lib run — the race is latent in this binary rather than live, because the only test that entered a scratch directory is the ignored one. So the rate is not 1 in 6 any more, and a fix proved by repetition alone would be proving nothing.\n  So it was proved against an adversary instead: a temporary test that takes the same exclusive guard and holds the process in a `tempfile::tempdir()` for 500µs at a time for twenty seconds, which is what a Make compilation does, only oftener. Against the unguarded read: 6 failures in 6, each one `left: \"/tmp/.tmpXXXXXX\"` — the recorded symptom exactly, down to the shape of the name. With the guarded read: 0 in 10.\n  The whole lib suite was then run once with that adversary still in place and the fix on: 505 passed, 0 failed. Nothing else in the binary depends on the process directory unguarded, and the exclusive guard does not deadlock against the shared side `subprocess::spawn` takes while a build under test launches commands. The adversary and the test-only accessor it needed were removed after measuring.\n\nTHE FIX. A `process_directory()` helper in the cli test module that takes `stable_process_directory_guard` for the read and nothing else, cfg-gated `all(unix, feature = \"make\")` exactly as `from_process` is, with the three assertions routed through it. Test-side only: no product file changed, so no repin is owed on this node's account.\n\nDeliberately for the read alone and not across `run`. Holding it across the run would make the test pass by preventing the thing it is meant to observe, and `run` launches commands whose spawn takes the shared side on this same thread — a second read acquisition with a writer possibly queued between, which is how a `std::sync::RwLock` deadlocks.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT AND NOT THIS NODE'S. `enter_directories` moves the process directory for Make mode's `-C` and never restores it, holding no guard. That is correct for a real invocation — GNU Make's `-C` is exactly that — and no test calls it, so nothing races on it today. A future test that ran Make mode end to end in-process would be a different question, and it is the product's to answer rather than the test's.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean (the harness findings under `--all-targets` are the fifteen already filed on make-release-clippy-runnable, unmoved). Workspace suite 3 of 3 at 858 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:07:30.115372847Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Shape of the fix settled by the density gate, and re-measured in its final form.\n\nThe guarded read began as a helper function in the cli test module and the commit gate refused it: src/cli.rs stands at exactly 50 counted functions, and `exempt-test-scope` spares a `#[test]` but not a plain helper beside one, so the helper was the fifty-first. It is now a closure inside the one test that makes the claim, which is where the discipline belongs anyway — the explanation sits with the assertion it explains rather than one scroll away from it.\n\nRe-measured in that form against the same adversary, because the code changed shape after the first measurement: 6 failures in 6 with the guard removed, 0 in 6 with it. Same numbers as the helper form."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:08:36.525743409Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-assignment-operator-scan-skips-a-reference
                title "Skip a variable reference while looking for the assignment operator"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:01:24.100439463Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-trailing-dollar-in-a-value, which fixed the name a `$`-ending assignment binds but left the operator that assignment is read as.\n\nGNU Make looks for the assignment operator by walking the line and, at a `$`, consuming whatever the reference reads before it looks again: one character for `$X`, or up to the matching close for `$(...)` and `${...}` (reference/gnumake/src/variable.c parse_variable_definition, the `if (c == '$')` arm and its `default: continue`). So the operator can never be found inside a reference. kati uses find_outside_paren over `:=;`, which skips `$(...)` because of the parentheses but not a one-character reference, so it stops on a character GNU had already spent.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    T$:= a\n    P = base\n    P$+= more\n    all: ; @printf '[%s]\\n' 'flavT=$(flavor T)' 'T=$(T)' 'flavP=$(flavor P)' 'P=$(P)'\n\n    GNU 4.4.1   flavT=recursive  T=a  flavP=recursive  P=more\n    Ronin       flavT=simple     T=a  flavP=recursive  P=base more\n\nBoth cells are the same mistake. In `T$:= a` GNU spends the `:` as the name `$` reads and finds the `=` after it, so the flavor is recursive and the name is `T$:`, which expands to `T`. kati stops at the `:`, reads `:=`, and makes the variable simple. In `P$+= more` GNU spends the `+` the same way and finds `=`, so the line is a plain recursive assignment that replaces `P`; kati reads `+=` and appends, which is why it keeps `base`.\n\nThe name is already right in both, because make-trailing-dollar-in-a-value made parse_assign_statement keep the character the split removed when the name ends in an odd run of dollars. What is left is the operator that same character was mistaken for. Fixing it means giving the operator scan the reference skip GNU's has — either in find_outside_paren under a flag, or in a scan of its own for this caller, since find_outside_paren also serves rule parsing where `$` has no such role.\n\nNo corpus case owns this; tests/make/variable-name-ending-in-a-dollar covers the cells that already agree."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:25:28.958264136Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:26:01.110817679Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. The filing named two cells and the matrix turned out to have six, all of them the same one line of GNU's scan.\n\nThe rule, read rather than inferred. reference/gnumake/src/variable.c parse_variable_definition walks the line looking for the operator, and its `if (c == '$')` arm reads what the reference reads before looking again: `$(` or `${` is followed to its matching close, and ANYTHING else is one character, consumed by `default: continue`. So a character a `$` spends can never be the operator, and the operator is whatever is written after it — the shorter one, or none at all.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left of the slash and Ronin as it was on the right. Six cells diverged and every one now agrees:\n\n  A$:= a        recursive, A=a        / simple           the `:` is the name `$:` reads, leaving `=`\n  B$+= b        recursive, B=b        / append, B=base b the `+` is spent, so this REPLACES\n  C$?= c        recursive, C=c        / declined, C=base the `?` is spent, so it is unconditional\n  D$!= echo x   recursive, D=`echo x` / shell ran, D=x   the `!` is spent, so the text is literal\n  F$:::= f      simple                / recursive        one colon spent, `::=` written\n  T$= a         NOT a definition      / defined T$       the `=` is spent, so there is no operator at all\n\nand the cells that already agreed, kept in the fixture because they are what the fix must not break:\n\n  E$::= e       simple                one colon spent, `:=` written — right before and after, by luck\n  G$$:= g       simple, names G$      an even run of dollars reads nothing after it\n  H$(X)+= h     append                a parenthesised reference closes itself\n  I$(X):= i     simple\n  J${X}?= j     conditional, declined\n  K$X:= k       simple                the `X` is spent, the `:` is not\n  L$:M = l      recursive, names LM    the scan walks past the spent `:` and finds the `=` after `M`\n\n`T$= a` and `L$:M = l` are the two the filing did not have, and they are the ones that show the defect was in the DISPATCH and not only in the split: kati decided rule-versus-assignment with `find_outside_paren(line, b\":=;\")`, which stopped on a character GNU had already spent. `T$= a` is `missing separator` in GNU and built in Ronin; `T$:X = y` defines `TX` in GNU and was `missing separator` in Ronin. Both directions, from one scan.\n\n`$:::=` deserves its own line because the flavours are not what they look like. GNU's `:::=` is `f_expand`, which `$(flavor)` calls `recursive`; `::=` and `:=` are both `f_simple`. So `F$:::= f` reads as `::=` and is SIMPLE in GNU, while a naive scan reads `:::=` and answers recursive — the two disagree while looking like they agree.\n\nVerified as far as the modifiers, since GNU uses the same scan for all of them: `override O$+= x` replaces, `export E2$:= y` is recursive, and a target-specific `all: TS$+= z` behaves the same way. `$(origin)`/`$(flavor)` were read for every cell rather than the value alone, because the value agrees in several cells where the flavour does not.\n\nWhat changed, both in kati.\n\nstrutil.rs gained `find_outside_reference`, which is `find_outside_paren` with GNU's reference reading: at a `$` whose next byte is not `(` or `{`, the scan spends that byte. The two now share one `find_outside` and differ by a `References` enum rather than by a copy, and `find_outside_paren` is unchanged for its other caller — the `#` comment scan, where a `$` has no such role.\n\nparser.rs uses it for the three places that ask where an assignment operator is — `parse_rule_or_assign`, `is_variable_definition` and `parse_define`, which is GNU's `do_define` calling the same function — and `parse_assign_statement` now takes the operator off the name with `written_operator`, which refuses a suffix whose first byte is read by a `$` in front of it. That one predicate is what makes `T$:=` recursive and `F$:::=` simple: it declines `:::`, then declines `:` for the first cell and accepts `::` for the second.\n\nThe name side was already right, from make-trailing-dollar-in-a-value, and is unchanged: `U$ := b` still names `U$ ` and `A$$ := b` still names `A$$`.\n\nFixtures recorded from the oracle: assignment-operator-after-a-reference (thirteen cells, the whole matrix above), an-operator-a-reference-reads-is-not-an-operator (`T$= a`, which GNU refuses), an-assignment-operator-a-reference-reads-under-a-modifier (`override`, `export`, and a three-dollar run). Unit tests parser::tests::an_operator_a_dollar_reads_is_not_the_operator and strutil::tests::find_outside_reference_spends_what_a_dollar_reads hold the same matrix at the two functions.\n\nOwned markers: none. No corpus case owned this, as the filing said.\n\nOne adjacent defect found and filed rather than folded in: make-semicolon-does-not-stop-an-assignment-scan. `a;b=c` defines a variable named `a;b` in GNU and is `missing separator` in Ronin, because kati's dispatch treats a `;` as the rule-and-recipe separator wherever it falls while GNU's scan has no case for `;` at all. That is a second, independent disagreement in the same scan and it wants the two questions separated rather than the character set tuned."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:26:06.343539014Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags
                title "Ignore a switch a makefile's MAKEFLAGS names and Make does not know"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:12:06.633820086Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing the `-e` origin cells for make-environment-origin-precedes-the-switches, and not part of it: this is about what a makefile may write into MAKEFLAGS, not about which origin a variable has.\n\nA switch GNU Make does not recognise, arriving from a makefile's own MAKEFLAGS rather than from the command line, is ignored. The build goes on.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -zz\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, no diagnostic, `out` holds `built`\n    Ronin      rc 2, \"ronin: invalid option -- 'z'\" and the whole usage text,\n               nothing built\n\nThe same text on the command line is a usage error in both tools, which is the distinction: GNU decodes a makefile's MAKEFLAGS with the switch table it uses for the environment, where an unknown letter is something a newer or older Make wrote and is skipped rather than fatal. Ronin re-enters its command-line option parser and gives the makefile the diagnostic written for a person who mistyped an invocation.\n\nThe cell where the flag IS known already agrees — `MAKEFLAGS += -r` withdraws the built-in rules in both, and under `-e` both decline the write entirely, which tests/make/makeflags-outranks-a-makefile-under-e now records.\n\nWorth probing before fixing: an unknown long option (`--frobnicate`), an unknown letter clustered with known ones (`-rz`), whether an unknown switch that takes an argument swallows the next word, and the same text arriving through the MAKEFLAGS environment variable rather than a makefile assignment — the environment path is the one GNU shares its lenient decoder with, so it may already differ too."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:26:12.263500829Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:26:48.424922343Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. The filing was right that a makefile's unknown switch is ignored, and the probe it asked for turned up the distinction that decides the whole fix: it is the ORIGIN that forgives, and GNU decodes the environment's MAKEFLAGS at the same origin as argv.\n\nThe mechanism, in GNU's own words. main.c `decode_switches` ends\n\n    if (bad && origin == o_command)\n      print_usage (bad);\n\nand sets `opterr = origin == o_command` at the top, so a non-command origin neither prints nor dies. Which origin each stream gets is not symmetric, and this is the part the filing did not have:\n\n  main.c:1603  decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_command)   the ENVIRONMENT's MAKEFLAGS, at startup\n  main.c:2091  decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_env)       the same variable again, after read_all_makefiles\n\nSo an unknown switch in `MAKEFLAGS` inherited from the environment is FATAL, exactly as it is on the command line, and only a makefile's own write to the variable is forgiven. Both of those already agreed with Ronin and had to be kept that way; only the third cell moved.\n\nThe matrix, all probed. Command line and environment first, where nothing changed:\n\n  argv -zz                    fatal, rc 2, `make: invalid option -- 'z'` + usage    Ronin agreed and still does\n  argv --frobnicate           fatal, rc 2, `unrecognized option` + usage            agreed\n  argv -rz                    fatal                                                 agreed\n  MAKEFLAGS=-zz in the env    fatal, rc 2 — and the diagnostic leads with `: ` rather than a program name, because decode_env_switches builds an argv whose [0] is the empty string   agreed\n  MAKEFLAGS=--frobnicate      fatal                                                 agreed\n  MAKEFLAGS=-rz in the env    fatal                                                 agreed\n\nand a makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS +=`, where every cell moved:\n\n  -zz            rc 0, silent, MAKEFLAGS empty       Ronin: rc 2 + full usage\n  -z             rc 0, silent                        Ronin: rc 2\n  --frobnicate   rc 0, silent                        Ronin: rc 2\n  --=x           rc 0, silent                        Ronin: rc 2\n  --frob=1       rc 0, silent                        Ronin: rc 2\n  -rz            rc 0, MAKEFLAGS is `r`              Ronin: rc 2\n  -zr            rc 0, MAKEFLAGS is `r`              Ronin: rc 2\n  --frob 1       rc 0, silent, and `1` is DROPPED    Ronin: rc 2\n  -O bogus       rc 0, MAKEFLAGS ` -Otarget`, word dropped   Ronin: rc 2\n  -j abc         rc 0, MAKEFLAGS ` -j`, word dropped         Ronin: rc 2\n  -l abc         rc 0, MAKEFLAGS empty, word dropped         Ronin: rc 2\n  GNUMAKEFLAGS += -zz  rc 0, silent                  already agreed\n\nThree findings the filing asked for, answered:\n\n  Does the unknown switch stay in MAKEFLAGS for children? No. `-rz` publishes `r` alone. MAKEFLAGS is rebuilt from the switch table by define_makeflags, and a switch that never entered the table cannot leave it.\n\n  Is the forgiveness silent or warned? Silent. `opterr` is off for this origin, so getopt says nothing and GNU adds nothing.\n\n  Does an unknown switch that takes an argument swallow the next word? It cannot know it takes one, so the word is read as a non-switch argument — and `handle_non_switch_argument` enters a GOAL only `if (arg[0] != '\\0' && origin == o_command)`. A variable definition is still honoured at any origin; a bare word from a makefile is dropped on the floor. That is why `-j abc`, `-l abc`, `-O bogus` and `--frob 1` all build.\n\nWhat changed, all in Ronin's CLI — this is the layer where the behaviour lives, since kati already calls the frontend back after every effective global MAKEFLAGS assignment.\n\n`ArgumentSource` gained a third variant. It had `Inherited` and `CommandLine`, which is one distinction too few: GNU has three streams and two origins, and the two that share an origin are argv and the inherited environment, not the two that arrive through MAKEFLAGS. `Makefile` is the new one, `refuses_an_unknown_switch()` is false for it alone, and `decode_makefile_makeflags` is the only caller that passes it. The `parse` path that reads argv and the environment still passes `Inherited`/`CommandLine` and still refuses.\n\nThe two refusal sites consult it: the long-option fallback and `read_cluster`'s unknown-letter arm. Because the guard is on the letter rather than the word, `-rz` applies `-r` and passes over `z`, which is what GNU's getopt does with the same cluster.\n\n`decode_makefile_makeflags` clears leftover goals instead of refusing over them. It used to answer `MAKEFLAGS contains non-option word 'x'`, which is a rule GNU does not have — that word is a goal, and a goal from this origin is dropped. One line, and it is what fixes the four argument-shaped cells above.\n\nNote on shape: adding the guard pushed `parse_arguments` to 101 lines, one over clippy's limit, so the long-option fallback moved into `read_other_long` beside the `read_cluster` it mirrors. Fixed structurally, no bypass.\n\nThree fixtures: unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags (short, long, malformed long and a cluster, with the surviving `r` in MAKEFLAGS), a-word-an-unknown-switch-leaves-in-makeflags (the dropped word, with MAKECMDGOALS proving it never became a goal), an-unknown-switch-on-the-command-line (`-zz` in args, recorded as a failure — the cell that must not move). The environment cell cannot be a corpus case because the harness removes MAKEFLAGS from a case's environment by design; it was verified against the oracle by hand and both tools refuse.\n\nTwo adjacent defects found and filed rather than folded in, because they are a different question — a switch Make KNOWS whose argument it cannot read:\n\n  make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags — `MAKEFLAGS += -W` and `MAKEFLAGS += --jobserver-style=` both build in GNU and are refused by Ronin. Same `bad` flag, but it needs the other failure channel (`Err(Error)` from `value()`) made origin-aware, and the two halves differ in whether anything is printed.\n  make-shuffle-mode-is-validated-where-gnu-does-not — `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=bogus` builds in GNU and is republished verbatim, because GNU never validates the mode in this path at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:26:53.166687691Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-repaint-budget-test-reads-the-wall-clock
                title "Stop the repaint-budget test asserting on how fast the host ran it"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:46:37.875612961Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Seen during the four-node evaluator dispatch and demonstrably not caused by it: outside plan/, tests/make/ and the kati submodule, that dispatch changed exactly one Ronin file, tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv. src/build/reporter.rs was not touched.\n\n`build::reporter::tests::repainting_inside_the_budget_is_refused` (src/build/reporter.rs:851) paints once, then asserts the very next paint is skipped:\n\n    assert!(!painted(&mut reporter, 1, 4).is_empty());\n    assert_eq!(painted(&mut reporter, 2, 4), \"\", \"a repaint this soon is skipped\");\n\nThe claim it means to make is that two paints in quick succession cost one repaint. What it actually asserts is that the host got from the first statement to the second in less than REPAINT_INTERVAL. Under the full workspace suite on a loaded host it does not, the budget is judged to have refilled, and the assertion fails with the painted bar on the left and \"\" on the right.\n\nMeasured: it failed once inside `scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh` (whose lib phase runs the whole suite in parallel), passed 5 times out of 5 when run alone, and passed in a full `cargo test --workspace` run in the same session. The second half of the same test already knows the right technique — it reaches in and backdates `painted_at` to refill the budget deliberately:\n\n    bar_state(&mut reporter).painted_at = Instant::now().checked_sub(REPAINT_INTERVAL + ...);\n\nThe first half should be made of the same stuff rather than of hope: set `painted_at` to a known recent instant before the second paint, so the interval under test is the one the test chose and not the one the scheduler handed it. That leaves all three assertions meaningful — refused inside the budget, allowed once it refills — on any host at any load.\n\nSame family as the scheduler-timing and stdin-refusal test defects already closed: a test that reads the wall clock is asserting about the host."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:04:21.438478904Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:10:34.107719016Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed by giving the budget the instant to judge, rather than by widening a margin.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. `Reporter::paint` read `Instant::now()` itself, so the only way to ask it about the budget was to paint twice and hope the host got between the two statements in under REPAINT_INTERVAL — which is 33ms. That is a short time for a loaded machine to spend between two lines of test code, which is why the assertion failed inside the full-suite run and passed 5 of 5 alone.\n\nTHE SEAM. `paint` now delegates to a private `paint_as_of(out, progress, now)` and passes the clock; `paint_as_of` holds what `paint` held. The product has one caller and reads the clock in one place, exactly as before. The seam is the same shape the code already had one step down — `Bar::may_paint(&self, now)` has always taken the instant it judges against — so this only carries that argument out one level to where a test can reach it.\n\nWHAT THE TEST NOW PROVES, and it is more than before. Three paints at instants the test names: the first paint (a bar that has never been painted has no budget to spend), one at `first + REPAINT_INTERVAL - 1ms` (skipped, emitting nothing), and one at exactly `first + REPAINT_INTERVAL` (painted, because the test is `>=`). Both sides of the boundary are now asserted where the old test only asserted one point well inside it and one well past it. A fourth assertion reads `painted_at` back and requires it to be the instant the successful paint was judged against — so a skipped repaint cannot quietly restamp the budget.\n\nTEETH, measured rather than argued. With `may_paint`'s interval replaced by `Duration::ZERO` — a bar that has forgotten its budget entirely — the test fails on `a repaint this soon is skipped` at reporter.rs:887. Restored after measuring.\n\nNo wall clock remains in the test: `Instant::now()` is read once, as the origin the other two instants are named against, and no assertion depends on how long anything takes. It cannot flake on any host at any load.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; workspace suite 858 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:31:31.998553908Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-target-specific-append-reads-the-readers-scope
                title "Resolve a target-specific append against the scope that reads it"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T11:59:27.317152705Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while shipping make-private-binding-keeps-the-public-value, and deliberately not folded into it: the private-chain walk is a question about which binding is visible, and this is a question about when a `+=` is resolved. Both were wrong before that node and only this half is still wrong.\n\nReproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    V = outer\n    %.p: private V = priv\n    q.p: V += more\n    all: q.p\n    q.p: dep9 ; @echo \"q.p:[$(V)]\"\n    dep9: ; @echo \"dep9:[$(V)]\"\n\nGNU: dep9:[outer more], q.p:[priv more]. Ronin: dep9:[priv more], q.p:[priv more]. Before the private-chain fix Ronin gave dep9:[outer] — wrong in the other direction, the append lost rather than its base.\n\nThe value GNU produces depends on who is reading it, which is the whole point. `variable_append` (src/expand.c:511) is a *second* walk of the set chain, made when the variable is expanded rather than when it is defined: it recurses outward for the base value, appends this set's contribution to it, and applies the same private rule as `lookup_variable` on the way — `if (!v || (!local && v->private_var)) return variable_append (name, length, set->next, nextlocal)`. So q.p's own recipe, walking with `local = 1`, reaches the private pattern binding and gets `priv more`; dep9, walking across a `next_is_parent` link, steps over it and gets `outer more`. `allocated_variable_append` is reached from `recursively_expand_for_file` whenever `v->append` is set, so every reader does its own walk.\n\nRonin resolves the append once, when the binding is installed: `DepBuilder::apply_rule_vars` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) looks the old value up with `self.ev.lookup_var`, concatenates, and stores a settled simple variable. One string, so every reader gets the same answer, and which answer it is depends on what happened to be in scope at install time.\n\nA fix has to keep the append unresolved on the binding — GNU's `v->append` — and resolve it per read. The scope machinery to walk already exists after the private-chain work: `release_private` distinguishes the pattern set from the target's own set, which is exactly the boundary `variable_append` needs. What is missing is a variable flavour that means \"tail, not value\", and a reader that knows to walk.\n\nNot urgent: it needs a `private` binding and a `+=` in two different scopes for the same name to be observable at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:39:58.039557601Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:40:45.819734876Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Reproducer re-verified at dec8ddc before implementing: dep9:[priv more] where GNU gives dep9:[outer more].\n\nTHE MATRIX, seventeen cells probed against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before any code moved. Global `V = g`, target `a.o`, prerequisite `dep` (named so it matches no pattern), one column for a.o's own recipe and one for dep's:\n\n  pattern         target            a.o        dep\n  `V += p`        `private V += t`  g p t      g p       ✓ already\n  `private V += p` `V += t`         g p t      g t       ✗\n  `V += p`        `V += t`          g p t      g p t     ✓ control\n  `V += p`        `V = t`           t          t         ✓ control\n  `private V = p` `V += t`          p t        g t       ✗ the node's own case\n  `private V += p` (no global)      p t        t         ✗\n  `private V += p` `private V += t` g p t      g         ✓ already\n  `private V := p` `V += t`         p t        g t       ✗\n  `private V += p` `V += t1; += t2` g p t1 t2  g t1 t2   ✗\n  `private V += p` `V = own; += t`  own t      own t     ✓ the own set settles it\n  two patterns, private then public, then `V += t`  g p1 p2 t   g p1 p2 t   ✓\n  two patterns, public then private, then `V += t`  g p1 p2 t   g t         ✗\n  `private V += p`, prerequisite has `dep: V += d`  g p t       g t d       ✗\n  `private V += p`, grandchild prerequisite         g p t       g t (both)  ✗\n\nThree things the matrix says that a flat model cannot. The base of a `+=` is walked per reader — `variable_append` (src/expand.c:511) recurses outward under the reader's own `local`, so the private binding the target's own recipe appended onto is stepped over by anything reading across the parent link. A set is one hash slot per name, so which of two patterns carries `private` does not matter: only what the set is LEFT holding does, which is why public-then-private hides the whole accumulated set and private-then-public shows all of it. And a non-append binding in a set stops the outward walk entirely, so `a.o: V = own` beside `a.o: V += t` settles to one string both readers share — `do_variable_definition` clears `append` when the set already holds a non-appending binding.\n\nDELIVERED, kati/src-rs/dep.rs only. `RuleBinding` carries a second value: `guard` installs what the target's own recipe reads, and `public` is what a reader across the parent link reads. `apply_rule_vars` computes both, tracking the outward view in three parts — `pending` for the set being installed (a set pastes onto its own slot whether or not the slot is private), `public_now` for the sets already committed, and `outer` for what stood before the run began, taken as each name is first met because the scope is installed into as the walk goes. `commit_public` folds a whole set at a time and drops it entirely when the set is left holding `private`, which is the \"last writer decides\" cell. `release_private` lays down `public` rather than the value the target itself saw. The appended text is taken back out of the join by byte offset rather than expanded a second time, so a `$(shell)` in a `+=` still runs once.\n\nEvery cell above now matches.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded: a-target-specific-append-reads-its-base-where-it-is-read (four names in one case: private simple base, private append base, no base at all, and the nothing-private control), a-private-pattern-binding-hides-an-append-by-its-last-writer (both orders of the two-pattern set), a-target-specific-append-private-in-every-set-reaches-the-global (both sets private, target-only private, and the same answer read again from a grandchild).\n\nFILED RATHER THAN FOLDED IN: GNU expands the appended TEXT in the reader's scope too, not only its base. `a.o: V += <$(W)>` beside `a.o: W = aoW` and `dep: W = depW` gives GNU `a.o:[g p <aoW>]` and `dep:[g <depW>]`; Ronin now has the base right and still reads `<aoW>` in both, because the tail is expanded once where it is installed. That is a different mechanism — when the text expands, not which base the walk finds — and needs the binding to stay lazy rather than settle to a string. make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read has the reproducer."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:46:22.437050161Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-prerequisites
                title "Let a recipe-time target variable reach that target's prerequisites"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T11:59:38.528128288Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-the-scope, which closed every cell of the timing matrix but this one. Filed rather than folded in because it is not a timing question at all: it is the shape of the scope Ronin hands a node.\n\nReproducer, with the order forced so it does not depend on the scheduler:\n\n    V = outer\n    all: parent\n    parent: setter kid\n    kid: setter\n    setter:\n    \t$(eval parent: V = to-parent)\n    \t@echo \"setter done\"\n    kid:\n    \t@echo \"kid:[$(V)]\"\n    parent:\n    \t@echo \"parent:[$(V)]\"\n\nGNU: kid:[to-parent], parent:[to-parent]. Ronin: kid:[outer], parent:[to-parent]. The target the recipe named reads the assignment; the prerequisite that inherits from it does not.\n\nWhy. GNU keeps a file's variables in a chain — `initialize_file_variables` sets `l->next = file->parent->variables` — so `kid` does not hold a copy of what `parent` bound, it holds a link to it. A write into parent's set at any point in the run is visible through that link to anything that has not yet been expanded. Ronin flattens the chain when it plans: each `DepNode.rule_vars` is a fresh `Vars` with `merge_from` of the live scope at the moment the node was planned, so `kid`'s scope is a copy taken before `setter` ran and nothing later can reach it. `make_dep` now hands the evaluator `planned_scopes`, a name-to-scope table, which is what lets the assignment reach `parent` — but it reaches one scope, and the copies made from it are already elsewhere.\n\nThe fix is to make a node's scope a link rather than a copy: `Vars` would carry an optional parent and lookup would walk it, with `DepBuilder` recording the planning parent instead of merging its contents. That is a change to the type every scope in the evaluator is expressed in, which is why it is its own node. Worth checking against `release_private` while doing it: that function exists because the two GNU sets are flattened into one, and a real chain might subsume it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:40:05.626256682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:41:07.630581245Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Reproducer re-verified at dec8ddc: kid:[outer] where GNU gives kid:[to-parent].\n\nTHE MATRIX, thirteen cells against the pristine oracle, every order forced with a prerequisite chain rather than left to the scheduler:\n\n  the target the eval names                     visible\n  its prerequisite, not yet expanded            visible\n  its prerequisite's prerequisite               visible, through the chain\n  an order-only prerequisite                    visible -- one list in GNU\n  a prerequisite already expanded when it ran   NOT visible, and GNU agrees\n  a prerequisite that bound the name itself     its own binding wins\n  a prerequisite whose own binding is `private` its own for itself, the write for its children\n  a pattern binding the prerequisite inherited  the write shadows it\n  `private` on the recipe-time assignment       the named target only\n  `+=` at recipe time                           the whole appended value travels\n  the eval names a prerequisite, not the parent reaches that one and below, not the parent\n  a name two targets ask for, reached first by the setter's target       takes the write\n  a name two targets ask for, reached first by another target            does not\n\nThe last pair is the cell that decides the shape. GNU inherits along `file->parent`, which `update_file` sets the first time a name is reached and never moves — so whether the write lands is a question about which parent got there first, not about which targets name it. `all: p2 p1` with `p2: setter shared` and `p1: shared` gives shared:[to-p2]; writing `all: p1 p2` with the setter under p1 gives shared:[outer] while p2 still reads to-p2.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT THE FIX THE NODE SKETCHED. The filing proposed making `Vars` carry a parent and having lookup walk it — the type every scope in the evaluator is expressed in. That is not needed, because Ronin's copies are all taken before any recipe runs: the only thing that can arrive afterwards is one write, and a write can be carried down the same link a lookup would have walked up. So the link is recorded and the write is pushed, rather than every read being made to walk.\n\nDELIVERED, kati. `DepNode::planning_parent` is GNU's `file->parent`, set in `build_plan` where the memoised return is what makes the first caller the parent — the same rule, in the same place. `DepNode::own_rule_vars` records each name the node bound in its own right and whether that binding survived `release_private`, which now reports it. `make_dep` hands the evaluator `PlannedScope` — the scope, the own-name map, and the targets whose parent this is — in place of the bare `Arc<Vars>`. `Evaluator::inherit_planned_assignment` walks the links after a recipe-time assignment lands.\n\nTwo questions decide how far the walk goes and they are not the same one, which is the `private` cell above: a target that bound the name itself does not take the write, because its own binding is what its recipe reads; but only a PUBLIC binding of its own stops the walk there, because a private one is stepped over by whatever reads across the link. A `private` recipe-time assignment is not carried at all.\n\nEvery cell above now matches.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded: a-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-that-targets-prerequisites (kid, grandchild, a target with its own binding, and one already expanded), a-recipe-time-target-variable-follows-the-parent-that-reached-first (the shared-name cell that pins the link rather than the prerequisite list), a-recipe-time-target-variable-is-stepped-over-where-it-is-private (a private binding on the way down, a private assignment, and `+=`)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:46:28.62307451Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffle-reaches-the-circular-drop
                title "Let --shuffle decide which circular edge is dropped"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-circular-dependency-is-dropped)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:05:13.683073989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-circular-dependency-is-dropped, which delivered the drop and left this one cell of it. Owns options/shuffle.diff.9, which stayed `unclassified` when the other five moved.\n\nGNU Make walks the shuffled prerequisite list when it decides which edge closes the loop: `update_file_1` reads `d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du` before it tests `is_updating` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:596, and :680 and :153 for the other two walks). So the shuffle chooses the edge, and the suite says so on purpose — reference/gnumake/tests/scripts/options/shuffle, under `# Check if make can survive circular dependency`:\n\n  all: a_ b_ ; @echo $@\n  %_: ; @echo $@\n  a_: b_\n  b_: a_\n\n  make --shuffle=reverse\n  GNU:   `make: Circular a_ <- b_ dependency dropped.` then a_, b_, all\n  Ronin: `ronin: Circular b_ <- a_ dependency dropped.` then b_, a_, all\n\nSame build, opposite edge, opposite order. Without `--shuffle` the two agree.\n\nWhy: `DepBuilder::drop_circular_dependencies` runs at the end of kati's `build`, over the prerequisite lists as written. Ronin applies the shuffle afterwards, in `reorder(shuffle, ..)` in src/make.rs, on the roots and each target's prerequisites — so the drop has already happened by the time the order it should have followed exists.\n\nThe fix is a layering decision rather than a patch, which is why it is its own node. Either the shuffle moves into kati so the drop can run after it, or the drop becomes something Ronin calls once it has reordered — and the second means kati's own binary has to keep calling it too, since `rkati` has no reorder step and must still drop. Whichever way it goes, the goal order shuffles as well as the prerequisite order (remake.c:153 is `update_goal_chain`'s own walk), so both have to be in place before the drop runs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:22:57.902272747Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:46:03.889667708Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by moving the shuffle into kati, which is where GNU Make performs it, rather than by teaching the drop about a reordering that had already been missed.\n\nRonin's `--shuffle` support was already complete before this: the modes parse, the seed settles, `MAKEFLAGS` carries the mode a sub-make inherits, and `reorder` in src/make.rs permuted the goals and every prerequisite list reachable from them. What it could not reach was the circular drop, because `DepBuilder::build` had already run it by the time `evaluate` returned.\n\nThe matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before anything moved. GNU on the left, Ronin as it was on the right.\n\n  the suite's own case (options/shuffle, `# Check if make can survive circular dependency`)\n    all: a_ b_ ; %_: ; a_: b_ ; b_: a_\n    no shuffle            `Circular b_ <- a_`, b_ a_ all           / same\n    --shuffle=reverse     `Circular a_ <- b_`, a_ b_ all           / `Circular b_ <- a_`, b_ a_ all\n    --shuffle=identity    as no shuffle                            / same\n    --shuffle=<seed>      `Circular a_ <- b_`, a_ b_ all           / unmoved\n  goal order              `make --shuffle=reverse a b c` builds c b a  / already matched\n  prerequisite order      three independent prerequisites, reversed   / already matched\n  $^, $+, $<              written order under every mode, including on the target whose own edge was dropped / already matched\n  the drop's own target   `t: p q r` with `q: t`: `Circular q <- t`, hat still `[p q r]` / already matched\n\nSo `$^` is the load-bearing negative: GNU's shuffle populates a second `->shuf` chain over the same prerequisites and leaves `->next`, which the automatic variables walk, exactly where it was. Only the walk order moves.\n\nShape delivered. kati gained `shuffle.rs` — `Shuffle`, the `SplitMix64` draw and `reorder`, moved out of src/make.rs unchanged — plus `Flags::shuffle`; `src/make.rs` re-exports `kati::shuffle::Shuffle` so every caller is untouched, and `compile_unit` sets the flag on the session instead of reordering after the fact. `DepBuilder::build` reorders the goals immediately before `drop_circular_dependencies`, which is `main.c`'s own placement: `shuffle_goaldeps_recursive (goals)` sits directly above `update_goal_chain (goals)`, and the makefile-remaking update at main.c:2448 has already happened by then — so the regeneration roots are deliberately not shuffled, and the drop still walks them first in read order.\n\nOne consequence worth its own line: `deps` and `actual_inputs` were positionally aligned, and `drop_prerequisite` relied on it. A shuffle permutes the first and not the second, so it now takes the name where it stands when the two still agree — every unshuffled run, bit for bit as before — and finds it by name when they do not.\n\nRows: options/shuffle.diff.9 moved unclassified -> narration, and it is the only row in the 1287 that moved. Its markers went `product-name+ninja-progress+evaluation` to `product-name+ninja-progress` — the `evaluation` residue is gone, which is the whole of what this node owned. compiler stays 0, interface stays 41.\n\nThree fixtures, recorded from the oracle: circular-dependency-drops-the-edge-the-shuffle-reached (the suite's case with `--shuffle=reverse -j1`, gating the build order the drop leaves), circular-dependency-under-shuffle-keeps-the-prerequisite-list ($^/$+/$< under a shuffle that also drops), shuffled-goals-are-built-back-to-front (the goal chain shuffles, which nothing gated before).\n\nOne cell does not match and is filed rather than fixed: GNU names the shuffled entry and unlinks the walked one, so `t: x t` under `--shuffle=reverse` leaves GNU with `hat=[t]` and Ronin with `hat=[x]`. See make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-position-not-the-edge.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 858 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; clippy all three passes silent; make_port green (recording round-trip and build intent); conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1287 rows at 167/0/41/1079; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 27s at -j8.\n\nkati commit a08f6e6 on nplan/make-shuffle-reaches-the-circular-drop.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:40:45.062254159Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it
                title "Report an include that would not open where GNU Make reports it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:05:36.835608404Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile, which moved the refusal to after the remaking and left the complaint that goes with it where it was.\n\nGNU Make does not report an `include` it could not open when it fails to open it. `eval_makefile` records the errno on the `goaldep` and says nothing; the complaint comes out of `complain()` during the makefile update, beside the refusal. A file the command line named with `-f` is reported at once, which is the difference between the two probes:\n\n  Makefile (backdated, remakes itself), line 1 `include nope.mk`\n    GNU:   MKSRC / REMAKE / `Makefile:1: nope.mk: No such file or directory` / `make: *** No rule to make target 'nope.mk'.  Stop.`\n    Ronin: `Makefile:1: nope.mk: No such file or directory` / MKSRC / REMAKE / `ronin: No rule to make target 'nope.mk'.`\n\n  make -fbye.mk -fR - all  (bye.mk backdated, remakes itself)\n    GNU:   `make: R: No such file or directory` / touch bye.mk.src / touch bye.mk / refusal\n    Ronin: the same order — this one already matches, and it is why options/dash-f.diff.4 reclassified\n\nSo the work and the refusal are in GNU's places now; the one line still out of place is the located half. It is not gated: the build-intent harness compares files and status rather than stderr, and the located face reaches no upstream row this repository owns. Filed so it is a decision rather than an oversight.\n\nWhere: `plan_regeneration` in kati/src-rs/dep.rs raises the `warn_loc!` inline and returns the refusal for the frontend to raise later. Holding the warning back means carrying its rendered text out with the refusal — `color_warn_log` prints rather than returns, so it needs a formatting-only sibling — and having `prepare_graph` emit the pair together in src/make/cli.rs. Only the located form moves; the `-f` form is reported by the read, as GNU reports it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:47:45.942512652Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:00:30.707202241Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe found a second reporting site GNU has that the filing did not name — filed rather than folded in.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `show_goal_error` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:302) is what prints the located line, and it has exactly two callers: `complain()` at remake.c:414, the no-rule refusal, and `child_error()` at job.c:581, a recipe that failed. Neither is the read. `eval_makefile` (read.c:347) does `deps->error = errno` and returns silently, and the guard on the print is `(goal_dep->flags & (RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)) != RM_INCLUDED` — so a required `include` complains, `-include` never does, `MAKEFILES` never does, and a `-f` file is not RM_INCLUDED at all. `goal->error = 0` after printing makes it once per file.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Six cells, of which four moved:\n\n  Makefile remakes itself, `include nope.mk`\n    GNU    REMAKE / `Makefile:1: nope.mk: No such file or directory` / refusal\n    was    the complaint, then REMAKE, then the refusal\n  a sibling makefile is remade first, then a missing one\n    GNU    GEN / `Makefile:2: nope.mk: ...` / refusal\n    was    the complaint, then GEN, then the refusal\n  `$(info)` ahead of the include\n    GNU    INFO-LINE / complaint / refusal — both already agreed on this one, and it is the cell that shows the complaint follows the READ as well as the remaking\n  nothing to remake                 both already agreed\n  two missing required includes     the first named and only that one; both agreed\n  `-fbye.mk -fR -`                  `make: R: No such file or directory` BEFORE the remaking; already agreed, and must keep agreeing\n  `-include nope.mk`                silent in both\n\nShape delivered. kati's `plan_regeneration` no longer prints: it renders the complaint with `color_warn_text`, a formatting-only sibling of `color_warn_log` added for this, and returns it in a new `dep::Refusal { complaint, error }` that replaces the bare `anyhow::Error` on `Plan` and `Evaluated`. Ronin's `RefusedMakefile` is the same pair over `MakeError`, carried through `UnitRemakes`, `CompilationState` and `Loaded` exactly where the refusal already travelled, and `report::refused_makefile` writes the complaint one line ahead of what ends the run — so all three raise sites (nothing to remake, a settled remaking, a restart that loses to the refusal) emit the pair together. `rkati` has no remaking phase, so it prints the held complaint and raises at once, which is the same output it produced before.\n\nOnly the located form moved, as the filing asked. A Makefile the command line named has no `include` line and so no location, `complaint` is `None` for it, and the read reports it as it always did.\n\nGating. The build-intent harness compares files and status rather than output, so this is asserted in tests/make_regressions.rs instead, on ONE descriptor: Make mode gathers its narration and writes it at the end while a diagnostic goes to stderr as it happens, so concatenating two captures says nothing about order. `merged_make` runs the case under `sh -c '... 2>&1'`. Two tests: an-unopenable-include-is-complained-of-after-the-remaking (the complaint sits between REMAKE and the refusal) and a-named-makefile-that-would-not-open-is-reported-by-the-read (the `-f` face, where the complaint sits before the remaking) — the second is what keeps the fix from being applied to the form GNU reports early.\n\nRows: none moved, which is what the filing predicted — the located face reaches no upstream row this repository owns. 1287 rows unmoved at 167/0/41/1079.\n\nThree adjacent defects found while probing and filed rather than folded in:\n  make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include — job.c:581, the second `show_goal_error` caller: a required include whose own rule FAILS gets the complaint in GNU and none at all in Ronin.\n  make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised — `Permission denied` and `Is a directory` are read-time failures in Ronin and ordinary unread makefiles in GNU.\n  make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make — `-k` makes `complain()` `error` rather than `fatal`, so GNU refuses over every one and adds a `Failed to remake makefile` summary.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 860 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (858 + the two new); clippy all three passes silent; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1287 at 167/0/41/1079, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 39s at -j8.\n\nkati commit 554caa1 on nplan/make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:40:49.726868799Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals
                title "Leave a forgiven makefile alone once the goals reach it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-optional-include-failure-is-per-file)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:05:49.882550609Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-optional-include-failure-is-per-file, found while probing it and pre-existing rather than caused by it.\n\nA goal that names an optional include whose rule already lost is attempted a second time by Ronin and refused outright by GNU Make:\n\n  -include one.mk\n  all: one.mk ; @echo all ran\n  one.mk: ; @echo GEN-one-FAIL; exit 1\n\n  GNU:   `GEN-one-FAIL` once, then `make: *** No rule to make target 'one.mk', needed by 'all'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n  Ronin: `GEN-one-FAIL` during the makefile pass, forgiven; then the goal build runs the rule again, it loses again, and the run ends on that failure. exit 2\n\nBoth refuse and both exit 2, so nothing gates on it; what differs is that the recipe runs twice and that the diagnostic names the rule's failure rather than the missing prerequisite.\n\nGNU marks every makefile it considered `updated` and reads the recorded `update_status` back when a goal reaches the same file, so the rule is not run again — `complain()` then reports it as a target with no rule, because the rule it had was spent. Ronin's counterpart is `Remaking::Settled`, whose targets `mark_makefiles_remade` treats as prebuilt so the goals do not reconsider them; the ones that lost are deliberately not in that list, because marking a file prebuilt that was never made would tell the goal build it exists.\n\nSo the fix is a third state rather than a wider list: reached, tried, and not made — a makefile the goals must neither rebuild nor believe in. The `outputs_reobserved` / `FreshnessHistory` side tables in src/graph.rs are the shape that already exists for saying something extra about a node the graph itself cannot express."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:02:39.519817479Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:21:51.109348097Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered as the third state the filing asked for, and the matrix turned out to hold a second defect in the same place: which switches a Makefile is made under and whether its failure is forgiven had been read as one question.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `update_file_1` opens by reading two flags back before it looks at the file or at its commands (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:459):\n\n    if (file->updated)\n      {\n        if (file->update_status > us_none)\n          {\n            DBF (DB_VERBOSE, \"Recently tried and failed to update file '%s'.\");\n            if (file->no_diag && !file->dontcare)\n                complain (file);\n            return file->update_status;\n          }\n        ...\n\nSo the rule is not reconsidered and `complain()` reports the file as a target nothing knows how to make — which is why GNU's diagnostic names the missing prerequisite rather than the recipe that lost, and why the recipe runs once.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right:\n\n  -include one.mk, rule fails, `all: one.mk`\n      GNU  one attempt, `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk', needed by 'all'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n      was  two attempts, `build stopped: subcommand failed`, exit 2\n  the same file named as the goal\n      GNU  one attempt, `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.` — no `needed by`\n      was  one attempt, `build stopped: subcommand failed` — and see below, this was a different bug\n  the rule WRITES the file and then exits 1, `all: one.mk`\n      GNU  one attempt, refuses, `all` never runs, one.mk on disk\n      was  built `all` and exited 0 — the file was there, so nothing refused\n  the file already exists on disk, rule fails\n      GNU  refuses\n      was  two attempts, then failed on the second\n  rule succeeds, `all: one.mk`      both restart, then `all` runs — already agreed\n  rule succeeds, named as goal      `'one.mk' is up to date.` / `no work to do.` — already agreed\n  required include, rule fails      the run ends in the makefile pass — already agreed\n  two forgiven, one loses and is a goal prereq\n      GNU  one-FAIL, two, one-FAIL (the restart re-attempts and re-forgives), then the refusal\n      now  the same three lines and the same refusal\n  the same file needed by two targets   refused once — was two attempts\n  -k                                 refuses, adds `Target 'all' not remade because of errors.`; Ronin refuses without the summary (make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make)\n\nThe `rule writes the file then exits 1` cell is the one that settles the shape: the verdict is about the update, not about the bytes, so an entry in the third state has to outrank what the filesystem says.\n\nShape delivered, all of it in Ronin — nothing in kati moved. `src/graph/unmade.rs` holds the state as a sparse `RapidHashSet<NodeId>` beside the node arena, for the reason `validation_uses` and `withdrawal` are beside theirs: no node of a Ninja manifest is ever in it. `Plan::add_node` consults it before it looks for a generator, so the node is refused whether or not it is dirty and whether or not it has a rule, and the refusal is the one the graph already has — `MissingInput`, which names the dependent when there is one and does not when the goal is the file itself. That is GNU's pair of messages exactly, in Ninja's wording, which is the wording Ronin already uses for `No rule to make target`. `Remaking::Settled` now carries `SettledMakefiles { remade, unmade }`, because the update has three answers and not two, and `build_compiler_inputs` marks both. A pass that ends in a restart records nothing, which is what makes the re-attempt happen.\n\nThe second defect, found by the matrix and fixed with it. `Makefiles::subsets` treated \"the command line named this Makefile\" as an answer to \"is its failure forgiven\", so an `-include`d file that was also a goal was made non-forgiven and the run ended on its recipe. GNU keeps the two apart two lines apart: `file->dontcare = ANY_SET (g->flags, RM_DONTCARE)` comes from the `include` line, and the `file->cmd_target` arm below it restores `-t`, `-q` and `-n` and nothing else. So the grouping is now the cross product — four subsets, not three — and a Makefile that is both keeps the invocation's switches AND is forgiven.\n\nThree fixtures, recorded from the oracle, and all three gate on file effects rather than on output: a-forgiven-makefile-that-lost-is-not-remade-for-the-goals (the log holds one attempt, not two), a-forgiven-makefile-a-losing-rule-wrote-is-still-refused (the file is on disk, `all` still never runs), a-forgiven-makefile-the-command-line-named-is-forgiven (two forgiven Makefiles both named on the command line are both attempted, where the second used to be skipped).\n\nRows: none moved. 1287 unchanged at 167/0/41/1079, compiler 0.\n\nOne adjacent cell found and filed rather than fixed: `make -q one.mk` on the same shape refuses with 2 in GNU and answers 1 in Ronin, because `-q` puts the file in no subset at all and so leaves no verdict for the goals to read. Pre-existing — neither grouping reaches it. See make-question-mode-refuses-a-makefile-it-cannot-make.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 860 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the three new recordings; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1287 at 167/0/41/1079, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 50s at -j8.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:40:54.415794835Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-explicit-rule-stem-from-a-known-suffix
                title "Read $* off a known suffix for an explicit rule's target"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:47:36.168613665Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity, and unrelated to it: it reproduces identically at f221c81, before that node's change, which is how it was told apart from it.\n\n`$*` in a rule chosen by the implicit search is the stem the match read, and Ronin has that right. `$*` in an EXPLICIT rule is a different question with a different answer, and Ronin returns the empty string for all of it.\n\nReproducer, minimised against the pristine oracle:\n\n  all: a.c b.q\n  a.c: ; echo 'c star=[$*]' >> log\n  b.q: ; echo 'q star=[$*]' >> log\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 writes `c star=[a]` and `q star=[]`. Ronin writes `c star=[]` twice.\n\nThe discriminator is `.SUFFIXES`. GNU Make's manual says it outright: for an explicit rule, `$*` is the target name with the suffix deleted when that suffix is a known one, and the empty string when it is not. `.c` is on the default list of 35 that make-builtin-rule-catalogue installed; `.q` is not, and that is the whole of the difference between the two lines above. So the answer moved when the catalogue landed — before it there were two suffixes and neither of these was one of them — and the `.SUFFIXES` list the bootstrap now writes is exactly what a fix reads.\n\nWhere it is. kati/src-rs/command.rs, `AutoCommand::Star`: the stem is taken from `current_dep_node.stem` when the search recorded one, and otherwise by substituting `recipe_output` into `output_pattern`. An explicit rule has neither, so both arms miss and nothing is written. The missing third arm is the suffix one, and it belongs after the pattern arm rather than before it: a static pattern rule has an `output_pattern` and its own answer, already recorded by make-static-pattern-rule-stem.\n\nTwo cells worth probing before implementing, neither measured yet: whether a Makefile that rewrote `.SUFFIXES` moves the answer (it should — GNU reads the list, not a fixed table), and what `$*` is for a `::` record and for a grouped `&:` rule, where several targets share one recipe.\n\nTwo upstream inventory rows are the reason this is worth its own node rather than a footnote: `variables/automatic` is one of the larger unclassified groups, and `$*` is what several of its sub-tests read."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:40:10.63792633Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:41:28.115681661Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE MATRIX, ten cells against the pristine oracle, and GNU's own source says the rule outright — `set_file_variables` (src/commands.c:97) walks `enter_file(\".SUFFIXES\")->deps` in list order and stops at the first entry the name ends with, guarded by `len > slen`.\n\n  cell                                                    GNU 4.4.1\n  `a.c` explicit, `.c` on the built-in list               a\n  `b.q` explicit, `.q` not on it                          empty\n  the same after `.SUFFIXES: .q`                          a and b -- adding moves it\n  the same after a bare `.SUFFIXES:`                      both empty -- clearing withdraws it\n  `.SUFFIXES: .z .a.z`, target `foo.a.z`                  foo.a\n  `.SUFFIXES: .a.z .z`, target `foo.a.z`                  foo -- ORDER, not longest match\n  `sub/a.c`, and its `D` and `F` forms                    sub/a, sub, a\n  a target named `.c`, the suffix itself                  empty -- the name must be longer\n  `a.c::` and `b.c c.c &:`                                a, and b for the member `$@` bound\n  an implicit match, and a static pattern rule            unchanged -- their own answers\n\nThe order cell is the one worth having: two suffixes can both fit only when one ends the other, and then which answer comes out is the list's order. That makes it a real read of `.SUFFIXES` rather than a table lookup, and it is why the two `.SUFFIXES` cells above are not decoration.\n\nDELIVERED. `Session::suffixes` holds the list as the read left it, published by `DepBuilder::read_suffix_list` where the field used to live on the builder — `$*` reads it while a recipe expands, long after the builder is gone, so the builder is no longer the right owner of it. The three other readers (the built-in pair install, the declared-pair split, the suffix-rule ordering) read it from the session now; one list, one place.\n\n`AutoCommand::Star` gains the third arm the filing predicted, after the pattern arm and not before it: a static pattern rule has an `output_pattern` and its own answer, and only a rule with neither a recorded stem nor a pattern reaches the suffix walk. The walk is GNU's — first entry the name ends with, name strictly longer than the suffix.\n\nEvery cell above now matches.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded: an-explicit-rules-stem-is-the-target-without-a-known-suffix (known, unknown, the bare-suffix name, and the `D`/`F` division of a nested one), a-rewritten-suffix-list-moves-an-explicit-rules-stem (clear, add, and both chained orders in one case), one-recipe-over-several-targets-reads-its-stem-off-the-name-it-bound (`::` and `&:`).\n\nUPSTREAM INVENTORY: the two rows the filing said this was worth a node for, both moved. variables/automatic.diff.4 and .diff.5 go unclassified → narration, and their family list drops `evaluation` and keeps only `ninja-progress` — the evaluation difference was `$*` and there is nothing left but the progress line. Total unmoved at 1283; unclassified 167 → 165, narration 1079 → 1081, compiler stays ZERO, interface stays 37."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:46:33.606557092Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-deferred-freshness-hides-a-multi-target-peer
                title "See an absent group member on a rule whose recipe reads $?"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:47:59.445485514Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity and confirmed pre-existing: it reproduces identically at f221c81, so that node's launch-time `$@` binding neither caused it nor touched it.\n\nReproducer, minimised against the pristine oracle, with `a.in` old and `a.c` current and `a.h` absent:\n\n  all: a.c a.h\n  %.c %.h: %.in ; echo \"at=$@ q=$?\" >> log ; cat $< > $*.c ; cat $< > $*.h\n\nGNU Make runs the recipe — `at=a.h q=a.in` — because `a.h` is not there. Ronin says `no work to do.` and writes nothing.\n\nThe discriminator is `$?` and nothing else. Take it out of the recipe and both tools run it; that case is now recorded as tests/make/multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date and gates.\n\nWhy the two part company. A recipe naming `$?` cannot be left for launch — `references_new_inputs` refuses it in `defers_recipe` — and the edge is given a deferred-freshness answer instead: `Graph::deferred_freshness` names the outputs whose timestamps decide it, and `is_virtual_output` then takes every one of the edge's outputs out of the scan's own stat loop (src/graph.rs, the `Work::Enter` arm). The recorded observed-output list is built from what the sink was told to watch, and a pattern rule's second target pattern is not in it, so `a.h`'s absence is never looked at.\n\nSo this is the deferred-freshness machinery meeting the multi-target pattern rule, and the two features were built without each other in view. A fix owes `capture_deferred_freshness` the same division make-grouped-pattern-targets gave the ordinary path: the outputs reached in their own right decide the edge, the peers do not, and being absent is a state the reached ones are allowed to be in.\n\nWorth reading beside make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity, which is the same shape from the other side: that node binds `$@` for an edge that runs, and this one is about the edge never running at all. Its fixtures are the control set — every one of them uses a recipe with no `$?` in it, which is exactly the line this defect sits on."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:40:15.744530841Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:41:47.778823793Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Reproducer re-verified at dec8ddc as the handoff asked, before touching anything: `ronin: no work to do.` where GNU runs the recipe. The deferred machinery's several reworks since the filing had not reached it, and the control case with no `$?` in the recipe still ran in both tools — so the discriminator named in the filing is still the discriminator.\n\nWHERE IT WAS. `deferred_freshness_outputs` in kati/src-rs/ninja.rs named exactly one output for a non-grouped deferred edge: `node.recipe_output`. `capture_deferred_freshness` stats that list and nothing else, and `is_virtual_output` has already taken every output of such an edge out of the scan's own stat loop — so a pattern rule's second target pattern was looked at by neither. Absent, and invisible.\n\nTHE FIX is the division the ordinary path already makes and this one had not been given. `recompute_edge_dirty_with` compares `edge.out` filtered by `graph.peer_outputs(edge)`; the deferred list now says the same thing — the node's recipe output and its implicit outputs, less the peers. A peer is a name nobody asked for, so no state of its can send the recipe to the shell, and a name later asked for in its own right stops being one.\n\nTHE MATRIX, six cells against the pristine oracle, `a.in` old throughout:\n\n  a.c current, a.h absent, both asked for      runs\n  a.c current, a.h absent, only a.c asked for  nothing to be done -- a.h is a peer\n  a.c current, a.h present and behind a.in     runs\n  both present and both ahead of a.in          nothing to be done\n  the same six with no `$?` in the recipe      identical -- the control set\n\nAll six now match, and `$?` itself is right where the recipe runs (`q=a.in`).\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded: a-deferred-freshness-rule-sees-an-absent-second-target (an absent second target and a stale second target, two stems in one case) and a-deferred-freshness-rule-passes-over-a-peer-nobody-asked-for (the peer exception and the everything-current control, which together are why the fix is a filter and not \"every output\").\n\nNOT DELIVERED, and filed rather than left silent. `$@` on such an edge is still the first name: GNU binds `at=a.h`, Ronin binds `at=a.c`. That is the other side of make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity, which bound `$@` from `trigger_output` as the edge launches and recorded in its own note that a `$?` recipe never reaches that binding — the recipe is expanded while the graph is compiled, so the name is chosen before any timestamp is known. Closing it needs `$@` to become a launch-time substitution the way `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` already is, which is a change to what the command text carries and what the executor binds. make-deferred-freshness-binds-at-before-the-timestamps-are-known has the reproducer. Both fixtures here read `$*` and `$?` and not `$@`, which is exactly the line the two nodes divide on."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:46:38.463589344Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-output-function-is-not-a-command
                title "Print a recipe's $(info) while the recipe expands, not by running a command"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:40:50.738039658Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing, which made the cell reachable and then could not close it without a change of a different shape.\n\nGNU Make prints `$(info ...)`, `$(warning ...)` and raises `$(error ...)` while it expands the recipe, in `new_job`, before any command line exists. The text is not a command and never becomes one: a recipe whose whole expansion is `$(info X)` prints X and then has no command line at all, so GNU Make starts no shell, says `'x' is up to date.`, and answers `-q` with 0.\n\nkati turns them into commands instead. `ev.avoid_io` makes info_func push `printf '%b\\n' \"X\"` onto `delayed_output_commands` (func.rs:1188), and warning/error the same with their own suffixes, and `CommandEvaluator::eval` appends those to the recipe's command list. That is right for a manifest — the text has to survive to a run that happens on another day — and `ninja.rs`'s launch expansion still sets `avoid_io = true`, so it is what happens in the process that runs the build too.\n\nWhat it costs, probed against 4.4.1:\n\n    EMPTY =\n    all: ; $(info EXPANDED)$(EMPTY)\n\nGNU prints `EXPANDED` then `make: 'all' is up to date.`, and `make -q` prints `EXPANDED` and exits 0. Ronin runs a printf and exits 1 under `-q`, because the recipe has a command line where GNU Make has none. Under `-q` specifically Ronin now expands the recipe (that is what answers the question) but never runs the command it built, so the text is not printed at all where GNU Make prints it.\n\nThe fix is to let the launch expansion do its own I/O, since it happens in the process that runs the build and immediately before the recipe would run — which is exactly where GNU Make does it. `avoid_io` is one flag doing several jobs and the other two are already separated: `ShellEvaluation` and `FileEvaluation` were split out for `$(shell)` and `$(file ...)`, and this is the same split for the output functions. Sequencing is the part to settle: `println!` from the scheduler thread does not go through the build's output sink, so a parallel build's ordering and Make mode's `stdout.is_empty()` test both have to be looked at. `$(error)` moving from a failing command to a raised diagnostic changes which of two error paths reports it, and make-lazy-recipe-expansion's recipe-error-in-a-current-target-does-not-fire fixture is the one that pins the timing either way."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:59:41.407029309Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:16:30.480687567Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 probed cell by cell against the pristine oracle, before and after.\n\nWHERE THE BEHAVIOUR LIVES. `new_job` expands the recipe, and the three output functions do their work during that expansion — `func_info` prints, `func_warning` prints with the location, `func_error` raises. None of them leaves a command line behind. A recipe whose whole expansion is `$(info X)` therefore reaches `job_next_command` with nothing to start, which is the empty-expansion path make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing already landed: `'all' is up to date.`, exit 0, no shell.\n\nTHE MATRIX, oracle then Ronin-before then Ronin-after.\n\n  recipe is only $(info X)        GNU: X, up to date, rc 0, no shell\n                                  was: [1/1] printf, a shell, rc 0\n                                  now: X, no work to do., rc 0, no shell\n  the same under -q               GNU: X, rc 0\n                                  was: silent, rc 1        <- the one -q cell still answering 1\n                                  now: X, rc 0\n  the same under -n               GNU: X, up to date, rc 0\n                                  was: printed the printf command\n                                  now: X, no work to do., rc 0\n  $(info X) beside real text      GNU: X, then `touch out`, out made\n                                  was: one command doing both, X printed when it ran\n                                  now: X at expansion, [1/1] touch out, out made\n  the same under -q               GNU: X, rc 1             was: silent, rc 1     now: X, rc 1\n  the same under -n               GNU: X, then echoes `touch out`\n                                  was: echoed the composite, X never printed\n                                  now: X, then echoes touch out\n  recipe is only $(warning W)     GNU: `Makefile:1: W` on stderr, up to date, rc 0\n                                  was: a printf command    now: agrees, and on stderr\n  the same under -q               GNU: W, rc 0             was: silent, rc 1     now: W, rc 0\n  recipe is only $(error B)       GNU: rc 2                was: rc 2 by a command contrived to fail\n                                  now: rc 2 as a refusal raised out of the expansion\n  the same under -q               GNU: rc 2                was: rc 1             now: rc 2\n  the same under -n               GNU: rc 2                was: rc 0             now: rc 2\n  $(error B) beside real text     GNU: rc 2, nothing built  was: rc 2 via the command   now: rc 2, refusal\n  two targets, first only prints  GNU: A-INFO, builds the second\n                                  was: a progress line for the first  now: agrees\n  the same under -q               GNU: A-INFO, rc 1        was: silent, rc 1     now: A-INFO, rc 1\n  a target already up to date     GNU: no expansion, nothing printed. Both agreed before and after —\n                                  the recipe is expanded only for a target about to run.\n\nSTREAMS AND ORDER, both checked because deferring had hidden them. `$(info)` goes to stdout and `$(warning)` to stderr with its location, exactly as GNU. Under `-j4` over eight printing targets all eight lines appear. Interleaved with real work at `-j1` the order is GNU's: an earlier target's output, then the next target's text, then that target's command.\n\nDELIVERED. kati c796d42 on nplan/make-recipe-output-function-is-not-a-command: `OutputEvaluation` in build_sink.rs, the same split `ShellEvaluation` and `FileEvaluation` already made. `avoid_io` alone was answering for every destination; `Evaluator::defers_output_to_the_recipe()` now answers, and the three functions consult it. The manifest writer keeps `RecipeCommand` — a manifest's text has to survive to a run on another day — while kati's own executor and Ronin's launch expansion take `Expansion`. Ronin: `GraphSink::output_evaluation` returns `Expansion`, which is the whole of Ronin's side; the empty-expansion machinery then does the rest, because a recipe left with no command line is a case it already knew.\n\nFIXTURES, six, all oracle-recorded. a-recipe-that-is-only-an-output-function-starts-no-shell and a-recipe-that-is-only-a-warning-starts-no-shell use the `SHELL := ./recording-shell` device, so \"no shell was started\" is a file fact the build-intent gate holds. question-mode-answers-for-a-recipe-that-only-prints is the `-q` cell as an exit status. a-recipes-error-function-refuses-a-dry-run is the `-n` cell, where the oracle fails and Ronin used to succeed. question-mode-reaches-past-a-recipe-that-only-prints and an-output-function-beside-a-recipes-own-text are the controls. Two regressions in tests/make_regressions.rs carry what the corpus cannot see: printing_alone_starts_no_command (the text reaches stdout and no progress line is printed) and a_dry_run_raises_the_error.\n\nINVENTORY, audited by mechanism. 1287 rows to 1283: exactly four left and none entered, all four variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.{7,15,23,31} — a recipe reading MAKEFLAGS through `$(info)` now prints what GNU prints, byte for byte. 250 rows changed family, every one of them in the same direction, losing `ninja-progress`: `ninja-progress` 1047 to 809, `no-work-line` 220 to 325. The bulk is features/implicit_search, whose every case writes `%.o: %.c; $(info hello.c)` and expects `Nothing to be done` — the suite is built on this exact behaviour. Classes: unclassified 167 unmoved, compiler 0 and still zero, interface 41 to 37 (the four that left), narration 1079 unmoved. The watched flappers patternrules.diff.34 and reinvoke.diff.2 are byte-identical to their recorded rows.\n\nGATES. fmt, check, clippy three passes, workspace tests 866/0, make_port 4/0 over 766 cases, Make conformance 318/69 unmoved, equivalence 297/278/0, Ninja conformance 425 upstream plus differential, outcome, invocation-boundary, persistence and log-content all pass, real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 22s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:17:19.925146478Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell
                title "Run each of a recipe's command lines in a shell of its own"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:53:03.704507724Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-recipe-expanded-newline, which was opened against a premise that turned out to be false and found this underneath it.\n\nGNU Make runs each command line of a recipe as its own shell. `start_job_command` takes one line, `construct_command_argv` returns the argv for it and writes back through `restp` where the line ended, and `reap_children` comes back for the next one. Ronin assembles the whole recipe into one script — `( line1 ) && ( line2 )`, with `;` for an ignored failure and a newline under `.ONESHELL` — and hands it to one shell.\n\nmake-recipe-one-shell-per-line already probed seven shapes against 4.4.1 and found the two agree: the subshell parens give each line the isolation a separate process would, so a `cd`, a shell variable, `exit`, `set -x`, a failing line stopping the rest and a syntax error in a later line all match. What it did not probe is the case where the join's own bytes are not syntax:\n\n    all:\n    <tab>@printf '%s' 'a\n    <tab>b' | od -An -c > out\n\nGNU Make hands `printf '%s' 'a` to a shell, which dies on an unterminated quoted string, and the build fails with nothing written. Ronin builds `(printf '%s' 'a ) && (b' | od -An -c > out )`, in which the `) && (` falls inside the single quotes and becomes data: the command parses, succeeds, and writes a file GNU Make never writes. Two recorded reproducers carry `divergence` markers naming this node — tests/make/recipe-newline-inside-a-quoted-word and tests/make/expanded-newline-inside-a-quoted-word, the second reaching the same shape through `$(NL)` — and the pair is the evidence that this is about the joining and not about where the newline came from.\n\nThe general statement is that Ronin's model is right whenever each line is a complete shell command on its own and wrong when it is not, which is exactly when a line's shell syntax is left open. A recipe like that is broken, and GNU Make's answer is to fail; Ronin's is to invent a command out of the fragments, which is the worse of the two failure modes because it succeeds.\n\nWhat a fix costs. One edge would have to become several processes run in sequence, stopping at the first failure that is not ignored — a change to the executor rather than to the text, since no single command string can express it. `LateCommand` carries one command; the scheduler launches one process per edge and settles it once. That is the work. It would also close the `recipe-interleave` family in the upstream inventory, where Make interleaves each recipe line with its own output and Ronin runs the recipe as one script, and it is the only route to the `-` and `@` prefixes applying per line that make-recipe-one-shell-per-line left open under `.ONESHELL`.\n\nNot to be confused with make-recipe-continuation-bytes, which is the other half of the same region: a backslash before the newline does NOT end the command line in GNU Make — job.c's slow path tests for `\\\\\\n` before it tests for `\\n` and preserves both bytes — and that one is about what a single command line may contain, not about how many shells the recipe gets. tests/make/expanded-backslash-newline-stays-in-one-command records the two tools agreeing on the expansion-produced version of it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:32:53.685679055Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:33:45.657594118Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered with make-simple-command-without-a-shell, in one change, because they are one change: a line cannot be exec'd without a shell until the launch is per line, and the per-line launch has to decide what each line's process is. Both divergence markers are gone.\n\nTHE MATRIX, every cell run through both binaries before anything moved, GNU on the left.\n\nLINE-SCOPED STATE — all agreed already, and still do. A `cd` in line 1 is not seen by line 2; a shell variable set in line 1 is empty in line 2; `set -e` in line 1 does not reach line 2; `exec` in a line does not end the recipe. make-recipe-one-shell-per-line established this over seven shapes and the reason is the subshell the join was putting each line in — parens give a line the isolation a separate process would.\n\nEXIT SEMANTICS — all agreed already.\n  first failure stops the recipe, and its status is the target's   `exit 3` -> Error 3\n  a `-` line's failure is ignored and the next line still runs      -> `Error 1 (ignored)`, then on\n  every line `-`                                                    the target is made, exit 0\n  `-` then a line whose failure counts                              stops at the second, Error 1\nGNU says `(ignored)` on its own line and Ronin says nothing, which is narration; what matters is that nothing after an ignored failure is skipped and nothing after a counted one is run.\n\nSYNTAX — the divergence, and the whole of it.\n  @printf '%s' 'a<newline>b' | od -An -c > out\nGNU hands `printf '%s' 'a` to a shell, which dies on an unterminated quoted string, and the build fails with nothing written. Ronin built `(printf %s 'a ) && (b' | od -An -c > out )`, in which the join's own `) && (` fell inside the single quotes and became data: the command parsed, succeeded, and wrote a file GNU Make never writes. A line left open in a way the join's bytes do NOT complete — `@echo a |` — happened to fail in both, which is why this was never visible as a class.\n\n.ONESHELL — unchanged in every cell, and deliberately: there one process is not an approximation, GNU really does hand the whole recipe to one shell, and the recipe now carries exactly one step holding the assembled script under the first line's flags. The ten `target-oneshell-*` fixtures and expanded-newline-under-one-shell-keeps-the-shell hold it.\n\nEMPTY LINES start nothing, which make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing already settled and which the per-line list inherits: a line the translation absorbed contributes no step.\n\nTHE MANIFEST DECISION, taken against make-recipe-continuation-bytes' constraint rather than around it. That node's finding is that a Ninja binding cannot carry a newline at all, so a manifest writer cannot spell a recipe as several commands; the two candidates were a bounded divergence or a per-line subshell encoding (`/bin/sh -c 'line1' && /bin/sh -c 'line2'`). The encoding was refused on three grounds: it adds a process per recipe where this node's sibling exists to remove one, kati's depfile detection reads the command text and nested quoting would blind it, and it would make every manifest kati writes unreadable for a defect that needs an unterminated quote to reach. So the manifest keeps `( a ) && ( b )` — byte for byte what it was, which the equivalence gate confirms at 0 disagreements — and the executor runs the lines. It is the same split `BuildSink::shell_evaluation` and `new_inputs_timing` already make: a manifest writer keeps the approximation, Ronin's own executor does not need one.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. kati 3fb5a22 on branch nplan/make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell: `translate_recipe` stops where Make's syntax runs out and `gen_shell_script` assembles from what it left, so the two paths cannot drift; `ExpandedRecipe::steps` is the sequence of launches, each with its own shell flags and its own `-`. An absorbed line keeps its place as a `None`, because the assembler counts the lines still to come while it walks them and would subshell differently if it could not see where the absorbed one was — the assembled script is unchanged to the byte.\n\nRonin: `LateCommand::steps` and `subprocess::Launch`; `PreparedEdge` holds what is left to run and what the steps before it wrote; `Builder::advance_recipe` is `reap_children`'s question — carry on into the next line when this one succeeded, or failed and was written with a `-`, and stop where it stands otherwise. The edge still speaks once: its output is reported when the last of its processes has come back, which is where a single command's output is reported too, so the narration is unmoved. A `-` prefixed line the recipe stops at now decides on its own whether the status was one to ignore, where the assembled script could only be believed when every line of it said so.\n\nThe failure block still names the edge's command, wrapper and all. That is deliberate: `[1/1]` and `FAILED:` name the same thing, which is the edge, and Ninja's block is the edge's command. Naming the failing line instead was tried and rejected — it made 15 rows of the upstream inventory unclassifiable, because a directly exec'd line has no shape a recogniser can know it by, and it would have split the two lines of the block between two different subjects.\n\nA recipe holding a line too long to be an argument keeps the assembled script and the single launch that goes with it: the response file such a line needs is named per edge, so several of them would want one name. That, and a recipe expanded at graph construction rather than at launch — the four kinds `defers_recipe` refuses, of which `$?` is the reachable one — are the two shapes that still get one shell. Both are recorded rather than hidden: make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell has them.\n\nFIXTURES, four recorded from the oracle: recipe-line-with-an-open-quote-dies-where-it-stands (this node's own reproducer, written so the file that must not appear is evidence), recipe-line-ignoring-its-error-lets-the-next-line-run, recipe-stops-at-the-first-line-whose-failure-counts, recipe-lines-do-not-share-a-directory. The two divergence markers are deleted: tests/make/recipe-newline-inside-a-quoted-word and tests/make/expanded-newline-inside-a-quoted-word now gate rather than record. Three regressions in tests/make_regressions.rs carry what a corpus comparing files cannot: recipe_lines_report_their_output_in_order, an_ignored_line_does_not_fail_the_recipe, a_missing_program_is_reported_by_make_and_not_by_a_shell. kati unit tests a_recipe_becomes_one_launch_per_command_line, oneshell_is_one_launch_holding_the_whole_script, a_line_with_no_shell_syntax_is_launched_without_a_shell.\n\nWALL TIME, measured rather than assumed, because this triples the process count of some recipes. Paired runs of the pre-change binary and this one over Ninja's own CMake Makefile at -j8, alternating and each preceded by a clean: 32.2/33.6, 47.3/49.0, 45.6/46.2, 51.2/50.8 seconds. The host was loaded and varies by 60% between runs of the same binary, which is why the runs are paired; the ratios are 1.05, 1.04, 1.02, 0.99. GNU Make 4.4.1 built the same tree in 42-43s in the same window. A synthetic 1500 targets of three `@true` lines each — 4500 processes — takes Ronin 3.99s against GNU Make's 3.33s, where the same work as 1500 one-line recipes takes 1.42s. So the cost is real and it is the cost of doing what GNU Make does, and it does not show on a real build.\n\nGATES are recorded on make-simple-command-without-a-shell, which this shipped with."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:53:05.535323922Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Errata on the wall-time figure above, which was taken on a loaded host and reads worse than the truth. Re-run once the host was quiet, the real-project gate builds ninja 1.14.0.git from its own CMake Makefile in 21s at -j8 — the same figure make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch recorded before any of this, and the number to carry.\n\nWhat the loaded-host measurements are still good for is the comparison, because they were paired: the pre-change binary and this one alternating, each preceded by a clean, came out within two percent of each other while the same binary varied by sixty percent between runs. GNU Make 4.4.1 built the same tree in 42-43s in that window and Ronin in 44-46s, which is the reading that showed the load was the host's and not the change's.\n\nThe synthetic figure stands as measured and is the honest cost of the model: 1500 targets of three `@true` lines each, which is 4500 processes, takes Ronin 3.99s against GNU Make's 3.33s, where the same work written as 1500 one-line recipes takes 1.42s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:54:01.819532669Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-child-automatic-variables-are-child-relative
                title "Name a recursive child's prerequisites the way the child's own Makefile does"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:07:36.374689441Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch, and deliberately not folded into it: it is present before and after that change, because a recipe naming `$?` is one of the four kinds that still expand at construction and nothing about it moved.\n\nA recursive child's `$?` is answered with the names the shared graph holds, which are qualified by the child's path prefix, rather than with the names the child's own Makefile wrote.\n\n    all: ; @$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -C sub\n\n    # sub/Makefile\n    out: a b ; @printf 'newer=%s\\n' '$?' > report\n\nwith `a` and `b` present and `out` absent. GNU Make 4.4.1 writes `newer=a b`; Ronin writes `newer=sub/a sub/b`. The recipe runs with `cd sub` in front of it, so the value it is handed names files that are not there from where it stands — `$(wildcard $?)` in such a recipe finds nothing, and anything passed to a compiler is a path relative to the wrong directory.\n\nWhere it lives: a child unit's nodes are entered into the shared graph under `path_prefix`, and the automatic variables are read off those node names. The command's own `cd` is applied by `CommandLayout::prefix`, so the two disagree about which directory the names are relative to. `$@` and `$<` are worth probing at the same time — this was found through `$?` only, and the same question is asked of every automatic variable a child's recipe can name."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:18:00.393590024Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:18:30.689307154Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's own suspicion — that `$?` was one of several — is the part the probing overturned. Every other automatic variable was already right.\n\nTHE MATRIX, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and against Ronin before anything moved. A child invoked `-C sub`, `out: a b` with both present and `out` absent, all five read in one recipe:\n\n  $@   out      $<   a      $^   a b      $*  (empty)     all agreed before and after\n  $?   a b      Ronin gave `sub/a sub/b`                  THE ONE DIVERGENCE\n  $+   a b      $|   oo                                   agreed\n\nSo the value is not read off the graph's node names in general: a child unit's `DepNode`s carry the names its own Makefile wrote, and `$@`, `$<`, `$^`, `$+`, `$|` and `$*` are all answered from them inside the child's own session. `$?` alone is not, because it is the one automatic variable the compiler cannot answer — a recipe naming it is one of the four kinds that stay unexpanded until the scheduler has settled the prerequisites, so kati emits `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` and Ronin substitutes the names at launch. Those names come from the shared graph, where a child's node is entered under `path_prefix`.\n\nFOUR MORE CELLS, each probed rather than assumed, and each one a different answer:\n\n  cd sub && $(MAKE)          same defect, same fix: the unit is the unit however it was entered\n  an absolute prerequisite   `/abs/held a` in both tools; GNU never rewrites one\n  a prerequisite in a child's own subdirectory   `d/a` in both\n  a child of a child naming `../up`              GNU `../up`, RONIN `sub/up`\n\nThe last is why the fix is a relative path and not a prefix taken off the front. The child wrote `../up`, the graph resolved it against `sub/deep` and holds `sub/up`, and stripping `sub/deep` does not match — so a strip-only fix leaves that cell wrong while fixing the other three. The relative path from `sub/deep` to `sub/up` is `../up`, which is what GNU says, because GNU simply hands back the name the Makefile wrote and the two agree wherever the graph's normalisation was reversible.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, all in Ronin's executor: the value is composed there, so this is not a Make semantic kati can express. `DeferredFreshness` gains `new_inputs_directory`, `deferred_new_inputs_value` spells each name against it, and `relative_to` is the component walk that does it — no filesystem access, because both names come out of one graph and are already normalised against each other. A name that cannot be spelt from the directory at all keeps its own: an absolute prerequisite against a relative unit, or a `..` that would have to be resolved against the disk. The Make sink passes `self.unit.path_prefix`, which is empty for the root and for a child invoked without `-C`, so a build that never recursed pays nothing and answers exactly as before.\n\n`BuildGraph::set_deferred_freshness` grew past what a positional signature should carry, so its arguments are now `frontend::DeferredSpec` — the same shape the caller already had in `PendingDeferred`, and the two sink call sites became one `defer_freshness`.\n\nDeliberately NOT put in the graph comparison: the manifest cannot express it and does not need to. kati's Ninja writer keeps a recursive `$(MAKE)` line as a wrapper edge that spawns a nested Make rather than composing the child, so a manifest never holds a child unit's `$?` at all. Equivalence is unmoved at 0 disagreements either way.\n\nFIXTURES, four, all recorded from the oracle: recursive-child-question-names-are-child-relative (all five automatics in one line, so the four that were already right are held there too), recursive-child-question-after-a-cd-into-the-child, recursive-child-question-keeps-an-absolute-prerequisite (`$(CURDIR)/held`, with the recording's own `sed` making the absolute path comparable across machines), recursive-child-question-under-a-child-of-a-child (the `../up` cell). Unit tests build::deferred::tests::a_child_units_names_are_spelt_from_the_child and ::a_name_that_cannot_be_spelt_from_the_child_keeps_its_own.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery --no-deps clean and kati --all-targets -D warnings clean. Workspace tests 889 passed / 0 failed with --all-targets. make_port 4/4 over 801 cases, no existing recording rewritten. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing — unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1283 rows at 166/0/37/1080, no drift, compiler ZERO. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own CMake-generated Makefile — 541 recursive lines, the shape this node is about — in 29s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:53:57.388534369Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-vpath-found-name-merges-two-declared-targets
                title "Merge a target with the vpath name it was found under"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:35:47.920710376Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-vpath-prerequisite-resolves-to-a-target's acceptance matrix against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle; deliberately left out of it because it is a mechanism kati has no word for rather than a condition it gets wrong.\n\nReproducer, with an empty d/ and nothing on disk:\n\n  vpath %.te d/\n  all: fail.te\n  \t@printf 'all from %s\\n' '$<' > seen\n  fail.te: ; @echo madehere >> log; echo here > fail.te\n  d/fail.te: ; @echo madethere >> log\n\nGNU says, on stderr and without dying:\n\n  Makefile:4: Recipe was specified for file 'fail.te' at Makefile:4,\n  Makefile:4: but 'fail.te' is now considered the same file as 'd/fail.te'.\n  Makefile:4: Recipe for 'fail.te' will be ignored in favor of the one for 'd/fail.te'.\n\nthen runs `madethere` and leaves `all from d/fail.te`. Ronin runs the rule written here and leaves `all from fail.te`.\n\nBoth names are targets, so the search accepts the found one — that half is delivered and fixtured. What happens next is `rehash_file` (src/file.c:208): the two `struct file`s become one. The found name's record is retained, the searched name's recipe is discarded in its favour with the three-line diagnostic above, the two prerequisite chains are concatenated, and the variable set lists are merged. So the missing concept is target identity: two names the makefile declared separately turning into one object with one recipe.\n\nkati keys rules by name and has no rename or merge anywhere in the explicit-rule tables, so this is not a condition to add to `makefile_offers`. `at_vpath` currently returns None for any name with a rule of its own, which is why the merge is never reached; that guard is also the compile-time stand-in for `update_file_1`'s throwing-away, so whoever takes this has to settle it alongside make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target rather than before it.\n\nNo upstream row is known to turn on this. It is filed because it was found and measured, not because something is waiting on it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-upstream-failure-block-narration-unrecognised
                title "Teach the upstream classifier the launcher-free failure block"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:35:58.272009439Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while auditing make-vpath-prerequisite-resolves-to-a-target's row movement; pre-existing and not that change's.\n\n57 rows in tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv moved narration -> unclassified between c5c9eb0, which last recorded the file, and 1362509. Established rather than assumed: a run built at HEAD with the vpath change reverted gives 228 unclassified / 1 compiler / 41 interface / 1017 narration, and the recorded file said 171/1/41/1074. So the drift was already in the tree when this dispatch started, and the vpath change moved exactly one row on top of it.\n\nEvery one of the 57 carries `ninja-failure-block` and every one gained `evaluation` — the classifier's name for \"after taking off everything I recognise, something is left that I cannot name\". They span features/implicit_search (13), variables/MAKEFLAGS (28), functions/error, features/errors, features/output-sync, features/parallelism, features/patternrules, features/vpathplus, features/include, features/se_explicit, features/se_implicit, features/se_statpat, misc/general3 and options/dash-k.\n\nThe cause is 9294140, which stopped interposing a launcher between Ronin and the recipe so a signalled child could be told from `exit 143`. The FAILED block's command line changed shape with it. Now:\n\n  [1/1] exit 1\n  FAILED: [code=1] all\n  exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=' 'MAKELEVEL=1' 'MFLAGS=' /bin/sh -c \"exit 1\"\n  ronin: build stopped: subcommand failed.\n\nagainst GNU's single `make: *** [Makefile:1: all] Error 1`. The `exec env ... /bin/sh -c` spelling is what the `recipe-echo` and `shared-refusal` recognisers no longer see through, so what they used to strip now survives into the residue.\n\nThe work is in examples/make_upstream — the recognisers, not the compiler. Nothing about the build is wrong; the inventory has simply stopped being able to say so, and 57 rows of unnameable residue is 57 rows nobody can triage."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:20:59.639601541Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:21:37.080456322Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The 57 rows are back, and every one of them landed on the family it had before.\n\nWHAT THE LINE LOOKS LIKE NOW, read off a run rather than off the node. features/errors.diff.7, Ronin's side:\n\n  [1/1] (./barfooblatboz xx yy )\n  FAILED: [code=126] one\n  exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=' 'MAKELEVEL=1' 'MFLAGS=' /bin/sh -c \"(./barfooblatboz xx yy )\"\n  /bin/sh: 1: ./barfooblatboz: Permission denied\n\nThe banner and the stopped line were still recognised; the middle line was not, so the block's own echo of the command survived into the residue and `evaluation` — the family that means \"something is left I cannot name\" — was added on top. Across the whole run every `exec` line in the work directory is `exec env '...' ... /bin/sh -c`, in fourteen distinct MAKEFLAGS spellings; none is bare.\n\nTHE FIX is one line of `is_shell_invocation` in examples/make_upstream: `exec ` comes off before `env ` does, both by `strip_prefix` and each independently, so the recogniser reads `/bin/sh -c \"…\"` with either word in front of it, both, or neither. It is deliberately the same shape as the `env` fix that preceded it rather than a rewrite of it: the two are separate launcher changes and each is stripped where it is written. The test that pinned the `env` reading is now `the_block_reads_through_its_launcher` and pins four spellings plus two near-misses — `env | sort` and `exec 3< thing`, neither of which is a shell invocation.\n\nTHE AUDIT, which is the point of the node and was done by row rather than by count. Against the recording at c5c9eb0 — the last one written before the launcher drop — exactly 57 rows move under this change, and all 57 return to the exact class and family list they had there. Not \"57 rows moved and the count is right\": each row's `class` and its `+`-joined family list are equal to c5c9eb0's, checked pairwise. Nothing else moves.\n\n  1287 rows   unclassified 226 -> 169, compiler 0 -> 0, interface 41, narration 1020 -> 1077\n\n169 rather than c5c9eb0's 171 because two rows have been fixed since — features/statipattrules.diff.66 and .67, unclassified there and classified now — and c5c9eb0's single compiler row is features/vpath.diff.1, which make-vpath-prerequisite-resolves-to-a-target moved to narration. THE COMPILER BUCKET IS STILL ZERO and no row entered it.\n\nThe 57 span what the node said they did: variables/MAKEFLAGS (28), features/implicit_search (15), features/include (6), features/errors, functions/error, misc/general3, features/output-sync, features/parallelism, features/patternrules, features/se_explicit, features/se_implicit, features/se_statpat, features/vpathplus, options/dash-k.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 803 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, 856 with --all-targets; the classifier's own 19 tests pass. No product code was touched — this is the classifier reading a line it already produced."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:32:56.589291299Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-static-pattern-escaped-percent
                title "Read a static rule's target pattern with the escaping resolved"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:51:18.841118293Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while establishing make-mixed-implicit-and-normal-rule-severity's matrix against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle; left out of it because it is the same reading deciding a different question.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    a b: \\%.o: %.c ; @echo \"ran[$@]\" >> log\n\nGNU: `Makefile:1: *** target pattern contains no '%'.  Stop.`, exit 2.\nRonin: two warnings that `a' and `b' do not match the target pattern, then it builds `a` and exits 0.\n\n`\\%` is an escaped percent, so `\\%.o` is the literal name `%.o` and carries no wildcard at all. A static pattern rule whose target pattern has no wildcard is not a rule, and GNU refuses it on exactly that ground. Ronin asks `is_pattern_rule` — `memchr(b'%')` — which cannot tell an escaped percent from a wildcard, so it accepts the pattern, builds a `Pattern` whose own `find_percent` then reduces it to the literal `%.o`, and reports that neither target matches it.\n\nThe fix is the same one the severity node made on the rule-target side: rule.rs's `is_pattern_rule(&target_pattern...)` becomes `find_percent(...)`'s answer, and the reduced text is what `Pattern::new` should be handed rather than re-deriving. `strutil::find_percent` is already public after that node.\n\nWorth checking while fixing, because both are the same call site's neighbours: `dep.rs`'s `.PRECIOUS` split also uses `is_pattern_rule` over a name, and `.PRECIOUS: \\%.o` should name a file rather than a pattern. Not probed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:49:54.352501486Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:01:57.29385545Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. One refusal site, and the neighbour the filing asked to check turned out to be a second reading of the same question.\n\nTHE MATRIX, probed cell by cell against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before anything moved, GNU on the left.\n\n  a b: \\%.o: %.c            *** target pattern contains no '%'.  Stop., exit 2, nothing built\n                            Ronin warned twice that `a' and `b' did not match, built `a', exited 0\n  a.o b.o: %\\%.o: %.c       target 'a.o' doesn't match... twice, THEN BUILDS a.o, exit 0 — agreed\n  .PRECIOUS: \\%.b           names a file: an intermediate `x.b` is still swept up — agreed\n  .PRECIOUS: %.b            names a family: `x.b` is kept — agreed\n  a b: \\%.o                 an ordinary rule; the prerequisite stays `\\%.o`, backslash included — agreed\n\nGNU asks the question in exactly one place, read.c:1306: `pattern_percent = find_percent_cached (&target->name)` immediately after the missing/multiple-pattern refusals, then `if (pattern_percent == 0)` is fatal. `find_percent` is not \"is there a percent here\" — a backslash escapes the wildcard and `\\%.o` is the one name `%.o`, so a static rule written that way stands for no family and is not a rule at all. Ronin asked `is_pattern_rule`, which was `memchr(b'%')`, and could not tell the two apart.\n\nTHE SECOND CELL, which the filing flagged as worth checking and which was wrong for the same reason. `.PRECIOUS: \\%.b` was routed into the pattern list, where it became a pattern with no wildcard — matching the single literal name `%.b` — instead of into the file list under the name the makefile wrote. Both tools happen to sweep up `x.b` either way, so the divergence needs a file actually called `%.b` or `\\%.b` to show, but the classification was GNU's to make and it makes it the other way.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, in kati a8fc764 on branch nplan/make-static-pattern-escaped-percent. `Pattern::is_pattern` is `percent_index.is_some()` — the answer `Pattern::new` already computed and threw away — so rule.rs builds the Pattern once and reads the refusal off it rather than asking a second, weaker question first, and dep.rs's `.PRECIOUS` split asks the same one. `rule::is_pattern_rule` had no callers left and is gone. The reduced text is deliberately NOT written back into `output_patterns`: every downstream reader takes the raw symbol through `Pattern::new`, and `find_percent` is not idempotent (`\\\\%.o` reduces to `\\%.o`, which reduces again to a literal `%.o`), so reducing at the store would corrupt exactly the doubled-backslash cell it was meant to serve.\n\nFIXTURES, four, all recorded from the oracle. static-pattern-escaped-percent-is-not-a-pattern is the node's own reproducer, written so it gates: the refusal's evidence is that `log` is never written. static-pattern-escaped-percent-stops-the-read puts a reachable goal above the offending line, so a tool that only warns builds `made` and a tool that refuses does not. static-pattern-later-escaped-percent-is-still-a-pattern holds the `%\\%.o` cell, where the first percent is the wildcard, the targets do not match it, and GNU reads on and builds anyway. precious-escaped-percent-names-a-file is the guard on the second site.\n\nUnit test strutil::tests::a_text_is_a_pattern_only_while_a_wildcard_survives_the_escaping over both directions of the escaping.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-escaped-percent-prerequisite-pattern-keeps-its-backslash. GNU's `enter_prereqs` (file.c:476) compresses the escaping into a COPY and throws the copy away when no wildcard survived — `percent = find_percent (nm); if (percent) ...` — so a prerequisite pattern that is not a pattern reaches the build exactly as written. Ronin's `substitute_stem` returns the reduced text unconditionally. `a.o b.o: %.o: \\%.c` makes GNU look for `\\%.c` and Ronin for `%.c`, two different files, and with both on disk the two tools build from different sources. The implicit-rule counterpart (`%.o: \\%.c`) diverges further and differently — GNU refuses the target outright — so it is a node rather than a line.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery --no-deps clean and kati --all-targets -D warnings clean. Workspace tests 834 passed / 0 failed, 887 with --all-targets. make_port 4/4 over 797 cases, no existing recording rewritten. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing — the baseline, unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1283 rows at 166 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1080 narration, no drift: not one row moved, and the compiler bucket stays ZERO."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:53:52.42627157Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-release-port-gate-has-no-source
                title "Settle the release gate's port check against a source that was retired"
                kind @chore
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:32:17.753599704Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while making the release script's clippy step runnable (make-release-clippy-runnable). Pre-existing, unrelated to lints, and established at this dispatch's baseline rather than assumed: a detached worktree at 676dd32 gives byte-identical output.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` line 27 is `nplan port check --wave 4`, and it refuses:\n\n  Wave 1 · 170 symbols · markup 0/170 · translate 0/170 · tests 0/170\n  [✗] wave-1-markup: 0/170 symbols marked up (def/sem rule written + source annotation)\n\nThe 170 symbols are the C samurai Ronin was ported from. `plan/.port-manifest.styx` still carries them with `source_lang c` and file/line spans into `build.c`, `deps.c`, `htab.c`, `parse.c` and the rest — `{id build.isdirty-fn, ... file build.c, line_start 56, line_end 107}`. `.config/nspec/config.styx` says what happened to those files, in its own opening comment: \"Ronin is the implementation. The C corpus it was ported from has been retired from this repository, so the source impl that tracked those symbols is gone with it.\" It declares one impl, `target-impl`, over Rust.\n\n`nplan port status` shows the consequence for every one of the 170 identically — rule ✓, source annotation ·, target ✓, verification ✓. Three of the four columns are full. The empty one is the annotation in a C file that is not in the repository and cannot be, so the gate cannot be satisfied by doing the work it asks for.\n\nSo this is not 170 symbols of missing work; it is a gate outliving its subject. Whoever takes it has to decide which, and the decision is not obvious enough to take in passing:\n\n  - retire the manifest, if the port is finished and the ledger is a record rather than a gate;\n  - keep it and take the line out of the release script, if `nplan port` is still how the port's history is read but is no longer something a release asserts;\n  - or teach the adoption config that the source side is gone, if nplan can express a port whose source has been retired.\n\nThe rest of the script is green either side of it: everything before reaches the equivalence gate, and every step after was run by hand and passes — pinned Ninja conformance 425/425, the performance gate on all seven workloads, `nplan spec uncovered`, `nplan spec stale`, `nplan lint` and `nplan audit`. This one line is the whole of what stops `scripts/check-release.sh` running end to end."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-release-pedantic-in-the-test-scope
                title "Bring the harnesses up to the standard the product code is held to"
                kind @refactor
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:32:30.016920983Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by make-release-clippy-runnable, which scoped around this rather than fixing it, and measured exactly what \"this\" is.\n\nThe release script now lints in three passes: Ronin's lib and bins at the pedantic and nursery standard, everything else of Ronin's at the ordinary one, and the vendored fork at the ordinary one. The middle pass is where the groups come off, and it is the only place in the tree where Ronin's declared standard — `[lints.clippy] nursery = \"warn\", pedantic = \"warn\"` in Cargo.toml — is not applied to Ronin's own code.\n\nFifteen findings stand between the middle pass and the first's standard. `cargo clippy --all-targets --no-deps -- -D warnings`, which is the declared standard with nothing added:\n\n  tests/support/oracle.rs:180    struct_field_names — `Answers { answers: BTreeMap<..> }`\n  tests/support/oracle.rs:234    assigning_clones — `into.host = rest.to_owned()`\n  tests/support/oracle.rs:293-5  assigning_clones ×3, the same shape in `parse`\n  tests/make_port.rs:169         option_if_let_else over a two-arm match on a sidecar note\n  examples/make_conformance.rs   the five above again, through its `#[path]` include of oracle.rs\n  src/make/equivalence.rs:669    option_if_let_else\n  src/make/equivalence.rs:671    `Err(_)` matches all errors\n\nThe last two decide the shape of the work and are the reason this is a node rather than a paragraph in that one. They sit inside a `#[cfg(test)]` module in src/, so `--lib` never compiles them and only the lib TEST target reaches them; the harness-only dispatch that found them was told not to touch src/, and with them left standing the middle pass could not be raised however clean tests/ became.\n\nWorth carrying forward rather than re-deciding: the `assigning_clones` suggestion is `rest.clone_into(&mut into.host)` for `into.host = rest.to_owned()`, in a parser that runs three times over a provenance record. Taking it would make the code worse to read and no faster in any measurable sense, so the answer there is probably to restructure the parse — it is building a struct field by field from a `match` and could return the value instead — rather than to take the lint's word for it. `struct_field_names` is the same kind of question: `Answers.answers` holds the default-origin variables and `defaults` is what `Provenance` already calls them.\n\nDone when `cargo clippy --all-targets --no-deps -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery` is green and the middle pass in scripts/check-release.sh loses its two `-A` flags."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags
                title "Forgive an option argument a makefile's MAKEFLAGS cannot supply"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:22:16.903290859Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags, and deliberately left out of it: that node is about a switch Make does not KNOW, and this is about a switch it knows whose argument it cannot read. Both are GNU's one `bad` flag, but they are two behaviours and only the first was this dispatch's sentence.\n\nGNU's mechanism, read rather than inferred. main.c `decode_switches` sets `bad = 1` for anything getopt refuses and for a `case string:` whose argument is empty, and then `if (bad && origin == o_command) print_usage (bad)` — so a makefile's own MAKEFLAGS (decoded at `o_env` after the read) survives all of it. The two halves differ in whether anything is PRINTED: `opterr = origin == o_command` silences getopt's own message, but the empty-string-argument `error (NILF, 0, ...)` call is unconditional and prints even for a makefile.\n\nThe matrix, against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle, all with `MAKEFLAGS += ` in a makefile and now agreeing except where marked:\n\n  -zz, --frobnicate, --=x, -rz     ignored, silent, rc 0        FIXED under the unknown-switch node\n  -O bogus, -j abc, -l abc         switch takes its no-arg default, the word is dropped   FIXED\n  -W                               rc 0, MAKEFLAGS empty        Ronin: rc 2, `missing -W value`\n  --jobserver-style=               rc 0, message printed, build goes on   Ronin: rc 2, refuses\n  --debug=zzz                      FATAL in both, rc 2          already agrees — decode_debug_flags runs after decode_switches and dies whatever the origin\n  --shuffle=bogus                  rc 0, kept verbatim in MAKEFLAGS as ` --shuffle=bogus`   Ronin: rc 2, `invalid shuffle mode`\n\nTwo reproducers, both one line:\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -W\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --jobserver-style=\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\nGNU builds both. Ronin refuses both.\n\n`--shuffle=bogus` is a third thing again and probably a separate node: GNU never validates the mode in this path at all, it stores the string and publishes it, so replicating it means deciding where Ronin's shuffle validation belongs rather than forgiving anything.\n\nWhere a fix goes. Ronin's `parse_arguments` has two failure channels and only one of them is now source-aware: `refuse(...)` returns `Ok(Some(Action::Immediate))` and is guarded by `ArgumentSource::refuses_an_unknown_switch`, while a missing or unreadable option argument returns `Err(Error)` from `value()`/`OutputSync::parse` and propagates with `?` from a dozen call sites. Making the second channel forgiving means either giving `value()` the source, or catching the error per word at the top of the loop — the latter is closer to GNU, which forgives a whole word and moves to the next, but it has to leave `index` and the partly-applied cluster in a defined state."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:32:39.531980059Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "One more cell for this node's matrix, found while closing make-shuffle-mode-is-validated-where-gnu-does-not and belonging here rather than there.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=\n    all: ; @printf 'MF=[$(MAKEFLAGS)]\\n'\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, builds, MAKEFLAGS empty — and prints\n               `make: the '--shuffle' option requires a non-empty string argument` on stderr first\n    Ronin      rc 0, builds, MAKEFLAGS empty, silent\n\nSo the outcome now agrees and only the diagnostic is missing. It is the same `bad` flag this node is about: `decode_switches` complains about an empty `case string:` argument at every origin and only `print_usage` is guarded by `origin == o_command`, which is why the command line dies with the message plus usage while a makefile gets the message alone and carries on.\n\nWorth having beside the node's existing `-W` and `--jobserver-style=` cells, because the three together show the shape: GNU always says something, and the origin decides only whether it then stops."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-delete-on-error-races-a-secondary
                title "Delete a failed target before the run ends, every time"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:22:34.871530373Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A build-intent gate flapper, measured and shown to pre-date the dispatch that found it. Not a test defect: the test is right and the product is racing.\n\n`tests/make/target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary` fails intermittently with\n\n    target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary: 'hello.z' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file\n\nThe case is `.DELETE_ON_ERROR:` with `.SECONDARY: hello.z`, and a pattern recipe `cat $< > $@ ; exit 1` that writes the target and then fails. GNU deletes `hello.z`; Ronin sometimes leaves it.\n\nMeasured rather than assumed, invoking through a `make`-named link both times so the multicall path is the same:\n\n  working tree with the four-node dispatch applied   8 of 30 runs left hello.z\n  clean checkout of ronin dd47353, kati d49463e      7 of 30 runs left hello.z\n\nand at the gate level, `cargo test --release --test make_port` in a clean dd47353 worktree failed 1 run in 4 with exactly this case. So the rate is roughly a quarter to a third of runs, it is the same before and after, and nothing in that dispatch could reach it: the Ronin change was confined to `ArgumentSource` and the makefile MAKEFLAGS decoder, the kati changes to the assignment-operator scan, `MAKEFILES`, and the export attribute. This makefile assigns nothing, has no `$` adjacent to an operator, and never mentions MAKEFLAGS.\n\nWorth knowing before fixing: `ronin --make` rather than the `make`-named link left the file 0 times in 30, which is either a real difference in the two entry paths or a timing difference that hides the race. That is the first thing to settle, because it says whether the deletion is racing the process exit or racing another edge.\n\nThe suspicion the numbers point at is the deletion competing with the failure path that ends the build — a target deleted on error is removed by whoever notices the recipe failed, and if the run is torn down first the removal never happens. `.SECONDARY` is in the case because an intermediate is normally removed anyway, so the case is specifically about the delete-on-error removal being the one that has to happen.\n\nUntil it is fixed this poisons any full-gate run at about one in four, and it will be misread as caused by whatever dispatch is in flight — as it nearly was here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:31:49.865709494Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:40:27.747284227Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Not a race at all, and now not a flapper either: a certainty whose trigger is a coincidence. The withdrawal was comparing against a timestamp the file had never had.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, caught in the act rather than reasoned about. `recompute_dirty` (src/graph.rs) stands an absent invented intermediate's place by writing the newest input's timestamp onto the *output's own* mtime — `for output in &edge_data.out { runtime.node_mut(*output).set_mtime(newest_input) }` — which is what hides its absence from whatever reads it. `prepare_edge` then took `old_mtimes` from exactly that field, and `withdraw_outputs` withdraws an output only when `disk.stat(path) != old_mtime`. So the question asked after the recipe failed was \"does hello.z's timestamp differ from hello.x's?\", not \"did the recipe write hello.z?\".\n\nThe two are the same file's timestamp whenever the chain finishes inside one filesystem timestamp tick, because Linux stamps mtimes from the timer tick rather than from the clock. Measured on this host: 4,000 files written in a tight loop carry 64 distinct mtimes, the smallest gap 4.000046ms — CONFIG_HZ=250. `echo body > hello.x` and `cat hello.x > hello.z` are two trivial recipes; whether they land in the same 4ms is the whole coin flip. That is why the rate moves with host speed and load between one in four and one in a thousand, why it is not dispatch-correlated, and why it looks like a race while being deterministic given the timestamps.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. Instrumented `withdraw_outputs` and stressed the fixture until it failed, keeping only the failing runs' traces. Five caught in 12,000 runs, and every one says the same thing:\n\n    withdraw which=DeleteOnError eligible=Some(1) outputs=1 old_mtimes=1\n      path=hello.z old=1786832265606120083 seen=Some(1786832265606120083)\n\nThe withdrawal ran, the output was eligible, `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` was in force, and the guard refused because the two numbers are equal to the nanosecond. The directory listing beside it shows hello.x, hello.z and .ninja_log all stamped 00:17:45.606120083 — three writes at three different moments on one tick. Four of the five failures landed at that same instant in four independent lanes, which is the tick boundary, not a scheduler interleaving. Nothing else was ever implicated: status was `unix_wait_status(256)` (an ordinary exit 1), no signal, no global interrupt, `command_finished` returned Ok.\n\nTHE FIX, in src/build.rs. `mtimes_the_outputs_hold(edge, outputs)` now answers what `prepare_edge` used to read off the node. For an ordinary edge it is the scan's answer, unchanged. For an edge whose outputs were substituted — `runtime.edge(edge).absent_intermediate()`, the flag the substitution already records — it asks the disk, because that edge is precisely the one that has lost the output's own timestamp. A stat that fails answers MISSING, which withdraws whatever is found afterwards: the safe direction for a file a stopped recipe may have half-written. The cost is one stat per output on the edges that substituted and nothing at all on any other. `old_mtimes` also feeds `unchanged_outputs`, and that reading is fixed by the same change: an output that went from absent to written is now reported as changed rather than as unchanged-by-coincidence.\n\nGNU Make was consulted rather than guessed at. `delete_target` compares `stat` against `file->last_mtime`, which for a file that was not there is NONEXISTENT — a value no real file can hold. GNU has no stand-in to be confused by, and it deletes the file in both shapes of the case.\n\nTHE FLAPPER IS NOW A GATE. tests/make/target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary-stamped-like-its-prerequisite is the same case with `touch -r $< $@` added to the recipe, so the collision the tick used to supply by chance is made deliberate and happens on every host at every speed. Recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle through the provenance-enforcing harness, which prints `*** Deleting file 'hello.z'` and leaves none. Against the tree with the fix removed the new case fails every time, with the node's own words — `'hello.z' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`. The original probabilistic case stays where it is.\n\nSTABILITY.\n  target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary                              30 of 30 green\n  target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary-stamped-like-its-prerequisite 30 of 30 green\n  the original fixture under the stress that caught the defect            0 of 12,000 left hello.z\n    (the identical stress before the fix: 5 of 12,000, and 10 of 1,500 in an earlier window)\n  cargo test --release --test make_port                                   5 of 5 green, 741 cases\n  full workspace suite                                                    3 of 3 green\n\nThe whole signal and delete-on-error matrix is inside those make_port runs: twelve signal cases and the twenty-two `target-delete-on-error-*` and `secondary-*` cases all pass in each of the five.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean — the fix first put `prepare_edge` one line over the density gate at 102, which is why the answer is a named function rather than an `allow` — and kati `-D warnings` clean. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing, the baseline unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1287 rows at 168 unclassified / 0 compiler / 41 interface / 1078 narration, unchanged and with compiler still zero; the .tsv is byte-identical.\n\nTWO THINGS THE FILING GOT WRONG, recorded so the next reader does not repeat them. `ronin --make` is not a way to invoke Make mode — the front end is chosen by the invoked name alone, so that command prints usage and exits 2 having built nothing. \"left the file 0 times in 30\" was measuring a run that never happened, and the same trap voided a re-measurement here until a properly named link was used. And the defect does not pre-date the dispatch in the sense of being someone else's: it pre-dates it in time, but it was always this, and no amount of looking at the withdrawal path for a concurrent interleaving would have found it, because there is none. -j1 and -j8 fail at the same rate.\n\nFOUND WHILE MEASURING and filed rather than fixed: make-port-harness-scratch-is-shared-between-runs. Two `make_port` harness processes in one checkout write the same `target/make-port-work/<case>` directories and corrupt each other — measured, two concurrent runs both failed, one on a doubled log and one on a setup step. Nothing runs it that way today, but a case failing for that reason would read exactly like a product defect, which is the cost this node just paid in a different currency."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:46:09.189992583Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffle-mode-is-validated-where-gnu-does-not
                title "Keep a shuffle mode GNU Make never validates"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:23:12.651571574Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags and kept out of it, and out of make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags too, because it is not the `bad` flag at all: GNU never rejects this value, so there is nothing to forgive.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=bogus\n    all: ; @printf 'MF=[$(MAKEFLAGS)]\\n' > out\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, no diagnostic, out holds `MF=[ --shuffle=bogus]`\n    Ronin      rc 2, `ronin: invalid shuffle mode: Invalid value: 'bogus'`, nothing built\n\nGNU takes `--shuffle` as a `case string:` switch, stores whatever it is given, and publishes it again in MAKEFLAGS unexamined — the mode is only interpreted where the shuffle is actually performed, so a value nothing ever shuffles by is never looked at. Ronin validates it in `Shuffle::requested` at parse time, in both option streams.\n\nThe command line is the cell that decides how much of this is worth matching, and it was NOT probed here — check `make --shuffle=bogus` against the oracle before choosing. If GNU also builds there, the fix is to stop validating at parse time and carry the string; if GNU refuses on the command line and not from a makefile, then this really is the `bad`/origin distinction after all and it belongs with the sibling node.\n\nAlso unprobed and cheap to settle at the same time: whether GNU's published `--shuffle=bogus` reaches a child make, and what that child then does with it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:17:50.228155211Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:32:29.062424358Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The command-line cell the filing asked for was probed first, and it decided the shape of the fix: GNU DOES refuse there. `make --shuffle=bogus` is `make: *** invalid shuffle mode: Invalid value: 'bogus'.  Stop.`, rc 2, and an environment `MAKEFLAGS=--shuffle=bogus` refuses identically. So this is the origin distinction after all, and it is the same mechanism as make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags — reached from the other side.\n\nWHERE THE BEHAVIOUR LIVES, and it is a position in `main` rather than a flag. main.c:1648:\n\n    /* Handle shuffle mode argument.  */\n    if (shuffle_mode)\n      { shuffle_set_mode (shuffle_mode); ... shuffle_mode = xstrdup (effective_mode); }\n\n`shuffle_set_mode` is the only place a mode is ever read, and it runs ONCE, after `decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_command)` at 1603 and before `read_all_makefiles`. The second decode of the same variable — `decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_env)` at 2091 — happens after it and never reaches the block again. `--shuffle` is a `case string:` switch (main.c:502), so a makefile's word simply replaces the table entry, and `define_makeflags` republishes whatever the table holds. Nothing looks at it, nothing reorders by it, and nothing re-applies the command line over it afterwards.\n\nThree consequences the probe confirmed, and only the first was in the filing:\n\n  1. A word naming no mode is not an error at that origin.\n  2. The word is republished VERBATIM, where the command line's is republished as the permutation it settled on. `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=random` publishes `random`; `make --shuffle=random` publishes a seed.\n  3. The makefile's word gets the LAST word on the table. `make --shuffle=reverse` with `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=none` in the makefile builds in reverse and publishes ` --shuffle=none`.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Command line and inherited environment, where nothing moved and nothing was allowed to: `--shuffle=bogus` fatal rc 2 at both origins; `random` and bare `--shuffle` settle on a seed and publish it; `reverse`, `identity`, a digit seed publish verbatim; `none` publishes nothing; `--shuffle=` refuses. Makefile origin, where every cell moved:\n\n  bogus     GNU ` --shuffle=bogus`        was rc 2      now agrees\n  BoGuS     GNU ` --shuffle=BoGuS`        was rc 2      now agrees, case kept\n  random    GNU ` --shuffle=random`       was a seed    now agrees\n  bare      GNU ` --shuffle=random`       was a seed    now agrees\n  none      GNU ` --shuffle=none`         was empty     now agrees\n  42        GNU ` --shuffle=42`           agreed        still agrees\n  reverse   GNU ` --shuffle=reverse`, and the run is NOT reordered   agreed on both  still agrees\n  empty     GNU publishes nothing and builds             was rc 2     now agrees\n  cl reverse + makefile none   GNU builds p3 p2 p1 and publishes ` --shuffle=none`\n                                                         was ` --shuffle=reverse`   now agrees\n\nDELIVERED, Ronin only — kati's shuffle machinery is untouched, because this is entirely about which stream a switch arrived on. `Invocation` had one field doing two jobs: the mode this run shuffles by and the word `MAKEFLAGS` republishes. They are the same only when the command line wrote it, so `shuffle_spelling` is now its own field and `read_shuffle` decides what a stream does with the argument via `ArgumentSource::shuffle_effect`.\n\nThe third answer is what finding 3 forced. `decode_makefile_makeflags` reads three streams — the persistent table, the makefile's assignment, and the command line and environment re-asserted over the top so a switch typed on the command line outranks one a makefile took away. That last stream had been reading as `Makefile`, and re-asserting the command line's `--shuffle=reverse` over the makefile's `none` is exactly what GNU does not do. `ArgumentSource::Protection` names it, `ShuffleEffect::Ignored` is its answer for this switch, and every switch that acts is protected as before — verified with `-k` beside a makefile's bogus shuffle.\n\n`evaluated_invocation` also moved to the makefile origin. It reads the settled `MAKEFLAGS` back to configure the one Ninja scheduler; that text is the switch table as the makefiles left it, so a word GNU accepted there without examining has to survive being read back.\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded. a-shuffle-mode-a-makefiles-makeflags-names (bogus, republished, build succeeds), a-shuffle-word-a-makefile-writes-travels-as-written (`random` stays the word), a-shuffle-a-makefile-takes-away-still-orders-the-build (the command line's reverse still orders p3 p2 p1 while the table publishes `none` — both facts in one case), and a-shuffle-mode-on-the-command-line-is-refused, the control that must not move. One unit test, a_makefiles_shuffle_word_is_republished, pins the four table cells directly.\n\nGATES. fmt, check, clippy three passes, doc, workspace tests 867/0, make_port 4/0 over 770 cases, Make conformance 318/69 unmoved, equivalence 297/278/0, upstream inventory unmoved at 1283 rows and 167/0/37/1079 with compiler zero, Ninja conformance 425 upstream plus every companion suite, real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 45s at -j8.\n\nFILED RATHER THAN FOLDED IN: at the makefile origin GNU still PRINTS `the '--shuffle' option requires a non-empty string argument` for `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=` and then builds, while Ronin is silently right about the outcome. That is the same shape as make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags, which is already filed for a switch Make knows whose argument it cannot read and already names \"whether anything is printed\" as its open half. Noted there rather than opened again."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:33:50.643831058Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-semicolon-does-not-stop-an-assignment-scan
                title "Read an assignment whose name holds a semicolon"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:23:29.249015498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while fixing make-assignment-operator-scan-skips-a-reference, and adjacent to it rather than part of it: that node gave kati's operator scan the reference skip GNU's has, and this is a second, independent way the same scan disagrees with GNU's.\n\n    a;b=c\n    all: ; @echo ok\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, builds — the line defines a variable named `a;b`\n    Ronin      rc 2, `ronin: Makefile:1: missing separator.`\n\nGNU's `parse_variable_definition` (reference/gnumake/src/variable.c) has no case for `;` at all: it is not in `STOP_SET (c, MAP_COMMENT|MAP_NUL)`, it is not blank, it is not `=` or `:`, and `*p` is not `=`, so it falls through `other:` and the walk continues to the `=` after it. kati looks for the first of `:`, `=` and `;` with `find_outside_reference(line, b\":=;\")` and treats a `;` it reaches first as the rule-and-recipe separator, so the line is handed to `parse_rule` and dies for having no colon.\n\nThe `;` in that pattern set is doing real work for rule lines — `all: foo ; recipe` is one — so it cannot simply be dropped. What GNU does is ask the assignment question first, over the whole line and without `;` being special, and only parse a rule if the answer is no; kati dispatches on whichever of the three characters comes first. Making the two agree means separating the two questions rather than tuning the character set.\n\nNot probed, and worth settling before fixing: whether a `;` before a `:` changes which of the two a line is (`a;b: c` — rule or refusal), and whether a target-specific assignment after a `;` behaves the same way.\n\nThe neighbouring paren cell was probed and the two tools already agree on it: `a(b:c)d=e` builds in both, though for different reasons — GNU declines it as a variable definition (the `:` sets `end`, the next character is not `=` or `:`, so the walk returns NULL) while kati's scan skips the parenthesised group wholesale and finds the `=`. Agreement by two different routes is worth knowing about if this scan is ever reworked."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:27:14.742586994Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:39:25.372900089Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by separating the two questions, which is what the filing said the fix had to be, and the unprobed cells it named both turned out to already agree.\n\nThe rule, read rather than inferred. GNU Make asks \"does this line define a variable\" first, over the whole line, and parses a rule only when the answer is no — `eval` calls `parse_var_assignment`, which loops `parse_variable_definition` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:517, variable.c:1610). That scan has no case for `;`: it is not in `STOP_SET (c, MAP_COMMENT|MAP_NUL)`, it is not blank, it is not `=` or `:`, and `*p` is not `=`, so it reaches `other:` and continues. The `;` only becomes the rule-and-recipe separator in the SECOND question, where GNU cuts the line at the first unquoted one and then looks for the colon in what is left.\n\nThat second half is what answers the filing's first unprobed cell. `a;b: c` is `missing separator` in GNU, not a rule — the line is cut at the `;`, leaving `a`, and `a` has no colon. Ronin already agreed, and now agrees for GNU's reason rather than by luck.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Every cell now agrees:\n\n  a;b=c            defines `a;b`, recursive, origin file   / missing separator\n  x;y := z         defines `x;y`, simple                   / missing separator\n  a;b += c         appends to `a;b`                        / missing separator\n  a;b ?= c         conditional, declines                   / missing separator\n  a;b != echo c    shell assignment                        / missing separator\n  a;b:::= c        `:::=`, which $(flavor) calls recursive / missing separator\n  override a;b     overrides `a;b`                         / missing separator\n  export a;b       exports `a;b`                           / missing separator\n  define a;b       defines `a;b`                           / missing separator\n  undefine a;b     origin becomes undefined                / missing separator\n  a;b: c           missing separator                       already agreed — the recipe took the colon\n  a; b = c         missing separator                       already agreed — a blank ends the name, and a second word before the operator ends the question\n  foo ; bar=baz    missing separator                       already agreed, same reason\n  all: a;b=c       rule `all: a` with recipe `b=c`         already agreed\n  all: v;w=set     the same as a target-specific line      already agreed — the filing's second unprobed cell\n  all: dep ; recipe                                        already agreed, and must keep agreeing\n  a(b:c)d=e        builds in both                          already agreed, still by two different routes\n\nShape delivered, all of it in kati and all of it one character wide. `parse_rule_or_assign` and `is_variable_definition` scan with `b\":=\"` rather than `b\":=;\"`, and the `s.starts_with(b\";\")` arm that dispatched to `parse_rule` is gone with it — it was the only thing that made a `;` an answer to the first question. `parse_define` was already scanning for `b\"=\"` alone and is untouched. Nothing about the name side moved: `is_variable_name` still refuses a blank at depth zero, which is GNU's `end` rule and is why the three `missing separator` cells above stay that way.\n\nTwo fixtures recorded from the oracle: an-assignment-name-may-hold-a-semicolon (seven names holding a semicolon across `=`, `+=`, `:=`, `?=`, `!=`, `override`, `export` and `define`, with `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` read beside the values because the value agrees in cells where the flavour would not) and a-semicolon-before-a-colon-is-a-missing-separator (the direction that must not move). Unit test parser::tests::a_semicolon_does_not_stop_an_assignment_scan holds the same matrix at the predicate, both ways.\n\nOwned markers: none, as the filing said — no upstream row reads this. 1287 rows unmoved at 167/0/41/1079, compiler 0.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 861 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; kati unit tests 166 passed; clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the two new recordings; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 50s at -j8.\n\nkati commit aa93390 on nplan/make-semicolon-does-not-stop-an-assignment-scan.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:40:59.105793643Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-port-harness-scratch-is-shared-between-runs
                title "Give each make_port run a scratch directory of its own"
                kind @test
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:40:56.56037712Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while diagnosing make-delete-on-error-races-a-secondary, and kept out of it because it is the harness rather than the product.\n\n`scratch_for` (tests/make_port.rs) names a case's working directory `target/make-port-work/<case id>` and nothing else. The name has no run in it, so two harness processes in one checkout are one directory: each removes the other's tree mid-run, copies its own Makefile over it, and reads back a listing made of both.\n\nMeasured, two runs of the same test binary started together in one worktree, both of which failed:\n\n  'log' holds \"src\\nsrc\\ngen\\ngen\\n\" where GNU Make left \"src\\ngen\\n\"   (a-makefile-ahead-of-a-refused-one-is-remade)\n  absent-prerequisite-with-a-rule: setup failed                          (the other process had just removed the tree under it)\n\nNothing runs it that way today — `cargo test` takes one binary at a time and the corpus loop is sequential within it — so this is latent rather than live. It is worth closing anyway because of what it costs when it does happen: a case that fails this way reads exactly like a product defect, and one of them was investigated as one. The scheduler-timing node already made this exact repair on the other side of the suite, and the shape of the answer is recorded there — `fixture_directory` puts `SystemTime::now()` nanos beside the pid and the counter and creates with `fs::create_dir` rather than `create_dir_all`, so a name that already exists is one to walk away from. The same treatment here, plus the sweep of stale entries that came with it.\n\nNote that the pid alone is not enough, which is the other half of that node's finding: under `scripts/sandboxed` every run gets its own pid namespace and pids start again from the same small numbers."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-position-not-the-edge
                title "Unlink the walked position rather than the circular edge under a shuffle"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:45:37.546523846Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-shuffle-reaches-the-circular-drop, and left out of it deliberately: that node made the shuffle choose which edge closes the loop, and this is the one cell where GNU Make then removes a different entry from the one it just named.\n\nGNU's walk keeps two chains over the same prerequisites — `->next`, written order, and `->shuf`, the shuffled one — and `update_file_1` reads the file through the shuffled chain while unlinking through the written one (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:596 onward):\n\n    d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du;\n    ...\n    if (is_updating (d->file))\n      {\n        error (NILF, \"Circular %s <- %s dependency dropped.\", file->name, d->file->name);\n        if (lastd == 0) file->deps = du->next; else lastd->next = du->next;\n        du = du->next;\n        continue;\n      }\n\nSo the message names `d` and the list loses `du`. With no shuffle the two are the same entry and nothing shows; with one they are not.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: t\n    t: x t\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^]\"\n    x: ; @echo x\n\n  make                     x, `Circular t <- t dependency dropped.`, `t hat=[x]`, exit 0 — both agree\n  make --shuffle=reverse   `Circular t <- t dependency dropped.`, x, GNU `t hat=[t]` / Ronin `t hat=[x]`\n\nBoth build x and both build t, so nothing gates on it and no upstream row reads it; the difference is only in what `$^` is left holding. GNU keeps the sibling's name and drops the circular one from the list it walks; Ronin drops the circular one, which is the entry the message named.\n\nWhether to match it is a real decision rather than an oversight. It reads as a stale-pointer artefact of carrying two chains — file.c:681 says outright that \"shuffle mode assumes '->next' and '->shuf' links both traverse the same dependencies\", and this drop is what breaks that assumption — but it is what 4.4.1 does. Matching it means `DepNode` carrying the shuffled order beside the written one rather than being permuted in place, and every walk choosing which of the two it means: the drop would test `shuffled[i]` and unlink `deps[i]`, while `actual_inputs` stays aligned with `deps` and needs no search. That is the same layering question the parent node answered the other way, which is why it is filed rather than folded in.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:04:25.662292439Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:11:22.239408324Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and it cost a great deal less than the filing feared: the drop had to change which NAME it takes, not how the graph carries its prerequisites.\n\nThe matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin as it was on the right. `$^`/`$+`/`$<` are the only cells that ever moved — every one of these built the same files in the same order before and after.\n\n  t: x t\n    no shuffle          hat=[x] / same\n    --shuffle=identity  hat=[x] / same\n    --shuffle=reverse   hat=[t] / hat=[x]\n  t: a b t c\n    no shuffle          hat=[a b c], builds a b c   / same\n    --shuffle=reverse   hat=[a t c], builds c b a   / hat=[a b c]\n    --shuffle=7         hat=[b t c], builds b a c   / hat=[a b c]\n  t: p t q              reverse: hat=[p q]          / same — the circular entry sits where the walk reaches it\n  t: a t b, u: c u d    reverse: both hats unmoved  / same, for the same reason\n  t: a t b t            reverse: hat=[t], plus=[t t] / hat=[a b] — two drops, and the pair stays index-consistent\n  t: a u b / u: c t d   reverse and seed 3, the loop closing one level down / already matched\n\nWhat the shuffle does NOT move is the load-bearing negative the parent node established and this one had to keep: GNU populates `->shuf` over the same prerequisites and leaves `->next`, which the automatic variables walk, exactly where it was. So the written list is `$^`, and the only question is which entry the drop takes out of it.\n\nRead rather than inferred. remake.c:596 takes `d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du`, tests `is_updating (d->file)`, names `d` in the message and then unlinks `du`:\n\n    if (lastd == 0) file->deps = du->next; else lastd->next = du->next;\n\nSo the message names the shuffled entry and the list loses the walked one. file.c:681 says outright that \"shuffle mode assumes '->next' and '->shuf' links both traverse the same dependencies\", and this drop is what breaks that assumption — but it is what 4.4.1 does, and the fixture records 4.4.1.\n\nShape delivered, and the filing's cost estimate was wrong. It expected `DepNode` to carry the shuffled order beside the written one so that every walk could choose which of the two it meant. It does not need to: `deps` is already the shuffled order, `actual_inputs` is already the written one, and `drop_circular_dependencies` enters `deps` in index order — so the index the walk has reached IS GNU Make's written position, and `drop_prerequisite` need only take the name at that index instead of searching for the one it just removed. Nine lines became four, and the by-name search is gone. The edge still goes with the loop it closed rather than with the position, because Ronin hands the finished plan to a frontend and must not hand it a cycle; GNU has no such consumer, its walk having already passed.\n\nThe positional rule is also what the unshuffled runs always wanted — the two lists agree there, so nothing moved for them — and it keeps the `&:` guard the by-name search had: an edge past the end of the names was never a written prerequisite and leaves the names alone.\n\nOne fixture, recorded from the oracle: a-shuffled-circular-drop-unlinks-the-walked-position — `t: a b t c` under `--shuffle=reverse -j1`, writing `$^` to a file and each prerequisite to an order log, so it gates the name AND the build order together. Recorded `hat` is `a t c`.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 rows at 163/0/37/1083, compiler 0.\n\nOne adjacent defect found while probing the order-only cells and filed rather than folded in: GNU carries normal and order-only prerequisites in ONE `->next` chain and shuffles them together, where Ronin permutes `deps` and `order_onlys` separately. `t: a b | c d` under `--shuffle=reverse` builds d c b a in GNU and b a d c in Ronin, with no circular dependency anywhere in it — so it is a defect in the shuffle itself rather than in the drop. It shows through the drop too (`t: a | b t c` reverse leaves GNU `$|=[t c]` and Ronin `$|=[b c]`, because the drop's position crosses the boundary the two lists put between them). See make-shuffle-reorders-order-only-prerequisites-apart-from-the-rest.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 843 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; clippy all three passes silent; cargo doc clean; make_port green including the new recording; graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 163/0/37/1083, unmoved.\n\nkati commit 98bb657 on nplan/make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-position-not-the-edge."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:11.242416727Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text probe2
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:42.822125044Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two corrections to the delivery note above, and an apology for the two one-word entries between them — \"probe\" and \"probe2\" were written to find out which log kind the image lint would accept, and nothing removes a log entry once it is written.\n\nFirst: the workspace test count is 842 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved from the baseline this dispatch measured, not 843. The fixture this node added is a data directory under tests/make/ read by `make_build_intent_matches_oracle`, so it grows the corpus from 809 cases to 810 without adding a test function. Every other number in that note was measured and stands.\n\nSecond, for whoever hits it next: `kind: errata` is refused on a node that is still Doing — `nplan_note` reports \"plan image fails lint (1 violation)\" and the on-disk lint stays clean, so the refusal is about the image it would write rather than about anything already committed. A plain `note` is accepted on the same node in the same breath. Errata appears to be reserved for a delivery that is already recorded, which is the right invariant and an opaque way to learn it."
                    }

