            {
                id make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include
                title "Report an unread include whose own rule failed"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:52:57.235512602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and kept out of it because it is a complaint Ronin never makes rather than one it makes in the wrong place.\n\n`show_goal_error` has two callers, not one. That node moved the first — `complain()` at reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:414, the no-rule refusal. The second is job.c:581, where a recipe that failed reports the held complaint before it names the failure:\n\n    include gen.mk\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GENFAIL; exit 1\n    all: ; @echo all\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `make: *** [Makefile:2: gen.mk] Error 1`, exit 2\n  Ronin      GENFAIL, the FAILED block, `ronin: build stopped: subcommand failed.`, exit 2 — no complaint at all\n\nBoth exit 2 and both leave the same files, so nothing gates on it; what is missing is the line saying why the file mattered. It is easy to read the failure as being about the rule alone when it is also about a read that did not happen.\n\nThe same hole shows in the mixed case, and there it is louder because Ronin used to print the wrong file's complaint:\n\n    include gen.mk\n    include nope.mk\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GENFAIL; exit 1\n\n  GNU     GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `*** [Makefile:3: gen.mk] Error 1`\n  Ronin   GENFAIL, the FAILED block, the stopped line — gen.mk's complaint is never made, and nope.mk is never reached\n\nWhere. kati's `plan_regeneration` renders the complaint only on the arm that has no rule to offer, so a missing include that IS remakable carries none. The fix is to render one for every required include that would not open — the `missing_includes` list already holds each name and its `include` line — and carry them out as a per-file table beside the refusal, the way GNU carries `goal->error` per goaldep. Ronin's side then emits a file's complaint where its own remake is settled as lost: `Builder::failed_edges` and `Pass::Lost` already name exactly those targets, which is what make-optional-include-failure-is-per-file built them for.\n\nGNU's guard is worth porting with it: `(goal_dep->flags & (RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)) != RM_INCLUDED` means a `-include` never complains however it fails, and `goal->error = 0` after printing means once per file however many times it is reached.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:49:29.475919129Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:07:08.866390327Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe corrected the filing on one point: the complaint belongs to the goal being updated, not to the recipe that lost.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `show_goal_error` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:302) reads the GLOBAL `goal_dep` — the goaldep `update_goal_chain` is currently working on — and not the file whose child died:\n\n    if ((goal_dep->flags & (RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)) != RM_INCLUDED) return;\n    for (goal = goal_list; goal; goal = goal->next)\n      if (goal_dep->file == goal->file)\n        { if (goal->error) { OSS (error, &goal->floc, \"%s: %s\", ...); goal->error = 0; } return; }\n\n`child_error` calls it at job.c:581, immediately before the `[Makefile:N: target] Error N` line. So `include gen.mk` whose `gen.mk: dep` loses over `dep` complains about gen.mk while naming dep as the failure — the two lines name different files on purpose, and that is the cell the filing did not have.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Seven cells, of which four moved:\n\n  include gen.mk, its own rule fails\n    GNU  GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `*** [Makefile:2: gen.mk] Error 1`, exit 2\n    was  GENFAIL, the FAILED block, the stopped line — no complaint at all\n  include gen.mk / include nope.mk, gen.mk's rule fails\n    GNU  the same three lines; nope.mk is never reached  / was: no complaint\n  include gen.mk whose rule WRITES the file and then exits 1\n    GNU  the same three lines — the bytes on disk do not answer the question / was: no complaint\n  include gen.mk with `gen.mk: dep` where DEP's rule fails\n    GNU  DEPFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: ...` / `*** [Makefile:4: dep] Error 1` — the complaint names the goal\n    was  no complaint, and this is the cell that settles which file is named\n  two required includes, both rules fail, -j1\n    GNU  the first one's complaint alone, and the run ends there  / was: none\n  one required include succeeds, the next fails\n    GNU  the SECOND one's complaint — the update walks them in read order / was: none\n  -include gen.mk whose rule fails\n    GNU  GENFAIL then the refusal, and no located line ever  / already agreed, and must keep agreeing\n  include gen.mk where gen.mk exists and is up to date\n    GNU  nothing at all  / already agreed\n\nShape delivered. kati's `plan_regeneration` renders the complaint for every required include the read did not get, rather than only for the one nothing can make, and a `RegenerationRoot` carries its own in `complaint`. The guard is GNU Make's, moved up to where both callers can share it: RM_INCLUDED without RM_DONTCARE, which is `required` and a location — `-include` never complains however it fails, and a Makefile the command line named has no `include` line to point at and was reported by the read.\n\nRonin holds them as `(Node, String)` beside the remakes, through `RegenerationNames`, `UnitRemakes`, `CompilationState` and `Loaded`, and says one where a non-forgiven pass loses. Which one is `complaints_for`: the first target of the pass whose own recipe lost, and failing that the first the pass did not regenerate. The second reading is what the prerequisite cell needs — gen.mk's own edge never ran, so it is not among the pass's failures, and it is still the goal. The update walks the makefiles in read order and ends on the first it cannot bring up to date, so everything before that one succeeded and everything after was never attempted, which is why one answer is the right number of answers.\n\nWhere it is printed is a decision rather than a translation. `finished` became `complained_of` with an empty list, and the complaint goes into stdout ahead of the `build stopped` line rather than onto stderr. The rule is the one `refused_makefile` already follows — the complaint goes on the stream carrying the line it precedes — and there both are stderr because both are diagnostics. Here the line that ends the run is Ninja's `build stopped`, which is narration on stdout, and a complaint written to the other stream is read after everything rather than beside what it explains. Verified in a merged capture: recipe output, complaint, ending line, in that order, which is GNU's shape.\n\nRows: features/include.diff.18 and .34 moved `unclassified -> narration`, both losing the `evaluation` marker and keeping only Ninja's narration families. Auditing them found GNU Make's own suite testing exactly these cells: `# Included file has a prerequisite that fails to build`, which expects `#MAKEFILE#:3: inc1: $ERR_no_such_file` beside `*** [#MAKEFILE#:5: foo] Error 1` — the goal named in one line and the recipe in the next — and `# SV 56301 Verify pattern rules creating optional includes`. 1283 rows at 159/0/37/1087. Compiler stays 0, interface stays 37.\n\nThree tests in tests/make_regressions.rs, where the corpus cannot reach — nothing about these cells touches a file, so the build-intent gate is blind to all of them: lost-remake-reports-its-unread-include (the complaint between the failure and the ending), a-lost-prerequisite-reports-the-goal (the complaint names gen.mk while the failure names dep, and gen.mk's own recipe never ran), a-forgiven-remake-makes-no-complaint (the negative, which is what keeps the guard honest).\n\nOne thing found while auditing the rows that node 4 needs and the filing has wrong. Its note says the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary is reached only under `-k`. GNU's suite has it without:\n\n    all:; @echo hello\n    -include inc_a.mk\n    include inc_b.mk\n    %_a.mk %_b.mk:; exit 1\n\nexpects `exit 1` then `#MAKEFILE#:4: Failed to remake makefile 'inc_b.mk'.` with NO `-k`, and the suite's next line asserts `-k` changes nothing about it. The summary comes from main.c's `us_failed` arm, which is reached whenever the update returns rather than dying — and a grouped rule whose first attempt was forgiven is one way to return. Probe that shape in make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make rather than assuming the switch is what decides.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 847 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (844 + the three new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, the two rows above audited; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 28s at -j8.\n\nkati commit 5e0f6ee on nplan/make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:34.615312085Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised
                title "Treat an include that opens but will not read as a target with no rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:53:18.626262888Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and separate from it: that node asked WHERE the complaint is made, and this asks whether the read may end the run at all.\n\nGNU Make treats every failure to open an `include`d file the same way, whatever errno says. `eval_makefile` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:347) stores `deps->error = errno` and returns; the only errnos it dies on are the ones that mean make itself is out of resources — EMFILE, ENFILE, ENOMEM. Everything else becomes a makefile that was not read, and the run carries on to the update, which refuses over it as a target with no rule.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    include secret.mk        (touch secret.mk; chmod 000 secret.mk)\n      GNU    `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied` / `make: *** No rule to make target 'secret.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n      Ronin  `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied`, exit 2\n\n    include adir             (mkdir adir)\n      GNU    `make: *** adir: Is a directory.  Stop.` — no location, and no refusal after it\n      Ronin  `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: adir: Is a directory`, exit 2\n\nBoth tools exit 2 in both cells, so nothing gates on it. What differs is the shape: Ronin raises the read error from the read, with an inclusion-stack line GNU has no counterpart for, and never reaches the refusal; and it never gets the chance to remake anything ahead of the file, which is the whole of what the positional-refusal work established.\n\nThe directory cell is the odd one and should be probed further before it is matched: GNU says `adir: Is a directory` with its own name and no location, and does NOT go on to refuse, which suggests it comes from a different site than the ENOENT path — `fopen` on a directory succeeds on Linux and the failure arrives at the read rather than at the open. Worth reading `readline`/`eval` in read.c for it rather than assuming it is the same mechanism.\n\nWhere. kati's read raises non-ENOENT errors as evaluation failures instead of recording them the way it records a missing file, so they never reach `missing_includes` and `plan_regeneration` never sees them. Making them reach it is the fix, with `MissingInclude` carrying the errno's words rather than assuming `No such file or directory`.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:14:41.956565437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:45:24.815849706Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe found that the filing had located only half of it. GNU Make records a failed open in two places on two adjacent lines, and the second is what the cells with a rule turn on.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `eval_makefile` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) does this and nothing else when `fopen` returns NULL:\n\n    errno = 0;\n    ENULLLOOP (ebuf.fp, fopen (filename, \"r\"));\n    deps->error = errno;                        /* :347 — quoted later */\n    switch (deps->error) { case EMFILE: case ENFILE: case ENOMEM:\n        OS (fatal, reading_file, \"%s\", err); }  /* the only three it dies on */\n    ...\n    if (ebuf.fp == 0) {\n        errno = deps->error;\n        deps->file->last_mtime = NONEXISTENT_MTIME;   /* :409 */\n        return deps;\n    }\n\nThe filing named the first line. The second is the one that matters most: a makefile that would not open is a makefile that is not there, for every question after the read. That is what puts its own rule out of date — a recipe with no prerequisites is up to date the moment its target exists, and an unreadable file exists — and so what lets such a rule repair the file and send the read around again.\n\nAnd the directory cell is a different site entirely, as the filing suspected and asked to have read rather than assumed. `fopen` on a directory succeeds on Linux; the failure arrives at the read, where `readline` finds `ferror` and calls `pfatal_with_name` (read.c:2744). That one is not deferred by anything, has no location, and is named under Make's own name.\n\nThe matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin as it was on the right. Ten cells, of which six moved.\n\n  include secret.mk (chmod 000, no rule)\n    GNU   `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied` / `*** No rule to make target 'secret.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n    was   `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / the complaint, exit 2 — raised at the read, no refusal\n  include locked/one.mk (the DIRECTORY has no search permission — EACCES from a different site)\n    GNU   the located complaint / the refusal          / was: raised at the read\n  include afile/one.mk (afile is an ordinary file — ENOTDIR)\n    GNU   the located complaint / the refusal          / was: raised at the read\n  include secret.mk WITH a rule that chmods and writes it\n    GNU   GEN, restart, the goals see the variable it defines, exit 0\n    was   raised at the read, exit 2\n  include adir (a directory)\n    GNU   `make: *** adir: Is a directory.  Stop.`, no refusal after it\n    was   `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: adir: Is a directory` — the same ending, the wrong shape\n  -include secret.mk                    silent in both, and still is\n  -f secret.mk\n    GNU   `make: secret.mk: Permission denied` from the read, then the refusal\n    was   the complaint alone — the refusal was missing\n  -f adir                               `adir: Is a directory` and stop; already agreed\n  MAKEFILES=secret.mk\n    GNU   silent, builds the goals, exit 0\n    was   `ronin: secret.mk: Permission denied`, exit 2\n  MAKEFILES=adir                        stop; already agreed\n\nThe `-f` and `MAKEFILES` rows are the ones that show the deferral is about the open rather than about `include`. GNU reports the `-f` face from `perror_with_name (\"\", *makefiles)` immediately after `eval_makefile` returns (read.c:219) — at the read, because no `include` line exists to point at — and refuses over it later all the same. `MAKEFILES` is read with `RM_DONTCARE` and its errno is never looked at, so it is as quiet as a name nothing is at.\n\nShape delivered. `Source` forks four ways where it forked three: `Absent`, `Unopened` (deferred, like absence, with the errno's words), `Unreadable` (opened and then not — not deferred, unlocated, Make's own name), and `Exhausted` (the three errnos that are about Make). `Makefile::from_file` takes the open and the read apart rather than doing both in one `fs::read`, because it is the only place that still knows which failed. `MissingInclude` gains `reason`, so the complaint quotes what the system said instead of assuming `No such file or directory`, and `note_missing_include` became `note_unread_include`.\n\nThe inclusion-stack line moved rather than being deleted: `In file included from X:` now wraps the included file's statements instead of the whole of `do_include`. A diagnostic about the open or the read already names the file and the line that asked for it, and a second mention of the same line above it says nothing — but a parse error inside the file still wants the stack, and still has it.\n\n`RegenerationRoot::unread` is `last_mtime = NONEXISTENT_MTIME` crossing the boundary. It travels through `RegenerationNames`, `UnitRemakes`, `CompilationState` and `Loaded` beside `forgiven`, and lands in `Graph::unread_makefiles` — a sparse set beside the node arena, where `unmade_makefiles` and `withdrawal` are, and for the same reason: no node of a Ninja manifest is ever in it. `nodestat_with` consults it and observes `FileTime::MISSING` instead of stat'ing, which is one line and puts the whole of the existing freshness machinery behind GNU's answer. Absence sets it too and means nothing there — the filesystem already agrees.\n\nIndependent confirmation, found while auditing the rows rather than while writing the fix: GNU Make's own suite tests this, in `features/include` under `if (defined $ERR_unreadable_file)`. Four cases — the refusal, the refusal with `-I` naming a directory holding a readable file of the same name, and then two headed \"Unreadable files that we know how to successfully recreate should work\", which expect `bar` and are exactly the cell the freshness half was needed for. Two of those four are the only rows in the 1283 that moved.\n\nRows: features/include.diff.24 and .25 moved `unclassified -> narration`, markers `product-name+evaluation -> ninja-progress`. Both residues are gone: the `evaluation` one because the two tools now evaluate to the same thing, and `product-name` because Ronin no longer has a diagnostic to name itself in. 1283 rows at 161/0/37/1085. Compiler stays 0, interface stays 37, and `features/se_explicit` is untouched.\n\nFixtures, recorded from the oracle: an-include-that-would-not-open-is-refused-after-the-remaking (ENOTDIR, and the gate is that `gen.mk` is written at all — the deferral is what lets the makefile ahead of the refusal be remade), a-makefiles-entry-that-would-not-open-is-passed-over (the goals run, where the run used to end). Two more in tests/make_regressions.rs, where the corpus cannot reach: an-unreadable-include-is-repaired-by-its-own-rule needs mode 000, which is not a thing a committed file can be, and asks the host whether it enforces permissions before trusting itself — a run as root would record the opposite case without saying so; a-named-makefile-that-would-not-open-is-refused-after-the-remaking is an ordering, the read's own complaint before the work and the refusal after it, which the build-intent gate cannot see.\n\nTwo adjacent defects found while probing and filed rather than folded in:\n  make-makefiles-variable-entries-are-never-remade — `MAKEFILES=gen.mk` with a rule for gen.mk: GNU runs it, restarts, and the goals see what it defined; Ronin never remakes it. The read notes those names and the plan never makes roots of them.\n  make-an-output-directory-is-created-where-gnu-make-would-refuse — Ronin creates an edge's output directory the way Ninja does, so a target under a path whose parent is an ordinary file ends the run with `File exists` where GNU Make simply runs the recipe.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 844 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (842 + the two new regressions); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the two new recordings; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 161/0/37/1085, the two rows above audited; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 48s at -j8.\n\nkati commit 8c0aa26 on nplan/make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:29.697351348Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make
                title "Report every unmakeable makefile under keep-going, not the first"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:53:44.113596898Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and filed rather than folded in because it is about `-k` rather than about where a complaint is printed.\n\n`complain()` reads `keep_going_flag` and chooses between `fatal` and `error` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c). Without `-k` the first unmakeable makefile ends the run — which is the positional refusal make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile delivered. With `-k` it does not: the update goes on to the next makefile, refuses over that one too, and `main.c`'s `us_failed` arm then adds a `Failed to remake makefile` line for each.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, `include nope1.mk` / `include nope2.mk` / `all: ; @echo all`:\n\n  make -k\n    GNU    Makefile:1: nope1.mk: No such file or directory\n           make: *** No rule to make target 'nope1.mk'.\n           Makefile:2: nope2.mk: No such file or directory\n           make: *** No rule to make target 'nope2.mk'.\n           Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'nope1.mk'.\n           Makefile:2: Failed to remake makefile 'nope2.mk'.\n           exit 2\n    Ronin  the first complaint and the first refusal, exit 2\n\n  make      both tools stop at nope1.mk — this cell already agrees\n\nNote the three shapes in GNU's `-k` output, which are three separate things to port and probably three separate decisions: the per-file complaint (which is the same `show_goal_error` the parent node moved, fired once per file), the undecorated refusal (`*** ` with no `  Stop.`, because `error` rather than `fatal`), and the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary that only the keep-going path reaches.\n\nSame family, same probe, not measured: `-k` with a makefile whose rule failed rather than one with no rule — GNU printed `Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'gen.mk'.` after the recipe-failure line there too, so the summary is about the update's verdict rather than about which of the two ways it lost.\n\nWhere. `plan_regeneration` stops collecting at the first unmakeable required makefile — the `break` is what reproduces the positional cells — and returns one refusal. Under `-k` it would have to keep walking and return a list, and Ronin's side would raise them together after the remaking rather than raising one.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:08:23.345789867Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:30:07.083208107Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe corrected the filing twice — once about what `-k` decides, and once about where the summary comes from.\n\nThe filing described three separate things to port: the per-file complaint, the undecorated refusal, and the summary. Only two of those are real. Ronin's refusal has carried no `*** ` and no `  Stop.` since Make mode was built — that is `diagnostic_body` in src/make/report.rs taking Make's ceremony off every diagnostic — so the difference the filing saw between `make: *** No rule to make target 'x'.  Stop.` and `make: *** No rule to make target 'x'.` is one Ronin does not have to make. Nothing was needed for it and nothing was done.\n\nWhat `-k` actually decides. `complain()` reads `keep_going_flag` and chooses `error` over `fatal` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:422), and that one choice has two consequences rather than one:\n\n  the update walks on, so every required makefile nothing can make is reported\n  rather than the first;\n  and the update RETURNS, which lets `main.c` reach two things it otherwise\n  never gets to — the `us_failed` arm that lists each one again (main.c:2544),\n  and, before that, `if (any_remade) goto re_exec`.\n\nThe second consequence is the one the filing did not have, and it is the larger. Without `-k` a refusal outranks a restart, which is what make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile established. With `-k` it outranks nothing: a makefile that WAS remade sends the read around again, and the refusals are made a second time on the pass that follows.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Nine cells, of which six moved.\n\n  include nope1.mk / include nope2.mk / all\n    make      the first complaint, the first refusal, and stop   / already agreed\n    make -k   complaint 1, refusal 1, complaint 2, refusal 2, then BOTH summaries,\n              exit 2, and `all` never runs\n              / was: the first complaint and the first refusal, exit 2\n  -include nope1.mk / include nope2.mk, -k\n    GNU  nope2 alone, complaint, refusal, summary — the optional one is not\n         mentioned at all                                  / was: no summary\n  -include nope1.mk / -include nope2.mk, -k     both build `all`, exit 0 — agreed\n  three includes, the first optional, -k\n    GNU  the two required ones, in read order, then both summaries  / was: the first only\n  a nested include: Makefile includes sub.mk which includes nope2.mk, -k\n    GNU  `Makefile:1:` and `sub.mk:1:` on their own complaints and their own\n         summaries — the location is the line that asked, at whatever depth\n                                                            / was: the first only\n  -f nope.mk, -k\n    GNU  `make: nope.mk: ...` from the read, `*** No rule...`, then\n         `make: Failed to remake makefile 'nope.mk'.` — unlocated, under Make's\n         own name, because no `include` line asked for it   / was: no summary\n  two missing includes AND a remakable gen.mk, -k\n    GNU  the two refusals, GEN, both summaries, and then THE WHOLE THING AGAIN\n         — refusals and summaries a second time — exit 2\n         / was: the first complaint and refusal, and gen.mk never made at all\n  the same without -k        the first refusal, and gen.mk never made — agreed,\n                             and it must keep agreeing\n  make -k with everything present    nothing to report — agreed\n\nShape delivered. kati's `plan_regeneration` collects into a `Vec<Refusal>` instead of returning at the first, and breaks after one unless `Flags::keep_going`. Each `Refusal` renders its own `summary` beside its `complaint` and `error`, at the `include` line that asked for the file and under the program's own name when nothing did — `color_warn_text` already had both shapes. `Plan` and `Evaluated` carry the list.\n\n`-k` reaches the evaluator the way `--shuffle` does and for the same reason: the makefile update is where GNU Make reads it first, and that update is kati's. It joins the three switches already in the flags literal whose whole effect is on evaluation.\n\nRonin's side. `refusal_report` splits a run's refusals into the two passes GNU Make writes them in — every complaint-and-refusal first, then every summary — because `main.c` makes the second pass after the update has finished and the two lists interleave nowhere. `report::refused_makefile` takes both. Under `-k` a restart no longer loses to a refusal: `settled_makefiles` returns `Again`, and the refusals of the read that is ending are written straight to the diagnostic stream by `report_refusals_now`, because a result is what ENDS a run and that one is not ending.\n\nRefactoring done to keep the gates green rather than to bypass them: `build_compiler_inputs` crossed the hundred-line ceiling twice while this grew, and came back under it by taking out three things that wanted names anyway — `Read::taken_from` for what one read hands the update, `settled_makefiles` for reconciling the update's answer with what the read already knew it could not make, and `refusal_result`. The `Settled` enum keeps `Over` and `Refused` apart for one reason: a build that failed has already said why and a failing persistence flush must not speak over it, where for a refusal the flush is the last thing that can go wrong — which is exactly what the two arms did before they were extracted.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, compiler 0, interface 37.\n\nOne fixture, recorded from the oracle, and it gates a file effect rather than the diagnostics: keep-going-remakes-past-a-makefile-it-cannot-make — two unmakeable includes with a remakable gen.mk behind them, where the recorded effect is that gen.mk is written at all. Without `-k` the run ends before it is reached; the old code ended there under `-k` too. Two tests in tests/make_regressions.rs for the order, which the build-intent gate cannot see: keep-going-refuses-every-makefile (six lines in GNU Make's order, and the goal never running) and one-refusal-without-keep-going (the negative — the second makefile is never mentioned and no summary is reached).\n\nNot delivered, filed with its own matrix: make-a-makefile-whose-recipe-lost-is-listed-under-keep-going. The same summary line for a makefile whose recipe RAN AND LOST rather than one nothing knows how to make. It is a different route to the same `us_failed` arm and lands in a different module — the build's failure rather than the read's refusal — and its third cell shows the filing's `-k`-only reading was wrong in the other direction too: `-include inc_a.mk` / `include inc_b.mk` sharing a losing pattern rule prints `Failed to remake makefile 'inc_b.mk'.` with NO `-k`, and GNU Make's own suite asserts that `-k` changes nothing about it.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 849 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (847 + the two new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the new recording; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 21s at -j8.\n\nkati commit f43d7fd on nplan/make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:39.993442486Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-question-mode-refuses-a-makefile-it-cannot-make
                title "Refuse under -q over a makefile goal nothing can make"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:21:19.972793858Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals, and pre-existing rather than caused by it: under `-q` neither the old nor the new grouping puts the file in a subset at all, so nothing about that node's change reaches this cell.\n\n    -include one.mk\n    all: ; @echo all ran\n    one.mk: ; @echo GEN-one; echo X=1 > one.mk\n\n    make -q one.mk\n      GNU 4.4.1  `make: *** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2, one.mk not created\n      Ronin      no output, rc 1, one.mk not created\n\nBoth decline to build and both leave the tree alone, so nothing gates on it; the exit status is what differs, and `-q` is a status. GNU answers 2 — \"the question could not be answered at all\" — where Ronin answers 1, \"something would have to run\".\n\nWhy GNU refuses. `update_goal_chain` restores `question_flag` for a file the command line named while the makefiles are being rebuilt (`file->cmd_target`, remake.c:169), so `one.mk` is asked about rather than made during the makefile update. The question comes back \"not up to date\", which for a makefile is a failed update; `dontcare` forgives it; and the goal chain then reaches the same file, finds it `updated` with a failing status, and complains — which is a fatal, so 2 wins over the question's 1.\n\nRonin's `Makefiles::subsets` returns an empty target list for both command-line groups under `-q`, on the reasoning that GNU records the question and carries on with the read. That is right about the makefile update doing no work, and wrong about it leaving no verdict behind: the question's answer is what makes the file one the goals must refuse over. The fix is for the `-q` path to record the same third state make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals introduced — `mark_makefiles_unmade` — for a makefile the question found wanting, rather than skipping the file entirely.\n\nNot probed and worth settling first: `-q` on a REQUIRED include with the same shape, and `-q` where the makefile is a goal but not an include at all.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:31:13.067739585Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:41:12.041631822Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the two cells the filing asked to have settled first are settled: the required-include face and the not-an-include-at-all face both already agreed, and both had to keep agreeing.\n\nRead rather than inferred, and it turns out to be four lines in three places. `update_goal_chain` restores the invocation's switches for a Makefile the command line named while the makefiles are being rebuilt (`file->cmd_target`, remake.c:169), so that one is asked about rather than made — and it does NOT stop for the answer, because `stop` is withheld while `rebuilding_makefiles` (remake.c:206). `update_file_1` copies `dontcare` into `no_diag` on the way in (remake.c:496). `update_goal_chain` clears `dontcare` when it lets go of the file (remake.c:236). And then the goals' own pass reads the pair back:\n\n    if (file->updated)\n      { if (file->update_status > us_none)\n          { if (file->no_diag && !file->dontcare) complain (file);\n\nSo the difference between 1 and 2 is entirely `no_diag`, and `no_diag` is entirely whether the `include` was forgiving. A required one leaves `us_question` and no flag, `main.c` reads that as \"did nothing\" (main.c:2473), the goals ask again and answer 1. A forgiven one leaves the same status AND the flag, and the goals complain — which is fatal, so 2 outranks the question's 1.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Ten cells, of which one moved and one moved the wrong way.\n\n  -include one.mk, one.mk has a rule, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2, one.mk not created\n    was    silence, rc 1                                        — the filed cell\n  include one.mk (REQUIRED), the same, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    silence, rc 1, one.mk not created                    / already agreed\n  one.mk is not included at all, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    silence, rc 1                                        / already agreed\n  -include one.mk that EXISTS and is up to date, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    rc 0                                                 / already agreed\n  `make -q` and `make -q all` — one.mk not a command-line goal\n    GNU    GEN-one really runs, rc 1 — `-q` does not stop a makefile being made\n                                                                / already agreed\n  `make one.mk` without -q          GEN-one, `'one.mk' is up to date.`, rc 0\n                                                                / already agreed\n  `make -q one.mk all`              rc 2, same as the single goal / was rc 1\n  an ordinary goal with no rule, under every combination of -q and -k\n    GNU    rc 2 in all four                                     / already agreed,\n           and this is the negative that keeps the fix off the ordinary path\n  `make -q -k one.mk`\n    GNU    the refusal, rc 1                                    / was rc 1, now rc 2\n  -include one.mk whose PREREQUISITE would have to be made, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    `*** No rule to make target 'src', needed by 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2\n    was    rc 1; now rc 2 with `one.mk` named instead of `src`\n\nShape delivered, all of it in Ronin. `Makefiles::subsets` stops emptying the forgiven command-line group under `-q` and marks it `question` instead; `remake_makefiles` asks that subset rather than running it, and an answer of \"not up to date\" puts its targets into `settled.unmade` — the third state make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals introduced. Everything after that is machinery that already existed: `mark_makefile_unmade`, `Plan::add_node` refusing whether or not the file is there, `MissingInput` naming the dependent when there is one.\n\nThe required command-line group is still left out of the update entirely, which is what `main.c` reading `us_question` as \"did nothing\" amounts to, and the unit test that gates that now asserts the absence of the question as well as the absence of the targets.\n\nTwo cells to be honest about, both filed as make-a-questioned-makefile-keeps-the-questions-status-under-keep-going with their reproducers.\n\n  `-q -k` moved from accidentally right to wrong. Ronin used to answer 1 to every\n  shape of `make -q <a makefile>`, so this cell agreed by having no opinion; it\n  now answers 2 where GNU answers 1. The mechanism is that GNU has TWO failing\n  statuses for a makefile — `us_question` from a question and `us_failed` from a\n  recipe that lost — and `-k`, by making `complain()` report rather than die, is\n  the only thing that lets them be told apart. Ronin has one third state where\n  GNU has two. Nine of the ten cells are right and this one is wrong, where\n  before one was right by accident and nine were wrong.\n\n  The prerequisite cell now answers 2 with GNU rather than 1 against it, and\n  names a different file: GNU's `complain()` recurses into `file->deps`\n  (remake.c:403) and names `src`, which HAS a rule and is named as though it did\n  not because `-q` left it `updated` with `us_question` too. Whether that is\n  worth porting is a decision rather than an omission — it reads as a defect in\n  4.4.1 — and it is recorded as one.\n\nNothing in kati moved, so there is no kati commit for this node.\n\nTwo tests, and neither could be a corpus fixture: the build-intent gate records success or failure and this whole node is the difference between 1 and 2, which it deliberately does not distinguish. question-refuses-a-forgiven-makefile-goal in tests/make_regressions.rs asserts the exact status for both faces of the same Makefile — 2 with `-include` and 1 with `include` — and that neither creates the file. question-asks-about-a-forgiven-named-makefile in src/make/cli/remake.rs gates the grouping that decides it, including that nothing is asked when nothing asked a question.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, compiler 0, interface 37.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 851 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (849 + the two new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 21s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:45.270928746Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-progress-total-shrinks-to-the-work
                title "Shrink the progress total to the work that will actually run"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:54:02.406423645Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Filed on an explicit operator decision, 2026-08-16: \"the total should be shrunk to what is actually GOING TO RUN\".\n\nThe complaint, from a sed aarch64 install log: `[1/79]` … `[4/79]` and then the build stops. Seventy-nine edges were planned, four ran, and the total never moved.\n\nNATIVE NINJA IS THE AUTHORITY here, under make.narration (\"a Ninja graph is narrated Ninja's way\"), so the convention was probed against reference/ninja-build/ninja 1.14.0.git rather than reasoned about. Ninja's total shrinks the moment an edge leaves the plan, and only lines printed after the prune carry the smaller number:\n\n    rule stamp        (restat = 1, output mtime pinned)\n    build base: stamp src\n    build o1..o8: copy base\n    build z: copy o1..o8 zsrc\n\n  touch src zsrc; ninja -j1\n    [1/10] touch -m -d @1000000000 base\n    [2/2]  touch z\n\nThe mechanism is Plan::CleanNode (build.cc:314): when a restat leaves an output unchanged and a consumer's inputs are all clean, the consumer's want becomes kWantNothing, `--command_edges_`, and `status_->EdgeRemovedFromPlan(edge)` — which is the exact mirror of EdgeAddedToPlan, decrementing total_edges_ AND the ETA bookkeeping (status_printer.cc:89-114). N never exceeds the final M by this route: CleanNode skips edges already erased from want_ (finished ones), and an edge it does prune had pending inputs and so had never started.\n\nRonin prints `[2/10]` on the same manifest — a Ninja-conformance bug, not only a Make one — and `[2/10]` in Make mode on the Makefile of the same shape. One mechanism is behind both.\n\nThis is a CONSISTENCY fix, not a rule bump. `[spec:ronin:sem:build.nodedone-fn]` already says the pruning path \"removes its non-phony total from the status count\"; Ronin's Plan::release_dependents settles the pruned consumer as complete without taking it out of the count."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:54:09.451345607Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:54:43.908508356Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED. One mechanism behind both graph kinds, in Ronin alone — kati is not involved, because the count of work is the runner's.\n\n`Plan::release_dependents` is Ronin's `Plan::CleanNode`: a dependent reached with every input settled and no dirty output is one a `restat` took out of the work. It settled such an edge as complete and left `wanted` standing, so `command_edge_count` — and therefore the progress total — still counted it. It now calls the new `Plan::unwant` (kWantNothing: the edge stays a dependency barrier and stops being work) and returns the pruned command edges, which `edge_finished` hands to the caller. `Builder::settle_edge` passes them to `forget_pruned_work`, and `BuildState::forget_edge` is the exact mirror of the two `expect_` calls, because Ninja's `EdgeRemovedFromPlan` is the exact mirror of `EdgeAddedToPlan` — the ETA weighting loses the edge along with the count, or `%P` would chase a total that no longer exists.\n\nThe two Make-mode paths that already decremented the total by hand — a recipe that expanded to no command, and a deferred edge whose freshness test came out negative — now go through the same `Plan::unwant` via `forget_unrun_edge`, so `wanted` and the count cannot drift apart. That matters beyond tidiness: the deferred-activation path recomputes the total from `command_edge_count`, and an edge pruned without being unwanted would have come back.\n\nWHY THE SED CASE NEVER SHRANK. Its prunes were not the Skip path that already decremented; they were consumers going clean under `release_dependents`, which is the path that did not. The same fixture reproduces in both languages:\n\n  before   ninja [1/10] [2/2]    ronin manifest [1/10] [2/10]    ronin make [1/10] [2/10]\n  after    ninja [1/10] [2/2]    ronin manifest [1/10] [2/2]     ronin make [1/10] [2/2]\n\nand the two-chain shape is byte-identical to `reference/ninja-build/ninja` now: [1/8] [2/5] [3/5] [4/5] [5/5].\n\nFIXTURES. tests/cli.rs `a_pruned_edge_leaves_the_progress_total` (the manifest, whose expected two lines are the reference ninja's own output on that manifest) and `make_mode_drops_a_re_observed_edge_from_the_progress_total` (the same shape as a Makefile — the reported case). src/build/tests.rs `plan_names_the_command_edges_a_restat_pruned` pins the plan-level answer directly.\n\nGATES. fmt, check, clippy three passes, doc, workspace tests 864/0 (861 + the three above). Ninja conformance: 425 upstream tests, 10 differential, 86 outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log-content — all pass. Make conformance 318/69 normalised, unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. make_port 4/0. Upstream inventory 1287 rows at 167/0/41/1079, unmoved, compiler zero. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 23s at -j8.\n\nNOT MOVED, and worth saying because the brief expected it might: no inventory row moved. The narration families read the progress line's payload, not its counter, so shrinking the total changes no classification.\n\nPRE-EXISTING RED, not this node's: `nplan port check --wave 4` fails at 0/170 on every wave. Confirmed identical on a pristine worktree at 0065e35, so scripts/check-release.sh has been stopping there before it reaches the conformance and performance steps; those were run individually here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:54:48.76750394Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-release-gate-stops-at-the-port-ladder
                title "Reach the conformance and performance gates past a port ladder that is 0/170"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:43:57.096571215Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while running the release gate for make-progress-total-shrinks-to-the-work, and filed rather than folded in because it is about the gate rather than about Make.\n\n    scripts/sandboxed scripts/check-release.sh\n\ndies at `nplan port check --wave 4` with\n\n    Wave 1 · 170 symbols · markup 0/170 · translate 0/170 · tests 0/170\n    [✗] wave-1-markup: 0/170 symbols marked up\n    [✗] wave-2-translate: 0/170 symbols ported\n    [✗] wave-3-tests: 0/170 symbols verified\n    [✗] wave-4-idiomatize: idiomatization is the last wave; complete waves 1–3 first\n    error: port: port check: one or more gates failed\n\nReproduced on a pristine worktree at 0065e35, so it is not any recent change's doing — the ladder reads 0/170 across every wave, which is what an unpopulated port register looks like rather than what a port that has not started looks like. The tree carries 396 formalization items and a full `docs/spec/port/**`, so the symbols exist; something in the register or its configuration is not seeing them.\n\nWhat it costs, and why it is worth a node rather than a shrug: `check-release.sh` is `set -eu`, and the port check sits AHEAD of\n\n    scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh\n    scripts/check-performance.sh\n    nplan spec uncovered / nplan spec stale\n    nplan lint / nplan audit\n\nso the one command that is supposed to say whether a release is sound has not reached the Ninja conformance suite or the performance baseline for as long as this has been red. Every dispatch that runs \"the gates\" has been running the tail by hand, which works only for as long as someone remembers the list.\n\nTwo ways out, and the choice is the work: either the port register is repopulated so the ladder measures something (and the gate keeps its position), or the ladder moves behind the suites that produce a verdict about the code, so a bookkeeping gap cannot hide a conformance regression. The second is cheap and the first is the one that makes the number mean anything."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read
                title "Expand a target-specific append's own text in the scope that reads it"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:42:11.042349194Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while shipping make-target-specific-append-reads-the-readers-scope and deliberately not folded into it: that node is about which BASE the second chain walk finds, and this is about where the appended TEXT is expanded. Both were wrong before it and only this half is still wrong.\n\nReproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    V = g\n    W = outerW\n    %.o: private V += p\n    a.o: V += <$(W)>\n    a.o: W = aoW\n    dep: W = depW\n    all: a.o\n    a.o: dep ; @echo \"a.o:[$(V)]\" >> log\n    dep: ; @echo \"dep:[$(V)]\" >> log\n\nGNU: a.o:[g p <aoW>], dep:[g <depW>]. Ronin: a.o:[g p <aoW>], dep:[g <aoW>]. The base is now right in both; the tail is not — `<aoW>` where GNU reads `<depW>`.\n\nWhy. `variable_append` (src/expand.c:534) ends each level with `variable_expand_string (buf, v->value, ...)` when the level is recursive, and that expansion runs in whatever `current_variable_set_list` the reader set — `recursively_expand_for_file` is reached per read. So every level of the chain, tail included, is expanded in the reader's scope and not in the writer's. A simple level is the exception and is copied verbatim (`if (! v->recursive) return variable_buffer_output (buf, v->value, ...)`), which is what makes the distinction observable rather than academic.\n\nRonin expands the tail once, in `DepBuilder::apply_rule_vars` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs), while the rule scope is being built — deliberately, with a comment saying why: `all: A += $(Z)` beside `all: Z = changed` must append `changed` rather than whatever Z was outside the rule, and expanding while the scope is half built reads the outer one. That reasoning is right about the target's own read and says nothing about a prerequisite's.\n\nA fix has to keep the binding lazy: the value a reader gets would be a recursive expression whose text is the base's text joined to the tail's, with any simple level spliced in as a literal so it is not expanded a second time. kati already has the machinery — `appended_values`/`appended_text` in var.rs build exactly that shape for an ordinary `+=`. What it changes is the flavour of the result: the settled simple string becomes recursive, so `$(flavor)`, `$(value)` and any `$(shell)` in the tail all move with it, and the recursion guard has to answer for `a.o: V += $(V)`. That is why it is worth its own node rather than a follow-on hunk.\n\nNot urgent: it needs a `+=` whose text names a variable that two readers in the same chain bind differently."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-deferred-freshness-binds-at-before-the-timestamps-are-known
                title "Bind a deferred recipe's $@ to the name that was out of date"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:42:34.673071186Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The last cell of make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity, reachable only now that make-deferred-freshness-hides-a-multi-target-peer has made the edge run at all. Filed from that node rather than folded into it: making the edge run is a question about which outputs decide its freshness, and this is a question about when `$@` can be chosen.\n\nReproducer, with `a.in` old, `a.c` current and `a.h` absent:\n\n    all: a.c a.h\n    %.c %.h: %.in ; echo \"at=$@ q=$?\" >> log ; cat $< > $*.c ; cat $< > $*.h\n\nGNU: `at=a.h q=a.in`. Ronin now runs the recipe and writes both files, with `q=a.in` right, and `at=a.c`. Take `$?` out of the recipe and Ronin says `at=a.h` too — that case is tests/make/multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date and it gates.\n\nWhy the two part company. `$@` for a pattern group is the member whose own state sent the recipe to the shell, which no compile-time reading can know: `trigger_output` (src/graph/peer.rs) is a stat-time answer, and `bind_late_command` asks for it as the edge launches. A recipe naming `$?` cannot be held for launch — `references_new_inputs` refuses it in `defers_recipe` — so its text is expanded while the graph is compiled and `AutoCommand::At` writes `node.recipe_output`, the first name, before any timestamp exists.\n\nThe route is the one `$?` itself already takes. `DEFERRED_NEW_INPUTS_REFERENCE` puts `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` in the command text and `resolve_deferred_new_inputs` (src/build/deferred.rs) substitutes the value as the edge launches, in the inline and response-file forms alike. `$@` would need the same: a second reference kati emits when it is expanding for a destination that cannot hold the recipe AND the node is a `pattern_group`, and a second substitution the executor makes from `trigger_output`. Both halves are small; what makes it a node is what moves with them — the command text carries a reference rather than a name, so the edge hash, `-n` output, the narration and the equivalence check all read the new text, and `escape_double_quoted_shell` has to be right for a path.\n\nScope note: only a pattern group is affected. An explicit `&:` group binds `$@` to the member that was reached, which is one decision over all its targets and is settled at plan time — make-grouped-pattern-targets recorded that and it is unaffected here."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-member-globbing
                title "Expand a member pattern inside an archive name"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:03.302420553Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which shipped the archive name shape, the built-in `(%): %` rule, `$%`, and member freshness read off the archive index — but not this.\n\n`lib.a(*.o)` written as a target or prerequisite is expanded by GNU Make's `ar_glob` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c) against the archive's own member list rather than against the filesystem: the pattern matches names the index holds, and the result is sorted and re-emitted as one `lib.a(...)` name per member. `ar_glob` is reached from `parse_file_seq` only when the member part holds a glob metacharacter, so an archive name without one costs nothing.\n\nRonin reads the name shape now, so `lib.a(*.o)` parses as archive `lib.a` and member `*.o` and then looks for a member literally called `*.o`. The reading is right and the expansion is absent.\n\nNeeds the index scan that make-archive-member-targets already built (`src/os/archive.rs` walks the members to date one), turned around to enumerate rather than to look up, and called from wherever a target or prerequisite name is expanded — which is before the graph exists, so it is front-end work rather than scheduler work."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:25:55.231294498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `lib.a(*.o)` is expanded against the archive's index wherever a target or prerequisite is written.\n\nThe characterisation, measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make rather than read off `ar.c` and assumed:\n\n* The index, not the filesystem. With `q.o` sitting on disk beside an archive that does not hold it, GNU matches `a.o b.o c.o` and never `q.o`. The converse holds too — a member whose object has been deleted still matches.\n* Sorted, and the index's own order is not it. An archive written `m3.o m1.o m2.o` answers `m1.o m2.o m3.o`; `ar_glob` sorts the finished `lib.a(...)` names with `alpha_compare`, which is `strcmp` once the first byte ties, so sorting the members is the same order.\n* `ar_glob_pattern_p` is deliberately not the filename test. `[` counts only once a `]` follows it, so `lib.a(m[13.o)` names a member spelt exactly that way and refuses; `m[13].o` globs. A backslash quotes the next character.\n* `fnmatch` runs with `FNM_PATHNAME|FNM_PERIOD`, so `*.o` does not match `.hidden.o` and `.*.o` does. Verified both directions.\n* No match is not an empty list: the member name stands as written and the build refuses over `lib.a(*.zzz)` with the wildcard still in the diagnostic. An archive that is not there behaves the same way.\n* Both halves glob, in their own places. `parse_file_seq` globs the archive name on the filesystem and only then asks that archive's index about the member, so `lib*.a(m.o)` works and costs no index scan.\n\nWhere it went. kati `src-rs/rule.rs` gains `file_sequence`, which is GNU's `parse_file_seq` in the order it does its three jobs — split into words, strip a leading `./`, reopen any archive group — and `glob_word` grew the archive branch that globs the two halves separately. The three sites that read a rule's names now share it: `eval.rs`'s `parse_rule_targets`, `rule.rs`'s `parse_inputs`, and `dep.rs`'s `.EXTRA_PREREQS` reader. The hot path is untouched: a word with none of `?*[` still interns without a syscall, and the group machinery is only entered for text holding a `(`.\n\nkati `src-rs/archive.rs` gains `member_is_a_pattern`, an `ar_scan` port that enumerates member names, and `glob_members`. The format read is the SysV/GNU one `ar` writes here, including the `//` long-name table and 4.4BSD `#1/LEN` extended names, and anything that does not parse is an archive with no members — which is what `ar_scan` reports to a caller that only wanted to match names.\n\nRecorded, not swept up: that scan is the second reader of the `ar` header format in the tree. `src/os/archive.rs` has the other, and it answers a different question — one member's date, at build time, on Ronin's side of a one-way crate dependency that kati cannot cross. Filed as make-archive-index-reader-is-written-twice.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-member-pattern-expands-against-the-archive-index (which carries the `q.o` decoy and the deliberately unsorted index), archive-member-pattern-that-matches-nothing-stands-as-written, and archive-member-pattern-and-name-expand-where-they-were-written. Five kati unit tests cover the pattern test, the index scan, sorting, long names and `FNM_PERIOD`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:27:26.019376578Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:29:38.872498647Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-suffix-rule-conversion
                title "Convert a .X.a suffix rule into its archive-member pattern too"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:17.541899528Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, where it was noted at filing and left there deliberately: until the archive name shape existed, the pattern this makes could not have fired.\n\n`convert_to_pattern` (reference/gnumake/src/rule.c:363) turns a suffix rule whose target suffix is `.a` into **two** pattern rules, not one: `%.a: %.X` and `(%.o): %.X`. Ronin's suffix conversion makes only the first. The second is what lets a makefile that writes its own `.c.a:` rule file an object straight into an archive.\n\nThere are no built-in `.X.a` entries, so this is reachable only from a makefile that writes one — which is why it is separate from the built-in catalogue and why it was not urgent. Now that `(%): %` fires and `$%` is bound, the conversion has somewhere to land.\n\nMeasured by GNU Make's own `features/archives` script, which the parent node leaves at eight compiler-class differences; this is one of the four pieces those eight are made of."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:26:30.136684195Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. A `.X.a:` suffix rule now makes both of the pattern rules GNU Make makes from it.\n\nProbed first, because the filing note asserted the conversion without saying which rules GNU actually installs. `make -p` over a makefile with `.SUFFIXES: .a .o .c` and a `.c.a:` recipe lists, in this order:\n\n    (%.o): %.c\n    %.a: %.c\n\nboth carrying the suffix rule's own recipe. So the conversion is two rules, the archive one first, and there are no built-in `.X.a` entries at all — `default.c`'s only archive rule is the pattern `(%): %` with `$(AR) $(ARFLAGS) $@ $<`, which the catalogue already had. The `$(AR) r $@ $*.o` + `$(RM) $*.o` shape the dispatch asked about is the GNU manual's example of a makefile-written `.c.a:` body, not a builtin; it is in the fixture's doc comment as the thing the conversion exists to serve.\n\nThe target pattern is literally `(%.o)` whatever the source suffix is — `convert_suffix_rule`'s null-target case writes it as a constant. That is what makes the two archive rules differ where it matters: matching `(foo.o)`, the converted rule binds `$*` to `foo` and `$<` to `foo.c`, where the built-in `(%): %` would bind them to `foo.o` and `foo.o`. Confirmed against the oracle, both halves: `at=libx.a pct=foo.o star=foo lt=foo.c` for the member, and `at=libz.a pct= star=libz lt=libz.c` for the `%.a` half.\n\nGating is `.SUFFIXES`, both ends. `convert_to_pattern` walks the declared list twice, so `.c.a:` converts only while both `.c` and `.a` are on it; with either withdrawn the rule makes nothing and GNU falls through to compiling the object and letting `(%): %` file it. Both directions verified, including a list cleared AFTER the rule was written — the list that counts is the one the last makefile left.\n\nThe corner that changed the implementation. The two conversions are installed independently, each with `new_pattern_rule`'s override off, so a makefile that also writes `%.a: %.c` by hand takes the name of the second and leaves the first standing — GNU still runs the `.c.a:` recipe for the member. The obvious implementation loses exactly that: deriving `(%.o): %.c` from whichever suffix rules survived kati's written-rule comparison finds nothing, the written rule having already emptied the entry. Caught by probing rather than by reasoning, and it is now a fixture of its own. So the `.X.a:` rules are kept aside in `DepBuilder::archive_suffix_rules` when they are read, and converted in `install_archive_suffix_rules` — after `.SUFFIXES` has settled, before the catalogue's `(%): %`, which is the order GNU installs them in and the reason the more specific pattern is reached first.\n\nWhere it went: kati `src-rs/dep.rs` — the new field, the capture in `populate_suffix_rule`, and `install_archive_suffix_rules` called from `install_builtin_rules`.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-suffix-rule-files-an-object-into-the-archive, archive-suffix-rule-also-makes-the-plain-archive-rule, archive-suffix-rule-survives-a-written-rule-taking-the-other-half."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:27:36.295754639Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:29:49.355896965Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-member-list-in-one-name
                title "Read several members named inside one pair of parentheses"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:32.012119616Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which reads exactly one member per archive name.\n\n`lib.a(a.o b.o)` is one word to the tokenizer and several targets to GNU Make: `parse_file_seq` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) reopens the parenthesis, splits the members on whitespace, and emits `lib.a(a.o)` and `lib.a(b.o)` as separate names. The same reopening handles `lib.a(a.o) lib.a(b.o)` written out longhand, which is why the two spellings behave identically in GNU Make and why a makefile is entitled to use either.\n\nRonin's splitter (kati `src-rs/archive.rs`) takes the whole of the parenthesised text as the member name, so `lib.a(a.o b.o)` looks for a member with a space in it. Nothing else is wrong: once the name is split into two, both halves already work, because everything downstream of the split — the `(%): %` rule, `$%`, the index lookup for freshness — is per-member and shipped.\n\nFront-end work at the same site as make-archive-member-globbing, and the two want doing together: both are `parse_file_seq` reopening a parenthesis, one splitting on whitespace and one expanding a pattern."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:26:10.99254707Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `lib.a(a.o b.o)` names two members, and does so at the same site as the member globbing — the two were done together, as the filing note said they wanted to be.\n\nThe tokenizer is a small state machine and every part of it is load-bearing. Measured against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle:\n\n* `lib.a(a.o b.o)` and `lib.a(a.o) lib.a(b.o)` produce the same two names. So do uneven runs of spaces and tabs.\n* A group only opens when some LATER word ends in `)`. GNU looks ahead over the remaining words before it reopens the parenthesis, and with none there the text is not a group at all — `lib.a(a.o b.o` names a file called `lib.a(a.o` and another called `b.o`, and the refusal names the first of those. That lookahead is the part an implementation is most likely to skip, and skipping it silently changes what a malformed line means.\n* A word that is only `lib.a(`, or only `)`, separates rather than names. That is what makes `lib.a(  a.o    b.o   )` mean what `lib.a(a.o b.o)` means, and it is why the suite's whitespace case passes.\n* A word beginning with `(` never opens a group, because the member name would be empty.\n* A closed group lets the words after it alone: `lib.a(a.o b.o) plain.txt` is three names, and `lib.a(a.o b.o) other.a(c.o)` is three too.\n* Globbing and listing compose in written order. `lib.a(c.o *.o)` gives `c.o a.o b.o` — each word inside the parentheses expanded on its own, where it stands, so a name keeps its place and a pattern contributes its sorted matches after it.\n* The split is textual and does not consult the index, so a list against an archive that does not exist yet still names every member.\n\nImplemented as `reopen_groups` in kati `src-rs/archive.rs`, reached from `file_sequence` in `src-rs/rule.rs` — see make-archive-member-globbing's note for the shared plumbing. Everything downstream of the split was already per-member and needed nothing: `$%`, the `(%): %` rule, the index lookup for freshness.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-member-list-in-one-name-names-every-member, archive-member-list-tolerates-spaces-inside-the-parentheses, archive-member-group-that-never-closes-is-not-a-group. Five kati unit tests cover the group shapes directly, including the two spellings asserted equal to each other."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:27:31.019997649Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:29:43.362036006Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-member-touch
                title "Touch an archive member's index date under -t"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:46.040308826Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which made the member's date readable but not writable.\n\n`-t` on an ordinary target is a `utime`. On `lib.a(member.o)` it cannot be: the date lives in the archive's own index, not in the filesystem, so GNU Make calls `ar_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c), which opens the archive, finds the member's header, and writes a fresh date into it in place — and warns and does nothing when the archive is one whose format has no date to write.\n\nRonin under `-t` treats the member name as a filename and touches a file called `lib.a(member.o)`, which is both wrong and litter.\n\nThe read half is already here — `src/os/archive.rs` locates a member's header to date it — so this is the same walk with a write at the end, plus the format check that decides whether writing is possible at all. Worth noting that on a modern Linux host `ar` defaults to deterministic mode and records every date as zero, so the touched date is the only real date such an archive will ever have."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:55:37.524545627Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "Blocked, and on a premise rather than on difficulty: this node was filed saying \"Ronin under `-t` treats the member name as a filename and touches a file called `lib.a(member.o)`, which is both wrong and litter.\" That is not what happens. `-t` does nothing at all — it is accepted and ignored, for every target, archive member or not.\n\nMeasured, ronin at e238d39 built release, invoked as `make` (argv[0]), against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle:\n\n    all: out.txt\n    out.txt: in.txt ; @echo \"REAL RECIPE RAN\" > out.txt\n\n  GNU `make -t`  → prints `touch out.txt`, out.txt is empty.\n  Ronin `make -t` → prints `[1/1] echo \"REAL RECIPE RAN\" > out.txt`, out.txt holds `REAL RECIPE RAN`.\n\nSo `-t` runs the recipe. `Switch::Touch` is read in exactly one place in the whole tree — src/make/cli.rs:498, the `propagated()` table that puts `-t` back into `MAKEFLAGS` for a sub-make. It is declared `OptionClass::NoOp` at src/make/cli.rs:250. There is no `touch` field on `BuildConfig` and no touch primitive on the disk interface.\n\nThere used to be. `Builder::touching`/`Builder::touch_outputs` in src/build/command.rs and `RealDiskInterface::touch` in src/os.rs arrived in aa4f3c3 and were removed in 95452af, \"refactor: Remove Make provenance from graph execution\".\n\nThat removal is authoritative, not incidental. docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md:99 lists \"src/build/command.rs: Make touch behavior\" under what the boundary removes, and the WBS disposition at :165-169 is explicit:\n\n  ### Accept without emulation (`N`)\n  - `make-option-output-sync`, `make-option-debug-and-trace`, `make-option-shuffle`,\n    and `make-option-touch-and-what-if` collapse into the complete interface table\n    owned by `make-interface-surface`. Their spellings are accepted; their Make\n    runtime behavior is not implemented.\n\nTouching an archive member's index date is therefore not the missing piece here. The missing piece is `-t` itself, and it is missing on purpose. Delivering this node means first reinstating the whole touch execution path — reversing a recorded architecture decision — and only then teaching it that a member's date lives in the archive index rather than in the filesystem. That is an operator's decision about the compiler boundary, not something to settle inside an archive dispatch.\n\nWhat the archive half would be, recorded now so it is not re-probed later. GNU's `ar_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c:132) parses the name, calls `f_mtime` on the archive, then `ar_member_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/arscan.c:923), which finds the member's header with `ar_member_pos`, opens the archive O_RDWR, `fstat`s it, and formats that mtime into the 12-byte `ar_date` field in place, space-padded. It writes the ARCHIVE's mtime, not \"now\", and it is read before the write, so the member ends a touch marginally older than the archive it sits in. The four outcomes, all measured against the oracle:\n\n  member present   → `touch lib.a(a.o)`, header rewritten, exit 0\n  member absent    → `make: touch: Member 'zzz.o' does not exist in 'lib.a'`, exit 2\n  archive absent   → `make: touch: Archive 'nope.a' does not exist`, exit 2, no file created\n  not an archive   → `make: touch: 'bad.a' is not a valid archive`, exit 2\n\nAnd the cell worth keeping: on a deterministic archive (`ar rc`, every date zero, so every member reads as absent), `-t` is the only thing that will ever put a real date in it. Measured — before `ar tv` shows `Jan 1 1970`, after `make -t` it shows the archive's own mtime, and the member is up to date from then on, so the second `make -t` says `'all' is up to date`. `-n` and `-q` leave the index alone; `-q` exits 1.\n\nNot a blocker for the other three nodes or for the `.FEATURES` advertisement: GNU's own `features/archives` script, which is the measure for `archives`, never invokes `-t`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:01:39.271799271Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-17, explicit: implement `-t` fully. This reverses the accept-without-emulation disposition recorded for `make-option-touch-and-what-if` in docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md:165-169, under which the touch path was removed in 95452af. `docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md` is updated to carry the reversal and its reason.\n\nWhy the original disposition did not hold. The `N` bucket is for options whose Make behaviour is reporting or scheduling — things `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1]` explicitly excludes from conformance. `-t` is not one of those. It decides what the run writes to disk, and filesystem effects ARE a conformance criterion under that same rule. Measured before this dispatch, `make -t` on a two-rule Makefile ran the recipes and overwrote the outputs: not an unimplemented no-op but the opposite of what was asked for.\n\nA second operator ruling, same day, scoped the reversal: the BEHAVIOUR lands as specified, the NARRATION does not. The brief's `print `touch <file>`` was withdrawn — that is GNU's runtime voice and goes through the same lens as every other Make-voiced output. So a touched edge is reported by the ordinary `[N/M]` progress line under `[spec:ronin:req:make.narration+1]`, and no fixture records GNU's `touch <file>` stdout. The fixtures gate the filesystem effect, which is what the harness can see and what the semantics rule makes binding.\n\nThe matrix as implemented, each cell measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n  ordinary target, out of date  → output dated, recipe not run, exit 0\n  target with no recipe         → not touched (nothing to stand in for)\n  `.PHONY` target               → not touched, recipe not run, no file of that\n                                  name invented. GNU declines it in\n                                  `update_file_1`; Ronin declines an edge whose\n                                  `always_dirty` is set, which is `.PHONY`.\n  `-n` with `-t`                → `-n` wins: reported, nothing written\n  `-s` with `-t`                → touched, nothing reported\n  `-q` with `-t`                → `-q` wins: question answered, nothing runs\n  MAKEFLAGS                     → carries `t`; a recursive child sees `-t`\n  makefile remake pass          → `-t` is turned OFF for it, as GNU turns it off\n                                  across `update_goal_chain`: a makefile left\n                                  dated and unwritten would make the read guess\n                                  what it said. Measured: GNU really runs the\n                                  remake recipe under `-t`.\n  makefile the command line named → keeps `-t` and IS touched rather than\n                                  remade, GNU's `file->cmd_target` exception.\n                                  Measured: `touch bye.mk`, no REMADE.\n  `::` chain and deferred rules → the file the Makefile named is touched, not\n                                  the `.ronin_grouped_join/N` name the graph\n                                  invented to sequence them. Found by the\n                                  upstream suite's own `options/dash-t` TEST 0,\n                                  which failed with `No such file or directory`\n                                  until the indirection was followed.\n\n  lib.a(member.o), present      → the member's index entry is dated, exit 0\n  lib.a(member.o), absent       → `Member 'zzz.o' does not exist in 'lib.a'`,\n                                  exit 2\n  archive absent                → `Archive 'nope.a' does not exist`, exit 2,\n                                  no file created\n  not an archive                → `'bad.a' is not a valid archive`, exit 2\n  deterministic archive         → the cell worth keeping: `ar` writes every date\n                                  as zero, so every member reads as absent, and\n                                  `-t` is the only thing that will ever put a\n                                  real date in that index. Measured identical to\n                                  GNU: before, `ar tv` says Jan 1 1970; after, it\n                                  says the archive's own mtime; the second run\n                                  finds it up to date.\n\nThe three archive failures are reported in Ronin's own shape — `ronin: build stopped: <reason>.` and exit 2 — rather than GNU's `make: touch: <reason>`. They are failures that must be reported at all, which is exactly the case the narration contract allows, and the information (which of the three ways it could not be filed) is what the diagnostic carries.\n\n`ar_member_touch` is `src/os/archive.rs::touch_member`, the same walk `member_date` makes stopped one step earlier — at the header's address rather than the date in it — with three details of GNU's `arscan.c:923` kept: the date written is the ARCHIVE's mtime and is read before the write, so the member ends fractionally behind the file holding it; the field is space-padded to its full 12 bytes so no tail of a longer previous date survives; and a member whose recorded date is zero still counts as found, because dating it is the point.\n\n`-W` (`--what-if`/`--new-file`/`--assume-new`) is NOT part of this ruling and stays an accepted no-op. Filed as make-what-if-assumed-new."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:05:21.638451061Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-escaped-percent-prerequisite-pattern-keeps-its-backslash
                title "Leave a prerequisite pattern that holds no wildcard exactly as it was written"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:02:45.199214188Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-static-pattern-escaped-percent's adjacent cells against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle; left out of it because the target-pattern side is one refusal and this is a substitution that has to stop rewriting.\n\nGNU Make compresses the percent escaping into a COPY of a prerequisite pattern and throws the copy away when no wildcard survived it. file.c:476 `enter_prereqs`:\n\n    char *nm = alloca (nl);\n    memcpy (nm, dp->name, nl);\n    percent = find_percent (nm);\n    if (percent) { ... o = patsubst_expand_pat (...); dp->name = ...; }\n\nso `dp->name` is untouched when `percent` is null, and a prerequisite pattern that is not a pattern reaches the build spelt exactly as the makefile wrote it — backslash included. kati's `strutil::substitute_stem` returns `find_percent`'s reduced text on that branch instead of the original.\n\nReproducer, with both `\\%.c` and `%.c` on disk:\n\n    a.o b.o: %.o: \\%.c ; @echo \"ran[$@]<$<\" >> log\n\nGNU writes `ran[a.o]<\\%.c` and reads the file called `\\%.c`; Ronin writes `ran[a.o]<%.c` and reads the one called `%.c`. Two different files, so this is build intent and not wording. With neither on disk the refusals name different files too — GNU `No rule to make target '\\%.c'`, Ronin `'%.c'`.\n\nThe one-line half is `substitute_stem` returning `prerequisite.clone()` rather than the reduced text when `find_percent` found nothing. What makes this a node rather than that line is the implicit-rule counterpart, which diverges further and in a different direction:\n\n    %.o: \\%.c ; @echo \"made $@ from $<\" > log\n    all: a.o\n\nGNU refuses outright — `No rule to make target 'a.o', needed by 'all'` — even with `\\%.c` present, so `pattern_search` is doing something to a non-pattern prerequisite that is not merely \"use the name as written\". Ronin matches the rule, builds `a.o` from `%.c`, and carries on into the builtin link rule. Whoever takes this has to establish what implicit.c does with such a prerequisite before changing the shared substitution, because both paths run through `substitute_stem`.\n\nThe neighbouring cells already agree and are fixtured: an ordinary rule's `\\%.o` prerequisite keeps its backslash in both tools, and `.PRECIOUS: \\%.b` names a file in both since make-static-pattern-escaped-percent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:19:21.157973638Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:39:04.76813331Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's warning was right to insist: `implicit.c` does something to a non-pattern prerequisite that is not \"use the name as written\" and not \"compress the escaping\" either. It is a THIRD answer, and finding it turned a one-line change into a split.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, read out of the source before anything moved and then measured cell by cell.\n\nA static pattern rule's prerequisites go through `enter_prereqs` (file.c:476) with the stem in hand. It compresses the escaping into a COPY, `nm`, and uses the copy only inside `if (percent)` — so a prerequisite pattern that holds no wildcard reaches the build spelt exactly as the makefile wrote it, backslash included.\n\nAn implicit rule's prerequisites go nowhere near it. `record_files` (read.c:2085) enters prerequisites only `if (! pattern && ! implicit_percent)`, with the comment saying why — \"We don't want to enter pattern rules at all so that we don't think that they ought to exist\". So a pattern rule's dependency text is stored RAW, and `pattern_search` (implicit.c:562) then finds its wildcard with\n\n    const char *cp = strchr (nptr, '%');\n\nwhich knows nothing about escaping and never compresses anything. The backslash is just a byte of the name, ahead of the stem. That is the answer the filing could not have guessed from the refusal it saw: `%.o: \\%.c` does not look for `\\%.c` and does not look for `%.c` — it looks for `\\a.c`, and refuses `a.o` when that file is not there.\n\nTHE MATRIX, fifteen cells, each run through both binaries with the files written by exact bytes rather than by a shell that would eat the backslashes. GNU on the left, and every one of them agrees now.\n\n  static  \\%.c   disk \\%.c        reads `\\%.c`              was: read `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk %.c         refuses `\\%.c`            was: built from `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk both        reads `\\%.c`              was: read `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk neither     refuses `\\%.c`            was: refused `%.c`\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk \\a.c        reads `\\a.c`              agreed already\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk a.c         refuses `\\a.c`            agreed already\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk \\\\a.c       refuses `\\a.c`            agreed already\n  implicit \\%.c  disk \\a.c        makes a.o from `\\a.c`     was: refused a.o\n  implicit \\%.c  disk \\%.c        refuses a.o               agreed already\n  implicit \\%.c  disk %.c         refuses a.o               was: built from `%.c`\n  implicit \\%.c  disk a.c         refuses a.o (both)        agreed already\n  implicit \\\\%.c disk \\\\a.c       makes a.o from `\\\\a.c`    was: refused a.o\n  implicit \\\\%.c disk \\a.c        refuses a.o               was: built from `\\a.c`\n  ordinary \\%.c  disk \\%.c        reads `\\%.c`              agreed already\n  ordinary \\%.c  disk %.c         refuses `\\%.c`            agreed already\n\nThe ordinary rule is the third code path and needed nothing: `enter_prereqs (deps, NULL)` skips the whole `if (stem)` block, so the name is never touched.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, kati 1e68f7f7396c2830138bd9c48183576e845b42af on branch nplan/make-escaped-percent-prerequisite-pattern-keeps-its-backslash. One substitution became two, because GNU asks the question in two places that share no routine.\n\n* `strutil::substitute_stem` is the implicit search's, and it stops calling `find_percent` at all: a raw `memchr(b'%')` into the raw text, which is `strchr (nptr, '%')`. Its only caller is `PatternMatch::prerequisite`, and every caller of THAT is the implicit search.\n* `Pattern::append_subst_prerequisite` is the static pattern rule's, added beside `append_subst`: when no wildcard survives the compression it hands back the ORIGINAL text rather than the compressed copy, which is `enter_prereqs` throwing `nm` away. `apply_output_pattern`'s generic branch is the one call site.\n\n`Pattern::append_subst` is deliberately left as it was, and the reason is a cell that would have broken had the branch gone into the primitive: `func_patsubst` runs `find_percent` over the replacement IN PLACE and uses what it left either way, so `$(patsubst abc,\\%,abc)` is a literal percent and must stay one. The two callers want different answers out of the same compression, which is why the difference is a caller's and not the routine's. Measured in both tools before and after.\n\nADJACENT CELLS PROBED and unmoved, twelve of them: a prerequisite whose second `%` is a literal (`x%y%.c`), an escaped percent AHEAD of the wildcard in both rule forms (`a\\%b%.c` — static reads `a%ba.c`, implicit refuses), the directory hold-aside (`%.x: 6%` on `lib/bye.x`), plain `%.c` in both forms, a prerequisite with no percent in both forms, both forms under `.SECONDEXPANSION:`, `$(patsubst)`'s escaped replacement, and a suffix rule.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, each gating file CONTENTS rather than existence so that which file was read is the evidence:\n\n* `tests/make/a-static-patterns-escaped-prerequisite-is-not-a-pattern` — `\\%.c` and `%.c` both on disk with different contents, plus the `\\\\%.c` cell as its guard. On the parent, `a.o` and `b.o` hold `bare` where GNU leaves `escaped`.\n* `tests/make/an-implicit-rules-escaped-prerequisite-is-not-escaped-at-all` — `\\a.c`, `\\\\a.c`, `a.c` and `\\%.c` all on disk, and the rule picks two of them. On the parent the rule does not match at all and the build falls through to the builtin `cc` rule and FAILS.\n\nTeeth measured in a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4 rather than assumed. kati unit test `an_escaped_prerequisite_is_one_name_in_each_rule_form` holds both directions and the `$(patsubst)` cell beside them.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 900 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156/0/37/1106, tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential.\n\nA NOTE ON RUNNING THE GATES: two of them started concurrently both died with `No such file or directory (os error 2)` on an unrelated corpus case. They share a work directory, so they have to be run one at a time; run serially each is green."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:40:49.681188141Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell
                title "Launch a recipe read at graph construction line by line too"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:34:08.762666165Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The residue make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell left, recorded rather than hidden: two shapes of recipe still reach one shell as one assembled script.\n\nA recipe expanded when its edge launches is handed over as the sequence of launches GNU Make would perform. A recipe expanded at graph construction is not, because it never goes through that boundary at all — the sink emits its command into the graph as a rule binding, and the launch answers `LateBinding::Settled`. `defers_recipe` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) refuses four kinds for reasons that have nothing to do with this: a recursive `$(MAKE)` line becomes a child graph, a declared or detected depfile rewrites the script, a grouped double-colon action and a `$?` are values the scheduler binds against an edge that has to declare them. Of the four, `$?` is the one an ordinary Makefile reaches.\n\n    out: a b\n    \t@printf '%s' 'x$?\n    \ty' > out\n\nis the reproducer shape: an open quote across two lines in a recipe naming `$?`. GNU Make fails; Ronin joins and writes the file, exactly as it did for every recipe before the per-line launch landed.\n\nThe second shape is narrower and is a decision rather than an omission: a recipe holding a command line longer than the 100,000-byte response-file threshold keeps the assembled script, because the response file such a line needs is named per edge (`<output>.rsp`) and several long lines in one recipe would want one name. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` is that test.\n\nWhat it would take. For the first shape, the sink would have to carry the per-line steps it already has — `translate_recipe` produces them for every recipe, deferred or not — across the boundary for an edge whose command it also settled, which is a second kind of entry in `PendingRecipes` rather than a new mechanism. The `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` substitution would then have to reach the step holding `$?` rather than the whole command, which is one call site in `Builder::prepared_steps`. For the second, a response file named per step rather than per edge.\n\nNeither is urgent. Both are the old behaviour rather than a new defect, and the second needs a hundred-kilobyte recipe line to reach."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:41:16.912249235Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:23:28.6585698Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered as the filing sketched it, after a smaller-looking alternative was tried first and MEASURED WRONG — which is the part of this worth reading.\n\nTHE ALTERNATIVE THAT FAILED, and why it is recorded rather than forgotten. `defers_recipe` refuses a `$?` recipe, and `NewInputsTiming::Launch` already exists and already answers `$?` from the timestamps on disk at expansion time — which is where GNU Make's `set_file_variables` reads it in `new_job`. So the obvious move was to delete the refusal: a deferred recipe already launches line by line, and the reproducer passed on the first build. Three more cells improved with it, all measured against the oracle: `$(?D)`/`$(?F)` started answering about the names instead of the placeholder, an `$(error)` in the recipe of an up-to-date target stopped firing, and the grouped double-colon cell agreed.\n\nThen the corpus caught it: SEVEN cases fail, because the launch-time stat loop is a strictly weaker model than GNU's `d->changed`.\n\n  an-archive-member-is-current-in-the-second-it-was-filed   `$?` empty, GNU `old.o`\n  an-archive-rule-refiles-the-members-that-are-new         nothing filed\n  an-empty-recipe-leaves-its-target-absent                 `ran []`, GNU `ran [mid bare]`\n  circular-dependency-leaves-the-prerequisite-list         `q=[]`, GNU `q=[b c]`\n  question-variable-drops-a-member-the-archive-already-holds\n  question-variable-names-an-archive-member-by-its-member\n  variable-automatic-absent-prerequisite-is-in-question    `[]`, GNU `[bar]`\n\nThree shapes a stat cannot see: a prerequisite that does not exist counts as changed for GNU and is not newer than anything on disk; one remade without its mtime moving — an empty recipe, a phony, a restat — counts and does not stat as newer; and an archive member's index date is not a file's. The scheduler's list is the accurate one, which is why the edge declares it, and `NewInputsTiming::Launch` is a fallback for a `$?` the compiler could not see rather than an equal. The refusal is load-bearing. Reverted.\n\nWHAT LANDED, kati 21f2b5fa0f7d1d93922e5f3961cfba7751d8340e on branch nplan/make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell, and it is the filing's own sketch.\n\n`SinkRule::steps` carries `translate_recipe`'s per-line launches for a rule whose command was SETTLED, beside that command. The assembled script stays the rule's command — a progress line, a log entry and a `-n` all want the whole of it — and a destination that can launch several runs the steps instead. `GraphSink` builds them into `LateStep`s with the unit's own layout as it declares the rule, hands them to the edge that names it, and `PendingRecipes` grows a second map keyed by edge with no session and no expansion behind it. `LateBinding::Steps` is the answer that says \"the command stands; these are the processes\", and `Builder::prepared_steps` was already substituting `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` per step, so the placeholder reaches the step holding `$?` and no other — the one call site the filing named.\n\nONE DEFECT THIS INTRODUCED AND THE INVENTORY CAUGHT, worth recording because the gate is the only reason it was found. Three rows of GNU Make's own suite moved out of `narration` — `targets/WAIT.diff`, `.1` and `.2` — and the reproducer is `all: .WAIT pre1 ... ; @echo '?=$?'`. kati's `direct_argv` had decided that line needed no shell, so the step was an argv to exec and `prepared_steps` substitutes only into shell launches; the recipe printed `?=${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` where GNU printed `?=pre1 pre2`. `recipe_steps` now refuses a direct launch for any line still holding `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`: `construct_command_argv` decides from the FINISHED text and a scheduler placeholder is not it. The line goes to a shell, which is where the substitution lands, and where the whole recipe went before it was one line at a time. Inventory back to 156/0/37/1106 with the tsv byte-identical.\n\nFIXTURE. `tests/make/a-recipe-naming-new-inputs-dies-where-its-line-does` is the filing's own reproducer, oracle-recorded and written so the refusal is the evidence: `out` is never written. Teeth measured in a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4, where it writes `xa b ) && (y` — the join's own `) && (` fallen inside the quote and become data.\n\nTHE SECOND SHAPE, decided rather than delivered, as the filing allowed. A recipe holding a line over the 100,000-byte response-file threshold keeps the assembled script and its single launch, because the response file such a line needs is named per edge and several long lines would want one name. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` is that test and the settled path asks it too. Delivering the sketched alternative — a response file named per step — means `PreparedStep` carrying its own, written before that step spawns and removed after it finishes, which is a change to the build engine's response-file lifetime and to `take_step`, and it has to leave every Ninja edge exactly as it is. Measured cost against measured benefit: it needs a hundred-kilobyte recipe LINE whose recipe also breaks its syntax across lines. Not taken here; filed as make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell with the two composed shapes.\n\nNote that the threshold is tested per STEP and not on the assembled script, so a recipe of 500 short lines totalling 138KB now launches line by line where it used to reach one shell through a response file. Measured against the oracle: 500 lines written, both tools, identical.\n\nWHAT STILL REACHES ONE SHELL, measured after the fix rather than predicted, all three with the same open-quote reproducer: a recipe beside a recursive `$(MAKE)` (the edge runs a segment, not the whole recipe), one with a declared depfile (the extraction rewrites the script, and it is reachable only through kati's `.KATI_DEPFILE` or `--detect_depfiles`), and one holding a line over the threshold. The grouped double-colon kind the filing named is FIXED and agrees. All three are on make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell with what each would take.\n\nTWO PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS FOUND BY THESE PROBES, both unmoved by this fix and both reproducing identically on the parent, each filed with an oracle-recorded corpus case carrying a `divergence` file naming its owner: make-new-inputs-path-forms-read-the-placeholder (`$(?D)` answers `.` and `$(?F)` the whole placeholder, where GNU answers `d .` and `a b`) and make-a-scheduler-bound-recipe-is-expanded-though-its-target-is-current (an `$(error)` in a `$?` recipe fires for a target that is already up to date). Both were briefly fixed by the alternative above, which is how they were found and what their notes record about the cost of fixing them properly. A third, make-always-make-rebuilds-nothing, came out of the same matrix and is unrelated to recipes: `-B` builds nothing at all.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 900 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 853 cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 0 disagreements — the manifest is byte-identical, which it has to be since a manifest writer ignores the new field by nature; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156/0/37/1106, tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 713 / 0 in a cold target directory; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 36s — no slower than the runs before this change, which is expected: only a recipe the compiler had to read for itself gains a process per line, and neither project has one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:31:17.251403752Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffle-reorders-order-only-prerequisites-apart-from-the-rest
                title "Shuffle a target's order-only prerequisites in the same chain as the rest"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:03.159371574Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text probe
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:14:04.854803672Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Ignore the one-word \"probe\" entry above it — that was written to find out which log kinds the image lint accepts, and nothing removes a log entry once written.\n\nFound while probing make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-walked-position over the order-only cells, and separate from it: that node is about which entry a circular drop takes out of the list, and this is about the list the shuffle permutes at all.\n\nGNU Make carries a target's normal and order-only prerequisites in ONE `->next` chain, distinguished by `d->ignore_mtime` rather than by being in separate lists, and `shuffle_deps_recursive` permutes that one chain. Ronin keeps `deps` and `order_onlys` apart and `crate::shuffle::reorder` permutes each on its own, so the two groups shuffle within themselves and never across.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, and note there is no circular dependency anywhere in it:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a b | c d\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n    a: ; @echo a\n    b: ; @echo b\n    c: ; @echo c\n    d: ; @echo d\n\n  make                     a b c d, hat=[a b] bar=[c d]  — both tools agree\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU builds d c b a / Ronin builds b a d c\n                           hat and bar agree in both, because the automatic\n                           variables read the written order either way\n\nSo what differs is only the build order, and only when a shuffle is asked for. Nothing gates on it and no upstream row reads it.\n\nIt shows through the circular drop too, and there it reaches `$|`, because the drop takes the entry standing at the walk's position and GNU's position counts across the boundary Ronin's two lists put between them:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | b t c\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU bar=[t c] / Ronin bar=[b c]\n\n  Written chain [a, b, t, c], reversed [c, t, b, a]. GNU walks index 1, reads\n  `t` from the shuffled chain, and unlinks written position 1 — which is `b`, a\n  member of the OTHER group. Ronin's order-only walk reaches `t` at index 1 of\n  its own three-entry list and unlinks `b` from that list's position 1... which\n  happens to be `t` itself in the reversed list, so it drops the loop and leaves\n  `b`. The two answers coincide only when the groups happen to line up.\n\nWhere. `crate::shuffle::reorder` (kati/src-rs/shuffle.rs) calls `draw.permute` on `node.deps` and `node.order_onlys` separately. Matching GNU means permuting the concatenation and splitting it back, with `actual_inputs` and `actual_order_only_inputs` left alone as they already are — but the split-back is the hard half, because an entry may cross from one group to the other under the permutation and the two lists are what the graph's compared and order-only edges are built from. GNU has no such split to preserve: `ignore_mtime` travels on the entry, so its chain can hold them in any order at all. The honest shape here is probably for `DepNode` to carry one prerequisite list with a per-entry order-only flag, which is a wider change than the shuffle.\n\nWorth settling before it is matched: whether `.WAIT`'s synthesised order-only prerequisites should travel in the shuffled chain too. The evaluator turns each barrier into order-only edges before this point, so they would be permuted with everything else and the barrier would stop meaning what it said — GNU dodges the question by leaving a list holding a single `.WAIT` group alone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:38:48.253416578Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Probed shuffle.c to settle how GNU Make shuffles order-only prerequisites, and the answer is that it does not shuffle them apart at all — there is nothing to shuffle apart, because there is only one list.\n\n`shuffle_deps` (reference/gnumake/src/shuffle.c:152) takes `struct dep *deps`, counts the whole `->next` chain, copies every entry into one array, permutes that array, and writes the result back as a second `->shuf` chain over the same entries. It never looks at `ignore_mtime`. `shuffle_file_deps_recursive` (:190) hands it `f->deps` and nothing else — there is no second call for a second list, because `f->deps` already holds a target's normal and order-only prerequisites together and tells them apart per entry.\n\nThat per-entry flag is the whole mechanism. `update_file_1` walks the WRITTEN chain and reads the SHUFFLED one at each step — `d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du` (remake.c:596) — and everything it then asks about the prerequisite it asks of `d`: `d->ignore_mtime`, `d->file`, `d->wait_here`. So order-only-ness travels with the entry under the permutation, which is exactly what lets GNU permute across the boundary Ronin's two lists draw.\n\nTwo switches gate the whole thing and both are already Ronin's: `sm_none` returns early, and `not_parallel` returns early (:225). `.WAIT` is the third — `shuffle_deps` returns without touching a list holding one (:162), where Ronin needs no exception because the evaluator has already turned the barrier into edges.\n\nThe chain genuinely interleaves, which is the finding that decides the cost. The note above reproduced the divergence from a single rule line, where the written chain happens to be normals-then-order-onlys and a group-preserving split could have been mistaken for a fix. It is not one — across rule lines the groups alternate:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | c\n    t: b | d\n    \\t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make                     GNU builds b d a c;  Ronin builds b a d c\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU builds c a d b;  Ronin builds a b c d\n  hat=[b a] bar=[d c] in both tools under every mode — the automatic\n  variables already agree, because both read the written order.\n\nSo GNU's chain here is [b(normal), d(order-only), a(normal), c(order-only)] and reversing it gives [c, a, d, b]. Ronin holds the same entries as deps=[b,a] and order_onlys=[d,c] — the correct per-group orders, with the interleaving discarded at construction. Permuting the concatenation and splitting it back by group reproduces exactly what Ronin already does, because the walk visits deps before order_onlys whatever the split says. The interleaving is not recoverable after the fact; it has to be recorded where the two lists are first written to.\n\nWhat the fix costs, measured rather than guessed. Five sites push a prerequisite (kati/src-rs/dep.rs:3016, 3929, 3934, 4000, 4023, 5365, 5398 — two of them synthetic: the double-colon serialisation and the `.WAIT` barriers, which have no written position at all), and three consumers read the order: `drop_circular_dependencies` (dep.rs:2513), `populate_ninja_node` (ninja.rs:703), and the emission at ninja.rs:1300. `drop_prerequisite` is the subtle one — it takes the edge out by the loop-closer's identity and the NAME out by the walk's position, and under one chain those two positions live in different groups, which is the whole of the `$|` cell below.\n\nDeliberately not attempted in this dispatch, and the reason is blast radius rather than difficulty. Making the walk honour a combined order changes the order edges are minted in for EVERY Makefile, not only shuffled ones, and mint order is what the equivalence gate compares and what the upstream inventory's 1101 compiler rows are recorded against. Gating the combined walk on a live shuffle would contain that, at the price of two representations of one chain. Neither is a change to make beside four other deliveries and a full gate run, for a divergence that moves no file, no exit code and no automatic variable — only the order two independent targets are built in, under a debugging switch.\n\nWhat is still owed, and it is the honest reason the node stays open: the circular drop under a shuffle, which is the one place this reaches something observable.\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | b t c\n    \\t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU bar=[t c] / Ronin bar=[b c]\n\n  Written chain [a, b, t, c] reversed to [c, t, b, a]. GNU stands at written\n  index 1 — `b`, an order-only entry — reads `t` off the shuffled chain, finds\n  it updating, and unlinks the entry it was standing on. Ronin's order-only\n  walk reaches `t` at index 1 of its own three-entry list and unlinks that\n  list's position 1, which is `t` itself. The two coincide only when the groups\n  happen to line up."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makefiles-variable-entries-are-never-remade
                title "Remake the Makefiles the MAKEFILES variable named"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:45:51.277130728Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised, and separate from it: that node asked what happens when a `MAKEFILES` entry will not open, and this asks what happens when one is not there and a rule says how to make it.\n\nGNU Make reads the variable's names with `eval_makefile (name, RM_NO_DEFAULT_GOAL|RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:204), and the goaldep that comes back joins `read_files` like any other. So the makefile update considers it, remakes it if a rule says how, and restarts the read on what it wrote — `RM_DONTCARE` forgives the failure, it does not excuse the attempt.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: ; @echo all X=$(X)\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GEN; echo X=1 > gen.mk\n\n  make MAKEFILES=gen.mk\n    GNU 4.4.1  GEN / `all X=1`, gen.mk on disk, exit 0\n    Ronin      `all X=`, no gen.mk, exit 0\n\nBoth exit 0 and neither reports anything, so nothing gates on it; what differs is that GNU built the file and read it and Ronin did neither, which means a Makefile that expects the variable to bootstrap a fragment gets an empty one.\n\nWhere. `read_makefiles_entry` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) returns `Ok(())` for a name that is not there and never calls `note_read_makefile` or `note_unread_include`, so the name reaches neither `read_makefiles` nor `missing_includes`, and `plan_regeneration` has nothing to make a root of. The `include` path does both and works. The fix is for the `MAKEFILES` path to note the name too, as not required — the forgiven grouping already exists and `-include` travels through it — and to note it whether the open failed with ENOENT or otherwise, since a `MAKEFILES` entry is forgiven either way.\n\nWorth settling with it, because the same line decides both: an entry that IS read should be noted as a read makefile as well, so that a `MAKEFILES` fragment which later becomes out of date restarts the read the way an `include`d one does. `read_makefiles_entry` does call `note_read_makefile(name, false)` on the arm where the file opened, so that half may already hold — probe it rather than assume, since nothing gates it today."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:39:56.580795773Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:34:49.090084391Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
