            {
                id make-an-output-directory-is-created-where-gnu-make-would-refuse
                title "Leave an output's directory to the recipe the way GNU Make does"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:46:09.580067796Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while looking for a fixture shape for make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised, and nothing to do with includes: it is Ninja's behaviour reaching a Makefile that never asked for it.\n\nNinja creates the directory an edge's output sits in before it launches the command, because a Ninja manifest is generated and its generator is entitled to assume it. GNU Make does not: `$@`'s directory is the recipe's problem, which is why every Makefile in the world writes `@mkdir -p $(@D)` and why automake generates it. Ronin runs Make's recipes through Ninja's launcher, so it does the creation too — invisible when the directory is absent, and a failure when the name is taken.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: ; @echo \"all X=$(X)\"\n    afile/one.mk: ; @echo GEN; rm -f afile; mkdir afile; echo X=1 > afile/one.mk\n    include afile/one.mk\n\nwith `afile` an ordinary file, so `afile/one.mk` cannot exist and the recipe's first act is to clear the way.\n\n  make all\n    GNU 4.4.1  GEN / `all X=1`, exit 0 — the recipe removed the file, made the\n               directory, wrote the fragment, and the read started over\n    Ronin      `ronin: build stopped: File exists.`, exit 2 — the launcher tried\n               to create `afile/` before the recipe could remove `afile`\n\nThe recipe is not exotic; replacing a file with a directory of the same name is what a build does when a layout changes. And the shape generalises past that: any recipe whose own first job is to arrange its output's directory is one Ronin has already tried to arrange for it, and the two only ever agree by luck.\n\nNot measured, and worth measuring before deciding: whether any of the corpus depends on the creation happening. Ronin's Make mode compiles a graph and hands it to the same launcher a manifest uses, so the switch cannot be global — a Ninja manifest must keep Ninja's behaviour. The honest shape is probably a per-edge property set by the Make sink and left off by the manifest parser, beside the ones the sink already sets."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:39:09.252481803Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Decided: recorded intentional divergence. Ronin keeps creating an output's directory before the recipe runs, and this node is retired rather than fixed.\n\nThe reproducer stands and was re-confirmed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with `afile` an ordinary file:\n\n    all: ; @echo \"all X=$(X)\"\n    afile/one.mk: ; @echo GEN; rm -f afile; mkdir afile; echo X=1 > afile/one.mk\n    include afile/one.mk\n\n  make all   GNU 4.4.1  GEN / `all X=1`, exit 0\n             Ronin      `ronin: build stopped: File exists.` and\n                        `Makefile:3: afile/one.mk: Not a directory`, exit 2\n\nSo the divergence is real, is not narration, and costs a build that GNU completes. It is recorded anyway, because the alternative is worse than the defect.\n\nWhy. The creation is `Builder::prepare_edge` (src/build.rs:1159) calling `make_dirs` over every completion output before it launches, which is Ninja's behaviour and is applied to every graph Ronin runs. Removing it globally is not on the table: a Ninja manifest is generated and its generator is entitled to assume the directory exists, and the Ninja conformance suite gates on it. So a fix means a per-edge property that the Make sink sets and the manifest parser leaves off — which is precisely what plan/decisions/make-compiles-to-ninja.md forbids.\n\ndocs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md states the invariant it applies: \"Kati evaluates that input and produces one valid Ninja-semantic graph. Ronin then plans, schedules, persists, runs, and reports that graph exactly as it does a graph loaded from a Ninja manifest. No Make provenance may cross that boundary.\" The audit permits graph properties in src/graph.rs \"when it represents a Ninja graph idiom such as `.PHONY`\". \"Do not create my output's directory\" is not a Ninja graph idiom — Ninja has no spelling for it and no manifest can ask for it. An edge property that exists only to make the executor behave differently because the graph came from a Makefile is Make provenance in the graph and a second executor in all but name, which is the `R` class the audit exists to keep out.\n\nThe operator ruling recorded in make-narration-contract-audit points the same way from the other side: the runtime speaks Ninja. It says that about output; the same boundary decides behaviour.\n\nWhat is given up, stated plainly so nobody rediscovers it as a bug. Any recipe whose own first job is to arrange its output's directory — replacing a file with a directory of the same name is the shape that reaches it — is one Ronin has already tried to arrange for. Where the name is free the creation is invisible and the two tools agree; where the name is taken by a non-directory, Ronin stops and GNU does not. Nothing in tests/make, the vendored kati corpus or the upstream inventory depends on either answer, which is why this surfaced from a hand-written probe rather than from a gate.\n\nIf this is ever reopened it should be reopened as a question about the executor's contract for every frontend — \"who owns an output's directory\" — and not as a Make special case. That is a decision at plan/decisions level, not a per-edge flag."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:17:52.866958472Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:34:59.871857388Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-makefile-whose-recipe-lost-is-listed-under-keep-going
                title "List a makefile whose own recipe lost among the ones not remade"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:29:14.208543227Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make, and left out of it at a seam GNU Make itself draws: that node ported the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary for a makefile nothing knows how to MAKE, and this is the same line for one whose own recipe RAN AND LOST.\n\nThe summary comes from one place for both — `main.c`'s `us_failed` arm (reference/gnumake/src/main.c:2544) walking `read_files` after the update has returned, naming every entry it cares about that was left `updated` with a failing status:\n\n    else if (! (d->flags & RM_DONTCARE))\n      {\n        OS (error, &d->floc, _(\"Failed to remake makefile '%s'.\"), d->file->name);\n        ...\n        makefile_status = MAKE_FAILURE;\n        any_failed = 1;\n      }\n\nbut the two ways of being left that way arrive by different routes, and Ronin's are in different modules: the refusal is kati's, held on the plan and raised by the frontend, while a recipe that lost is the build's and is reported where the pass fails.\n\nThe filing for the parent node said this summary is reached only under `-k`. That is wrong, and GNU Make's own suite says so — see the cells below. The switch decides whether the update RETURNS; a forgiven failure is another way it returns, and then the summary appears without `-k` at all.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin as it is on the right.\n\n  a read makefile that is stale and whose rule fails\n      include gen.mk / gen.mk: src ; @echo GENFAIL; exit 1   (gen.mk exists, backdated)\n    make      GENFAIL / `*** [Makefile:2: gen.mk] Error 1`               / Ronin: the same, in Ninja's words\n    make -k   the same, then `Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'gen.mk'.` / Ronin: no summary\n    Note there is no complaint on either line — the read GOT this file, so\n    `goal->error` is zero and `show_goal_error` says nothing. The summary is\n    about the update's verdict alone.\n\n  a required include whose rule fails and whose file was never read\n      include inc_a.mk / -include inc_b.mk / %_a.mk %_b.mk:; exit 1\n    make      `exit 1` / `Makefile:2: inc_a.mk: No such file or directory` /\n              `*** [Makefile:4: inc_a.mk] Error 1`   — Ronin matches this today\n    make -k   the same three, then `Makefile:2: Failed to remake makefile 'inc_a.mk'.`\n              / Ronin: no summary\n    This is GNU Make's own suite, features/include, `# SV 56301 Verify pattern\n    rules creating optional includes`, second block.\n\n  the same two includes the other way round — and this is the cell that\n  disproves the `-k`-only reading\n      -include inc_a.mk / include inc_b.mk / %_a.mk %_b.mk:; exit 1\n    make      `exit 1` / `Makefile:3: Failed to remake makefile 'inc_b.mk'.`, exit 2\n              — no `-k`, no complaint, no `*** [...] Error N` at all\n    make -k   identical, and the suite asserts that identity outright\n    Ronin     `exit 1`, the FAILED block, `Makefile:3: inc_b.mk: No such file or\n              directory`, the stopped line — a complaint where GNU has a summary\n    The pattern rule has two target patterns, so one invocation makes both\n    files. The first goaldep is `-include`d and forgiven, so its failure is\n    reported by nothing and the update carries on; the second is required and\n    finds the file already tried and failed, which leaves `us_failed` to be\n    returned rather than died on. That is the whole mechanism, and `-k` is not\n    part of it.\n\nWhere. Ronin's `Passes::run` already emits the held complaint when a non-forgiven remake pass loses — make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include built `complaints_for` for exactly that. The summary is a sibling of it with three differences: it belongs to every required makefile the update left unmade rather than only to the one the pass stopped at; it is a second pass, so it comes after the line that ends the run rather than before it; and it wants the `Failed to remake makefile` text, which means `RegenerationRoot` carrying a `summary` beside its `complaint` the way `Refusal` already does.\n\nThe third cell needs more than that, and should be settled before any of it is written: it is the forgiven-then-required grouped shape, where Ronin says a complaint GNU does not and omits a summary GNU does. Ronin's `mark_makefile_unmade` third state already models \"tried and failed\", so the machinery is there; what is missing is the required goaldep reading that state and turning it into a summary rather than into a complaint. Probe `no_diag` in `update_file_1` (remake.c:469) alongside it — the same flag decides which of the two GNU produces."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:40:01.463288498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:40:26.350112099Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered as the refusal accounting under `-k`, and NOT as the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary. Both halves of that were probed rather than assumed.\n\nThe summary is not coming. It is `main.c`'s `us_failed` arm walking `read_files` after the update returned and naming, a second time, every makefile the run has already reported on — GNU runtime narration in the exact sense the operator ruling recorded in make-narration-contract-audit rules out, and the sense docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md already classified `R` under src/make/report.rs (\"keep-going summaries\"). The sibling node make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make left it out for the same reason and its test asserts the absence; this node's test now asserts it too. Note also that the summary is gated on `! (d->flags & RM_DONTCARE)` (main.c:2540), so it never speaks for a forgiven makefile at all.\n\nWhat was left was accounting, and it was really wrong. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    include one.mk\n    include two.mk\n    all: ; @echo all ran\n    one.mk: ; @echo GEN1; exit 1\n    two.mk: ; @echo GEN2; exit 1\n\n  make -k  GNU  GEN1, `Makefile:1: one.mk: No such file or directory`,\n                `*** [Makefile:4: one.mk] Error 1`, GEN2,\n                `Makefile:2: two.mk: No such file or directory`,\n                `*** [Makefile:5: two.mk] Error 1`, then both summaries, rc 2\n           Ronin (before)  both recipes ran and lost, then ONE complaint —\n                one.mk's — and rc 2\n  make     GNU and Ronin both stop after one.mk's complaint, rc 2\n\nSo Ronin ran the second makefile's recipe under `-k` and then said nothing about the makefile it failed to produce. `complaints_for` returned at most one complaint by construction, which is right without the switch and wrong with it: `complain()` chooses `error` over `fatal` on `keep_going_flag` (remake.c:422), so without `-k` the first complaint ends the run inside the update and the goaldeps after it are never reached, and with `-k` `update_goal_chain` walks on and each required makefile it could not make complains for itself.\n\nThe fix is that shape exactly: `Passes` now carries `keep_going`, and under it `complaints_for` returns the held complaint of every target the pass left unmade or did not regenerate, in the order the read reached them — which is the order GNU Make walks `read_files` in. Without it the single-complaint reading is unchanged, and the fallback (\"the first the pass did not regenerate\") still catches a makefile whose own recipe never ran because something it needed lost first. Forgiven makefiles hold no complaint and are untouched, which is `file->dontcare` suppressing the diagnostic as well as the failure.\n\nExit codes were already right in every cell, and stay right: 2 with and without `-k`, and no fragment is written either way. What moved is which failures get reported, which is information rather than narration — every line Ronin now adds names a makefile the run genuinely could not make and had not otherwise mentioned.\n\nAlso probed and deliberately left alone: the forgiven-then-required grouped shape (`-include inc_a.mk` / `include inc_b.mk` / `%_a.mk %_b.mk:; exit 1`), where GNU prints only its summary and Ronin prints its own compiler diagnostic for the unread include. Ronin's line is a front-end evaluation diagnostic, which docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md explicitly permits to keep Makefile locations and language, and GNU's silence there is `no_diag` bookkeeping in service of the summary Ronin does not print. Both exit 2 and neither writes a file, so there is nothing left to reconcile that is not the narration itself."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:34:51.099887742Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-questioned-makefile-keeps-the-questions-status-under-keep-going
                title "Keep the question's status when keep-going reports over a questioned makefile"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:40:36.828419309Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-question-mode-refuses-a-makefile-it-cannot-make, and it needs saying plainly: that node moved this cell from accidentally right to wrong. Before it, Ronin answered 1 to every shape of `make -q <a makefile>` — including the nine cells where GNU answers something else — so this one happened to agree. It now answers 2 where GNU answers 1.\n\n    -include one.mk\n    all: ; @echo all ran\n    one.mk: ; @echo GEN-one; echo X=1 > one.mk\n\n  make -q one.mk        GNU `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2\n                        Ronin the refusal, rc 2 — delivered\n  make -q -k one.mk     GNU `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.`, rc 1\n                        Ronin the refusal, rc 2\n\nNeither creates one.mk, and both report; only the status differs.\n\nWhy GNU answers 1. There are two failing statuses a makefile can be left in, and `-k` is what makes the difference between them visible. `complain()` chooses `error` over `fatal` on `keep_going_flag` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:422), so under `-k` the run does not die inside the complaint — it returns whatever `update_file_1` was going to return, which is the status the early-return path read back off the file:\n\n    if (file->updated)\n      {\n        if (file->update_status > us_none)\n          {\n            if (file->no_diag && !file->dontcare) complain (file);\n            return file->update_status;      /* remake.c:459 onward */\n\nFor a makefile the `-q` pass ASKED about, that status is `us_question`, and `main.c` turns it into MAKE_TROUBLE — 1. For one a recipe TRIED and lost it is `us_failed`, and `main.c` turns that into MAKE_FAILURE — 2. Without `-k` the complaint is fatal and 2 wins either way, which is why the distinction only shows here.\n\nConfirmed not to be a general `-k` rule: an ordinary goal with no rule answers 2 under every combination, `-k` and `-q` included.\n\n    all: missing ; @echo all\n  make all / make -k all / make -q all / make -q -k all\n    GNU rc 2 in all four; Ronin rc 2 in all four — already agreed, and it must\n    keep agreeing. `missing` was never asked about in a makefile pass, so\n    `update_file_1` takes the ordinary route and returns `us_failed`.\n\nWhere. Ronin has ONE third state where GNU has two. `Graph::unmade_makefiles` — introduced by make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals — means \"the goals must treat this as a target nothing can make\", and the `-q` delivery puts questioned makefiles into it alongside the ones whose recipe lost. `Plan::add_node` then refuses over either with `MissingInput`, which is an error, and the `-q` goals path in src/make/cli.rs turns any error into 2.\n\nThe fix is a second set beside it — questioned rather than attempted — and a `-q` goals path that, under `-k`, reports a `MissingInput` over a member of it and still answers the question with 1. Both sets must stay: a run can hold both states at once, because a forgiven makefile the command line did NOT name is built for real under `-q` and may lose while a named one is merely asked about. GNU takes the max of the two statuses, so any attempted-and-lost one still makes the answer 2.\n\nWorth settling with it: whether the same second state fixes the `$^`-shaped cell below, which is the other place Ronin names a different file from GNU under `-q`.\n\n    -include one.mk\n    all: ; @echo all ran\n    one.mk: src ; @echo GEN-one; echo X=1 > one.mk\n    src: ; @echo GEN-src; touch src\n\n  make -q one.mk   GNU `*** No rule to make target 'src', needed by 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2\n                   Ronin `'one.mk' missing and no known rule to make it`, rc 2\n\n  Both refuse and both answer 2, so only the name differs. GNU's `complain()`\n  recurses into `file->deps` looking for the entry that actually caused the\n  failure (remake.c:403) and finds `src` — which HAS a rule, and is named as\n  though it did not, because `-q` left it `updated` with `us_question` too. That\n  is arguably a defect in 4.4.1 rather than a behaviour to port, and it should\n  be decided on its merits rather than folded into the status fix."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:25:12.859658038Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
