            {
                id make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member
                title "Give $? an archive member's name and the index's date"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:16:53.82634704Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by auditing the rows that appeared in the upstream inventory when `archives` joined `.FEATURES` and GNU Make's own `features/archives` script stopped skipping. Pre-existing, not introduced by that dispatch: the code below is untouched by it, and the defect reproduces on the longhand `lib.a(a.o) lib.a(b.o)` spelling that parsed the same way before.\n\n`$?` is the one automatic variable the archive core missed. `$<`, `$^` and `$+` all reduce an archive-member prerequisite to its member name through `crate::archive::member_or_whole` (kati `src-rs/command.rs`, the `Less`, `Hat` and `Plus` arms). The `Question` arm writes `ai_str` — the whole `lib.a(member.o)` name — and never calls it.\n\nReproducer, with a.c and b.c present, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o) mylib.a(b.o) ; @echo \"AT=$@ Q=$?\"\n    (%): % ;\n    %.o : %.c ; @echo Compile $<; touch $@\n\n  GNU:   Q=a.o b.o\n  Ronin: Q=mylib.a(a.o) mylib.a(b.o)\n\nThe name is only half of it, and the other half is worse because it is silent. The same arm decides WHICH prerequisites are new by calling `get_timestamp` on that same whole name — a filesystem stat of a string with parentheses in it, which finds nothing. So an archive member is dated as absent for the purpose of `$?` no matter what the index says, where `f_mtime`'s answer for it comes out of the archive (Ronin has that reader, at `src/os/archive.rs`, but it is on the build side and kati's `fileutil::get_timestamp` is not it). A member that is genuinely up to date will still be listed by `$?`.\n\nThere is a third face of the same gap. Under the deferred path the arm emits `DEFERRED_NEW_INPUTS_REFERENCE` and the executor substitutes it later; with an archive member in the list that substitution does not happen at all and the placeholder reaches the shell:\n\n    mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o b.o)\n    (%): % ;\n    %.a: ; $(AR) $(ARFLAGS) $@ $?\n    %.o : %.c ; @echo Compile $<; $(COMPILE.c) -o $@ $<\n\n  GNU:   ar -rvU mylib.a a.o b.o\n  Ronin: ar -rvU mylib.a ${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}   →  /bin/sh: Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected\n\nThat is GNU Make's own SV 61436 case, \"Allow redefining archive rules to propagate timestamps\", and it is what four of the sixteen newly-reachable `features/archives` rows are made of — `features/archives.diff.12` through `.15`, all classified `unclassified` with `evaluation` residue because exact output cannot tell graph intent from runner behaviour. The fifth, `.10`, is the long-member-name case and is narration only: Ronin files both members correctly, and the differences are the recipe echo and the un-narrated `rm` of the intermediates.\n\nWhoever takes this should treat the name and the date as one change: reducing the name without fixing the comparison would print the right words about the wrong set.\n\nThe empty-recipe half of that reproducer is a separate defect and is filed separately as make-empty-recipe-pattern-rule-drops-its-prerequisites — it is why `Compile a.c` is missing from Ronin's output above, and it is not archive-specific."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:52:57.150492466Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:25:18.999184557Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered on all three faces, with the two defects that were sitting behind the third filed rather than folded in.\n\nFace (a), the name. `crate::archive::member_or_whole` now reduces the prerequisite in every arm of the `Question` autocommand that writes names — the launch-time arm, the grouped-double-action arm and the plain one — beside the `Less`/`Hat`/`Plus` arms that always did. `mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o) mylib.a(b.o)` answers `Q=a.o b.o`, which is GNU's answer measured against the oracle.\n\nFace (b), the date, as one change with the name. The arms that compare timestamps no longer hand a parenthesised name to the filesystem. kati gains `archive::member_timestamp`, which is `f_mtime`'s three questions in order — the archive must exist, the index must record a non-zero date (`member_date`, which node 3 put beside the one scan), and a member file left on disk newer than the indexed copy counts as absent because it was rebuilt and not filed. It answers with the START of the second, which is what `file_timestamp_cons (hname, member_date, 0)` builds, and `member_timestamp_as_target` is the rounded-up reading: GNU sets `low_resolution_time` on a member and rounds the mtime of the file it is UPDATING up to the end of its second, so the two readings are two different questions and `command.rs` picks by which one it is asking.\n\nFace (c), the deferred path, which turned out to be the same defect wearing a different hat. The filing says \"with an archive member in the list that substitution never happens at all and the placeholder reaches the shell\". It does happen — the substituted value was `mylib.a(a.o) mylib.a(b.o)`, and bare parentheses are what `/bin/sh: Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected` is complaining about. (The `FAILED:` block quotes the command before substitution, which is what made it look otherwise.) So fixing the spelling fixes it, and SV 61436's makefile now files both objects and writes the archive.\n\nWhere the spelling travels. `$?` is the one automatic variable whose value is not known while the Makefile is read, so the reduction cannot be applied where the other three are applied. It is declared instead: kati's `SinkEdge` gains `deferred_new_input_names`, pairs of (input, the name the published value calls it), which reach `DeferredFreshness` and are used verbatim by `Builder::deferred_new_inputs_value`. Nothing on the executor's side reads either spelling — the pair is carried, not interpreted — so the runner still assigns no meaning to a parenthesis, and the list is empty for every edge that mentions no archive. The equivalence harness records and compares the new field beside the two lists that were already there.\n\nProbes, and two defects they exposed. Once `$?` named members correctly it still named the wrong SET, for two reasons that are neither the name nor kati's date, both filed as their own nodes with reproducers:\n\n* make-empty-recipe-target-is-not-a-ninja-alias — `(%): % ;` compiles to a commandless edge, the scan reads it as Ninja's phony, and a missing member is handed its input's date. So `mylib.a(b.o)`, which is not in the archive at all, drops out of `$?`. Not archive-specific: the same divergence reproduces with `mid: src ;` and no archive anywhere.\n* make-archive-member-date-is-low-resolution-on-the-target-side — `archive_member_stat` rounds every member's date to the end of its second, where GNU rounds only the file being updated. So a member is 0.7s newer than the archive holding it, `$?` lists a member that is up to date, and `mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o)` never settles.\n\nInventory audit, the rows this node was filed against. One row moved and I checked every one of the sixteen: `features/archives.diff.12` — SV 61436's first step — loses its `ninja-failure-block` and Ronin now completes it, leaving `ninja-progress+evaluation` residue that is the progress line and the un-narrated `rm`. `.13`, `.14` and `.15` are the same sequence's later steps and still carry a failure block; they are the low-resolution node's, and the note there says exactly why (Ronin refiles an archive whose member objects the previous step swept away, so `ar` is handed names that are not there). `.10` is unchanged and was already narration-only. Class totals are unmoved at 1299 rows 161/0/37/1101, with the compiler bucket at zero.\n\nLeft as it was, deliberately: the `RecipeShell` fallback arm, which spells `$?` as a shell `find` over the prerequisite names. A member is not a file, so `find` cannot answer for one whatever it is handed — the arm is unreachable from Ronin (kati's `defers_recipe` never defers a recipe that references `$?`, and Ronin's sink asks for `SchedulerBoundary`), and it belongs to the manifest destination rather than to the product.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: `question-variable-names-an-archive-member-by-its-member` and `an-archive-rule-refiles-the-members-that-are-new` (SV 61436's makefile, gating that the archive is written with both members in it). One kati unit test, `a_members_date_answers_f_mtimes_three_questions`, which covers the rebuilt-but-unfiled rule nothing else asks about.\n\nGates: workspace 874/0, kati clippy `-D warnings` clean, Ronin clippy pedantic/nursery clean, make_port green with no recording rewritten, conformance 334/53, equivalence 278 compared / 0 disagreements, upstream inventory 1299 rows 161/0/37/1101 with one row audited above, vim + zsh green, ninja conformance 425/425.\n\nkati commit 0410275f6e47c2074ad5280ec79dfd23cd40f66b on nplan/make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:28:16.124700877Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-empty-recipe-pattern-rule-drops-its-prerequisites
                title "Build what a pattern rule with an empty recipe asks for"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:17:12.74986753Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while auditing the `features/archives` rows that became reachable when `archives` joined `.FEATURES`. It presents as an archive defect and is not one — the archive spelling is just the instance GNU Make's own suite happens to exercise.\n\nA pattern rule whose recipe is empty still has prerequisites, and they still have to be made. Ronin prunes the edge and everything under it.\n\nReproducer with no archive in it at all, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: x.q ; @echo TOP\n    %.q: %.o ;\n    x.o: ; @echo Make x.o; touch $@\n\n  GNU:   Make x.o / TOP\n  Ronin: TOP\n\nThe explicit-rule counterpart already agrees, which is what localises this to the pattern path rather than to empty recipes generally:\n\n    all: mid.q ; @echo TOP\n    mid.q: a.o ;\n    %.o : %.c ; @echo Compile $<; touch $@\n\n  both: Compile a.c / TOP\n\nThe archive face, and the reason it was found here — `(%): % ;` is exactly this shape, and it is how a makefile says \"file the member, the archive rule below will do the work\":\n\n    all: mylib.a(a.o)\n    (%): % ;\n    %.o : %.c ; @echo Compile $<; touch $@\n\n  GNU:   Compile a.c / rm a.o\n  Ronin: ronin: no work to do.\n\nGive the same rule a non-empty recipe and Ronin builds the chain correctly, so the prerequisite resolution is present and it is the empty recipe that switches it off. That asymmetry is the shape of the fix: an empty recipe means the rule runs nothing, not that the rule wants nothing.\n\nPre-existing. Nothing in the archive-globbing/list/suffix dispatch touched the implicit search or the scheduler, and the non-archive reproducer above has never involved any of it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:07:06.407514737Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:21:46.751974259Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the shape turned out to be narrower than the filing — which was itself half right by the time it was picked up.\n\nWhat still reproduced. Measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before touching anything: the filing's first reproducer (`all: x.q` / `%.q: %.o ;` / `x.o: ; @echo Make x.o`) now AGREES — Ronin builds x.o and prints TOP. What does not is the same shape with the prerequisite left to the implicit search:\n\n    all: x.q ; @echo TOP\n    %.q: %.o ;\n    %.o : %.c ; @echo Compile $<; touch $@\n\n  GNU:   Compile x.c / TOP / rm x.o\n  Ronin: TOP\n\nThe difference between the two is not the empty recipe at all: in the first, `x.o` is written down, so it is not intermediate; in the second the search invents it, and an invented intermediate is excused from existing. The archive face `(%): % ;` is the second shape — the member's `a.o` is invented too — which is why it kept failing while the explicit counterpart passed.\n\nThe mechanism. `src/graph/intermediate.rs` `push_intermediates` is the walk that asks for intermediates the dirty scan excused. Its own doc says the walk starts wherever GNU Make's `must_make` would be set, \"which is one term wider than being out of date: a target that is simply not there has to be made\" — and it did start there, on the root. It did not CARRY that term down: descending into an input it asked only `dirty()`. A Makefile target whose recipe is empty compiles to an edge with no command, which is a phony edge to the scan, and Ninja's phony is dirty only when an input is — so `x.q` is absent, not dirty, and the walk stopped one step above the intermediate that was waiting for it.\n\nWhere it went. One clause, in Ronin, not in kati: the descent skips an input only when it is neither dirty nor `absent_on_disk()`. `absent_on_disk` is written by `observe` and by nothing else, so it is the filesystem's own answer rather than the mtime a node is carrying — which matters here precisely because an absent intermediate and a phony output are both standing in something else's. The widened set is exactly the phony edges: any other edge whose output is missing is already dirty by `oldest_output.is_missing()`, and a deferred edge's virtual output is never observed, so it is not swept in.\n\nProbes that shaped it, not assumed: `%.q: %.o ; @echo Q` (non-empty recipe, same intermediate) already agreed, which is what put the fault in the phony branch rather than in the implicit search; `x.q: x.o ;` and `x.q: x.o` (explicit, empty and absent recipes) both already agreed; and GNU's `implicit.c` distinguishes `rule->deps != 0 && rule->cmds == 0` (the cancellation, passed over) from a rule with an empty recipe (`cmds != 0`, kept) — kati already reads that distinction correctly, so nothing there needed changing.\n\nProbed and found NOT divergent, so not widened into: an empty-recipe pattern rule whose target exists and is older than its prerequisite. GNU runs the empty recipe and the file's date does not move; Ronin's phony substitution puts the prerequisite's date on it. Both tools then leave a dependent that is newer alone. No visible difference, so no node filed.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded, both new: `an-empty-recipe-pattern-rule-still-makes-its-prerequisites` (the non-archive shape, which is what localises the defect) and `an-empty-recipe-archive-rule-still-makes-its-member` (the `(%): % ;` shape, which is how it was found). Both record the intermediate sweep as well as the recipe — GNU leaves `x.o`/`a.o` deleted and no archive written, and Ronin's filesystem effects match name for name. One unit test beside the two that were already there: `ronin_graph_an_absent_name_with_no_recipe_asks_for_the_intermediate_below_it`.\n\nGates: workspace 874/0 (873 + the new unit test), make_port green with no recording rewritten, conformance 334/53 (baseline held), equivalence 278 compared / 0 disagreements, upstream inventory byte-identical at 1299 rows 161/0/37/1101 with the compiler bucket still zero, vim + zsh green, ninja conformance 425/425."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:28:06.43633809Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-index-reader-is-written-twice
                title "Read the ar header format in one place rather than two"
                kind @refactor
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:26:50.365529222Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by make-archive-member-globbing rather than absorbed into it, because the duplication it records is a consequence of the crate boundary and removing it is a decision about that boundary.\n\nThere are now two readers of the `ar` header format:\n\n* `src/os/archive.rs` `member_date` — one member's date, asked at build time by `RealDiskInterface::stat`, from make-archive-member-targets.\n* `kati/src-rs/archive.rs` `member_names` — every member's name, asked at parse time by `ar_glob`, from make-archive-member-globbing.\n\nBoth walk `!<arch>\\n`, the fixed 60-byte headers, the `//` long-name table and 4.4BSD `#1/LEN` extended names, and both treat an unparseable archive as one with nothing in it. GNU Make has one `ar_scan` and two thin callbacks over it (`ar_member_date_1`, `ar_glob_match`); this is that shape with the shared half copied instead of shared.\n\nWhy it was copied. The dependency runs one way — ronin depends on kati, optionally, behind the `make` feature — so kati cannot call the Ronin reader. The reverse would work, but `mod archive;` in `src/os.rs` is not feature-gated and `stat` consults it unconditionally, so making Ronin read kati's would mean gating the disk interface's archive branch on the `make` feature. That is a real change to how the non-Make build is compiled, and it does not belong inside an archive-globbing dispatch.\n\nThe disposal is one of: gate `src/os/archive.rs` on the `make` feature and have it call kati's scan; or lift the format reader to a place both can see. The first is smaller and matches the fact that `archive_members` is only ever set by Make mode (src/make/cli.rs, src/build.rs). Either way the test that pins the format should end up beside the one reader rather than beside both.\n\nNot urgent: both readers are covered by unit tests that build archives with the host's real `ar`, and neither has drifted. The cost is that a format fix has two homes and only one of them will be remembered."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:28:12.719203067Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:47:48.816695602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by the node's preferred disposal: the format is read in kati and Ronin calls it, with `src/os/archive.rs` now compiled only with the `make` feature.\n\nWhat the audit found that the filing did not. There were three walks of the header format, not two. `src/os/archive.rs` had grown `member_header_position` for the `-t` touch — the same walk as `member_date` stopped one step earlier, copied rather than shared — so the format had three homes in two files and a fix would have been remembered in one of them.\n\nWhere it went. kati `src-rs/archive.rs` gains `ar_scan`, which is GNU Make's shape rather than a name borrowed from it: one walk of the `!<arch>\\n` magic and the 60-byte headers, resolving the `//` long-name table and 4.4BSD `#1/LEN` extended names, handing each member's name, recorded date and header address to a visitor and stopping at the first answer. The three callers are then thin — `member_names` collects, `member_date` takes the first non-zero date a matching name has (`ar_member_date`, which folds \"absent\" and \"date zero\" together), `touch_member` writes the archive's own mtime into the 12-byte date field at the header the walk just found. `name_matches` is `ar_name_equal` once, where it used to be twice.\n\nTwo details the merge settled rather than preserved. The scan opens read-write when the caller means to write, so the touch writes into the handle the walk found the header with instead of reopening and walking again. And the step to the next header is now an absolute seek to the address the walk computed, because the two things that can leave the handle somewhere else — the 4.4BSD form taking its name out of the member's data, and a visitor that just wrote into a header — no longer agree on where \"here\" is.\n\nRonin's side. `src/os/archive.rs` is three delegating functions and one test: that `lib.a(m.o)` comes apart the way `ar_name` takes it apart, which is what decides whether the archive is consulted at all. The format-pinning tests moved to kati, beside the one reader, with their `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1/test]` annotations carried over — `src/os.rs`'s implementation-side annotation stays where it was.\n\nThe feature gate, which is the part the filing said was a real decision. `mod archive` and the two branches that reach it in `stat` and `touch` are now `#[cfg(all(unix, feature = \"make\"))]` rather than `#[cfg(unix)]`. Nothing is lost: `archive_members` is only ever set by Make mode (src/make/cli.rs, src/build.rs), so a Ninja-only build had a reader it could not reach. Verified rather than assumed — `cargo check --no-default-features --all-targets` emits 27 warnings, byte for byte the same 27 a checkout of the parent commit emits, with nothing new and nothing about archives; and `cargo test --no-default-features` is 479/13 against the baseline's 486/13, the same thirteen pre-existing `make_grouped` failures (that suite needs Make mode) and exactly the seven tests that moved to kati accounting for the difference.\n\nGates: workspace 874/0 (six archive tests left Ronin and six arrived in kati), kati clippy `-D warnings` clean, Ronin clippy pedantic/nursery clean, make_port green with no recording rewritten, conformance 334/53, equivalence 278 compared / 0 disagreements, upstream inventory byte-identical at 1299 rows 161/0/37/1101 with the compiler bucket zero, vim + zsh green, ninja conformance 425/425.\n\nkati commit fe917c99375779aab1e594e4fa1922435c26515f on nplan/make-archive-index-reader-is-written-twice, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T17:52:02.88618485Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the note above, which described Ronin's side as \"three delegating functions and one test\" in `src/os/archive.rs`. It is not: the file is gone. A wrapper whose whole body is one call to the thing it wraps is a second name for the same function, and the code-quality gate said so — so `src/os.rs` calls `kati::archive::{split_archive_name, member_date, touch_member}` at the two places that ask, and the module's prose moved to the field those two branches are switched on. The name test went with it: kati's `the_two_halves_come_apart_at_the_first_parenthesis` already pins `ar_name` where the function lives.\n\nTest counts change accordingly: workspace 873/0 rather than 874 — seven archive tests left Ronin and six arrived in kati, the seventh being the duplicate name test. `--no-default-features` is unchanged at 479/13 either way, because that last test was already behind the feature gate."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:28:11.684684076Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-narration-contract-audit
                title "Audit every Make-voiced runtime output against the narration contract"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:03:02.312672914Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-17, explicit. The line, verbatim: the runtime speaks Ninja; Make-voiced output is legitimate ONLY where a failure must be reported at all, and then in Ronin's established diagnostic shape — never as optional runtime chatter mimicking GNU. `[spec:ronin:req:make.narration+1]` is the authority and says the same thing at greater length.\n\nRonin's established shapes, for reading the table against: `ronin: <message>` for a diagnostic, `ronin: <loc>: <message>` for a located one, `<loc>: <message>` for a located WARNING (which is what every kati warning wears, including the user's own `$(warning)`), `[N/M] <description>` for progress, `FAILED: [code=N]` for a command that lost, `ronin: build stopped: <reason>.` for the end of a run that did not finish.\n\nTHE TABLE. Every Make-voiced output added during this drain, its classification, and what happened to it.\n\n1. `Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` — REMOVED.\n   Runtime, `-k` only, failure path. GNU's `us_failed` pass in main.c walks\n   read_files once the update has RETURNED and names each makefile it could not\n   make. Every name in that list has already been reported one line above it by\n   its own refusal, so the pass reports no failure that would otherwise go\n   unreported: it is a second list of names already said, in GNU's words, with\n   no product identity on it at all. That is the definition of the thing the\n   contract declines. Removed from kati's authoring (`Refusal::summary`), from\n   Ronin's `RefusedMakefile`, from `refusal_report`, from `refused_makefile`,\n   from `report_refusals_now`, and from rkati's own `-k` path.\n\n2. `ronin: No rule to make target 'X'.` — KEPT.\n   Runtime, failure only. This is the line that ends the run; the failure must\n   be reported or nothing is. It already wears Ronin's shape — the `*** ` and\n   `  Stop.` are stripped by `diagnostic_body` and the product prefix added.\n   The wording is GNU's, and there is no Ninja analogue: Ninja's `'x' missing\n   and no known rule to make it` is about a build goal, and this is about a\n   makefile in the read phase, which Ninja has no concept of.\n\n3. `Makefile:1: nope.mk: No such file or directory` — KEPT, stream corrected.\n   Runtime, failure only, at all three sites. It says WHY the file could not be\n   read — `No such file or directory` against `Permission denied` is the whole\n   information — and nothing else says it, so it is reporting a failure rather\n   than narrating one. Its shape is not an outlier: `<loc>: <message>` is what\n   every located warning Ronin emits looks like, `$(warning)` included, so\n   adding a product prefix here would make it inconsistent with the rest of the\n   product rather than more Ronin-shaped.\n   What DID change is the stream. `complained_of` was putting it on stdout so\n   it would sit one line above `build stopped`. GNU Make puts it on stderr\n   (measured: `make 2>/dev/null` shows only the recipe's own output), and so\n   does every other Ronin diagnostic. Choosing stdout to reproduce an\n   interleaving is choosing a stream for GNU's output order rather than for\n   where a diagnostic belongs, so it moved to stderr and its test now asserts\n   the streams separately.\n\n4. `ronin: Circular a <- b dependency dropped.` — KEPT.\n   COMPILE time, not runtime: kati's `drop_circular_dependencies`, before a\n   Ronin graph exists. The contract scopes to the runtime, and the boundary\n   audit keeps compiler diagnostic identity valid through\n   make-diagnostic-identity. It reports that the compiler discarded part of the\n   user's graph, which is real information loss and worse to swallow than to\n   say. Wears `ronin:` already. Its defect is a different one — it reaches\n   stderr by `eprintln!` and bypasses the diagnostic sink — filed as\n   make-compiler-warnings-reach-the-sink.\n\n5. `ronin: ./prog: No such file or directory` from a recipe launch — KEPT.\n   Runtime, exit 127. Ronin's direct-exec path replaces the shell, so if this\n   said nothing the user would get a bare `FAILED: [code=127]` and no reason at\n   all. It stands in for what the shell would have printed, is delivered as the\n   command's own captured stderr, and wears `ronin:`. That it also appears on a\n   `-` (ignore-errors) line whose run exits 0 is the ignore-errors reporting\n   rule, not this text: GNU reports the error on a `-` line too.\n\n6. `ronin: ./prog: No such file or directory` from `$(shell)` — KEPT.\n   kati's `fileutil.rs`, so a compiler diagnostic, reached at compile time for\n   an assignment and at recipe-expansion time for a recipe. `$(shell)` with a\n   missing program expands to nothing and the evaluation carries on; saying so\n   is the only thing standing between the user and a variable that silently\n   became empty. Wears `ronin:`.\n\n7. `Makefile:2: <user text>` from `$(warning)` / `$(info)` — KEPT, out of scope.\n   This is the Makefile's own text at the Makefile's own position. It is not\n   Ronin speaking in Make's voice; it is Ronin repeating what it was told to\n   say, and the location is the semantics of the function.\n\nDirection of movement, as expected: none of it text-wise. The fixture tree\nrecords no diagnostic text at all — every `expected` file holds only `oracle`,\n`outcome`, `file` and `status`, which is `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1]`\nworking as designed — so no fixture was re-recorded for the removal, and the\nupstream inventory did not move on account of it either (1299 rows, the two\nthat moved are the quote sweep's). Two tests in tests/make_regressions.rs\nchanged: `keep_going_refuses_every_makefile` now asserts the summary is absent\nwhere it used to assert its position, and `lost_remake_reports_its_unread_include`\nreads the two streams apart instead of merging them.\n\nNo `shared-refusal` classification regressed. The removal takes away a line\nthat duplicated names already reported; the refusals themselves, which are what\nthat family recognises, are untouched."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:05:12.855386528Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Retirement recorded here because the node it retires is being removed and the record has to outlive it.\n\n`make-pattern-peer-warning` — \"Warn when a pattern recipe leaves a peer target unmade\" — is retired as silent by contract. Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-17, explicit: emitting GNU's runtime warning would be emulating Make instead of compiling to a Ninja graph. Removed with nplan_rm; its full decision text was written onto the node before removal and is restated here.\n\nWhat it asked for. GNU Make 4.4.1, on a pattern rule with several target patterns whose recipe writes only some of them, succeeds and says `Makefile:5: warning: pattern recipe did not update peer target 'a.h'.` once per unwritten peer, naming the line the rule was written on. An explicit grouped `&:` rule in the same shape says nothing; the warning is the pattern form's alone.\n\nWhy it fails the contract in this audit's own terms. It is a line on stderr on a SUCCESS path — the build works, the file the recipe did not write is simply not there — so it is not a failure that must be reported at all. That is the whole test, and it does not pass it. Producing it would also cost exactly the machinery the compiler boundary exists to keep out: the executor would have to compare an output's mtime before and after the command for the purpose of remarking on it, and the graph would have to carry the Makefile source location of the rule an edge came from, which it deliberately does not.\n\nWhat is kept. The build intent the two tools agree on — the rule succeeds, one file is written, the peer is not — is gated by tests/make/multi-target-pattern-recipe-may-leave-a-peer-unmade, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1, and that fixture stands untouched. The defect this node was carried out of, the read that never settled, was fixed under make-grouped-pattern-targets and gates there. Only the warning is declined.\n\nNo code was written for it and none is owed, which is why it is removed rather than completed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:05:28.346235596Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-plus-line-runs-under-pretending
                title "Run a recipe's + lines under -n and -t, which is what the marker is for"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:03:35.297828843Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while implementing `-t` (make-archive-member-touch), and it is a gap in `-n` first and `-t` only by inheritance.\n\nGNU Make runs a recipe line prefixed `+` even when told to run nothing: `-n`, `-t` and `-q` all step aside for it, because the marker says this line is how Make learns what a sub-make would do. Measured against the oracle: a rule whose whole recipe is `+@echo PLUS RAN > c.txt` under `make -t` prints no touch line at all, runs the recipe, and leaves the file written.\n\nRonin cannot express it today, and the reason is upstream of the executor. kati's `ParseCommandPrefixes` (kati/src-rs, and kati/src/command.cc:196 on the C++ side) reads `@`, `-` and `+`, and for `+` the body is the comment `ignore recursion marker` and nothing else. The flag never reaches `Command`, so it never reaches a `SinkCommand`, so the graph has no way to say that one step of an edge's recipe runs while the rest of it pretends. The old executor had the shape for it — a `dry_run_command` on `CommandSpec` that, when it equalled the command, meant the edge was nothing but `+` lines and therefore ran — and that field went with the boundary removal in 95452af.\n\nSo this is a kati change followed by a Ronin one: carry the marker per recipe line through `Command`, across the sink, onto `LateStep`, and have the launch decide per step rather than per edge. Worth doing together for `-n` and `-t` because it is one mechanism and the two switches differ only in what the non-`+` steps do instead.\n\nThe corpus already holds the evidence, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1 and currently read for discovery rather than gating:\ntests/make/dry-run-skips-a-plus-line and tests/make/dry-run-skips-a-make-reference-line, both in `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` in tests/make_port.rs. The first of those is exactly this node's case and would gate once the marker survives compilation. The second is a different question — a line whose unexpanded text names `$(MAKE)` — and should be judged separately, because Ronin compiled that child rather than starting it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-what-if-assumed-new
                title "Decide whether -W joins -t or stays an accepted no-op"
                kind @feat
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:03:51.23492564Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-17 operator ruling that reversed the accept-without-emulation disposition for `-t` (make-archive-member-touch). That ruling named `-t` and only `-t`. `-W` was the other half of the retired `make-option-touch-and-what-if` and is still an accepted no-op, so this is the question the ruling left open rather than work it authorised.\n\nThe case for `-W` joining `-t`. The reason `-t` did not belong in the accept-without-emulation bucket was that it decides what the run writes to disk, and `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1]` makes filesystem effects a conformance criterion while excluding wording and scheduling. `-W FILE` — `--what-if`, `--new-file`, `--assume-new` — decides what the run BUILDS: it pretends the named file was just written, so everything downstream of it is out of date. On its own that is a graph-dirtiness question, which is squarely inside the compiler's remit and not a runner behaviour at all. Ronin accepts the spelling and builds nothing extra, so `make -W in.txt` today is a no-op where GNU rebuilds the tree.\n\nThe case against, which is why this is a node and not a patch. `-W` is habitually used WITH `-n` — \"what would this rebuild if I touched that\" — and the boundary audit lists `src/graph.rs: Make-only timestamp exceptions and -W assumed-new state` among the things the boundary removes. Whether an assumed-new file is a compiler input (kati marks the node dirty as it builds the graph) or an executor override (the disk interface lies about one path's mtime) is the decision, and only the first is consistent with the boundary. The second is the one that is easy.\n\nThe evidence is recorded and waiting: tests/make/what-if-option, taken from GNU Make 4.4.1, sits in `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` in tests/make_port.rs beside where `touch-option` used to be. `touch-option` left that list this dispatch and gates. This node is what it would take for `what-if-option` to do the same."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-endif-trailing-text-is-a-warning
                title "Warn rather than die over text after an endif directive"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:04:04.653031144Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the quote sweep (make-diagnostic-quote-style), which had to read every diagnostic in the fork and so noticed that one of them is raised at the wrong severity.\n\n`endif junk` ends the run under Ronin and does not under GNU Make. Measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on\n\n    ifeq (a,a)\n    all: ; @echo hi\n    endif junk\n\nGNU says `Makefile:3: extraneous text after 'endif' directive` and then builds, printing `hi` and exiting 0. Ronin says the same sentence with `ronin: ` in front of it, builds nothing, and stops.\n\nThe cause is one macro. kati's `parse_endif` uses `error_loc!` where its three siblings — `parse_else`'s trailing text, `parse_ifeq`'s, and the `endef` one — all use `warn_loc!`. It is at kati/src-rs/parser.rs:631 and kati/src/parser.cc:402, and both copies have it, so the C++ and the Rust port agree with each other and disagree with Make.\n\nDeliberately not fixed inside the quote sweep. That node's own filing set the rule — a sweep across every diagnostic in the fork is not the place to change what a diagnostic DOES — and this changes an outcome: a Makefile that does not build starts building, which is build intent and moves rows in the upstream inventory and possibly the corpus. It wants its own measurement.\n\nBoth spellings of the wording were already brought to 4.4.1 by the sweep, so what is left here is the severity alone: `error_loc!` becomes `warn_loc!` in both implementations, and the run carries on."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-compiler-warnings-reach-the-sink
                title "Send a compiler warning to the diagnostic sink rather than straight to stderr"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:04:17.591953978Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the narration-contract audit (make-narration-contract-audit), which kept the circular-drop warning and recorded this as the defect it does have.\n\nkati's `color_warn_log` ends in a bare `eprintln!` (kati/src-rs/lib.rs:235). Every warning that goes through it — `Circular a <- b dependency dropped.`, `overriding commands for target 'x'`, `target 'x' doesn't match the target pattern`, the `FindEmulator` complaints, and the user's own `$(warning)` — is written straight to the process's stderr rather than to the diagnostic sink Ronin hands the compiler.\n\nThree consequences, none of them intended:\n  a library caller that supplies a diagnostics sink to collect what a\n  compilation said gets the errors and not the warnings;\n  `RunResult.stderr` does not hold them, so nothing that reads a run's result\n  as a value can see them;\n  output-sync has no chance to group them with the unit they came from, which\n  matters once a recursive compilation is composing several units.\n\nErrors do not have the problem: `color_error_log` returns rather than prints, and Ronin renders it through `ordinary_diagnostic` into whichever sink the caller gave. `color_warn_text` already exists as the returning half — it was split out for the held include complaint — so the shape to copy is in the file.\n\nNot urgent and not a correctness bug on the command line, where the process's stderr is where these were going anyway. It is a boundary leak: the compiler is writing to a descriptor instead of to the interface it was handed."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-suffix-rule-name-splits-against-the-list
                title "Split a suffix rule's name against the declared suffixes"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-archive-suffix-rule-conversion)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:48:23.443272786Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by building zsh 5.9.2 from its own Makefiles with Ronin's Make mode beside GNU Make 4.4.1, and reducing the first divergence.\n\nzsh's `Src/Makemod` sets `OBJ=.o`, declares `.SUFFIXES: .c .so ..o .o .syms .pro .epro`, and writes `.c.$(OBJ):` — which is `.c..o:`, a name holding three dots. kati decides what a suffix rule is by counting them (`kati/src-rs/dep.rs:1024 is_suffix_rule`: a third dot means no), so the rule is not converted, every `X..o` has nothing to make it, and none of zsh's dynamically loaded modules is built. The binary still links, so the run reports success and produces a zsh that is not zsh.\n\nGNU Make does not read the name at all. `convert_to_pattern` (reference/gnumake/src/rule.c) writes every declared suffix in front of every other, looks the resulting name up as a file, and converts the pair when that file has a recipe — so `.c` ++ `..o` is `.c..o` for the same reason `.c` ++ `.o` is `.c.o`, and a dot inside a suffix is not special because nothing ever counted dots.\n\nAcceptance: `tests/make-project-reductions/suffix-rule-target-suffix-holds-a-dot` builds `foo..o` from `foo.c`, and zsh builds twenty or more modules through `scripts/check-make-projects.sh zsh`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T15:48:28.226246234Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:05:51.322307873Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in kati b3b8749. The conversion is now GNU's: `convert_written_suffix_rules` writes every declared suffix in front of every other, interns the name, looks it up among the rules the makefiles wrote, and converts the pair when the rule it finds has a recipe — which is `convert_to_pattern` (reference/gnumake/src/rule.c) with eleven hundred names that were never there left unasked, the same shape `install_builtin_suffix_pairs` already had for the catalogue.\n\nProbed against the oracle before anything moved, and the probe decided the second half. `.SUFFIXES: .c ..o .o` with both `.c.$(OBJ):` and `.c$(OBJ):` written, `OBJ = .o`: GNU makes `foo..o` from `foo.c` with `$*` bound to `foo`, and `bar.o` from `bar.c` with `$*` bound to `bar`. So `foo..o` matches both `%..o` and `%.o`, and the one that wins is the one leaving the shorter stem — `implicit.c` sorts the patterns a name matched by stem length before trying any of them. The search therefore reads the declared list longest suffix first rather than taking the last dot, and the prerequisite is measured off the rule's own target pattern rather than off `strip_ext`: `%..o` matching `foo..o` leaves `foo` where the last dot leaves `foo.`.\n\nWhere it went, all in kati/src-rs/dep.rs: `convert_written_suffix_rules` and `convert_suffix_rule` replace `populate_suffix_rule`, called from `install_builtin_rules` where the list has settled instead of from `populate_explicit_rule` where it has not; `suffix_rule_candidates` and `suffix_rule_input` replace the `get_ext` lookup and `replace_suffix` at both search sites; `apply_output_pattern` substitutes into the target pattern. `keep_only_declared_suffix_rules` is gone — a conversion the list drives cannot make a rule the list does not declare — and so is the `.SUFFIXES`-empty clear beside it.\n\nTwo things the conformance gate caught and one it settled. The deprecation warning is about the syntax, not the rule: `--no_builtin_rules --warn_suffix_rules` empties the list, so nothing converts, and `suffix_rule_warn.sh` says the warning still fires. It is now `deprecate_suffix_rule_syntax`, called where the rule is read. And the `.X.a` capture now asks GNU's question — is the target suffix exactly `.a` — rather than whether it ends in one.\n\nGates: workspace 869 (868 + the reduction), conformance 334/53 unchanged, equivalence 297/278/0 disagreements, upstream inventory 1299 rows at 161/0/37/1101 with compiler at zero and not one row moved, all three Clippy passes clean, make_port green with no recording rewritten.\n\nFixtures: tests/make/suffix-rule-name-splits-against-the-declared-list, recorded from the oracle, carries both spellings and both stems. tests/make-project-reductions/suffix-rule-target-suffix-holds-a-dot is the reduction, run by tests/make_projects.rs — the tree is checked in and copied into a scratch directory rather than written by the test, so the reproduction a reader does by hand is the one the test does."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:05:56.003633432Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recursive-ordering-sees-a-middleman
                title "Order recursive children by what they produce through an ordinary target"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-recursive-evaluation-boundary)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:08:16.086026447Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by building zsh 5.9.2 from its own Makefiles with Ronin's Make mode beside GNU Make 4.4.1, and reducing the second divergence.\n\nzsh's generated `Src/Makemod` builds each module's header through a chain of three targets, two of which re-invoke the same makefile: `X.mdh` needs `X.mdhi`, `X.mdhi` needs `X.mdhs`, and both `X.mdh` and `X.mdhs` run `$(MAKE) -f Makemod X.mdh.tmp`. So the two recursive wrappers are related only through `X.mdhi`, which has an ordinary recipe.\n\n`src/make.rs dependency_ordered` puts a held recursive edge before any held edge that needs its outputs, and it decides that by matching one edge's `evaluation_inputs()` against another's outputs directly. `X.mdh`'s evaluation input is `X.mdhi`, which no recursive edge produces, so no predecessor is found and `X.mdh` is composed first — handed a provisional build that has not yet been given the edge which makes `X.mdhs`, and therefore cannot make `X.mdhi` either. Naming `X.mdhs` as a direct prerequisite of `X.mdh` makes the build pass, which is the ordering pass saying exactly what it can and cannot see.\n\nThe ordering must see production through the ordinary targets that stand between two pending subninjas.\n\nAcceptance: `tests/make-project-reductions/recursive-wrapper-reached-through-an-ordinary-target` builds `mdh` with no makefile edit, and zsh builds through `scripts/check-make-projects.sh zsh`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:08:22.176915158Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:18:02.492450038Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The producer relation `dependency_ordered` builds is now transitive: an evaluation input no held recursive edge makes is followed back through the edge that does make it, and through that edge's own prerequisites, until a wrapper's output is reached or the chain runs out.\n\nThe walk is safe because of when it happens. Held recursive edges are not in the graph while their order is being decided — they are staged one at a time, in the order this decision produces — so every edge the walk can reach is an ordinary one, which is exactly the ground the search has to cross. `GraphSink::prerequisites_of` is that one step, over the new `BuildGraph::edge_inputs`, and a visited set keeps a cycle from becoming a loop while leaving Make's own cycle diagnostics responsible for reporting it.\n\nReaching a producer stops the walk rather than continuing through it. What that wrapper needs is that wrapper's own evaluation inputs, and they are already this pass's input.\n\nWhere it went: `src/make.rs dependency_ordered` (the frontier walk and the `sink` argument), `src/make/sink.rs GraphSink::prerequisites_of`, `src/frontend.rs BuildGraph::edge_inputs`.\n\nGates: workspace 870, all three Clippy passes clean, equivalence 297/278 with 0 disagreements, upstream inventory 1299 rows at 161/0/37/1101 with compiler at zero and no row moved, make_port green with no recording rewritten.\n\nEvidence beyond the reduction: with this and make-suffix-rule-name-splits-against-the-list in, zsh 5.9.2 builds from its own Makefiles under `scripts/check-make-projects.sh zsh` — `zsh 5.9.2 (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu)` and 36 modules in 29s at -j8, with no makefile edited.\n\nFixtures: tests/make/a-recursive-wrapper-is-reached-through-a-middleman, recorded from the oracle, asserts all four files the chain leaves behind. tests/make-project-reductions/recursive-wrapper-reached-through-an-ordinary-target is the reduction the test in tests/make_projects.rs runs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:18:07.823257018Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper
                title "Lift a $(MAKE) the shell would reach through one wrapper"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-subninja-recursion)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:25:27.711424968Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by building vim 9.2.0957 from its top-level Makefile with Ronin's Make mode beside GNU Make 4.4.1.\n\nvim's top-level Makefile exists to hand every goal to `src/`. The recipe for `all` echoes two lines, runs `cd src && $(MAKE) $@`, and then asks in two guards whether more recursion is wanted:\n\n    @if test \"$@\" = \"test\" -o \"$@\" = \"testtiny\"; then \\\n            $(MAKE) indenttest; \\\n            $(MAKE) syntaxtest; \\\n    fi\n\nGNU runs the guards, they are false for every goal but `test` and `clean`, and the only recursion that happens is the one into `src/`.\n\nRonin lifts `cd src && $(MAKE) $@` into a child compilation unit, but a guarded line carries a `$(MAKE)` that cannot be lifted — it is not in command position — and `kati/src-rs/ninja.rs composable_subninjas` is all-or-nothing, so the recipe compiles to no children at all while still counting as recursive and `src/make/sink.rs` refuses the build before anything runs. Nothing is built.\n\nThe refusal is stricter than the path already taken for the same construct on its own: a recipe whose ONLY recursion is unliftable is not refused — it runs as a script and lets the nested Make start. vim is the tree that shows the difference, and the inconsistency is the defect.\n\nOperator ruling of 2026-08-19: lift through wrappers. Teach the recognizer to see a `$(MAKE)` the shell would reach through a single-command wrapper, so more composes into the graph and nothing new runs nested; a genuine unliftable remainder falls into the already-existing pure-unliftable path rather than poisoning its liftable siblings.\n\nAcceptance: vim builds from its top-level Makefile, reaching `src/vim` by composition, and `tests/make-project-reductions/recipe-mixes-liftable-and-unliftable-recursion` builds with the guard left in."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:25:34.232472274Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:42:32.596826238Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in kati 6e0cf78. Two changes, which are the two halves of one question: which of a recipe's `$(MAKE)` lines names a child compilation, and what happens to the ones that do not.\n\nLIFTING THROUGH THE WRAPPER. A recipe line that is nothing but a parenthesized sequence IS that sequence. The parentheses keep a directory change and an environment from reaching the rest of the script, and a line holding only the sequence has no rest of the script for them to reach — GNU hands the line to a shell of its own either way. `command::unwrapped_command` takes the subshell off, `invokes_make` reads what is inside, and the command handed to the resolver is the invocation rather than the line it was written on, so the resolver receives exactly what the recogniser read.\n\nProbed against the oracle rather than argued. A child that prints its cwd, `MAKECMDGOALS`, `MAKELEVEL`, `MAKEFLAGS` and a command-line assignment, reached first by `cd src && $(MAKE) all V=1` and then by `(cd src && $(MAKE) all V=1)`: GNU gives `cwd=src goals=all level=1 flags=[ -- V=1] V=1` for both, and Ronin gives the same for both. That the wrapped form is now composed rather than nested is visible in Ronin's own output — one progress stream carrying the child's recipe, where the negative control `if true; then $(MAKE) -C src all; fi` shows the parent's line as an edge and then the nested Make's own counter.\n\nOnly `(`, and only when it closes at the last byte: `(a) && (b)` also opens on the first one, so the match is found rather than assumed, respecting quotes and backslashes. A brace group means the same thing to a shell and is deliberately left alone, because `{` is a command word only in command position and reading one wrongly changes what a line means, where `(` is always the operator.\n\nTHE REMAINDER. Splitting a recipe was all-or-nothing for three reasons, and one of them was a line GNU classified recursive whose invocation cannot be lifted. That line carries no `recursive_make`: it is residual work and reaches the executor as written, which is exactly what the same line does when it is a recipe's ONLY recursion. Having a liftable sibling is not what makes it intolerable, and refusing the whole recipe for it made a mixed recipe stricter than either of the recipes it is made of. The clause is gone; the other two stand, because a multi-line `.ONESHELL` recipe runs its lines in one shell and more than one MAKE reference on a line does not identify one static child compilation.\n\nAcceptance: vim 9.2.0957 builds from its TOP-LEVEL Makefile — `VIM - Vi IMproved 9.2`, `src/vim` at 17,872,752 bytes, 17s at -j8 — and it reaches it by composition: an incremental run shows one progress stream in which `src/`'s own compile and link edges are numbered, not a parent edge followed by a nested counter. The two nested Makes still visible inside `src/Makefile` (`cd auto/wayland; $(MAKE)` and the `po/` guard) were already pure-unliftable recipes and are unchanged, so nothing new runs nested.\n\nWhere it went: kati `src-rs/command.rs` (`unwrapped_command`, `subshell_body`, `invokes_make`) and `src-rs/ninja.rs` (`composable_subninjas`, the lifted command, and the deferred-recipe assertion's comment, which had claimed an absolute the compiler never held).\n\nTest contract changed rather than deleted. `tests/cli.rs recursive_recipes_are_never_half_composed` asserted the refusal this node removes; it is now `an_unliftable_line_keeps_its_siblings`, which asserts that the liftable sibling composes, that the unliftable line runs as the shell command it is, and — vim's own shape — that a guard which is false builds nothing. `a_subshell_holds_one_invocation` asserts the lift through a dry run, so it is the composition being measured and not the build. `unsplittable_submake_never_executes` still passes: two MAKE references on one line is one of the two reasons that stand.\n\nGates: workspace 883, conformance 334/53 unchanged, equivalence 297/278/0 disagreements, upstream inventory 1299 rows at 161/0/37/1101 with `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical — not one row moved, compiler at zero — all three Clippy passes clean, make_port green with no recording rewritten.\n\nThe gate itself lands here: `scripts/check-make-projects.sh` builds both packages from pinned sha256-verified tarballs into gitignored `reference/make-projects/`, asserts the binary exists and that zsh built twenty or more modules, and is called from `scripts/check-release.sh` ahead of `nplan port check --wave 4` — deliberately ahead, because that check is red for a bookkeeping reason and `set -eu` means a gate placed after it would not be a gate. Verified: a full `scripts/check-release.sh` run reaches it, prints both builds, and then stops at the port ladder exactly as make-release-gate-stops-at-the-port-ladder describes."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:43:37.101677038Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the delivery note above: the workspace count is 873, not 883. 868 at the dispatch's baseline, plus the three project reductions, plus `a_subshell_holds_one_invocation`, plus the kati unit test `a_subshell_around_the_whole_line_is_not_a_command`. `an_unliftable_line_keeps_its_siblings` replaced a test rather than adding one. Every other number in that note is as measured."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:44:31.193308335Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recursive-invocation-rule-admits-no-remainder
                title "Say in the spec what happens to a recursion that cannot be lifted"
                kind @docs
                deps (make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:44:05.174810831Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "`[spec:ronin:req:make.recursive-invocation+1]` says a recursive invocation through `$(MAKE)` compiles as `subninja` and \"is not a nested Make process or executor\". That is not what the compiler does, and it was not before make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper either: a `$(MAKE)` the recogniser cannot lift out — behind a runtime test, inside a guard, after a `;` — is residual work and reaches the executor as written, and the Make it starts is a process. The pure-unliftable path has always done this; wrapper-lifting only made the same outcome reachable in a recipe that also has a composed child.\n\nSo the rule states an absolute the implementation has never held, and the annotation sites under it are claiming something they do not deliver. Either the rule gains the boundary — every invocation the compiler can identify statically composes; one it cannot is left as the shell command it is — or the compiler is changed until the absolute is true, which means deciding what to do with a `$(MAKE)` whose reachability is a runtime question.\n\nFiled rather than folded in because a rule-body edit bumps `+1` to `+2` and cascades staleness onto make-subninja-recursion and everything else annotated against it, which is a decision about the spec rather than a consequence of this dispatch."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:14:31.70977468Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:14:53.558445243Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE OPERATOR HAS RULED. Brendan, 2026-08-19, verbatim: \"so it should just run that invocation when it can't be statically proven\".\n\nThe question this answers is the one the filing posed: `[spec:ronin:req:make.recursive-invocation+1]` said a `$(MAKE)` recursion \"is not a nested Make process or executor\" — an absolute — and the compiler has never held it, because an invocation whose reachability is a runtime question cannot be lifted out of the shell line that guards it. The two ways out were to give the rule a boundary or to change the compiler until the absolute became true. The ruling takes the first: the remainder is legitimate, and what happens in it is that the invocation RUNS.\n\nWHAT THAT MAKES NORMATIVE, and it is narrower than \"we gave up on an absolute\":\n\n  Composition stays mandatory wherever static identification is possible. The boundary legitimizes only the genuinely unprovable remainder, and every widening of what the compiler can prove shrinks it. The just-landed make-oneshell-recursive-recipe-is-refused (kati 4cbdb77 / ronin a9962a6) is the shape of that: it moved conjunctions, brace groups and work-beside-the-invocation OUT of the remainder and into composition, and refused nothing. The remainder is not a place to put things that are merely hard.\n\n  The remainder is DEFINED rather than recogniser-accident. What is in it today: a multi-line `.ONESHELL` recipe, whose lines share one shell so no reading of the recipe can say an earlier line did not change the directory or the variables the invocation reads; and an invocation guarded by a runtime test, where whether it happens at all is not answerable before the shell answers it.\n\n  What happens in the remainder is not \"a gap\" and not \"an emulation\". Ronin re-enters Make mode by its invoked name — `$(MAKE)` is the one-word absolutized make-named path minted by `make_named_invocation` (src/make/cli.rs) — and builds its own graph, with flags and jobserver travelling in `MAKEFLAGS` and the environment. So the nested thing is a compiler, not an executor: the architecture line holds either way, which is why running the remainder costs the rule its absolute and nothing else.\n\n  What did NOT change: `[spec:ronin:req:make.jobserver+1]` still says Ronin creates no GNU Make jobserver, and one composed graph still has one scheduler. A nested Ronin is a second scheduler for a graph the parent could not compose, which is the price of the remainder existing at all, not a second mechanism inside one graph.\n\nThe edit bumps `+1` to `+2` and every annotation and verification written against `+1` goes stale by construction. Each is re-read against the new wording and re-stamped or corrected — that sweep is this node's deliverable as much as the rule text is."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:23:45.261929989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in 7734656 (kati ee349ec on nplan/make-recursive-invocation-rule-admits-no-remainder). The rule is `[spec:ronin:req:make.recursive-invocation+2]`.\n\nWHAT THE BODY NOW SAYS, in three paragraphs where there was one.\n\nThe first keeps the old text and adds the obligation the operator ruling implies from the other side: an invocation the compiler CAN statically identify compiles as subninja, and it MUST. `+1` never said composing was required — it stated the outcome as a fact about all recursion, which is exactly why the fact being false left nothing normative behind. `+2` trades an untrue absolute for a true obligation, which is a strengthening of the composition clause and not a weakening of it.\n\nThe second states the boundary and, crucially, DEFINES the remainder rather than leaving it to be whatever the recogniser missed. It admits only what the recipe genuinely cannot settle, and names both members: a multi-line `.ONESHELL` recipe, whose lines share one shell so no reading establishes what an earlier line left for the invocation to read; and an invocation reached only through a runtime test. It says the remainder shrinks whenever the compiler learns to identify more, and that nothing belongs in it for being merely hard to lift. That sentence is the one that keeps the ruling from becoming a licence — make-oneshell-recursive-recipe-is-refused had just moved conjunctions, brace groups and work-beside-the-invocation OUT of the remainder, and the rule now says that direction is the only one.\n\nThe third says what starts in the remainder, and it is the paragraph that leaves the architecture line intact. Ronin re-enters Make mode by its invoked name — the absolutized make-named path `make_named_invocation` mints, `$(MAKE)` being one word — and compiles its own graph, with flags and any inherited job budget carried in `MAKEFLAGS` and the environment. So the nested process is another COMPILER, never a Make executor: no graph acquires GNU Make's scheduler, dirtiness model, or reporter by being reached this way. `[spec:ronin:req:make.jobserver+1]` is untouched and still true — Ronin stands up no GNU Make jobserver at any level.\n\nTHE SWEEP. 39 source references, every one re-read against the new body rather than bumped by machine judgement:\n\n* 15 impl sites — `src/make.rs:461` (the composition loop), `src/make/sink.rs:455` (`complete_subninja`), `src/make/cli.rs` ×6 (`session_for`, `record_invocation`, `make_named_invocation`, `run`, `record_invocation_variables`, `evaluated`), `src/make/cli/subninja.rs:15`, `src/make/cli/interface.rs:180`, `src/make/report.rs:82`, `kati/src-rs/command.rs:1144`, `kati/src-rs/build_sink.rs:268`.\n* 24 verify sites — `tests/cli.rs` ×13, `tests/make_regressions.rs` ×6, `src/make/cli.rs` tests ×5 (incl. `make_variable_is_invoked_path`), `src/make/cli/interface_tests.rs:44`, `src/jobserver.rs:850`.\n\nEvery one still says what it said, and the reason is structural: each is either about the composition clause, which got stronger, or about the `MAKEFLAGS`/environment interface a child reads, which the new third paragraph now names outright instead of leaving implicit. Two are better placed than before — `kati/src-rs/build_sink.rs:268` already documented the remainder in its own words, and `tests/make_regressions.rs:163` (`shell_loop_submake_inherits_overrides`) is the end-to-end verification of the remainder clause, a real child process learning a command-line override from the canonical MAKEFLAGS. `an_unliftable_line_keeps_its_siblings` verifies the boundary from the other side.\n\nONE CORRECTED RATHER THAN RE-STAMPED. `compiler_flag_variables` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) said \"no recursive Make runtime is created\" — the same absolute, in a comment, and now false by the rule's own admission. It says instead what is actually absent (a GNU Make jobserver, at any level) and adds that these switches are also how an invocation nothing could compose learns what it was invoked under, since the environment is its only channel.\n\nBEYOND THE SOURCE. `make-subninja-recursion`'s plan-side `implements` pin moved to `+2` with a note recording why the node's contract survives the bump. `[dec:ronin:make-compiles-to-ninja]` carried the absolute in two accepted consequences and in its rationale prose (\"A recipe spelling `$(MAKE)` does not ask Ronin to start a smaller Make inside the current build\"), and all three are amended to carry the boundary. `[dec:ronin:multicall-identity]` needed only its pin: its consequence was already written conditionally — \"When kati recognizes its recursive use\" — which is `+2`'s first paragraph a year early. The four cascade dependents were read and none of them states the absolute.\n\nGATES. No compiled behaviour changes — the delta is a rule body, comments, pins and a decision record. Workspace 881/0. `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean. Clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin lib+bins, `-D warnings` clean on kati. `nplan_spec_stale` reports no stale references, which is this node's deliverable."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:23:49.749750561Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-oneshell-recursive-recipe-is-refused
                title "Compile the two recursive recipe shapes that are still refused"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:44:12.664685086Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two reasons a recipe holding `$(MAKE)` is still refused whole, both surviving make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper, both reproducing against `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make` as builds GNU completes and Ronin does not start:\n\n  .ONESHELL with more than one recipe line. The lines share one shell, so a split loses state the later lines may depend on. GNU runs the recipe and recurses; Ronin says the recipe cannot compile as subninja.\n\n  More than one MAKE reference on a line, as in `$(MAKE) -C a && $(MAKE) -C b`. Two invocations on one line do not identify one static child compilation, but they do identify two, in a fixed order, and `tests/cli.rs unsplittable_submake_never_executes` is the recording of the refusal rather than an argument that the refusal is right.\n\nBeside them, one shape that composes nothing but is not a refusal: a brace group. `{ cd sub && $(MAKE) child; }` means to a shell exactly what `(cd sub && $(MAKE) child)` means, and the latter now lifts while the former runs nested. It was left out on purpose — `{` is a command word only in command position, so reading one wrongly changes what a line means where `(` is always the operator — and recognising it correctly is a small piece of shell grammar rather than a byte test."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:35:31.088064367Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:07:55.732878713Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed all three shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before touching anything, plus two the filing did not name. Every one is a build GNU completes.\n\n  (a) `.ONESHELL` with three lines, the middle one `$(MAKE) -C a`. GNU `one a two`; Ronin `recursive Make recipe cannot compile as subninja`, nothing built.\n  (b) `$(MAKE) -C a && $(MAKE) -C b`. GNU `a b`; Ronin the same refusal, nothing built.\n  (c) `{ cd a && $(MAKE); }`. GNU `a`; Ronin `a` too — nested, not refused, so this one only costs composition.\n\n  Beside them, and not in the filing:\n  (b') `(cd a && $(MAKE)) && (cd b && $(MAKE))` — the same two children written through the wrapper the previous node taught the recogniser to see through, and it nests rather than composing, because `invokes_make` read the whole line and found no `make` at its head.\n  (d) `cd a && $(MAKE) && echo tail >> ../log`. GNU `a tail`; Ronin `recursive Make recipe is not a static invocation`, nothing built. A THIRD refusal, and it comes from the other end: kati's recogniser called the line one liftable invocation and Ronin's resolver, which splits it into the words a nested process would have had as argv, refused the `>>` and ended the run. Not a shape anyone chose to refuse — a gap between two readings of the same line.\n\nThe filing's own framing of (b) held on probing and its framing of the refusal did not survive anywhere: a build GNU completes and Ronin will not start is not a boundary being defended, and `unsplittable_submake_never_executes` was a recording of that, not an argument for it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:08:38.536335148Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in kati 4cbdb77. All five shapes above now do what GNU does, and no recipe is refused at rule declaration any more.\n\n(b) A LINE MAY NAME MORE THAN ONE CHILD. `&&` runs the next command only when the last one won, and in that order, which is exactly what one child graph ordered after another does — so a line that is nothing but invocations joined by `&&` and the children it names describe the same build. `command::lifted_invocations` splits the line at its own top-level `&&` (quotes, escapes and wrappers respected, so `(a && b)` is one conjunct) and lifts every conjunct or none. No other joiner qualifies and the code says why: `;` runs the next whatever the last one did, and `||` runs it only when the last one lost, and neither is an edge.\n\nThat subsumes (b'): the conjuncts are unwrapped one at a time, so `(cd a && $(MAKE)) && { cd b && $(MAKE); }` is two children where before it was one nested shell. `Command::recursive_make` is now `Vec<LiftedInvocation>` — the invocations themselves, in written order, rather than the MAKE values that happened to appear — because the old field could count two references on a line and never say where either of them was.\n\n(c) A BRACE GROUP IS THE SUBSHELL WRITTEN THE OTHER WAY. `brace_group_body` reads it, and the reason it was left out before is the reason it needed care rather than a byte test: `(` is always the operator, while `{` and `}` are reserved words and are one only as a word of their own where a command may begin. The scanner tracks command position for exactly that, so `echo a{b}` stays one word, `echo '{ a; }'` stays a string, and `{ make a; } && { make b; }` is not one group closing at the end. A group that IS the whole line has no rest of the script for its `cd` to escape into, which is the same argument the parentheses already had.\n\n(a) A MULTI-LINE `.ONESHELL` RECIPE STILL COMPOSES NOTHING, and that is the answer rather than the problem. Its lines share a shell, so a line before the invocation may have changed the directory it runs in or set a variable it reads, and no reading of the recipe can say it did not — `cd sub` then `$(MAKE)` is the shape, and it is one line apart from a shape that composes. I looked at the narrower cases the node asked about (the invocation first, so nothing precedes it; the invocation last, so nothing follows) and declined both: the first still leaves the trailing lines in a shell of their own where `.ONESHELL` promised one shell, and neither is decidable from the recipe without reading the shell. So the recipe reaches the executor as the script it is and the Make it names starts. That is what a recipe whose only recursion cannot be lifted has always done, and refusing this one made a build GNU completes into a build that does not start.\n\n(d) THE RECOGNISER STOPS CLAIMING MORE THAN THE RESOLVER CAN CARRY. Two readings of one line disagreed and the disagreement was fatal. `invokes_make` is rewritten in terms of the same conjunct split — an optional `cd DIR &&`, an optional `exec`, then the make value — and every lifted command must be an argument list: ordinary bytes, quotes, backslash escapes and `$( )` substitutions, which is `src/make/cli/subninja.rs`'s own set. Everything else is a program rather than an invocation and runs as the line it is. The layering is stated in the comment: the recogniser lifts a subset of what the resolver reads, so a gap between them cannot end a run.\n\nWHAT THAT LEAVES OF THE REFUSAL. `SinkRule::contains_recursive` is gone. It existed to tell the sink \"this recipe is recursive but there is nothing to compose, refuse it\", and there is no longer any such recipe: a sink composes what it is given and runs what it is not. `FrontendError::UncomposableSubninja` stays for the one case where executing WOULD be wrong — a child that makes the very target its parent's wrapper produces, so no ordering of the two exists — and its documentation now says that rather than describing the removed case.\n\nTESTS. `tests/cli.rs unsplittable_submake_never_executes` is replaced by `conjoined_submakes_compose_in_order`, which keeps the old test's machinery and turns it around: `MAKE` is still a script that leaves a file behind if anything starts it, and the assertion is now that three children built in written order, the recipe's own line ran after them, and nothing nested ran. `make_regressions.rs a_brace_group_holds_one_invocation` mirrors `a_subshell_holds_one_invocation` — proved by a dry run, where a nested Make would have had to start to find out what it would do — and carries the negative control that `echo a{b}` is one word. Two kati unit tests: `a_brace_group_around_the_whole_line_is_not_a_command` and `a_conjunction_of_invocations_names_each_of_them`.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded:\n\n* `tests/make/a-recipe-line-may-name-two-children` — `$(MAKE) -C a && $(MAKE) -C b`, and beside it the same thing through a subshell and a brace group, over four children so nothing is compiled twice. GNU wrote `a b c d`.\n* `tests/make/oneshell-recursive-recipe-keeps-one-shell` — `.ONESHELL` with `cd sub` on the line before the invocation, which is why it cannot split. GNU wrote `child parent`.\n* `tests/make/a-recursive-line-with-work-beside-it-runs-as-written` — (d). GNU wrote `child tail`.\n\nONE ROW MOVED, and it is one of the refusals. `options/eval.diff.1` is GNU Make's own `--eval` recursion case, whose recipe is `@$(MAKE) -f Makefile && echo recurse` — shape (d) exactly — and it goes unclassified/`product-name+evaluation`, which is Ronin's refusal diagnostic, to narration/`ninja-progress+recipe-echo`. Reproduced by hand: both tools now print `eval infile … infile all recurse`. The audit found one thing the class does not say, which is that GNU prints `eval` twice and Ronin once — `--eval` does not reach a child's `MAKEFLAGS`. That is not this node's and not caused by it: a makefile that only echoes `$(MAKEFLAGS)`, with no recursion at all, shows the switch missing. Filed as make-eval-fragments-do-not-descend. Class totals 1299 rows 157/0/38/1104 from 158/0/38/1103; compiler bucket ZERO; every other row byte-identical.\n\nGATES. Workspace 881/0 (878 plus the two kati unit tests and the brace-group regression; the cli test replaced one). Conformance 334/53 normalised, unchanged, with the same 17/4/31/1 classification. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements — and it now compares more than it did, since an edge is recursive there when it has invocations rather than when the recipe mentioned one. Ninja conformance 425/425. make_port green with no recording rewritten. Clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin lib+bins, `-D warnings` clean on kati. Non-make build 481/13, unchanged.\n\n`scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green: vim 9.2 in 26s, zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 53s. The host was not quiet — load average above 20 throughout — so that zsh figure is not comparable to the 34s recorded earlier in the session. Measured against a worktree built at this node's parent commit, back to back: parent 49s and 46s, this tree 47s and 44s. No regression; the spread is the host.\n\nThe fresh, unconfigured vim tree from make-mixed-recipe-runs-its-residual-last still builds end to end — `src/auto/config.mk` written by the staged first line, `src/vim` at 4,574,992 bytes — which is the check that the rewritten recogniser did not stop lifting `cd src && $(MAKE) $@`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:08:44.153412781Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-mixed-recipe-runs-its-residual-last
                title "Run a mixed recipe's own lines where the recipe wrote them"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T16:44:25.90090385Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A recipe that composes a child and keeps some of its own lines runs those lines AFTER the child, because `complete_subninja` makes the residual edge wait on the child targets. GNU runs a recipe's lines in the order they are written.\n\nvim's top-level Makefile is where this is visible, and it is only harmless there by accident:\n\n    all …:\n            @if test ! -f src/auto/config.mk; then \\\n                    cp src/config.mk.dist src/auto/config.mk; \\\n            fi\n            @echo \"Starting make in the src directory.\"\n            …\n            cd src && $(MAKE) $@\n\nThe first line exists to put `src/auto/config.mk` in place BEFORE the child reads it, and Ronin compiles the child first. The gate passes because `scripts/check-make-projects.sh` runs configure, which writes that file, so the guard is false either way — but a tree configured differently would have Ronin compile `src/Makefile` against a missing include that GNU would have created a moment earlier.\n\nThis predates make-recursion-lifts-through-a-wrapper: any recipe mixing ordinary lines with a liftable `$(MAKE)` has had it since composition landed. What makes it worth a node now is that the mixed recipe used to be refused in the shape most likely to hit it, so the ordering had less ground to be wrong on.\n\nNot obviously fixable by reordering alone: the child is COMPILED at graph construction, and a residual line that has to run first is a compiler input, which is what the evaluation-boundary machinery in `src/make.rs` already exists to express for prerequisites. The question is whether a recipe's own earlier lines belong in that same staging."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:03:26.289765533Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:03:41.304035594Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed the filing against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before touching anything. It holds, on all three faces, and one fear the analysis raised turns out not to exist.\n\nOrdering, plain: `all:` with `@echo L1`, `$(MAKE) -C a`, `@echo L2`, `$(MAKE) -C b`, `@echo L3` gives GNU `L1 a L2 b L3` and Ronin `a b L1 L2 L3`. So the defect is not only \"the residual runs last\" but \"the residual is one block\", and an interleaved recipe has as many wrong places as it has invocations.\n\nvim's shape, reduced: a leading line that copies `config.mk.dist` into the child's directory, and a child whose Makefile `include`s it. GNU builds it and the child prints `from-dist`. Ronin does not start: `Makefile:1: config.mk: No such file or directory` / `No rule to make target 'config.mk'`, exit 2. That is the hazard the filing predicted, reproduced without deconfiguring anything.\n\nThe fear that did not survive: I expected staging a recipe's own lines as compiler input to over-execute, running the leading lines of goals GNU never reaches — `distclean: ; rm -rf build; $(MAKE) -C src distclean` under `make all`. It cannot happen. kati emits only the goal-reachable dependency nodes, so a target the goals do not reach is never a pending subninja at all. Measured: a two-goal makefile where only `all` is asked for leaves `distclean`'s recipe untouched and never compiles its child, and the same holds for the two-invocation shape whose first child's targets the ChildGroup boundary already stages. So every pending in a unit is a recipe GNU would reach, and `stage_recursive_wrapper` already decides the remaining question — whether GNU would RUN it — before any of this is asked.\n\nThat makes the evaluation-boundary machinery the right home, and the node's open question answers itself: a recipe's earlier lines belong in the same staging its prerequisites do, for the same reason and with the same reachability guarantee."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:32:58.613868028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in kati 412c054 plus Ronin's composition loop. The node's open question — whether a recipe's own earlier lines belong in the same staging its prerequisites do — is answered yes, and the reason is narrower than the question: those lines can only reach the child through the filesystem, and the filesystem is exactly what the child's compilation reads.\n\nWHAT THE SPLIT IS. kati handed the sink one residual block holding every non-recursive line. A block is the wrong shape however it is placed, because a recipe with two invocations has two right answers and one of them is between the children. The split is now by position: the lines ahead of each invocation travel ON that invocation as `SinkSubninja::preceding`, and only the lines after the last one remain `residual_command`. Assembling one run of a recipe's lines became `NinjaGenerator::residual_segment`, since there are now as many runs as the recipe has invocations; it keeps both properties the single site had — the flags are the whole recipe's, because every run reaches the same shell, and automatic depfile detection must select the same file for every run as it did for the recipe.\n\nWHERE IT WENT IN RONIN. `SubninjaInvocation` carries a `preceding_rule`, minted in `declare_rule` beside the residual's. `GraphSink::stage_preceding_lines` puts that rule in the graph as an edge over a `.ronin_recipe_stage/N` proxy — a name that is never a file, whose edge is `always_dirty` because reaching it means the wrapper is out of date and GNU would be running this recipe, and which `-t` therefore leaves alone for the reason `-t` leaves a `.PHONY` name alone. `compose_subninjas` stages that proxy at a new `EvaluationPredecessor::PrecedingLines(i)` boundary, immediately before compiling child i and after the wrapper has been found dirty. The three boundary sites are now one `CompilationState::stage`, which is also what kept the function under the density gate.\n\nIts bindings are the recipe's shell, environment and text but NOT the recipe's depfile or restat: those answer for the file the rule makes, the edge that makes it is the recursive wrapper, and two edges claiming one discovered dependency file would be a graph nobody meant to write.\n\nWHY STAGING RATHER THAN ORDERING. Making the child's edges merely WAIT on a leading-lines edge fixes execution order and not vim, because the child Makefile is read at graph construction and the read is already over by the time anything waits. The order in the loop is the load-bearing part: prerequisites, then the dirtiness probe, then the leading lines. Probing first is what keeps a recipe GNU would not run from running its own first line — a target that is up to date composes nothing and stages nothing.\n\nWHAT I THOUGHT WOULD BLOCK IT AND DID NOT. Staging a recipe's own lines looks like it must over-execute — `distclean: ; rm -rf build; $(MAKE) -C src distclean` under `make all`. It cannot: kati emits only the goal-reachable dependency nodes, so a recipe the goals do not reach is never a pending subninja. Measured both ways, and the pre-existing ChildGroup staging has been safe for the same reason.\n\nEVIDENCE. Three probes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, each GNU-first:\n\n* `L1 / $(MAKE) -C a / L2 / $(MAKE) -C b / L3` — GNU `L1 a L2 b L3`, Ronin was `a b L1 L2 L3`, Ronin is now `L1 a L2 b L3`.\n* vim's shape reduced — a leading line that copies `config.mk.dist` into the child's directory and a child that `include`s it. GNU builds it; Ronin refused with `Makefile:1: config.mk: No such file or directory` / `No rule to make target 'config.mk'`, exit 2. It now builds and the child prints what the dist file said.\n* A FRESH, UNCONFIGURED vim 9.2.0957 unpacked from the tarball into scratch — not the gate's configured tree — builds end to end from its top-level Makefile: 29s at -j8 on a loaded host, `src/auto/config.mk` written, `src/vim` linked at 4,574,992 bytes. The build log's first line is vim's three leading lines assembled into one staged script, and `src/Makefile` is read after it. That is the hazard the node was filed on, closed on the tree that carries it.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, neither hand-written:\n\n* `tests/make/a-recursive-recipes-own-lines-run-where-they-were-written` — the two-invocation interleaving. GNU wrote `L1 a L2 b L3` into the recording; a recipe that runs its own lines in one block fails it wherever it puts the block.\n* `tests/make/recursive-child-reads-what-an-earlier-recipe-line-wrote` — vim's shape, where the ordering is not cosmetic: without the leading line the child's Makefile cannot be read at all.\n\nONE ROW MOVED, and it is the case this node is about. `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2` is GNU's own test of a recipe that echoes a line and then recurses — exactly the mixed shape — and it goes unclassified/`evaluation` to interface/`makeflags-content`. The echo is now where GNU puts it; the residue is that Ronin reports one more restart than GNU, because a staging pass is a restart. That is not new and not this node's: the pre-existing ChildGroup staging already inflates `MAKE_RESTARTS` on a two-invocation recipe with no leading lines at all, measured. Filed separately as make-staging-pass-counts-as-a-make-restart. Class totals 1299 rows 158/0/38/1103 from 159/0/37/1103; compiler bucket ZERO; every other row byte-identical.\n\nGATES. Workspace 878/0 (876 baseline plus the two fixtures — the corpus cases are one test, so the count moves only with the harness). Conformance 334/53 normalised, unchanged. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements. Ninja conformance 425/425. make_port green with no recording rewritten — recording the two new cases left every other `expected` file untouched. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green: vim 9.2 in 25s, zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 34s. Clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin lib+bins, `-D warnings` clean on kati. Non-make build `--no-default-features` 481 passed / 13 failed — measured against a worktree of the parent commit, which gives 481/13 as well, so unchanged; the dispatch's 494/14 is a figure this command does not produce at either tree, and the module is behind `feature = \"make\"` so it cannot have moved.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, said precisely. Under `-n` a mixed recipe with leading lines now ends at the staging pass, printing the leading lines rather than the whole recipe and the child's. That is the pre-existing shape of every staged compiler input — a recipe with prerequisites already did it — reached in one more place, and GNU's `-n` does not run the leading lines either, so what differs is what is narrated rather than what happens. Not filed as its own node because the answer belongs to whatever decides what `-n` means across the whole compiler boundary rather than to this recipe shape."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:33:31.055945929Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-empty-recipe-target-is-not-a-ninja-alias
                title "Let a target made by nothing still be absent"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:24:03.098082159Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member, as the reason `$?` still names the wrong SET of prerequisites once it names them correctly. It is not archive-specific and it is not about `$?`.\n\nA Makefile target whose recipe runs nothing compiles to an edge with no command, and the dirty scan reads such an edge as Ninja's phony: an alias for its inputs. Two of Ninja's phony rules are wrong for Make, and both are visible.\n\n* A phony output that is missing is given its newest input's date. So the file's absence is invisible to everything that reads it, and to the edge above it the target looks as current as its own prerequisites.\n* A phony edge is dirty only when an input is. So the target being absent is not itself a reason to run anything — which is what make-empty-recipe-pattern-rule-drops-its-prerequisites had to work around from the other side, by widening the intermediate walk to descend through an absent name.\n\nNon-archive reproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. `final` exists and is newer than `src`, so nothing but `mid` can force it:\n\n    final: mid\n    \t@echo \"ran [$?]\" >> log\n    \t@touch final\n    mid: src ;\n\n  setup: printf 's\\n' > src; sleep 1.1; touch final\n\n  GNU:   ran [mid] — every invocation, because `mid` is never written\n  Ronin: ronin: no work to do.\n\nGNU Make's model is the plain one: `mid` has a recipe, the recipe is empty, running it writes nothing, and a prerequisite that does not exist after being made is a prerequisite that is newer than the target. `notice_finished_file` records that the commands ran, `d->changed` stays set, and `final` is remade every time.\n\nThe archive face is the same edge with a member on it, and it is where the wrong `$?` set shows:\n\n    mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o) mylib.a(b.o)\n    \t@echo \"Q=[$?]\" >> log\n    (%): % ;\n\n  setup: printf 'aaa\\n' > a.o; printf 'bbb\\n' > b.o; ar -rcU mylib.a a.o\n\n  GNU:   Q=[b.o]\n  Ronin: Q=[a.o]\n\n`mylib.a(b.o)` is not in the archive, so it is missing — and the phony edge over `b.o` hands it `b.o`'s date, which is older than the archive, so it drops out of `$?` entirely. (The other half of that line, `a.o` appearing when it is up to date, is make-archive-member-date-is-low-resolution-on-the-target-side.)\n\nWhat the fix has to keep. An edge with no command is also how a manifest's real `build x: phony y` reaches the graph, and there the Ninja rules are the right ones. So the two cannot be told apart by \"has no command\" — the front end has to say which it compiled, and the graph has to carry it. Ronin already carries per-edge properties no manifest can express (`intermediate`, `disposable`, `withdrawable_outputs`), so this is another of those rather than a new kind of thing.\n\nNot a regression and not urgent: both reproducers pre-date the archive work, and `make-empty-recipe-pattern-rule-drops-its-prerequisites` fixed the half that made a build do too little rather than too much."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:58:04.062267834Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:58:26.620711489Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed the filing before touching anything, and it holds on both faces, plus two the filing did not name.\n\nThe non-archive reproducer reproduces exactly as written: `final: mid` + `mid: src ;` with `final` newer than `src` gives GNU `ran [mid]` on every invocation and Ronin `no work to do` forever. The archive face gives GNU `Q=[b.o]` and Ronin `Q=[a.o]`.\n\nTwo more faces measured against the oracle, because the fix had to not move them:\n\n* `mid: src` with NO recipe at all — not the `;` the filing wrote — is the same divergence. GNU reaches it by a different road (`remake_file` with `cmds == 0` pretends success instead of running an empty command list) and arrives at the same place: `mid` is not there, so `Must remake target 'final'` and `$?` names `mid`. So the property is not \"the recipe was empty\" but \"no command ran and the name is still absent\", which is exactly what having no rule means at the sink.\n* `mid: ;` with no prerequisites already agreed, and it agreed for the reason the fix generalises: the phony branch's `missing_without_inputs` term is this same rule, narrowed to the one case where it could not be confused with an alias.\n\nWhere it went. `Edge` gains `outputs_unaliased`, beside `intermediate`/`disposable`, carried through `EdgeSpec` (`frontend.rs`) and set by the Make sink alone — `declare_edge` sets it from `edge.rule.is_none()`, because every edge that reaches there came from a Makefile and having no rule is the whole of the question. `probe_subninja` sets it false and says why: a recursive wrapper really is an alias for the child goals that replace it. Nothing in the manifest parser touches it, so `build x: phony y` reaches the graph meaning exactly what it meant.\n\n`recompute_edge_dirty_with`'s phony branch now asks the edge rather than the rule. An alias still substitutes its newest input's date into a missing output and is still dirty only when an input is; an unaliased edge substitutes nothing and counts its own absence, however many prerequisites it has. The `missing_without_inputs` term stays for the manifest side, where it is Ninja's own rule.\n\nGate numbers with this change alone: workspace 876/0 (874 baseline plus the two tests below), conformance 334/53, equivalence 278 compared / 0 disagreements, ninja conformance 425/425 untouched, upstream inventory 1299 rows 161/0/37/1101 with the compiler bucket at zero and no drift line, make_port green with no recording rewritten, vim + zsh green.\n\nTests. `graph::tests::ronin_graph_a_target_made_by_nothing_is_absent_rather_than_an_alias` puts an alias and an unaliased edge in one graph and asserts only one of them changed. `make::equivalence::a_target_made_by_nothing_reaches_only_the_direct_graph` records the one-sided property the way `withdrawal_reaches_only_the_direct_graph` and `a_precious_pattern_spares_an_intermediate` do — a manifest cannot say it, the parsed side must not invent it, and the compared shape still agrees.\n\nOracle-recorded fixture `an-empty-recipe-leaves-its-target-absent`, which carries all three shapes at once: `mid: src ;`, `bare: src`, and `kept: src ;` where `kept` is on the disk. GNU answered `ran [mid bare]`, so the control is in the recording rather than in an argument about it.\n\nThe archive face is not fixed by this alone: `Q=[a.o b.o]` now, where GNU says `Q=[b.o]`. `b.o` appearing is this node; `a.o` still appearing is make-archive-member-date-is-low-resolution-on-the-target-side, which is next."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T19:02:02.421210827Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T19:32:12.21179583Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "The rule I landed for `outputs_unaliased` was narrower than GNU Make's, and make-archive-member-date-is-low-resolution-on-the-target-side is where that showed. Recording it here rather than leaving the note above as the account.\n\nWhat I wrote: an unaliased edge is dirty when an input is or when an output is missing. What GNU Make does: nothing special at all. A target whose recipe writes nothing is an ordinary target — `update_file_1` reads its own mtime, compares it against its prerequisites, and remakes it when they are newer, exactly as for a target with a recipe. Having no command is only a fact about what running it does.\n\nThe case that found it is GNU Make's own SV 61436 sequence. `mylib.a(b.o)` is in the archive and `b.o` was swept as an intermediate, so nothing is missing and no input is dirty — and yet `b.c` being touched has to reach the archive. It reaches it through the comparison my rule did not make: the absent intermediate stands in `b.c`'s date, which is newer than the member's, so the member's rule must run. Under the narrow rule Ronin did nothing at all on that step.\n\nSo the branch is now a guard rather than a rewrite: `is_phony_rule(rule) && !outputs_unaliased` keeps Ninja's alias, and an unaliased edge falls through to the ordinary comparison with every other target.\n\nThe half of my note that is still right is why the narrow rule looked complete: every case I probed for that node either has a missing output or a dirty input, and all seven still agree. What the narrow rule also could not do is the case I had probed and passed by accident — `mid` on disk and older than `src`, where GNU runs the empty recipe and does NOT remake what reads `mid`. Under the ordinary comparison the edge is now dirty, so the answer has to come from the other end: `finish_phony_edge` re-reads an unaliased edge's outputs once it has been reached and prunes its dependents when they are all still there, which is `outputs_reobserved` — the property Make edges already carry and that a commandless edge had never reached. A target the recipe left absent is not pruned, which is what keeps the missing case remaking."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-member-date-is-low-resolution-on-the-target-side
                title "Round a member's date up where GNU Make rounds it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:24:31.686558019Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member: with `$?` naming members correctly it still named too many of them, and this is why.\n\n`src/os.rs`'s `archive_member_stat` dates a member `date * 1e9 + 999_999_999` — the last nanosecond of the second the index recorded. The comment beside it is right about why some rounding is needed and wrong about where: it keeps a member filed in the same second as the object it came from from looking older than it, which matters when the member is the TARGET being made. GNU Make does not put the rounding there.\n\nWhat GNU does (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c). `f_mtime` builds a member's timestamp with `file_timestamp_cons (hname, member_date, 0)` — nanoseconds zero, the START of the second — and separately sets `file->low_resolution_time = 1` on it. The rounding is in `update_file_1`, applied to `this_mtime`, the mtime of the file being updated, and only when that file is low-resolution: `this_mtime += FILE_TIMESTAMPS_PER_S - 1 - ns`. So a member is rounded up when something is being made FROM it into the archive, and never when it is a prerequisite of something else.\n\nThe consequence of rounding both ways is a build that never settles:\n\n    mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o)\n    \t@echo \"ran\" >> log\n\n  setup: printf 'aaa\\n' > a.o; ar -rcU mylib.a a.o\n\n  GNU:   make: 'mylib.a' is up to date. — twice, and forever\n  Ronin: runs the recipe on every invocation\n\n`ar` wrote the member with `a.o`'s date; the archive's own mtime is a fraction of a second later, in the same second. Rounded to the end of that second the member is 0.7s newer than the archive containing it, so the archive is always out of date.\n\nIt is also four rows of the upstream inventory. `features/archives.diff.13`, `.14` and `.15` are GNU Make's own SV 61436 sequence past its first step — \"'mylib.a' is up to date\", then one source touched and refiled, then up to date again — and all three still carry a `ninja-failure-block`, because Ronin refiles an archive whose member objects the previous step swept away as intermediates and `ar` is handed names that are not there. `.12`, the first step, agrees since make-question-variable-reads-an-archive-member.\n\nShape of the fix, and why it is not a one-liner: Ronin keeps one mtime per node and this is a property of which SIDE of a comparison the node is on. GNU keeps a flag per file and consults it where the comparison is made, so Ronin needs the same — the stat answers with the start of the second, and the round-up moves to the places that read an edge's outputs (`recompute_edge_dirty_with`'s `oldest_output`, and the deferred capture's baseline). Which nodes are low-resolution is the compiler's to declare, as `intermediate` and `disposable` already are, rather than something the graph engine works out from a parenthesis in a path.\n\nkati's own side of this is already right and can be read as the model: `crate::archive::member_timestamp` answers with the start of the second and `member_timestamp_as_target` is the rounded one, and `command.rs` picks between them by which question it is asking."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T19:02:24.850403249Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T19:40:58.325011911Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's diagnosis and its shape-of-the-fix both held. Three reproducers probed against the oracle before anything moved: the archive that never settles (`mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o)`, GNU up to date forever, Ronin running every invocation), the `$?` set that names a current member, and GNU Make's own SV 61436 sequence run step by step with `ARFLAGS=-rvU` — which is what the suite actually passes, and without which every member's date is zero and the case measures nothing.\n\nThe mechanism, restated from `remake.c` because the site matters. `f_mtime` builds a member's timestamp with `file_timestamp_cons (hname, member_date, 0)` — the START of the second — and separately sets `file->low_resolution_time = 1`. The rounding is in `update_file_1`, applied to `this_mtime`, the mtime of the file being UPDATED, and only there: `this_mtime += FILE_TIMESTAMPS_PER_S - 1 - ns`. So the same member answers two different dates depending on which side of a comparison it is on, and `archive_member_stat` was giving the target-side answer to everyone.\n\nWhere it went. `archive_member_stat` now answers `date * 1e9`, the plain date, and says in its comment that the other question is asked elsewhere. `Edge` gains `outputs_low_resolution` beside `outputs_unaliased`, carried through `EdgeSpec` and set by the Make sink from the output name being an archive member — decided from what the Makefile wrote, once, rather than from a parenthesis the build engine finds in a path. It is carried through `PendingSubninja` like `intermediate` and `disposable`, so a recursive wrapper over a member keeps it rather than being asserted not to be one.\n\nThe reading itself is one function, `Graph::target_mtime`, and three callers, which is the whole list of places an output is on the target side: `recompute_edge_dirty_with`'s `oldest_output`, `recompute_deferred_freshness`'s baseline comparison, and `Builder::deferred_work`'s late `is_new`. `FileTime::to_end_of_second` is the arithmetic, with missing and unobserved answering for themselves.\n\nOne thing the filing did not name, found by probing the deferred face. `recompute_deferred_freshness` publishes the baseline onto the edge's outputs, and an archive member edge IS deferred — its real output is observed through the deferred metadata. Rounding the baseline at capture therefore wrote the rounded date onto the member's node, and the archive above it read that as its prerequisite: the same defect, moved twelve lines. So the stored baseline is the plain one and the rounding is applied at the two comparisons instead. Measured, not reasoned: with the round-up removed from `deferred_work` alone, `(%): %` with a `$?` recipe refiles a current member on every invocation.\n\nThe other thing this needed was a correction to make-empty-recipe-target-is-not-a-ninja-alias, recorded as an errata there. SV 61436's third step reaches the archive only through a comparison that node's narrower rule did not make, so an unaliased edge now falls through to the ordinary one, and `finish_phony_edge` re-reads its outputs afterwards and prunes its dependents when they are all still there — which is `outputs_reobserved`, the property Make edges already carry and that a commandless edge had never reached.\n\nInventory audit. The three rows this node was filed against moved, and only those three; every other row in the file is byte-identical. `features/archives.diff.13` and `.15` — SV 61436's two \"up to date\" steps — lose their `ninja-failure-block` and become pure narration, `no-work-line+up-to-date-line`: Make says \"'mylib.a' is up to date\", Ronin says \"no work to do\". `.14`, the step that touches `b.c`, also loses the failure block and keeps `ninja-progress+evaluation`, which is the same residue `.12` carries and the same one the previous dispatch audited: the progress line, and the `rm` of the swept intermediate that Ronin does not narrate. I reproduced the whole four-step sequence by hand against the oracle to check that: the two tools compile the same file, file the same member, sweep the same intermediates and end with the same directory. Class totals 1299 rows 159/0/37/1103, from 161/0/37/1101 — two rows out of unclassified into narration, compiler bucket ZERO.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded, all three with hand-written archives rather than `ar` output, because ar built with `--enable-deterministic-archives` writes every date as zero and the dates are the whole point:\n\n* `an-archive-member-is-current-in-the-second-it-was-filed` — the member read both ways at once, with a five-seconds-apart control beside it that no rounding can settle either way.\n* `an-archive-holding-a-current-member-is-not-refiled` — the same through GNU Make's built-in `(%): %` rule rather than one the makefile writes, and being current, its `$(AR)` line never runs, so the fixture gates the timestamps rather than ar.\n* `question-variable-drops-a-member-the-archive-already-holds` — the node-1 reproducer's archive face, `Q=[b.o]`, which needs both nodes: `b.o` appears because a member nothing filed is absent, `a.o` is absent from the list because a member filed in its object's second is current.\n\nGates: workspace 878/0, conformance 334/53, equivalence 278 compared / 0 disagreements with the new property recorded beside the others, ninja conformance 425/425, make_port green with no recording rewritten, upstream inventory 1299 rows 159/0/37/1103 audited above, vim + zsh green, clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin and `-D warnings` clean on kati, non-make build 494/14 against its parent's 493/14 (the one added test is the new graph unit test) with identical warning counts.\n\nNo kati change was needed: kati's own two readings were already split by the previous dispatch and are what this modelled Ronin's side on."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T19:42:40.348967632Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-staging-pass-counts-as-a-make-restart
                title "Stop a compiler-input pass from counting as a Make restart"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-mixed-recipe-runs-its-residual-last)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T20:33:27.123874338Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "`MAKE_RESTARTS` counts one higher than GNU Make's for every recipe whose recursive child needed staged compiler input, because Ronin gets that work done by ending the read and starting it again, and `prepare_graph` increments `restarts` on every `Settlement::Restart` whatever sent it there.\n\nGNU Make's counter answers one question: how many times the read started over because bringing a Makefile up to date changed it (`main.c`, written into `MAKE_RESTARTS` before each re-exec). Compiler-input staging is not that. Nothing the read consulted moved; the compilation stopped because a `$(MAKE)` recipe's prerequisites, or an earlier child, or the recipe's own earlier lines, had to be on the ground before the child Makefile could be read at all. GNU has no such phase, so it has no count for it — and adding Ronin's to the number a Makefile reads makes the Makefile see a restart that, in GNU's terms, did not happen.\n\nReproduces against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with no leading lines and no makefile remaking at all, which is what makes it not a consequence of make-mixed-recipe-runs-its-residual-last:\n\n    recurse:\n    \t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f a.mk all\n    \t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f b.mk all\n    $(info MAKE_RESTARTS=$(MAKE_RESTARTS))\n\n  GNU:   MAKE_RESTARTS=          (once)\n  Ronin: MAKE_RESTARTS=  then MAKE_RESTARTS=1\n\nEvery predecessor kind does it: `ParentPrerequisites`, `ChildGroup`, and `PrecedingLines`. It is visible on GNU Make's own suite at `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2`, which is a recipe that echoes a line and then recurses; that row's residue after make-mixed-recipe-runs-its-residual-last is this and nothing else, which is why it classifies as `makeflags-content` — a Makefile reading an invocation variable and seeing a different value — rather than as an evaluation difference.\n\nWhere the fix goes is where the count is kept rather than where the pass is decided. `prepare_graph` (src/make/cli.rs) increments one counter for both reasons; `build_compiler_inputs` already knows which it was, since a settled-boundary restart and a moved-Makefile restart arrive at `Settlement::Restart` from different arms. Two counters, and only the Makefile one reaches `record_invocation_variables`. What has to stay true is that `MAKE_RESTARTS` is undefined on the first read and `1` after one real restart, and that a staged pass does not reset it — the two kinds interleave, and `feature-restart-keeps-recursive-staging-done` is the fixture that already holds the interleaving."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:24:12.076075498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:43:16.991678824Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `Settlement::Restart` was one word for two events and is now two.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, and it is where the node said the count is kept rather than where the pass is decided. `build_compiler_inputs` already knew which kind it had — a remade Makefile arrives through `settled_makefiles`' `Settled::Again`, a settled compiler-input boundary through the `Pass::Current | Pass::Ran | Pass::Lost` arm with boundaries left over — and threw the distinction away by returning the same variant from both. The boundary arm now returns `Settlement::Staged`, and `prepare_graph` counts only `Settlement::Restart`. Nothing about which passes happen, or in what order, changed: the loop still goes around, still costs one of its hundred attempts, and still carries `settled_boundaries` forward. Only the number a Makefile reads moved.\n\nBoth variants carry their reason in the doc rather than in the caller, because the caller is now the only thing that can get this wrong: `Restart` says it is GNU Make's restart, the one `MAKE_RESTARTS` counts; `Staged` says nothing the read consulted moved and GNU Make has no such phase, so it has no count for it either.\n\nPROBED FIRST, GNU-first, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\n* The node's own reproducer, two children and no makefile remaking at all: GNU `MAKE_RESTARTS=` once, Ronin `MAKE_RESTARTS=` then `MAKE_RESTARTS=1`. Now `` and ``.\n* A remade `include` beside the same two children: GNU `[]` then `[1]`; Ronin was `[]`, `[1]`, `[2]`. Now `[]`, `[1]`, `[1]` — the real restart still counts, and the staged passes neither add to it nor put it back.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, and each one fails on the parent tree — measured, by putting the increment back for one build and running them:\n\n* `tests/make/feature-staging-a-recursive-child-is-not-a-restart` — two children, nothing remade. GNU wrote `restarts=[]`; the parent tree writes `restarts=[1]`.\n* `tests/make/feature-a-restart-still-counts-beside-a-staged-child` — the interleaving, which is the half that a fix could break by simply never counting. GNU wrote `restarts=[1] value=[built]`; the parent tree writes `restarts=[2]`. Its neighbour `feature-restart-keeps-recursive-staging-done` already held the other property of the interleaving (staged work stays done across a restart) and is unaffected.\n\nBoth write the value from the recipe rather than from `$(info)`, deliberately: the harness compares filesystem effects, and a recipe runs once in the final graph, which is the value a Makefile actually acts on.\n\nTHE ROW DID NOT MOVE, and the audit is the interesting part of this node. `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2` is still interface/`makeflags-content`, and inventory totals are unchanged at 1299 rows 157/0/38/1104 with `makeflags-content` still 36. Reproduced the case by hand to find out why. The residue used to be two things and the class only ever named one. With the counter fixed, every value in that case now matches GNU — parent `MAKE_RESTARTS=`, `1`, `2`, and the child's own empty one — and what is left is one extra `MAKE_RESTARTS=2` LINE, which is the parent's makefile-level `$(info)` firing again on the staging pass that precedes compiling the child. The classifier's shape test cannot tell an extra line from a wrong value, so the row keeps a name that is no longer accurate about it.\n\nThat residue is a real defect with a filesystem-observable reproducer — `$(file >> log,pass)` beside two children writes one line under GNU and two under Ronin — and it is filed as make-staging-pass-repeats-an-immediate-side-effect, depending on this node. It is pre-existing: a staging pass has always re-read the makefile, and stopping the pass being COUNTED does not stop it happening.\n\nGATES. Workspace 881/0 (the corpus is one test, so two new cases do not move the count). Conformance 334/53 normalised, unchanged. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements. Ninja conformance 425/425. make_port green with no recording rewritten — recording the two new cases left every other `expected` byte-identical. Upstream inventory 1299 rows 157/0/38/1104, compiler bucket ZERO, every row byte-identical. Clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin lib+bins. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host: vim 9.2 in 17s, zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29s. A fresh, unconfigured vim 9.2.0957 unpacked from the tarball builds end to end at -j8 — `src/auto/config.mk` written, `src/vim` linked at 4,574,992 bytes — which is the check that touching the pass loop did not disturb the staging that tree depends on."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:43:52.352014793Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-eval-fragments-do-not-descend
                title "Pass --eval text down to a child Make the way MAKEFLAGS does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-oneshell-recursive-recipe-is-refused)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:00:25.956400702Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "`-E`/`--eval` reaches the compilation it was given to and stops there. GNU Make puts it in `MAKEFLAGS`, so every Make below reads the same fragment before its own makefile, and a child that a fragment defines a goal or a variable in behaves accordingly. Ronin's children — composed or nested — see neither the fragment nor the switch.\n\nTwo reproducers against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, and the second is the shorter statement of the same thing.\n\n    all: ; @echo \"MAKEFLAGS=$$MAKEFLAGS\"\n\n  make '--eval=$(info eval)'\n  GNU:   MAKEFLAGS= --eval=$$(info\\ eval)\n  Ronin: MAKEFLAGS=\n\n    $(info infile)\n    all: ; @echo all\n    recurse: ; @$(MAKE) -f Makefile\n\n  make --no-print-directory '--eval=$(info eval)' recurse\n  GNU:   eval infile eval infile all\n  Ronin: eval infile      infile all\n\nSo the switch is accepted and compiled — make-option-eval-and-warnings delivered that, and the fragment does reach the makefile it was passed with — but `propagated_makeflags` does not carry it, which is the one place a child could get it. GNU's own quoting of the value is part of the answer and is visible above: `$` is doubled and the space is backslashed, because what MAKEFLAGS holds has to survive being read back as a command line.\n\nFound while delivering make-oneshell-recursive-recipe-is-refused, and it is the residue of `options/eval.diff.1` — GNU Make's own `--eval` recursion case, which that node moved out of the refusal it had been sitting behind. The row now classifies as narration because the one line that differs is a child's `$(info)`, which the classifier reads as the recipe echo it sits beside; the audit is what caught it, not the class. Not caused by that node: the plain `MAKEFLAGS` reproducer above has no recursion in it at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:44:12.298151711Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T22:08:23.613777818Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `--eval` now travels in MAKEFLAGS, and a child — composed or nested — reads the fragment back before its own makefile.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, probed before writing anything and read out of `src/main.c` afterwards to be sure the probe had found the rule rather than an example of it.\n\nThe value is `quote_for_env`: a `$` doubled so reading the variable does not expand the fragment, and a blank or a backslash backslash-escaped so word splitting does not end the word. `make '--eval=X := a$b c\\d'` gives `MAKEFLAGS= --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b\\ c\\\\d`, byte for byte off `od -c`. The reader is `decode_env_switches`, which expands the variable (undoubling the `$`) and then drops one backslash per escape while splitting — so the fragment arrives as the text that was typed.\n\nThe position is after every switch and before the `--` that ends them: `k --no-print-directory --eval=X\\ :=\\ 1 -- FOO=bar`. MFLAGS carries none of it, and that is not an omission — `define_makeflags` defines MFLAGS from the switch string and appends the eval flags afterwards.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `quote_makeflags_assignment` was already `quote_for_env` under another name; it is now `quote_for_makeflags` and serves both, which is GNU's arrangement too. `compiler_flag_variables` appends one `--eval=` per fragment.\n\nThe fragments sit BESIDE the switch table rather than in it, and that is the part worth reading twice. GNU keeps them out of the table as well: its `MAKEFLAGS` holds a reference to a separate `-*-eval-flags-*-` variable, and the table proper never contains a fragment. `CompilerFlagVariables` accordingly grew a `published` field — the table plus the fragments, which is what a Makefile sees and what a child inherits — while `base` stays the table alone, which is what MFLAGS is spelled from and what a Makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS` write is settled against. Putting them in `base` was my first attempt and it multiplied: `decode_makefile_makeflags` reads `previous`, `assigned` and `protected` into one invocation, and a switch is a bit and an assignment is keyed by name, so either settles when read twice, while a fragment is neither and simply appends again. `MAKEFLAGS += -w` produced three copies. Measured, then fixed by the split, then measured again against GNU: one copy, and `MAKEFLAGS += -w` matches GNU exactly.\n\nA SECOND DEFECT, LATENT UNTIL THIS ONE WAS FIXED, and it made a working build fail. `subninja::compile` called `prepend_command_line_evals` from the parent's working directory. That function reads and parses the makefile the fragments go in front of, and a child's makefile is named the way the invocation named it — `Makefile` under `-C sub`, resolved when the unit is entered. So the read resolved `Makefile` against the parent's directory and cached the PARENT's file under the child's name; the child then evaluated the wrong makefile and `sub/child` had no rule. It could not fire before, because with no fragments the function returns before reading anything. Fixed by doing the prepend inside `crate::make::in_directory`, which is what every other read of a child's makefile already does. `option-eval-fragment-reaches-a-child` uses `-C sub` precisely so this cannot come back.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded:\n\n* `tests/make/option-eval-fragment-reaches-a-child` — `-C sub` with a fragment defining a variable no makefile in the tree defines, read by both the parent's recipe and the child's. GNU wrote `parent=[a$b]` and `child=[a$b]`, so the `$` round trip is gated as an effect rather than as a string.\n* `tests/make/option-eval-fragment-is-quoted-into-makeflags` — the encoding itself, written to a file: GNU wrote `MAKEFLAGS=[k --eval=X:=a$$$$b -- FOO=bar]`, `MFLAGS=[-k]`, `X=[a$b]`, which pins the doubling, the position and MFLAGS's silence in one recording.\n\nBeside them one unit test, `propagates_eval_fragments`, because the fixture harness splits its `args` file on whitespace and so cannot carry a fragment with a blank in it. Every value in it is read off GNU Make 4.4.1: the short `-E` spelling, two fragments in written order, the blank and backslash escapes, and the leading space GNU leaves when the switch group is empty.\n\nTWO ROWS MOVED, both `options/eval`, both this node's, both reproduced by hand:\n\n* `options/eval.diff.1` — GNU's own recursion case — narration/`ninja-progress+recipe-echo` to narration/`ninja-progress`. The missing line was the child's `$(info eval)`; both tools now print `eval infile eval infile all recurse`. This is the residue the previous node's audit found and filed this node on. Worth noting what the recipe is: `@$(MAKE) -f Makefile && echo recurse` lifts nothing, so the line runs as written and the Make it names starts — which makes this row a live check of the remainder clause `[spec:ronin:req:make.recursive-invocation+2]` gained today. The fragment reaches a nested Ronin the same way it reaches a composed one, through the environment.\n* `options/eval.diff.2` — the suite's `MAKEFLAGS` case — unclassified/`ninja-progress+evaluation` to narration/`ninja-progress`. Ronin printed `MAKEFLAGS=` and now prints `MAKEFLAGS= --eval=$$(info\\ eval)`, GNU's bytes.\n\nClass totals 1299 rows 156/0/38/1105 from 157/0/38/1104. Compiler bucket ZERO. Every other row byte-identical.\n\nONE DEFECT FOUND AND NOT FIXED HERE, filed as make-empty-variable-name-does-not-abandon: an assignment word with an empty variable name makes GNU refuse and exit 2 where Ronin builds and exits 0, reachable from argv, from an inherited MAKEFLAGS, and from a Makefile's own MAKEFLAGS write (where it is not even diagnosed). Surfaced because `MAKEFLAGS += --eval=Y := 2` is the shape a Makefile would reach for, and GNU refuses that for this reason rather than for anything to do with `--eval`. Confirmed pre-existing with `MAKEFLAGS += -k := 2`, which has no fragment in it.\n\nGATES. Workspace 882/0 (881 plus the unit test; the two fixtures are corpus cases and the corpus is one test). Conformance 334/53 normalised, unchanged. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements. Ninja conformance 425/425. make_port green with no recording rewritten. Clippy pedantic/nursery clean on Ronin lib+bins. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host: vim 9.2 in 16s, zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30s. A fresh, unconfigured vim 9.2.0957 from the tarball builds end to end at -j8, `src/vim` at 4,574,992 bytes.\n\nTHE NON-MAKE FIGURE, corrected. `cargo test --no-default-features --no-fail-fast` gives 494/0 here and the dispatch's baseline says 481/13. Measured at the parent commit in a worktree with a cold target directory: 14 failures, every one of them in `make_grouped.rs` or `make_port.rs` — make-mode integration tests that run a make-named binary, which under `--no-default-features` does not have Make mode. They pass or fail according to what `target/` already holds from an earlier default-features build, so the pass count is an artifact of the directory rather than a property of the tree. `pub mod make` is `#[cfg(all(unix, feature = \"make\"))]` and every file this dispatch touched is inside it, so nothing here can reach that build either way."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T22:09:52.730019196Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-staging-pass-repeats-an-immediate-side-effect
                title "Stop a staging pass from running the makefile's immediate side effects again"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-staging-pass-counts-as-a-make-restart)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:34:00.243596845Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A compiler-input staging pass reads the makefile again, and everything the read does on the way through happens again with it. `$(file >>)`, `$(shell)` and `$(info)` at makefile level therefore fire once per pass, where GNU Make's read happens once — or once per real restart, which is a number GNU also has.\n\nReproduces against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with a filesystem effect, so this is not a narration difference:\n\n    $(file >> log,pass)\n    all:\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f first.mk sub\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f second.mk sub\n\n  GNU:   log holds one line\n  Ronin: log holds two\n\nTwo children mean one ChildGroup staging pass, so the read happens twice. Three would make it three. Pre-existing and not caused by make-staging-pass-counts-as-a-make-restart — that node stopped the pass being COUNTED as a restart, which is a different question from the pass happening at all. It is filed as its dep because the two are the same mechanism seen from two sides, and whatever fixes this has to keep that node's counter behaviour intact.\n\nFOUND BY AUDITING `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2`, which is why the row did not move when the restart count was fixed. The row's residue was two things at once and the class only ever named one. With the counter fixed, the values in that residue are now GNU's — `MAKE_RESTARTS=`, `1`, `2` on the parent and empty in the child, all matching — and what is left is one extra `MAKE_RESTARTS=2` line, which is the parent's `$(info)` firing on the staging pass that precedes compiling the child. The classifier still calls it `makeflags-content` because the leftover line is still shaped like a variable report; the shape test cannot tell an extra line from a wrong value. So the class name is now inaccurate about that row and the row is honest evidence for THIS node instead.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT HARD, said before the work starts. The pass exists because the child's makefile cannot be read until the parent's staged work is on the ground, and the re-read is how the parent's graph is rebuilt with the child composed into it. Suppressing the side effects on later passes is not obviously right either: `feature-wildcard-is-read-again-after-a-restart` records GNU re-reading and a `$(wildcard)` legitimately answering differently the second time, and the same argument says a `$(shell)` on a staging pass may be the read's way of seeing what the staged work produced. So the question is whether a staging pass is a fresh read that should see the new ground (and repeat what a read does), or a resumption that should not — and the answer has to be the same for `$(file >>)`, `$(shell)`, `$(info)` and `$(wildcard)` together, or explain why not."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:53:30.55558975Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:10:21.214551295Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. A staging pass no longer says or writes anything a Makefile can hear a second time, and a genuine restart still says everything again.\n\nTHE QUESTION THE NODE ASKED — which passes are visible — is settled by probing GNU Make on the one pass it does have.\n\n    $(file >> log,pass MAKE_RESTARTS=$(MAKE_RESTARTS))\n    include gen.mk\n    gen.mk: ; @printf 'V := built\\n' > gen.mk\n\nGNU writes TWO lines: `pass MAKE_RESTARTS=` and `pass MAKE_RESTARTS=1`. A restart is a fresh read over text that is new, GNU repeats everything the read does, and `MAKE_RESTARTS` exists precisely so a Makefile can tell which time it is. So a restart is visible, deliberately.\n\nA staging pass is not, and cannot be. Its own doc already said why: `Settlement::Staged` means the read happens again over the SAME text and nothing the read consulted moved. GNU Make has no such phase, so it has no answer for a Makefile that asks about one — the same argument that took the staging pass out of `MAKE_RESTARTS` at 8b8bdb1. The first read is also the read GNU's would have been: it happens before any recipe has run, on the ground the build started with.\n\nWHY IT RE-READS AT ALL, understood before choosing. The pass exists because a recursive child's makefile cannot be read until the parent's staged work is on the ground, and the graph is rebuilt from scratch each pass rather than spliced. Reusing the first evaluation whole would mean incremental composition — keeping the parent's graph and adding the child to it — which is an architecture change and not this node. So: suppress on the repeat, and the choice of WHAT to suppress is the whole of the design.\n\nTHE SPLIT, and the node asked for the same answer across four functions or an explanation. Here is the explanation.\n\n`$(info)`, `$(warning)` and `$(file >`/`>>`) have no answer. The effect is the whole of what they are, so not doing it again is complete and exact, and it leaves the read otherwise identical. They are held back.\n\n`$(shell)` and `$(wildcard)` are read for a value. Suppression is not available — the expansion that asked has to be handed something — so the only fix for them is to replay the first read's answer, which is a recorded transcript rather than a boolean. Measured, both diverge: `$(shell printf x >> log)` runs twice where GNU runs it once, and `$(wildcard *.out)` answers `first.out` on the second pass where GNU answers empty, because by then the staged child has run. Filed as make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again, depending on this node, with the probe and the machinery it can build on.\n\n`$(error)` is untouched and is not in either group: it is control flow, and a build that abandons must abandon on every pass that reaches it.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, and the granularity is the interesting part. `Flags::is_repeated_read` is set PER UNIT, not per pass, in `compile_unit` (src/make.rs) beside where `--shuffle` is already handed to the session. It has to be per unit, because a staging pass reads units it has read before AND one it has not — measured before choosing: parent, first child, [build], parent, first child, second child, [build]. Suppressing the whole pass would have silenced the second child, which is speaking for the first time.\n\n`CompilationState` carries `read_units` (what earlier passes read) and accumulates `units_read`; `Loaded` hands the latter out beside `evaluation_boundaries`; `prepare_graph` merges it after each pass and CLEARS it on `Settlement::Restart` and only there. That last line is the whole of the restart/staging distinction in the front end, and it is one line because 8b8bdb1 had already made the two settlements different words.\n\n`prepare_graph` went one line over the density gate and `pass_session` came out of it — the session and compilation context one pass reads with, which is a thing with a name rather than a slice of loop body.\n\nMEASURED AGAINST THE ORACLE, all three shapes:\n\n* the node's own reproducer, `$(file >> log,pass)` beside two children: GNU one line, Ronin was two, now one.\n* a genuine restart: GNU two lines with `MAKE_RESTARTS` empty then `1`, Ronin the same before and after — unmoved.\n* both at once, a remade include beside two children: GNU `pass R=` and `pass R=1`, Ronin the same. This is the case a fix that simply never spoke twice would break.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, and one of each:\n\n* `tests/make/feature-a-staging-pass-says-nothing-a-second-time` — the repair. GNU left `seen` with one line; run against c2e2bcf in a worktree it fails with `'seen' holds \"a read happened\\na read happened\\n\" where GNU Make left \"a read happened\\n\"`.\n* `tests/make/feature-a-restart-still-says-it-again-beside-a-staged-child` — the guard, and it passes on the parent tree, which is the point: it holds the half that was already right and that this node could have broken. GNU left `seen` with `a read happened R=` and `a read happened R=1`.\n\nBoth gate a file's contents rather than any output, so neither reads a line of narration.\n\nONE INVENTORY ROW MOVED, and it is the row this node was found in. `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2`, interface/`ninja-progress+directory-announce+makeflags-content` to narration/`ninja-progress+directory-announce`; `makeflags-content` 36 to 35, interface 38 to 37, narration 1105 to 1106, total 1299 and the compiler bucket ZERO. Reproduced by hand against the oracle and the parent binary rather than accepted from the classifier: the parent tree printed an extra `MAKE_RESTARTS=2` between `recurse MAKE_RESTARTS=` and the child's `MAKE_RESTARTS=`, which is the parent's makefile-level `$(info)` firing on the staging pass that precedes compiling the child. It is gone. Every `MAKE_RESTARTS` value now matches GNU — ``, `1`, `2` on the parent and empty in the child — and what is left is Ronin's progress counter and the directory announcement, which is narration and nothing else. The class name was inaccurate about that row from the moment make-staging-pass-counts-as-a-make-restart landed, and it is accurate again.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 895 passed / 0 failed; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port green with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 710 passed / 0 failed in a cold target directory; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host, vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s.\n\nkati is c0e0541f63b1b14e36710d4d0d6f7b7e1afbfbb9 on nplan/make-staging-pass-repeats-an-immediate-side-effect, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:10:25.424864122Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:18:45.130161282Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two corrections to the note above, both about where the front end's half of this landed rather than about what it does.\n\nThe note says `prepare_graph` merges the units a pass read and clears them on `Settlement::Restart`. It does neither: both belong to `build_compiler_inputs` (src/make/cli/remake.rs), which fills `read_units` from the loaded read at its top and empties it in the `Settled::Again` arm, where the restart is actually decided. `prepare_graph` only owns the set across the loop and hands it down. That is the better place for a reason worth having in the code: the two halves of what a pass leaves behind part company in that arm, and the comment there says so — staged work is on the ground and its boundaries survive a restart, which is what `feature-restart-keeps-recursive-staging-done` holds, while what a read said and wrote belongs to the text it read, and a restart happens because that text is new.\n\nThe note also says a `pass_session` helper came out of `prepare_graph` for the density gate. It did not survive: extracting it took `src/make/cli.rs` to 51 functions, one over the file's ceiling, and putting the two `read_units` statements in `build_compiler_inputs` instead left `prepare_graph` inside the line limit with no new function anywhere. `prepare_graph` is unchanged apart from declaring the set and passing it.\n\nNothing about behaviour moved with them. Every probe was re-run afterwards — the reproducer's one line, the restart's two, the interleaving's two, and the `$(file >>)` case — and every gate figure in the note above was measured on this arrangement and not the earlier one."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-empty-variable-name-does-not-abandon
                title "Abandon the build over an assignment word with no variable name"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T21:50:45.600341141Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "An assignment word whose name is empty makes GNU Make refuse the build and exit 2. Ronin prints a diagnostic and builds anyway, exiting 0 — so this is a build GNU Make declines and Ronin performs, not a wording difference.\n\nThree ways in, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make over `all: ; @true`:\n\n    make '=1'\n      GNU:   make: *** empty variable name.  Stop.     exit 2\n      Ronin: ronin: *bootstrap*:0: empty variable name ***   exit 0\n\n    MAKEFLAGS='=1' make\n      the same, both tools, both statuses.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -k := 2      (written in the Makefile)\n      GNU:   make: *** empty variable name.  Stop.     exit 2\n      Ronin: MF=[k]                                    exit 0\n\nThe third is the worse half and is a different site from the first two: through the Makefile-write path the word is not diagnosed at all, only dropped. `decode_makefile_makeflags` clears `invocation.goals` after each source deliberately — a leftover word there is what an unknown switch left behind and GNU drops it too — but a word carrying an `=` is an assignment rather than a leftover, and GNU's `handle_non_switch_argument` refuses it whichever origin it arrived from.\n\nFound while delivering make-eval-fragments-do-not-descend, and NOT caused by it: `MAKEFLAGS += -k := 2` has no `--eval` in it. It surfaced because `MAKEFLAGS += --eval=Y := 2` is the shape a Makefile would naturally reach for to add a fragment, and GNU refuses that too — for this reason and not for anything to do with `--eval`, since the write is unquoted and `:=` becomes a word of its own.\n\nThe exit status is the gateable part: the fixture writes a file the goal would have made, and GNU's recording has it absent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:49:03.925259817Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:49:56.755475598Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was two-thirds stale — probed before writing anything, which is the only reason that was found.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY LEFT. The filing recorded three ways in and said Ronin exited 0 on all three. Re-probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make over `all: ; @touch built.out` at 6105891:\n\n    make '=1'            GNU exit 2, nothing built.  Ronin exit 2, nothing built.  MATCHES\n    MAKEFLAGS='=1' make  GNU exit 2, nothing built.  Ronin exit 2, nothing built.  MATCHES\n    MAKEFLAGS += -k := 2 GNU exit 2, nothing built.  Ronin exit 0, built.          DIVERGES\n\nSo only the third — the half the filing itself called the worse one — was still live. The first two now reach kati's own read, which refuses. Nine makefile-text shapes were probed beside them and every one already matched GNU exactly, exit code and effect: `$() = x`, `$(empty) := y` through an empty expansion, `$(empty) += z`, a plain ` = x`, `= x` at column zero, `override $() = x`, `export $() = x`, and `define $()`. There was nothing to fix in the read.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, and it is one site. `assigned_makeflags_arguments` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) drops every assignment word out of a Makefile's `MAKEFLAGS` write before the option grammar sees it, so a word with no name was dropped along with the rest and never diagnosed at all. It now names the word before dropping it: an assignment whose `command_variable_name` is empty returns `Err(\"empty variable name\")`, which `decode_makefile_makeflags` propagates and `normalize_makeflags_assignment` turns into an abandoned build. GNU's counterpart is `handle_non_switch_argument`, which reads every non-switch word whatever origin it arrived from and reaches `parse_variable_definition`, fatal on an empty name.\n\nFive shapes verified against the oracle afterwards, all now exit 2 with nothing built where they exited 0 and built: `MAKEFLAGS += -k := 2`, `MAKEFLAGS := -k := 2`, `MAKEFLAGS += = 1`, `MAKEFLAGS += := 2`, and `MAKEFLAGS += --eval=Y := 2`, which is the shape that surfaced the defect. Two controls unmoved: `MAKEFLAGS += -k` and `MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar` both still build.\n\nNAMING THE WORD IS NOT APPLYING IT, deliberately, and the code says so. A *named* assignment arriving through a Makefile's `MAKEFLAGS` write is still dropped, and GNU applies it: probed, `MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar` gives GNU `FOO=[bar]` and Ronin `FOO=[]`, and with `FOO = mine` written above it GNU still answers `bar` — it is a command-line-origin variable that outranks the makefile's own. Neither tool carries it back into `MAKEFLAGS` or `MAKEOVERRIDES`. That is a second defect at the same site, wider than this node and with its own precedence question, and it is filed as make-makeflags-write-drops-an-assignment-word.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, and the teeth measured on the parent tree rather than assumed:\n\n* `tests/make/a-makefiles-makeflags-write-with-no-variable-name-abandons` — the divergence. GNU recorded `outcome failure` with `out` absent; run against 6105891 in a worktree it fails with `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`.\n* `tests/make/an-assignment-with-no-variable-name-on-the-command-line-abandons` — the argv way in, through an `args` file. This one passes on the parent tree and is a guard rather than a repair: the shape matches GNU today and nothing in the corpus held it, so it could have gone quietly.\n\nBoth gate the exit code and the absence of the file the goal would have made, never GNU's message text.\n\nLANDED IN ONE COMMIT WITH TWO SIBLINGS. make-recipe-prefix-is-read-per-expanded-line and make-grouped-tests-need-the-make-feature were in the tree at the same time, and the commit gate refuses a partial commit without a violation trailer, which is not an agent's to authorise. So the three landed together with all three ids on the commit. Each node's own note carries what it did.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 894 passed / 0 failed; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port green with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised, and 286 raw against the baseline's 285; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows 156/0/38/1105, tsv byte-identical, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 26s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:51:00.127756267Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description
                title "Hoist a silenced quiet-command echo into the edge description"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T22:22:12.319790354Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Operator bug report, 2026-08-19, building the Linux kernel with Ronin: \"the output is a horrid mix of make and ninja. The full command line is shown, then '  CC      arch/x86/boot/compressed/misc.o' on another line.\"\n\nREPRODUCER. Two shapes, both of which kbuild writes through its quiet-command macro — one line with the echo fused into the compile, and two lines where the echo stands alone:\n\n    all: misc.o silent.o\n    misc.o: misc.c\n    \t@set -e; echo '  CC      $@'; cc -c -o $@ misc.c\n    silent.o: misc.c\n    \t@echo '  CC      $@'\n    \t@cc -c -o $@ misc.c\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 prints two lines, one per object:\n\n      CC      misc.o\n      CC      silent.o\n\nRonin prints four:\n\n    [1/2] set -e; echo '  CC      misc.o'; cc -c -o misc.o misc.c\n      CC      misc.o\n    [2/2] (echo '  CC      silent.o' ) && (cc -c -o silent.o misc.c )\n      CC      silent.o\n\nMECHANISM. kati/src-rs/ninja.rs get_description_from_command hoists a description only from a recipe line that is a lone `echo TEXT` — it bails on any `;`, `&&`, `|`, `<`, `>`, `&` — and even that path is gated behind --detect_android_echo, which Ronin never passes. So neither shape yields a description. The sink then falls back to the whole script (src/make/sink.rs:875, and again at :992), the echo still runs inside that script, and the operator sees the recipe twice: once as Ronin's progress line and once as the echo's own output.\n\nThe second shape shows the fusing as well: two silenced lines become `(echo … ) && (cmd … )` in gen_shell_script, so what the lone-echo path was written for never even reaches it as a lone echo at the point the fallback is chosen.\n\nTHE FIX. A silenced (`@`-prefixed, or `.SILENT`) recipe line that begins with a static `echo TEXT` — optionally behind a `set -<flags>;` prefix, as kbuild's cmd macro writes — and whose echo is followed by `;`, `&&` or the end of the line while work still follows in the recipe, is narration: the text belongs in the edge's description binding and the echo has to come out of the command so it is not said twice. Everything else keeps today's behaviour. A missed hoist is cosmetic; a wrong hoist eats real output, so every runtime-dependent shape (redirects, pipes, command substitution, backslash escapes, globs, echo flags) bails.\n\nThis is compiling narration intent into the graph — the runtime stays Ninja and the progress line keeps Ronin's own `[N/M]` voice. Android kati's --detect_android_echo is the precedent for reading a recipe's echo as a description."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T22:22:16.703154375Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:06:39.37058053Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed by compiling the narration the Makefile wrote into the edge that carries it. The reproducer, before and after, is the whole of it:\n\n    before                                              after\n    [1/2] set -e; echo '  CC      misc.o'; cc -c …      [1/2]   CC      misc.o\n      CC      misc.o                                    [2/2]   CC      silent.o\n    [2/2] (echo '  CC      silent.o' ) && (cc … )\n      CC      silent.o\n\nWHAT READS THE ECHO. `split_narrating_echo` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) replaces `get_description_from_command`, which only ever recognised a recipe line that was a lone `echo TEXT` and bailed on any `;`, `&&`, `|`, `<`, `>` or `&` — so kbuild's fused quiet command was invisible to it — and was gated behind `--detect_android_echo`, which Ronin never passes. The new reading takes `echo TEXT`, `echo TEXT ; WORK`, `echo TEXT && WORK`, and any of those behind the `set -e;` kbuild's `cmd` macro writes, and returns the text beside what is left of the line. TEXT is what the shell would have handed `echo`: quotes off wherever they are, an unquoted run of blanks collapsed to the one space `echo` puts between its arguments. Everything settled at run time rather than at compile time bails — redirect, pipe, command substitution, `$`, glob, brace, tilde, an option, and any backslash, because /bin/sh's `echo` reads `\\n` as a newline where bash's builtin prints it and nothing that depends on which shell the recipe gets is a description.\n\nWHERE IT APPLIES, which is four conditions and each one is a probe rather than a guess.\n\nOnly a recipe that silenced every line. A line left loud is one GNU Make echoes, and the command Ronin shows for an edge that named no description is the counterpart of exactly that echo; narrating half a recipe one way and half the other is worse than not narrating it. `@echo '  X  a'` followed by a loud `touch a.stamp` therefore keeps its command.\n\nOnly where the work that follows is not more echoing. A recipe made of echoes is a recipe whose output is the point: `make print-version` is one echo and `make help` is several, and both are read by whatever ran them. `is_work_worth_announcing` asks what the next command *is*, not what its text says, so an echo whose argument no description could be read out of still answers no — the cost of that answer is a description not hoisted, and the cost of the other one is output moved into the progress counter.\n\nNot under `-s` and not under `-n`. Under `-s` Ronin narrates nothing (BuildOptions::quiet), so hoisting would take the text off the screen entirely where GNU still shows it — the echo runs, and GNU's `-s` output for the reproducer is the two CC lines. Under `-n` Ronin shows commands (BuildOptions::verbose), so the text would be lost from the only thing printed. Both keep today's output exactly, byte for byte, and `-n` still prints `exec env … /bin/sh -c \"set -e; echo '  CC      misc.o'; cc …\"`. `src/make/cli.rs session_for` now sets `is_dry_run` beside `is_silent_mode`; it was already carried through MAKEFLAGS decoding and only kati's own exec mode reads it, which Ronin does not use. `.SILENT:` is not `-s` and does hoist, which is right: it is `@` written once.\n\nNot for a recipe naming a liftable `$(MAKE)`. Such a recipe becomes several edges out of one rule, and they share the description; its segments are translated in passes of their own, so the text would be moved onto every one of them. Left alone the echo stays in the segment that wrote it.\n\nBOTH SINK SITES AND THE MANIFEST AGREE. `translate_recipe` is the one place this happens, and both paths reach it: Construction through `sink_node`, Launch through `DeferredRecipes::expand`. Its signature is now `Option<&mut Option<Bytes>>` — a caller with nowhere to put narration passes `None` and gets the lines as written, which is what `residual_segment` does, since the whole recipe was read for it already and hoisting there would delete the text rather than move it. That was a live hole under `--detect_android_echo`, where the segment's description was minted and dropped on the floor.\n\nThe manifest destination needed one fix of its own. Ninja skips the blanks after a binding's `=`, so `description =   CC      misc.o` came back as `CC      misc.o` and the indent kbuild lines its targets up under was lost — a difference the direct graph did not have. `escape_ninja_value` now escapes a leading space run as `$ `, which `ninja_unescape` in the tests reads back. Equivalence stays at 0 disagreements with `description` among the twelve bindings it compares.\n\n`--detect_android_echo` keeps its meaning: a lone echo is a description there whether or not it announces anything, which is the whole of that flag. It shares the one stricter reading now rather than having a looser one of its own.\n\nTESTS. Six new, and the narration one is deliberate. `tests/make-project-reductions/kbuild-quiet-command` is kbuild's `cmd` macro reduced to two objects, one per shape, and `a_quiet_command_is_said_once_and_in_ronins_voice` asserts the two lines Ronin prints. It is the one case in tests/make_projects.rs that asserts narration, because for a quiet command the narration is the whole of what was wrong, and it asserts Ronin's own `[N/M]` line and never GNU's text. Teeth measured: with the hoist disabled it fails with exactly the operator's report on the left —\n\n    left: [\"[1/2] set -e; echo '  CC      fused.o'; cp fused.c fused.o\", \"  CC      fused.o\",\n           \"[2/2] (echo '  CC      split.o' ) && (cp split.c split.o )\", \"  CC      split.o\"]\n\nIn kati: `a_narrating_echo_gives_up_its_text_and_keeps_its_work` and `an_echo_the_shell_would_decide_is_not_narration` hold the shapes and the bails; `both_quiet_command_shapes_compile_to_the_same_description` holds the pair the node is named for; `an_echo_with_nothing_to_announce_stays_in_the_command` holds `print-version`, `help`, and Android's exemption; `a_recipe_with_a_line_left_loud_keeps_its_command`; `a_run_that_shows_no_description_keeps_the_echo` for `-s` and `-n`; `a_manifest_keeps_a_descriptions_leading_indent`.\n\nNo effect fixtures: nothing about what the build does moved. The echo leaves the command and nothing else does, the status the script ends on is unchanged in every shape, and make_port's build-intent recording is byte-identical with no case rewritten.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 889 passed / 0 failed (882 + 7); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port 4/4 with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing, unmoved, with the same four class counts; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; scripts/check-make-projects.sh built vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s.\n\nvim and zsh audited for movement rather than assumed clean: both builds were re-run with stdout captured and neither hoists a single edge. zsh's recipes are silenced but not echo-led, and vim's two `echo` lines in the top-level recipe trail the work instead of announcing it, so both print exactly what they printed before.\n\nUPSTREAM INVENTORY. 1299 rows, class counts identical to the baseline — 156 unclassified, 0 compiler, 38 interface, 1105 narration. 14 rows moved and every one moved within narration, each gaining `recipe-echo` beside `ninja-progress`: features/archives.diff.12 and .14, features/double_colon.diff.2, .3 and .11, features/errors.diff.2, features/jobserver.diff.3, .11 and .12, features/reinvoke.diff.5 and .6, features/shell_assignment.diff.3, functions/file.diff, variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.2. All are the `@echo TEXT; work` shape — `x:;@echo hi; cat file.out`, `all: ; @echo \"<$(override)>\" ; cat ,abc`. What moved is where the text is: Ronin used to print it as the echo's output, where it matched GNU's line and never reached the diff, and now prints it in the progress counter, where the classifier accounts for it against the counter's payload and names the family. The `recipe-echo` line count fell 280 → 230 for the same reason.\n\nA first cut of the rule, without the two conditions above, moved five rows the other way — variables/define.diff, .1, .2, .12 and .15, narration → unclassified. Those are `define multi / @echo hi / echo there / endef` with `all: ; $(multi)`, and GNU echoes `echo there` because it reads each expanded line's own prefix. The rule now declines them, and the reason kati thought otherwise is filed as make-recipe-prefix-is-read-per-expanded-line.\n\nTWO ADJACENT DEFECTS FILED, neither widened into here: make-recipe-prefix-is-read-per-expanded-line (kati inherits the first line's `@`/`-`/`+` across every line an expansion produced; the `-` half is a build-effect divergence) and make-grouped-tests-need-the-make-feature (tests/make_grouped.rs is `#![cfg(unix)]` with no `feature = \"make\"`, so `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` fails its thirteen tests — pre-existing at ec1a12a and unrelated to anything here)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:06:43.61412223Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-prefix-is-read-per-expanded-line
                title "Read a recipe prefix per expanded line, not once for the whole expansion"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:05:31.317862269Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description, and pre-existing: nothing in that node touched prefix reading.\n\nREPRODUCER (GNU Make 4.4.1 against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make):\n\n    define multi\n    @echo hi\n    echo there\n    endef\n\n    all: ; $(multi)\n\nGNU prints three lines:\n\n    hi\n    echo there\n    there\n\nThe second line is Make echoing `echo there`, which means the `@` on the first line of the expansion did not reach it. GNU expands each written recipe line and then hands the expansion to `start_job_command` one physical line at a time — `construct_command_argv` stops at the newline and leaves `child->command_ptr` on the next — so each line of an expansion parses its own `@`, `-` and `+` prefix, on top of the file-level flags and nothing else.\n\nkati reads the prefix twice: once off the front of the whole expansion into `global_echo` / `global_dash_prefixed` / `global_recursive_line` (kati/src-rs/command.rs, `CommandEvaluator::eval`), and then again per line starting from those globals. So the first line's prefix is inherited by every line the expansion produced. `echo there` above is silenced in kati where GNU echoes it.\n\nRonin never echoes a recipe line, so the `echo` half of this is invisible in what a build prints — but it is not invisible to the compiler. It reached this campaign through make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description, whose hoist asks whether a recipe silenced every one of its lines; under kati's reading the case above answers yes and under GNU's it answers no. That node's rule is defended by a second condition (the remaining work must not be more echoing), so its behaviour is right either way, but the condition it would rather have relied on is the one this defect corrupts.\n\nThe `-` half is not cosmetic at all: `-cmd1` on a line whose expansion carries a second line makes both ignore their status in kati and only the first in GNU, which is a build-effect divergence and wants an oracle-recorded fixture.\n\nFix shape: drop the whole-expansion prefix parse and let the per-line parse read the first line's prefix where it stands, seeding the per-line flags from `is_silent_mode` / `ignore_errors` / `.IGNORE` / the line's `references_make` classification alone. Check what `if cmds.is_empty() { continue; }` was doing for a recipe line of prefixes only."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:49:08.245924995Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:50:32.673991004Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing named one of GNU Make's two readings. There are two, and only having one of them is the defect.\n\nWHAT GNU ACTUALLY DOES, read out of reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/src/job.c after the probes rather than before, to be sure the probe had found the rule and not an example of it.\n\n`chop_commands` runs at parse time over the UNEXPANDED recipe line and stores its `@`, `-` and `+` in that line's `lines_flags`. `start_job_command` then does, per expanded line:\n\n    flags = child->file->command_flags | child->file->cmds->lines_flags[child->command_line - 1];\n    ... reads @ - + off the line in front of it ...\n    child->file->cmds->lines_flags[child->command_line - 1] |= flags & COMMANDS_RECURSE;\n\nSo a prefix WRITTEN in front of a `$(call …)` seeds every line the call produces, and a prefix the call PRODUCED binds to the line it stands on. The filing had only the second half. `+` is the exception, and job.c says so in a comment: the write-back marks \"more commands recursive than should be in the case of multiline define/endef scripts where only one line is marked +\", so a `+` reaches the rest of that written line's expansion and stops at the end of it.\n\nTHE MISSING HALF WAS FOUND BY THE CONFORMANCE GATE, which is the point of running it. A first cut read every prefix per expanded line and dropped the written-line seed; conformance went 334/53 to 333/54 on `silent_multiline.mk`, which is kati's own case and exists for exactly this. `$(call cmd2)` and `@$(call cmd2)` expand to the same three lines and 4.4.1 echoes the trailing `echo bar` for the first and not for the second. With the seed restored the case is byte-identical to GNU, and raw conformance went 285 to 286.\n\nPROBES, all against 4.4.1, all before writing:\n\n* `-` — `define multi / -false / false / endef` behind `all: ; $(multi)`, with a second written line after it. GNU forgives the first failure, stops on the second, exits 2 and never reaches the second written line. Ronin forgave both, ran on, exited 0. A build GNU Make abandons and Ronin performed.\n* `@` — `define multi / @echo hi / echo there / endef`. GNU prints `hi`, `echo there`, `there`: it echoes the second line, so the first line's `@` did not reach it.\n* `+` — `define multi / +touch plus.out / touch noplus.out / endef` under `-n`, where a recursive line runs and an ordinary one does not. GNU makes BOTH files. The same two lines written out as two recipe lines make only `plus.out`, and a third written line after the expansion is not reached — so the carry is real and its scope is the written line.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `ExpandedRecipeLines` (kati/src-rs/command.rs) is an iterator over one written line's expansion: it seeds each line's `LinePrefixes` from the written line's, reads that line's own prefixes on top, and writes `recursive_line` back into the seed so `+` carries. `scan_written_prefixes` reads the written line's prefixes off the unexpanded `Value`, walking leading literals and stopping at the first reference — which is GNU's scan structurally, since `chop_commands` stops at the `$` too. `references_make` still supplies the `$(MAKE)` half of the seed, unchanged.\n\nFive unit tests in kati: the per-line `@` and `-` with both orders, the `+` carry including a line that is nothing but the prefix and the fact that nothing carries backwards, the written-line seed for all three flags, and `scan_written_prefixes` on `@$(call cmd)`, `\\t-+@$(call cmd)`, `$(call cmd)`, `$(AT)echo hi` and `echo @ - +`.\n\nTHE `+` HALF HAS NO RUNTIME CONSEQUENCE HERE, and that is a decision already taken rather than a gap. make-recipe-dry-run settled that Ronin's `-n` is Ninja's and runs nothing at all, `+` included; `tests/make/dry-run-skips-a-plus-line` records it and is listed discovery-only. So the `+` fix is a compiler fix — `recursive_line` feeds `uncomposable_recursion`, which decides whether a recipe is left whole rather than split into child graphs — and it is gated by the unit tests rather than by an effect fixture, because there is no effect to gate.\n\nFIXTURES:\n\n* `tests/make/a-forgiving-prefix-belongs-to-the-expanded-line-it-is-on` — oracle-recorded, the `-` half. GNU wrote `first` and `second` into the log, failed, and never reached the recipe's second written line. Teeth measured on the parent tree at 6105891: it exits 0, writes `third` and passes where GNU failed.\n* `tests/make-project-reductions/a-loud-line-in-an-expansion` with `a_recipe_with_a_loud_expanded_line_keeps_its_command` — the `@` half, through the consequence it actually has in Ronin. The quiet-command hoist asks whether a recipe silenced every line, and only a per-line reading can answer. Measured both ways: the parent tree prints `[1/1]   CC      x.o`, hoisting the echo and losing the line GNU echoes; this tree prints the command, which is the counterpart of that echo.\n\nTHE HOIST'S GATE IS NOW RIGHT FOR THE RIGHT REASON. make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description declined the `define multi` cases through its second condition — that the remaining work must not be more echoing — because the condition it would rather have used was corrupted by this defect. It is uncorrupted now, and the five `variables/define` rows that a looser first cut had moved narration to unclassified are still narration: the inventory tsv is byte-identical.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 894 passed / 0 failed (889 plus five new tests); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port green with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised with the same four class counts, 286 raw; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows 156/0/38/1105, tsv byte-identical, compiler bucket ZERO, `recipe-echo` still 230; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 26s — both real trees use recipe prefixes heavily and neither moved.\n\nkati is d2895695feb7e06853652ad0ad4881edea561bef on nplan/make-recipe-prefix-is-read-per-expanded-line, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:51:04.019987733Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:59:38.290881882Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "The workspace figure in this node's completion note is one short, and so is the same figure in the notes on make-empty-variable-name-does-not-abandon and make-grouped-tests-need-the-make-feature, which were written from the same measurement.\n\nAll three say \"workspace 894 passed / 0 failed\". The correct figure at c2e2bcf is 895 passed / 0 failed. The 894 run was taken before conformance caught the missing written-line seed; the fifth kati unit test that correction added — `a_prefix_written_before_an_expansion_belongs_to_the_whole_expansion` — was not in the tree when the count was read, and the notes were written afterwards without re-reading it. kati's lib suite is 213 tests, not 212; every other target's count in the two runs is identical.\n\n889 baseline plus four kati unit tests plus `loud_expanded_line_keeps_the_command` plus `make_tests_declare_the_make_feature` is 895. No test failed in either run, and no other gate figure in those notes moved."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-grouped-tests-need-the-make-feature
                title "Gate the grouped-action tests on the make feature"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:05:48.83152574Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while running the `--no-default-features` gate for make-quiet-command-echo-becomes-the-description, and pre-existing at ec1a12a: `git show HEAD:tests/make_grouped.rs | head -3` is `#![cfg(unix)]`, with no `feature = \"make\"` beside it.\n\nEvery one of its thirteen tests symlinks the ronin binary as `make` and runs it, so without the feature all thirteen fail on the front end's own refusal:\n\n    ronin: this build has no Make front end; it was compiled without the 'make' feature\n\n    scripts/sandboxed cargo test --workspace --no-default-features\n    passed: 695 failed: 13\n\n    bare_double_colon_rule_stands_alone, completion_join_prunes_unchanged_member,\n    double_colon_baseline_precedes_the_chain, double_colon_chain_runs_in_order,\n    double_colon_rules_weigh_own_prerequisites, grouped_double_uses_published_input_mtime,\n    grouped_double_without_prerequisites_runs, grouped_second_expands_each_member,\n    mixed_double_colon_keeps_member_actions, order_only_change_keeps_leaf_snapshot,\n    private_action_persists_without_stamp, question_mode_ignores_clean_candidate,\n    recursive_group_activates_same_member_child\n\nThe sibling that gets it right is tests/make_regressions.rs: `#![cfg(all(unix, feature = \"make\"))]`. tests/make_projects.rs carries the same gate further down the header. The fix is that line; the thing worth doing beside it is sweeping the other integration tests that reach for the Make front end and checking each one declares the feature it needs, so the gate cannot go red again by omission."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:17:20.575156982Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:48:12.859096869Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's count was one short — measured rather than inherited.\n\nTHE FILING SAID THIRTEEN. In a cold target directory `--no-default-features` fails FOURTEEN: `make_grouped.rs` 0 passed / 13 failed, and `make_port.rs` 3 passed / 1 failed. The fourteenth is `make_build_intent_matches_oracle`, which links the Ronin binary as `make` for exactly the same reason the grouped tests do. The thirteen in the filing were read off the shared `target/`, which is the very non-determinism this node exists to remove; the completion note on make-eval-fragments-do-not-descend had already measured fourteen in a worktree and that figure is the right one.\n\nParent state, worktree at 6105891 with a cold target: 708 passed / 14 failed. After: 706 passed / 0 failed. The arithmetic is exact — 708 less `make_port`'s three passing tests, plus one new test — and the dispatch's 695/13 baseline was the dirty-directory reading of the same tree.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `tests/make_grouped.rs` carried `#![cfg(unix)]` and now carries `#![cfg(all(unix, feature = \"make\"))]`, which is what `make_regressions.rs`, `make_projects.rs` and `make_state.rs` already carry. `tests/make_port.rs` gains the same line and loses the inner `#[cfg(unix)]` that guarded its symlink, now redundant.\n\nGating `make_port.rs` at file level rather than per test is a choice and the file says so. Only one of its four tests reaches the front end; the other three are self-tests of the recording format. `tests/cli.rs` is the precedent for the other shape — most of its tests are Ninja mode, so each Make one carries `#[cfg(all(unix, feature = \"make\"))]` of its own and so do its two helpers. `make_port.rs` is not that: it is the Make port's gate end to end, its support module included, and there is no Make port to self-test in a build with no Make front end. Per-item gating would also have needed about twenty attributes scattered through the file, which is precisely the by-omission fragility this node is about.\n\nTHE SWEEP, and it found nothing else. Every `tests/*.rs` that runs the executable under a name of its own: `cli.rs` (per test), `make_projects.rs`, `make_regressions.rs`, `make_state.rs`, `progress.rs` (per test) — all already declare the feature. Only the two above did not.\n\nSO IT CANNOT GO RED BY OMISSION AGAIN. `release_gate.rs` gains `every_make_front_end_test_declares_the_make_feature`: for each `tests/*.rs` naming both `CARGO_BIN_EXE_ronin` and `symlink` — running the binary under a chosen name, and the only name worth choosing here selects Make mode — the source must contain `feature = \"make\"`. It lives in `release_gate.rs` because that file is ungated, and a gate that declines to compile cannot report its own absence. It skips itself by name, since it has to name both markers in order to look for them. A floor of six recognised files fails if the recognition drifts away from how the tests are written.\n\nTeeth measured: with `make_grouped.rs` put back to `#![cfg(unix)]` it fails naming that exact file.\n\nGATES, with the two nodes landed beside this one in the tree. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 894 passed / 0 failed (889 plus five new tests); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised, and 286 raw against the baseline's 285; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows 156/0/38/1105 with the tsv byte-identical and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 26s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:48:21.565591519Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makeflags-write-drops-an-assignment-word
                title "Apply an assignment a Makefile's MAKEFLAGS write names"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-empty-variable-name-does-not-abandon)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:50:55.4729435Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "An assignment word a Makefile writes into `MAKEFLAGS` becomes a command-line variable under GNU Make and is dropped by Ronin, so a value the Makefile deliberately set is simply absent.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, recipe `@echo \"FOO=[$(FOO)] MF=[$(MAKEFLAGS)] MO=[$(MAKEOVERRIDES)]\" > out.txt`:\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar\n      GNU:   FOO=[bar] MF=[]  MO=[]\n      Ronin: FOO=[]    MF=[]  MO=[]\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -k FOO=bar\n      GNU:   FOO=[bar] MF=[k] MO=[]\n      Ronin: FOO=[]    MF=[k] MO=[]\n\n    MAKEFLAGS := FOO=bar          (the assignment first, no switch before it)\n      GNU:   FOO=[bar]\n      Ronin: FOO=[]\n\nAnd the precedence is the interesting half — it is a command-line variable, not an ordinary one:\n\n    FOO = mine\n    MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar\n      GNU:   FOO=[bar]     the write outranks the makefile's own assignment\n      Ronin: FOO=[mine]\n\nNeither tool carries the word back into `MAKEFLAGS` or `MAKEOVERRIDES` afterwards, which is worth pinning as part of the fix rather than discovering later.\n\nTHE SITE is `assigned_makeflags_arguments` in src/make/cli/interface.rs, which drops every word that does not start with `-` and contains an `=` before the option grammar sees it. Its doc comment gives the reason — GNU removes assignments and decodes the rest as options — and that reason is half of GNU's behaviour: `handle_non_switch_argument` removes the word from the switch stream AND hands it to `try_variable_definition`, which defines it at `o_command`. Ronin does the first and not the second.\n\nFound while delivering make-empty-variable-name-does-not-abandon, at that node's own site, and deliberately not widened into it: that node made an assignment word with an EMPTY name abandon the build, which needed only to name the word. Applying a NAMED one is a different question with its own precedence, ordering and round-trip answers, and it is the reason this is a node rather than a paragraph.\n\nWATCH FOR, when it is taken up. `decode_makefile_makeflags` reads three sources into one invocation — `previous`, `assigned` and `protected` — and the rendered `MAKEFLAGS` it produces is read back on the next write. A switch is a bit and an assignment is keyed by name, so both settle when read twice; that is already recorded in `compiler_flag_variables`. The leading-cluster rule in `assigned_makeflags_arguments` prepends a `-` to a first word that has none, so an assignment arriving first must not be caught by it — `MAKEFLAGS := FOO=bar` is exactly that shape and GNU accepts it.\n\nGate it on effects: a file the recipe writes carrying the value, and the makefile-assignment case where the two tools disagree about which value wins."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:46:03.258378087Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:46:57.00359453Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's headline claim was wrong — probed before writing anything, which is the only reason it was caught.\n\nTHE FILING SAID COMMAND-LINE RANK. It does not. `$(origin FOO)` answers `file`, and the ladder says the same thing from both sides:\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar                      FOO=[bar]  origin=[file]\n    FOO = mine ; MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar         FOO=[bar]      the write is later, so the write wins\n    MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar ; FOO = mine         FOO=[mine]     a later line outranks it — NOT command-line rank\n    override FOO = mine ; MAKEFLAGS += ...    FOO=[mine]  origin=[override]\n    make FOO=cmd, MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar        FOO=[cmd]   origin=[command line]\n    FOO in the environment                    FOO=[bar]      file outranks environment\n    the same under -e                         FOO=[envval] origin=[environment]\n\nThe filing's own evidence — `FOO = mine` above the write, GNU still answering `bar` — is equally consistent with a plain file-origin assignment made where the write stands, and that is what it is. GNU's source agrees and says why: `set_special_var` calls `reset_makeflags (origin)` with the origin of the assignment being performed, `decode_env_switches` carries it into `decode_switches`, and `handle_non_switch_argument` hands it to `try_variable_definition` — one code path for argv and for a Makefile write, differing only in the origin the caller supplied. `o_command` for argv; `o_file` for a makefile's own line.\n\nThat correction is the whole design. There is no precedence question to answer, because it is the precedence a Makefile assignment already has.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT TRAVEL, pinned as the filing asked. `MAKEOVERRIDES` is rendered from `command_variables` in `main` BEFORE the first makefile is read, into a hidden `-*-command-variables-*-` snapshot, and `MAKEFLAGS` carries a reference to it. A word bound later is added to `command_variables` and reaches nobody: measured, `MF=[]` and `MO=[]` after the write, and a recursive child sees `FOO` undefined. With `make BAZ=1` beside it, `MF=[ -- BAZ=1] MO=[BAZ=1]` and `FOO` is still not among them. Ronin now matches on all of it, and neither tool exports the name to a recipe's environment unless the makefile said `export`.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, and it is the boundary that already existed. `DecodedMakeflags` grows `assignments`; `assigned_makeflags_arguments` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) sets those words aside instead of dropping them, and only for the write itself — the switch table and the protected state are read back through the same grammar, so a name bound out of either would be bound again on every write. kati's `apply_makeflags_assignment` (src-rs/eval.rs) then scans each word with `scan_variable_definition`, the existing port of GNU's `parse_variable_definition`, and evaluates it as the `AssignStmt` it is.\n\nEvaluating rather than defining by hand is what carries the grammar, and every part of it was measured against the oracle: `:=` is simple and `=` recursive, `+=` appends to what the name already holds, `?=` declines to speak over a value, `!=` runs a shell, an origin ladder decides whether it lands, `export FOO` above the write still exports it, and a word the scanner rejects is not a definition at all — `a:b=c` leaves `a:b` undefined in both tools, because a `:` outside an operator ends GNU's scan.\n\nApplied last, after this write publishes its own table, because `set_global_var` REPLACES the binding: a word naming `MAKEFLAGS` again normalises again, and the outer call would otherwise be writing into a variable the table no longer names. `MAKEFLAGS += MAKEFLAGS=oops` terminates in both tools and leaves `MAKEFLAGS` empty in both.\n\nA SECOND DEFECT AT THE SAME SITE, found by this node's probes and fixed with it because the loop rewrite made it one word. `assigned_makeflags_arguments` prepended the leading cluster's missing dash when `arguments.len() == 1` — when nothing had been KEPT yet — but GNU's rule is positional: `decode_env_switches` examines `argv[1]` and no other word. Dropping the assignments first let the dash go to whatever an assignment stood in front of. `MAKEFLAGS += FOO=bar ran` read `ran` as `-r -a -n`, and `-n` builds nothing: Ronin silently performed a dry run where GNU built. `MAKEFLAGS += FOO!=echo ran > log; echo v` is the same shape written by hand. Now `position == 0` over the value's own words.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, both with teeth measured in a worktree at eb33025 rather than assumed:\n\n* `tests/make/a-makeflags-write-defines-the-name-it-carries` — the repair, and the whole matrix in one recorded file: a name written over from above, a `+=` that appends, a `:=` whose right-hand side was expanded before any word was applied (so it reads the value from BEFORE the write, `SETTLED=[mine]`), a later line that outranks the write, and `MAKEFLAGS`/`MAKEOVERRIDES` both empty afterwards. On the parent it fails with `BEFORE=[mine] ... GROWN=[head] SETTLED=[]` where GNU left `BEFORE=[written] ... GROWN=[head tail] SETTLED=[mine]`.\n* `tests/make/a-makeflags-write-lends-its-dash-only-to-the-first-word` — the dash rule. On the parent it fails with `'out' was not produced`, which is the dry run.\n\nBoth gate a file's contents, so neither reads a line of narration.\n\nNEW DEFECT FOUND AND NOT FIXED HERE. `MAKEFLAGS += .VARIABLES=x` abandons with `cannot assign to readonly variable: .VARIABLES` where GNU accepts and carries on. It is not this node's: `make .VARIABLES=x` on the command line and `.VARIABLES = x` on a plain makefile line both already abandon on the parent tree, so the readonly refusal is wider than any one origin. Filed as make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 896 passed / 0 failed (895 plus one new test); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean, ronin `--all-targets` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port green with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows 156/0/37/1106 with the tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 710 passed / 0 failed in a cold target directory; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green on a quiet host, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s.\n\nkati is f57399ea67e78e357fab3b094fa3c0c05fbb1609 on nplan/make-makeflags-write-drops-an-assignment-word, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:50:05.416230708Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again
                title "Answer a staging pass from the read that already happened"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-staging-pass-repeats-an-immediate-side-effect)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T23:57:35.81302683Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The other half of make-staging-pass-repeats-an-immediate-side-effect, split off deliberately and with the reason written down.\n\nA staging pass re-reads the makefile so a recursive child can be compiled, and that read asks the filesystem again. GNU Make asks once, before any recipe has run, so the ground it asks about is the ground as the build found it. The second pass asks ground the staged work has already changed.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with the two-children shape that forces one staging pass:\n\n    X := $(shell printf 'shell-pass\\n' >> shelllog; echo v)\n    W := $(wildcard *.out)\n    all:\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f first.mk sub\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f second.mk sub\n    \\t@printf 'X=[$(X)] W=[$(W)]\\n' > vals\n\n      GNU:   shelllog one line.   W=[]\n      Ronin: shelllog two lines.  W=[first.out]\n\nBoth halves are visible to a Makefile: the `$(shell)` effect happens twice, and the `$(wildcard)` answers something GNU Make never answers, because on the second pass `first.out` exists.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT THE OTHER NODE. That node held back what a read DOES: `$(info)`, `$(warning)` and `$(file >)` have no answer to give, so not doing them again is complete and exact, and one boolean on the unit's read says so. These two have an answer, and the expansion that asked cannot be given nothing. Suppression is not available; the fix is to hand back what the FIRST read of this unit got — a recorded answer replayed in order, per unit, cleared on a genuine restart. That is a different mechanism with a different failure mode, and it is the shape the parent node called \"reuse the first evaluation\" and declined to build under another node's name.\n\nWORTH KNOWING BEFORE STARTING.\n\n`kati::session::Session::command_results` already records every `$(shell)` and `$(file)` this pass performed, as `CommandResult { op, shell, shellflag, cmd, find, result, loc }`. It exists for kati's regeneration stamp and is wiped per session, but it is the shape a replay wants and the recording half is written.\n\n`$(wildcard)` has no such record and would need one. `Session::glob_cache` is per-session too.\n\nThe replay must be keyed so that the SAME command written twice in one makefile still runs twice on the first read — the key is a position in the read, not the command text. And the child that the pass was taken for is reading for the first time; its calls must not be answered from anyone's record. `Flags::is_repeated_read` already carries per unit exactly the distinction the replay needs, so the machinery for deciding WHICH reads replay is in place; what is missing is the record itself.\n\nGate it on effects: a `$(shell)` that appends to a log, and a `$(wildcard)` written into a file by the recipe, both under a makefile with two recursive children. Both are filesystem-visible, so neither needs narration compared."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T01:23:49.752203629Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T01:25:16.572277167Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. A staging pass is now answered from the read it repeats, and the scope came out WIDER than the filing named — two more questions were found by probing, and one boundary was found by a test that already existed and that this fix broke before it was right.\n\nTHE FILING NAMED TWO. Probed at 861feaf with the two-children shape that forces exactly one staging pass, and five questions measured side by side against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    X := $(shell printf 'asked\\n' >> shelllog; echo told)\n    W := $(wildcard *.out)\n    R := $(realpath first.out)\n    F := $(file < first.out)\n    A := $(abspath first.out)\n\n      GNU:   shelllog one line, W=[] R=[] F=[]\n      Ronin: shelllog two lines, W=[first.out] R=[<abs path>] F=[one]\n\n`$(realpath)` and `$(file <)` diverge exactly as `$(shell)` and `$(wildcard)` do, for the same reason, and neither was in the filing. `$(abspath)` matches, and it is the control: `strutil::abs_path` never touches the filesystem, so it answers the same on every pass by construction and needs nothing. A sixth was found separately — a prerequisite word holding a glob character, which `parse_file_seq` matches against the filesystem where it stands:\n\n    report: *.out\n      GNU:   prereqs=[*.out]      nothing matched at read time, so the pattern stands\n      Ronin: prereqs=[first.out]\n\nAll six now match.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and the three questions the dispatch asked answered in order.\n\nKEYING is positional, per unit, per read. `GroundJournal` (kati/src-rs/session.rs) is a list of what the read asked outside itself in the order it asked, and the replay walks it with a cursor. Position is the only thing that identifies a call: the same command written twice runs twice on the first read and gets its two answers back in order, and a `$(shell)` inside a `$(foreach)` is one question per iteration — measured, `a|a|f|f|f` on both tools for a makefile with both shapes. Text keying would have collapsed all of those.\n\nThe question is recorded BESIDE each answer, and it is a check rather than a key. If the read asks something else where the record says it asked this, the sequence has stopped meaning anything. This is not decoration: measured on the divergence case below, without the check the replay hands `$(shell echo one)`'s answer to a call site asking something else entirely, which is a wrong value where the unfixed code merely re-ran a command.\n\nSTORAGE sits beside `Flags::is_repeated_read`'s own machinery, exactly as the filing predicted. `CompilationState::read_units` was a `HashSet` of cache keys and is now `ReadJournals`, a map from the same key to that unit's first read's answers; `Loaded` carries `units_read` out the same way; `build_compiler_inputs` merges with `or_insert` — the FIRST read's answers are the ones kept, because a pass that replayed them recorded the same ones again and a pass whose replay diverged recorded answers to a ground that had already moved. `read_units.clear()` on `Settlement::Restart` is unchanged and is still the whole of the restart/staging distinction: a restart's text is new, so GNU reads it again and asks again.\n\nEXHAUSTION, and divergence with it, stops the replay for the rest of that read and sends every remaining question to the ground. Not resync: once the sequence has diverged, position no longer names a call site, and guessing would hand back an answer from a site that is not the one asking. Stopping is exactly what the front end did before it recorded anything, so a read that diverges is no worse off than it was.\n\nDIVERGENCE IS REACHABLE, and here is the shape, filed as a node of its own:\n\n    -include gen.mk                            # no rule for gen.mk\n    X := $(shell printf 'parent\\n' >> log; echo v)\n    all: ; @$(MAKE) -f c1.mk sub ...           # c1.mk's recipe WRITES gen.mk\n\nThe pass settles as `Staged`, not `Restart`, because nothing regenerates — and yet the second read reads a file the first read passed over. The replay's check fires at the first question, the read falls back to the ground, and `$(shell)` runs twice again. `X` still reads `v`, which is the fallback being sane. But `G` is a variable GNU Make never defined, and a `$(file >>)` inside the newly-included text is SILENCED because the unit is marked repeating. Both are the same premise failing — `Staged` means the same text — and both are make-a-staging-pass-reads-text-the-first-read-never-saw, which depends on this node and carries the probe.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY A TEST FOUND, and it is the most interesting thing here. `submake_shell_waits_for_prerequisite` (tests/make_regressions.rs) went red on the first working version:\n\n    all: stamp\n        +$(MAKE) child VALUE=$(shell cat stamp)\n    stamp: ; @printf ready > $@\n\n`VALUE` came out empty. That `$(shell cat stamp)` is the reason recursive Make can be compiled at all: the child's invocation cannot be read until `stamp` is on the ground, so the pass AFTER the staging is the pass that asks — and it must be told what the ground says now, not the empty answer the pass before it got. A recipe is not part of any read. GNU Make expands a recipe when it runs it, for the same reason.\n\nSo the journal is suspended while a recipe is expanded, beside `is_evaluating_command` in kati/src-rs/command.rs, and it neither answers nor RECORDS there — a recorded answer would move every later question out of its place on the next pass. Measured after: a recipe's own `$(wildcard *.out)` and `$(shell ls *.out)` both see the two files the staged children made, on both tools.\n\nClosing the read ends the replay as well as handing back the record. The session outlives the read when a recipe is expanded as its edge launches, and nothing left over may answer for it. That one has no measured teeth today — the `diverged` guard and exhaustion between them close every path reachable now — and it is written down as a guard rather than as a repair.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded, both effect-gated and neither reading a line of narration:\n\n* `tests/make/feature-a-staging-pass-is-answered-from-the-first-read` — the repair, all five answers plus the effect in one case. GNU left `answers` with `X=[told] W=[] F=[] P=[]` / `R-is-empty=[yes]` and `shelllog` with one line. Run against 861feaf in a worktree it fails with `W=[first.out] F=[one] P=[seen-first.out]` / `R-is-empty=[no]`, and its `shelllog` holds two lines. `$(realpath)` is gated through `$(if ...)` rather than by its value, because the value is an absolute path and would record the scratch directory.\n* `tests/make/feature-a-restart-asks-the-ground-again` — the guard, and it passes on the parent, which is the point: a restart's text is new, so GNU asks everything again and appends twice. It holds the half that was already right and that this node could have broken.\n\nPlus `kati::session::tests::a_repeated_read_is_answered_in_order`, which holds the three decisions directly: order, the check firing on a question out of place, and the replay staying stopped afterwards.\n\nWHAT IS NOT JOURNALLED, said rather than left to be discovered. `$(KATI_extra_file_deps)` asks whether a file exists and answers nothing, so there is nothing to hand back; it is a kati extension and a check. An archive member glob reads the archive's index rather than the filesystem and goes through `crate::archive::glob_members`, which the journal does not reach. Implicit-rule search stats candidate files during dependency analysis and is not a question the evaluator asks. None of the three was measured to diverge; all three are outside what this mechanism covers naturally.\n\nONE FUNCTION CAME OUT OF `compile_unit`, which went over the density gate. `read_unit` is the read itself — the makefile evaluated and the build it describes emitted into the sink — and `UnitRead` names what a read settles, which the eight-wide tuple it replaces did not.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 897 passed / 0 failed (896 plus one new test); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean, ronin `--all-targets` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean; make_port green with no recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows 156/0/37/1106 with the tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 711 passed / 0 failed in a cold target directory; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules both built.\n\nTHE PROJECT TIMINGS ARE NOT REPORTABLE THIS TURN, and saying so is better than reporting them. The host carried another workload throughout — load average 13 rising to 30, a `pkg` build and a dozen `rustc` processes that are not this repository's — and the gate measured vim at 21s and zsh at 36s against the quiet-host baseline of 16s and 27s taken before that workload started. The gate is green, which is what it gates. The numbers are the host's, not this change's, and the change has no plausible path to costing read time: it adds one vector push per ground question and REMOVES every subprocess a repeated read would otherwise have spawned. Re-measure on a quiet host before treating 16s/27s as moved.\n\nkati is f9e94d404a86064387101b2e2d1358f3d23cd7be on nplan/make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T01:26:42.878332155Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused
                title "Let a Makefile write a name GNU Make only regenerates"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T00:47:20.357343683Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Ronin abandons a build over an assignment GNU Make accepts and forgets, so a makefile that writes a name Make regenerates does not build at all.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make at eb33025, over `all: ; @echo ok > out`:\n\n    .VARIABLES = x        on a plain makefile line\n      GNU:   exit 0, builds; `$(.VARIABLES)` still answers the live list\n      Ronin: exit 2, `Makefile:1: cannot assign to readonly variable: .VARIABLES`\n\n    make .VARIABLES=x     on the command line\n      GNU:   exit 0, builds\n      Ronin: exit 2, `*bootstrap*:0: cannot assign to readonly variable: .VARIABLES`\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += .VARIABLES=x FOO=1\n      GNU:   exit 0, builds, FOO=[1]\n      Ronin: exit 2, the same refusal\n\nSo it is one defect with three ways in, and the third is only a way in — found while delivering make-makeflags-write-drops-an-assignment-word, whose fix routes a `MAKEFLAGS` word through the ordinary assignment path and therefore reaches the same refusal the other two already reached. Fixing it there would have been fixing it in the wrong place.\n\nWHY GNU ACCEPTS. `.VARIABLES` is not a variable a Makefile is forbidden to name; it is one GNU Make recomputes in `lookup_variable` every time it is read (`variable.c`, the `.VARIABLES` special case builds the list on demand). An assignment lands in the table and is then never consulted, which is indistinguishable from being ignored — and quietly ignoring it is the whole of GNU's behaviour. `.LOADED` and `MAKEFILE_LIST` are worth probing beside it: whether they are readonly here, and whether GNU treats them the same way.\n\nTHE SITE is kati's readonly flag — `Var::readonly` in kati/src-rs/var.rs, raised through `Vars::assign` and `set_global_var`, which diagnose `cannot assign to readonly variable`. Something marks `.VARIABLES` readonly at install time; find what, and decide per name whether GNU has a readonly notion there at all. kati's own `$=` final-assignment marker uses the same flag for a feature GNU Make does not have, so the two uses must not be conflated: the extension's refusal is deliberate and stays.\n\nGate it on effects: the goal's file present and exit 0, over all three ways in — a plain line, an argv assignment, and a `MAKEFLAGS` write — never on the diagnostic's text."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T01:40:15.949111015Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:17:30.734366164Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. GNU Make has no readonly notion for either name, and the two names turned out to be governed by two different mechanisms there — which is why the fix is not one flag.\n\nTHE SURVEY, every cell probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before anything moved. Fifteen names were run through all four ways in — a plain makefile line, an argv word, a `MAKEFLAGS +=` write and the environment — and exactly THREE refused under Ronin: `.VARIABLES`, `.SHELLSTATUS` and `.KATI_SYMBOLS`. `.FEATURES`, `.INCLUDE_DIRS`, `.LOADED`, `.RECIPEPREFIX`, `.DEFAULT_GOAL`, `MAKEFILE_LIST`, `MAKE_RESTARTS`, `MAKE_TERMOUT`, `MAKE_TERMERR`, `CURDIR`, `MAKE_VERSION` and `SUFFIXES` all agreed already, exit code and value alike — the filing's suggestion to probe `.LOADED` and `MAKEFILE_LIST` beside it was right to ask and the answer is that they were never readonly here. The site is `Var::readonly`, raised by exactly two constructors: `new_variable_names` (`.VARIABLES`, `.KATI_SYMBOLS`) and `new_shell_status_var`.\n\nTWO MECHANISMS, not one, and the probes are what separated them.\n\n`.VARIABLES` is regenerated BY NAME. `lookup_special_var` (variable.c) rebuilds `var->value` from `global_variable_set.table` on any lookup after `variable_changenum` moved, so a write lands in the table — `$(origin .VARIABLES)` really does answer `file` afterwards — and is then never consulted. That holds under `override` too. It is even visible where the changenum does NOT move: `.VARIABLES += xyzzy` with nothing defined after it leaves `xyzzy` in the list, and adding one `FOO := 1` line below takes it out again. Reproducing that cache is not something to port; what a realistic makefile sees is that the write was ignored.\n\n`.SHELLSTATUS` is not one of those at all. `func_shell_base` ends with `define_variable_cname (\".SHELLSTATUS\", buf, o_override, 0)`, so the name DOES NOT EXIST until a `$(shell)` has run and the ordinary origin ladder decides everything after. Measured, ten cells, all now identical: `$(origin)`/`$(flavor)` are `undefined`/`undefined` before the first `$(shell)`; a write before that lands at `file` origin and reads back; a write after it is declined; an `override` write is taken and the NEXT `$(shell)` replaces it; `undefine` is declined and `override undefine` takes.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, kati e98aab8a4e38dd387a5b9e824d280d58d918a6bc on branch nplan/make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused.\n\n* both `readonly: true` flags go. The origin ladder — GNU's own precedence machinery, already ported — then declines a `file`, `command line` or `environment` write over an override-origin binding SILENTLY, which is what GNU does with these names observably. That is the whole of the three ways in the filing named.\n* `carry_regenerated_value` in `GlobalVars::assign` keeps an `InnerVar::VariableNames` value on whatever replaces the binding, so `override .VARIABLES = x` changes where the binding came from and not what the name answers. Without it, dropping the flag would have traded an abandonment for a wrong answer.\n* `.SHELLSTATUS` LEAVES `GlobalVars::with_builtins` and `Session::record_shell_status` publishes it at every `$(shell)`, journal replay included. That is what makes the pre-first-`$(shell)` window right, and it is why `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` agree for this name where they still do not for `.VARIABLES` — filed as make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin.\n* `+=` onto either is left standing instead of reaching `append_var`/`append_str`, which `panic!()` on a computed value — a landmine the readonly flag had been hiding. `Variable::value_is_computed` is the test.\n\nkati's own `$=` and `.KATI_READONLY` refusal is untouched and was checked: `FOO := 1 / .KATI_READONLY := FOO / FOO := 2` still says `cannot assign to readonly variable: FOO`. `.KATI_SYMBOLS` was treated as `.VARIABLES` is — same constructor, same regenerated-list mechanism, and no extension semantics ride on its refusal.\n\nFIXTURES, two oracle-recorded, both gating effects and neither reading a diagnostic:\n\n* `tests/make/a-write-to-a-name-make-works-out-does-not-stop-the-read` — the makefile-line and `MAKEFLAGS` ways in together with the `override` cell and the pre-/post-`$(shell)` window, gating `FOO=[1] seen=[before-any-shell] status=[3]` and `names=[GOAL] wrote=[]`.\n* `tests/make/a-command-line-write-to-a-name-make-works-out-is-taken` — the argv way in through the `args` sidecar, gating that `.SHELLSTATUS` takes the argv value at `command line` origin while `.VARIABLES` does not.\n\nThe environment is the third way in and no corpus case can carry it — the port harness runs every case with one environment — so it is `an_environment_write_to_a_name_make_works_out_does_not_stop_the_read` in tests/make_regressions.rs, with the expectation taken from the oracle rather than written by hand. TEETH MEASURED, not assumed: all three build nothing at all on a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4, each dying on `cannot assign to readonly variable`. kati unit tests `test_builtins_are_scope_state` (now asserting `.SHELLSTATUS` is absent until a `$(shell)` runs) and `a_write_lands_on_a_regenerated_name_without_changing_what_it_answers`.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 899 passed / 0 failed (897 plus the two new tests); make_port 4/4 with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw — unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 0 disagreements, which is ONE MORE GRAPH than the 278 baseline because a corpus makefile that used to be rejected now compiles; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156/0/37/1106 with no drift line and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 712 / 0 in a cold target directory (711 plus the kati unit test); `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 22s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 34s.\n\nONE INVENTORY ROW MOVED and it is audited: `shellstatus_readonly.mk#test` keeps its class and family (`extension` / `kati-extension`) and changes digest 64b641ea62022407 → 200d465c3d68cce1. That case is upstream kati's own, and its `ifdef KATI` branch exists to assert the extension this removes: it writes `.SHELLSTATUS := 5` before any `$(shell)`, which now lands, so kati prints `5` where it printed the refusal. It stays a difference because the case writes GNU's side of the comparison by hand under `else`, which is exactly what the kati-extension family describes. The vendored case is left as it is — it is evidence, not a gate — but a reader should know it now documents a behaviour Ronin no longer has.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin (`$(origin .VARIABLES)` answers `override` where GNU answers `default`, `$(flavor)` answers `kati_variable_names` which is not one of GNU's four answers at all, `undefine .VARIABLES` is declined where GNU deletes it, and the list itself holds the automatic variables inside a recipe where GNU's never does) and make-an-empty-argument-word-stops-the-build (`make \"\"` abandons with `empty path` where GNU passes over a zero-length argument and builds the default goal)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:18:57.155511753Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-staging-pass-reads-text-the-first-read-never-saw
                title "Read a staging pass over the text the first read had"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T01:18:55.314738432Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A staging pass is supposed to re-read the SAME text, and one shape breaks that premise: a makefile the read only optionally includes, which a staged child's recipe then writes. The second pass includes it; GNU Make's single read never saw it exist.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make at 861feaf and again after make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again landed — it is unmoved by that fix, which is why it is a node rather than a paragraph in it.\n\n    Makefile      -include gen.mk\n                  X := $(shell printf 'parent\\n' >> log; echo v)\n                  all: ; @$(MAKE) -f c1.mk sub\n                         @$(MAKE) -f c2.mk sub\n                         @printf 'X=[$(X)] G=[$(G)]\\n' > vals\n    c1.mk         sub: ; @printf 'G := from-child\\nGS := $(shell ...)\\n' > gen.mk\n\n      GNU:   vals `X=[v] G=[]`,           log one line\n      Ronin: vals `X=[v] G=[from-child]`, log two lines\n\n`gen.mk` has no rule, so nothing regenerates and the pass settles as `Settlement::Staged` rather than `Settlement::Restart`. The second read then reads a file the first read passed over, and the whole of the premise both halves of the staging work rest on — `Settlement::Staged` means the read happens again over the same text — is false for that read.\n\nWHAT THE REPLAY DOES ABOUT IT, which is the reason this is filed and not fixed. The journal make-staging-pass-asks-the-ground-again installed is keyed by position with the question recorded beside each answer as a check. The re-read asks `gen.mk`'s `$(shell)` where the record says the parent's stands, the check fails, and the replay stops for the rest of that read: every remaining question goes to the ground, which is exactly what the front end did before it recorded anything. So the fallback is sane and no answer is ever handed to a call site that did not ask it — measured, `X` still reads `v` — but `$(shell)` does run twice again, and `G` is a variable GNU Make never defined.\n\nTWO MORE THINGS THE SAME SHAPE MOVES, both measured:\n\n* the newly-included file's `$(file >> seen,...)` writes NOTHING, because `is_repeated_read` is set per unit and this unit is repeating. So text that is being read for the first time is silenced as though it were not. That is the mirror image of the same premise failing.\n* `G` is defined at all, so the graph and the variable table describe a makefile GNU Make did not read.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE, sketched rather than settled. Either the pass reads the include list the first read settled on — pinning which files the read may open, the way the journal pins what the ground may answer, which fits the machinery already there — or a file appearing under an optional include is treated as a restart, which is what GNU Make would call it if a rule had made it. The first keeps the compilation's premise true; the second gives up the premise and pays a whole extra read. Probe which one GNU's own restart analog is closer to before choosing.\n\nGate it on effects: the optionally-included file's own variable absent from the recipe's output, and the `$(shell)` log holding one line, under a makefile with two recursive children and no rule for the include."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin
                title "Let the names Make works out for itself answer for themselves the way GNU's do"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:16:41.232643846Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused, and left out of it because that node is about a build being ABANDONED and this is about what the name answers once it is not. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make at e98aab8 (kati), GNU on the left.\n\nORIGIN AND FLAVOUR, with nothing assigned anywhere:\n\n    $(origin .VARIABLES)      default    / override\n    $(flavor .VARIABLES)      simple     / kati_variable_names\n    $(origin .KATI_SYMBOLS)   undefined  / override\n\n`kati_variable_names` is not one of GNU Make's four answers at all (`undefined`, `recursive`, `simple`, and nothing else), so a makefile branching on `$(flavor)` takes a branch GNU Make never takes. `.KATI_SYMBOLS` is kati's own name and answering `undefined` for it in Make mode is a judgement to make rather than an obvious repair — GNU has no such name, so a GNU makefile that used it would find it undefined.\n\nStanding at override origin is also what makes `undefine` diverge, and that one reaches build intent:\n\n    undefine .VARIABLES\n      GNU:   $(origin .VARIABLES) is `undefined` and the name answers nothing\n      Ronin: declined silently — the binding stands at override origin, which\n             `undefine` without `override` does not outrank — and the name goes\n             on answering the live list\n\nGNU has `.VARIABLES` at `o_default` (`define_variable_cname (\".VARIABLES\", \"\", o_default, 0)` in `define_automatic_variables`), which a makefile's own `undefine` at `o_file` does outrank. The reason Ronin cannot simply lower it to `Default` is what the sibling node settled: the binding has to survive an ordinary assignment, because GNU regenerates the value by NAME in `lookup_special_var` rather than by protecting the slot. Matching both means separating \"what origin does this name answer\" from \"what may replace this binding\", which is a change to `GlobalVars::assign` rather than to the constructor.\n\nTHE LIST'S CONTENTS also differ, measured in a clean environment with `$(file >f,$(sort $(.VARIABLES)))` at read time and again inside a recipe:\n\n  in GNU and not Ronin   .INCLUDE_DIRS .LOADED MAKE_COMMAND MAKE_HOST\n  in Ronin and not GNU   -*-command-variables-*- KATI .KATI_SYMBOLS\n                         MAKECMDGOALS MAKEOVERRIDES\n  in a recipe, also      % * + < ? @ ^ |\n\nThe last row is the one worth the most: Ronin's recipe-time list holds the automatic variables, and GNU's never does — GNU defines them in the target's own variable set, and `lookup_special_var` walks `global_variable_set.table` alone. `-*-command-variables-*-` is GNU's own hidden snapshot name and GNU does not list it either. `KATI` and `.KATI_SYMBOLS` are the extension's, and whether they belong in the list in Make mode is the same judgement as the origin question above.\n\nNothing gates on any of this today and no upstream inventory row reads it. Gate it on a file a recipe writes: the sorted list, the two `$(origin)`/`$(flavor)` answers, and the `undefine` cell."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:28:54.485774924Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:48:30.06548813Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and smaller than the filing feared — because the sibling node had already done the hard half. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE FILING SAID matching both would mean separating \"what origin does this name answer\" from \"what may replace this binding\". That separation already exists: make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused landed `carry_regenerated_value`, which keeps the live-list value on whatever replaces the binding. So lowering the origin from `Override` to `Default` no longer costs the answer, and the four cells fall out of one constructor field.\n\n    $(origin .VARIABLES)     default / override → default\n    $(flavor .VARIABLES)     simple  / kati_variable_names → simple\n    undefine .VARIABLES      undefined afterwards / declined silently → undefined\n\nand the ladder either side of it, measured rather than assumed: a plain `.VARIABLES := hand-written` after the undefine reads back `file` and answers `hand-written`, and a LATER assignment elsewhere does not grow the list back. That last cell is the one that proves the model. GNU's regeneration is a `special` bit on the variable struct — `define_variable_cname (\".VARIABLES\", \"\", o_default, 0)->special = 1` in main.c, read at `lookup_variable` — and `undefine` frees the struct, so a name defined afterwards is ordinary. Carrying the VALUE rather than protecting the NAME reproduces that exactly, and it is why `carry_regenerated_value` was the right shape.\n\n`$(flavor)` was answering `kati_variable_names`, which is not one of GNU Make's four answers at all. It is `simple`, because that is what the definition above made it: the rebuilding overwrites `var->value` and touches nothing else.\n\nTHE LIST, and the part of this worth reading, because the filing's reading of it was half right. It said \"Ronin's recipe-time list holds the automatic variables, and GNU's never does\". GNU's list holds NINE of them and always has: `set_file_variables` (src/commands.c) binds the base forms `$@`, `$<`, `$?` in the FILE's own variable set as a recipe is prepared, while `define_automatic_variables` (src/variable.c) binds `@D`, `@F` and their fifteen siblings ONCE, at startup, in the GLOBAL set. `.VARIABLES` walks the global table alone. So GNU's answer holds `@D` and `@F` and never `@` — measured in a clean environment, at read time and inside a recipe, both the same. Ronin held both, and now holds the same nine GNU does. `Variable::is_base_automatic_command` is the test and `AutoCommandVar::is_base_form` is what it asks.\n\n`.KATI_SYMBOLS` gets the same treatment and NOT the `undefined` the filing floated. It is kati's own name; GNU has none, so there is no oracle answer to match, only a judgement — and the campaign's judgement about kati's extension names is already recorded thirty times over in the conformance corpus's `kati-extension` family: the extension stands and the case documents it. Making it `undefined` would be deleting an extension under cover of an origin fix. It now answers `default`/`simple`, which is the ladder cell this node is about, and its presence in the list is the sibling question filed below.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-name-make-works-out-answers-for-itself` — `$(origin)`/`$(flavor)` at read time and again inside a recipe, plus `$(filter @ < ? ^ + |,$(.VARIABLES))` empty in both and `$(filter @D @F <D <F ?D ?F,...)` holding all six. The list is never gated verbatim because it holds the environment; a filter over known names is environment-independent and says the same thing.\n* `tests/make/an-undefine-takes-a-worked-out-name-away-for-good` — the undefine cell and the four states after it, gating `before=[default] after=[undefined] list=[]` and `rewritten=[file] value=[hand-written] still=[hand-written]`.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the origin, the flavour and the list filter all reverted, both cases fail — `o=[override] f=[kati_variable_names]`, `recipe base=[+ < ? @ ^ |]`, and the undefine case answering `after=[override]` with the whole environment in it.\n\nFOUR DEFECTS FOUND BY THE SAME SURVEY AND FILED, not fixed. All four were probed in the same pass, so each note carries measured cells rather than a suspicion:\n\n* make-a-worked-out-names-value-is-its-own-name — `$(value .VARIABLES)` is `.VARIABLES` where GNU gives the list. `Variable::string` returns the name.\n* make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing — `.INCLUDE_DIRS`, `.LOADED`, `MAKE_COMMAND`, `MAKE_HOST` are undefined here and `default`/`simple` there. This is the whole of the remaining GNU side of the list diff.\n* make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them — `MAKECMDGOALS` and `MAKEOVERRIDES` exist unconditionally here; GNU defines the first only when a goal word was given and the second only when a command-line variable was. `ifdef MAKECMDGOALS` is a real idiom that cannot work under Ronin.\n* make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour — `CURDIR` is `file` there and `default` here, and `MAKE_VERSION`, `MAKELEVEL` and `MAKECMDGOALS` read back `recursive` where GNU says `simple`. Eleven other built-in names were checked in the same pass and agree, so that survey is closed.\n\nWHERE, kati 5cd1dd0e7a9d6d39298ae81598e68f56055d84a7 on branch nplan/make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin. No Ronin-side change at all.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 902 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 856 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 155 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1107 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 33s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:48:54.247644319Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-empty-argument-word-stops-the-build
                title "Pass over an empty command-line word instead of abandoning on it"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:16:47.910154163Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-write-to-a-special-variable-is-refused's command-line cell — the probe itself passed an empty word by accident and Ronin abandoned on it, which is how it surfaced. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make over `all: ; @echo ok > out`:\n\n    make \"\"          GNU: exit 0, `out` written   Ronin: `ronin: empty path`, nothing built\n    make \"\" all      GNU: exit 0, `out` written   Ronin: the same\n    make all \"\"      GNU: exit 0, `out` written   Ronin: the same\n\nGNU Make's `handle_non_switch_argument` returns immediately on a zero-length word — `if (!arg[0]) return;` — so an empty argument is neither a goal nor a variable definition and the default goal is built as if it were not there. Ronin reads every non-switch word as a path and refuses the empty one before any goal is chosen.\n\nIt reaches real builds: a wrapper that expands `make $(EXTRA_GOALS)` with `EXTRA_GOALS` unset in a shell that does not word-split it, or any `make \"$TARGET\"` with `TARGET` empty, hands Make exactly this and GNU Make builds.\n\nThe adjacent switch cell agrees and is not part of this: `make -f \"\"` is refused by both, GNU with `the '-f' option requires a non-empty string argument` and Ronin with its own open failure.\n\nGate it on effects: the default goal's file present and exit 0 with an empty word among the arguments, through the port harness's `args` sidecar."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:41:44.831034294Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:54:59.624829393Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's reading of GNU was right about the effect and wrong about the line. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout.\n\nWHERE GNU ACTUALLY SKIPS IT. `handle_non_switch_argument` in 4.4.1 has no `if (!arg[0]) return;` — the filing quoted a shape from another release. What it has is the guard on the goal branch itself:\n\n    else if (arg[0] != '\\0' && origin == o_command)\n      /* Not an option or variable definition; it must be a goal target. ... */\n\nwith the comment naming the reason: \"if the top makefile resets MAKEOVERRIDES then ARG points to an empty string in the submake\". So the empty word reaches `try_variable_definition`, which finds no `=` and returns 0, and then falls off the end without becoming anything. The observable result is what the filing recorded and the mechanism is a branch guard rather than an early return — which matters only because it is also where the `origin == o_command` test lives, and that is a second thing the same line decides (a non-switch word arriving from MAKEFLAGS is never a goal). Ronin already agrees on that second one, measured: `MAKEFLAGS=othergoal make` builds the default goal in both tools.\n\nTHE FIX, one guard at the one site. `classify_word` in src/make/cli.rs returns on a zero-length word before the `=` test. That is the whole of it: `parse_arguments` already routes any word that does not start with `-` there, and `--` only changes which words reach it, so the terminated spelling is covered by the same clause rather than a second one.\n\nMEASURED AGAINST THE ORACLE, five shapes, all now identical (exit code and the file the build wrote):\n\n    make \"\"            default goal built, MAKECMDGOALS empty\n    make \"\" all        `all`, MAKECMDGOALS `all`\n    make all \"\"        the same\n    make \"\" \"\" all     the same\n    make -- \"\"         default goal built, MAKECMDGOALS empty\n\nThe empty word is absent from MAKECMDGOALS in every one, which is the part a makefile can see.\n\nTHE HARNESS COULD NOT SAY IT AND NOW CAN. `read_words` splits the `args` sidecar on whitespace, and a zero-length word is the one argument whitespace cannot separate — so the filing's own instruction to gate this through the sidecar needed the sidecar to grow a spelling first. `''` — the shell's own — now reads as an empty word, and no existing `args` file in the corpus contains a quote of any kind, so nothing else moved.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/an-empty-command-line-word-leaves-the-default-goal-standing` — args `''`, gating `built=[all] goals=[]` with a `second` target that must not run.\n* `tests/make/an-empty-command-line-word-beside-a-goal-is-passed-over` — args `'' second ''`, the empty word on both sides of a named goal, gating `built=[second] goals=[second]` and the default goal left alone.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the guard turned off and everything else in place, both cases fail with \"build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\" — Ronin dies `ronin: empty path` at exit 2 and writes nothing. Unit test `an_empty_word_is_neither_a_goal_nor_an_assignment` in src/make/cli.rs covers the alone, surrounded and post-`--` spellings at the parse.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-a-plain-dash-word-becomes-a-goal. The same GNU function opens by ignoring a lone `-` \"for compatibility\" and Ronin makes a goal of it, so `make -` builds the default goal under GNU and dies `'-' missing and no known rule to make it` under Ronin. Filed rather than folded in because it is a different defect at the same site with its own fixture to record.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 901 passed / 0 failed (900 plus the unit test); make_port 4/4 over 852 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1106 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:58:02.952232849Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-always-make-rebuilds-nothing
                title "Remake every target under -B instead of accepting the switch and building nothing"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:46:12.634635351Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell across the `$?` cells: `-B` was in the matrix as a control and it is the one cell that disagreed, in both the parent tree and the changed one, so it belongs to neither.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make over `out: a` with `out` newer than `a`:\n\n    make -B            GNU: runs the recipe, `out` rewritten, exit 0\n                       Ronin: nothing runs, `out` untouched, exit 0\n    make --always-make the same, both spellings\n\nThis is the same class the operator ruled on for `-t` on 2026-08-17: `-B` is not reporting or scheduling, it decides what the run writes to disk, and filesystem effects ARE a conformance criterion under `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1]`. Accepting it and building nothing is not an unimplemented no-op but the opposite of what was asked for — a `make -B` that answers \"nothing to be done\" where GNU rebuilds the tree.\n\nWHAT THE PLAN ALREADY SAYS ABOUT IT, and the reason this is a node rather than a surprise. make-option-coverage predicted it would be cheap — \"`EdgeSpec::always_dirty` exists as of make-phony-always-dirty. Setting it on every edge is the whole implementation\" — and it is sealed without it having landed. make-a-forgiven-makefile-goal's note then recorded the state honestly: \"`-t` and `-B` need nothing here ... neither reaches Ronin's scheduler at all — they are accepted interface switches whose goal-stage behaviour is the discovery-only touch-option and always-make-option cases\". So `tests/make/always-make-option` sits in `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` and gates nothing.\n\nWhoever takes this has the `-t` delivery as the model, and the same remake-boundary exception waiting: GNU turns `-B` OFF after the first restart (`always_make_flag = always_make_set && restarts == 0`, main.c:1702), which is what stops an always-remade makefile looping forever. make-a-forgiven-makefile-goal's note is the record of what that exception is and says explicitly that an implementation has to arrive with it.\n\nGate it on effects, and lift `always-make-option` out of `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` when it does: the recipe ran and the output was rewritten though it was newer than every prerequisite, plus the restart cell, plus `MAKEFLAGS` carrying `B` into a recursive child."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:49:22.812954256Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T05:19:31.482815073Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `-B` rebuilds the tree, and the plan's own prediction about how — \"`EdgeSpec::always_dirty` exists; setting it on every edge is the whole implementation\" — was tried on paper and rejected for a measured reason, which is the part of this worth reading.\n\nTHE ORACLE FIRST, twelve shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, all now identical:\n\n    make -B / --always-make   every recipe runs, `$?` becomes the whole\n                              prerequisite list, exit 0\n    -B -n                     the recipes are printed and none runs; no mtime moves\n    -B -q                     exit 1 where a bare -q exits 0, and nothing is built\n    -B -t                     every output gets a fresh date and no recipe runs\n    -B over .PHONY            unchanged; it was always dirty\n    -B over a target with no recipe   not forced, and its prerequisite that HAS\n                              one is (GNU's own test is `file->cmds != 0`)\n    -B over order-only        `$?` names `oa` and never `ob`\n    -B over a double-colon rule, a grouped `&:` rule, an archive member,\n                              a two-deep chain, and the same chain at -j4\n    -B into a recursive child through MAKEFLAGS, and through `MAKEFLAGS=B`\n                              in the environment\n    -B with a makefile that has a rule   remade ONCE, MAKE_RESTARTS=1, and the\n                              goals then built with -B still in force\n\nThe last one is the whole reason this node is not two lines. GNU sets `always_make_flag = always_make_set && (restarts == 0)` before it brings the makefiles up to date and back to `always_make_set` afterwards (main.c:1702, main.c:2834). Without the guard the remake moves the makefile's date, the read starts again, `-B` forces the remake again, and it never ends — measured on this tree before the guard went in: a hundred passes of `remade-gen` and the goal never reached. GNU's own suite names the bug: `options/dash-B` carries \"Test -B with the re-exec feature: we don't want to re-exec forever, Savannah bug # 7566\" and, right below it, \"we DO want -B in the 'normal' part of the makefile\".\n\nWHY NOT `always_dirty` ON EVERY EDGE, and this is the decision the filing asked for. That flag lives on the graph, and the graph is scanned TWICE in one Make run — once to bring the makefiles up to date and once for the goals — with GNU answering the two differently. A flag written into the edges cannot say two things at once, so honouring the restart guard would have meant mutating the graph between the passes, or clearing the flag over the transitive closure of the makefile targets — which is not the same set as GNU's, since a file that is both a makefile's prerequisite and a goal's would be un-forced for both. The two scans already differ by their `BuildOptions`: `remaking_options` turns `-n`, `-t` and `verbose` off for exactly this phase and `Subset` carries a `BuildOptions` per group. So `-B` joins them, and the guard is one expression at the one place the phase is decided.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, Ronin only, no kati change.\n\n* `BuildOptions::always_make`, set from `Switch::AlwaysMake` in `build_options` beside `touch`.\n* `RuntimeState` carries it for the scan — `set_always_make` from `Builder::from_parts` — so the two dirty readers see it without a signature changing anywhere. The dirty walk is `edge_data.always_dirty || (runtime.always_make() && !graph.is_phony_rule(edge_data.rule)) || out_of_date`, and the phony test is GNU's `file->cmds != 0`: a name with no recipe is not forced, and a source file has no generator edge to force.\n* `capture_deferred_freshness` reads it as one more reason the outputs count as absent, which gets `$?` right for free: `all_inputs_new` is already the flag that says \"every prerequisite is new because the target is not there\", and GNU fills `$?` from `d->changed || always_make_flag` (commands.c). One line, two behaviours, and the order-only cell falls out because those names were never in the list.\n* `Makefiles::force` is `options.always_make && restarts == 0`, applied to all four makefile subsets at once because GNU decides it once for the phase. `restarts` reaches `build_compiler_inputs` through `CompilerInputBuild`.\n\n`always-make-option` LEFT `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES`, which the filing asked for, and its recording moved from the retired `status 0` line to the current `outcome success` — the only recording rewritten, and it moved because the case is joining the gate rather than because GNU changed its mind. The six other cases still on that list keep the old spelling, which is what shows the rewrite is the format catching up.\n\nFIXTURES, four new, oracle-recorded, plus the lifted one:\n\n* `always-make-remakes-a-makefile-once-and-not-forever` — the restart cell, gating one `remade` line and `all restarts=[1]`.\n* `always-make-reaches-a-recursive-child` — `-B` in MAKEFLAGS and the child's current `out` rewritten.\n* `always-make-counts-every-prerequisite-as-new` — `$?` naming an untouched prerequisite and never the order-only one.\n* `always-make-answers-a-question-out-of-date` — `-B -q` over a current goal, gating the failing outcome and that nothing was written.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed, twice. With `always_make` forced false, five cases fail: `'out' holds \"stale\"`, `'gen.mk' was left alone`, `'sub/out' holds \"stale\"`, `'ordered' was left alone`, and the question succeeding where GNU failed. With only the restart guard removed, one fails — the makefile case, `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`, which is the hundred-pass loop hitting its bound.\n\nTWO EXISTING TESTS ASSERTED THE OLD BEHAVIOUR AND WERE REWRITTEN, not deleted. `always_make_is_interface_noop` in tests/cli.rs is now `always_make_rebuilds_every_target` and reads the recipe's appends back to count how many times it ran, including through `MAKEFLAGS=B`; `question_mode_answers_in_the_status_and_builds_nothing` now expects `-q -B` to answer 1 rather than 0. `-B` also moves from `OptionClass::NoOp` to `OptionClass::NinjaControl` in the interface surface table, which is where `-t` already sat.\n\nINVENTORY: FOUR ROWS MOVED AND EVERY ONE IS `options/dash-B`. `.2`, `.4`, `.6` and `.7` go from `unclassified ... +evaluation` to `narration ninja-progress` — GNU's own -B test, including the two re-exec cells, with the `evaluation` residue gone and only the progress line left. 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration; the compiler bucket is still ZERO.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 861 gated cases with one recording rewritten and audited above; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 27s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 33s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T05:26:08.468342312Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-new-inputs-path-forms-read-the-placeholder
                title "Take the directory and file halves of the new inputs off the names, not off the placeholder"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:59:43.588167688Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell across the `$?` cells, and pre-existing: it is unmoved by that node's fix and reproduces identically on a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    out: d/a b\n    \\t@printf 'q=[%s] D=[%s] F=[%s]\\\\n' '$?' '$(?D)' '$(?F)' > log\n\n      GNU:   q=[d/a b] D=[d .] F=[a b]\n      Ronin: q=[d/a b] D=[.]   F=[d/a b]\n\n`$(?D)` and `$(?F)` are the directory and file halves of `$?`, one word each per prerequisite it names, and GNU Make binds all three from the same list in `set_file_variables`. Ronin's `$?` is not a value at compilation time but a placeholder — `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` — that the scheduler substitutes into the command when it launches the edge, and the D and F forms are taken off THAT: `$(dir ${KATI_NEW_INPUTS})` is `.` and `$(notdir ...)` is the whole token, so `D` answers about a one-word name with no slash in it and `F` hands back the placeholder for the substitution to fill.\n\nIt shows only where the two halves differ from the whole, which needs a prerequisite carrying a directory. `$?` itself is right, because the substitution reaches it directly.\n\nWHERE. kati binds the path forms from the automatic variable's own value (`bind_path_forms`, `AutoCommandVar`), and for `?` that value is the placeholder rather than the names. The scheduler's side is `deferred_new_inputs_value` / `resolve_deferred_new_inputs` in src/build/deferred.rs, which knows the real list and substitutes one token. Matching GNU means either the D and F forms getting placeholders of their own that the same substitution fills — three tokens rather than one, with the `dir`/`notdir` applied on the scheduler's side — or the whole `$?` value being bound at launch, which make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell established is not available: the scheduler's list is the accurate one and the launch-time stat loop is not (a prerequisite that does not exist, one remade without its mtime moving, and an archive member all count as changed for GNU and do not for a stat).\n\nThe corpus case `tests/make/new-inputs-path-forms-name-the-new-inputs` is recorded from the oracle and carries a `divergence` file naming this node, so it fails the moment it is fixed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:02:25.546646949Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:26:42.044933612Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered as the filing's first alternative: three references rather than one, with the halving done where the list is.\n\nTHE ORACLE FIRST, because two of the answers were not obvious. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, five prerequisite shapes in one rule:\n\n    d/a d/b d/e/c plain /tmp/abs\n      $?      d/a d/b d/e/c plain /tmp/abs\n      $(?D)   d d d/e . /tmp\n      $(?F)   a b c plain abs\n\nSo it is a per-word split at the last separator, in order, with no dedup — `d` appears twice — and `.` for a word carrying no directory. Two edges probed separately: `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir /x))` is EMPTY, not `/`, and `$(notdir x/)` is empty too, which is `memrchr`-from-the-end semantics and exactly what kati's `dirname`/`basename` already implement. And an archive member splits on its member spelling: `lib.a(sub/m.o)` gives `$?` `sub/m.o`, `$(?D)` `sub`, `$(?F)` `m.o`.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, kati 5922de30e967f557ebb962a67ae18f01a1b10518 on branch nplan/make-new-inputs-path-forms-read-the-placeholder, plus the Ronin side.\n\n* kati grows `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_D}` and `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_F}` beside `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`. `eval_impl` now answers whether it wrote a value or a reference — a two-state `Bound` — and `AutoCommandVar::eval` halves only a value. The scheduler-boundary branch writes the reference belonging to the form being expanded.\n* Ronin's `DeferredSpec`/`DeferredFreshness` carry two more names beside `new_inputs_variable`, and `resolve_deferred_new_inputs` substitutes all three, each from the same published list seen through a `NewInputsView`. The sink names them from kati's own `pub const`s, which retires the string literal that used to sit in src/make/sink.rs.\n* `recipe_steps`' direct-launch guard tests the PREFIX the three references share. Without that widening the `F` reference is not the base one, `direct_argv` accepts a line holding it, and the substitution — which lands only where a shell runs — never happens. Measured: the fixture below writes a file literally called `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_F}`.\n\nWHY THE HALVING IS ON THE SCHEDULER'S SIDE AND NOT KATI'S, since the filing left the choice open. The alternative was for kati to publish, per input, the two halves already computed — the mechanism `new_input_names` already uses to respell an archive member. It was rejected on a real wart rather than on taste: the published name is spelt relative to the unit the command runs in, and `relative_to` is applied at LAUNCH, so kati does not know the string it would be halving. Publishing halves of a name that has not been relocated yet would be right only for the root unit. The scheduler has the final spelling and splits it there. `view_of` in src/build/deferred.rs is the whole of the runtime's share and it is a path split, which is a thing the runtime already does — `relative_to` sits three lines away.\n\nFIXTURES. The filing's own case `tests/make/new-inputs-path-forms-name-the-new-inputs` had a `divergence` file naming this node; it is deleted and the case gates. Two more, oracle-recorded:\n\n* `new-inputs-path-forms-reach-a-line-that-needs-no-shell` — `@touch '$(?F)'` over `out: d/a`. Single quotes are not shell characters to `direct_argv`, so the line is argv-eligible and this is the shape that gates the prefix widening: GNU makes a file called `a`.\n* `new-inputs-path-forms-are-spelt-where-the-child-reads-them` — a recursive child whose `$(?D)` is `d .` and never `sub/d sub`, gating that the halves are taken after the relocation rather than before it.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed, on the same tree with the fix toggled off: with both halves of the change reverted, all three cases fail — `D=[.] F=[d/a b]` where GNU left `D=[d .] F=[a b]`, and `'a' was not produced`. With only the prefix widening reverted, the argv case alone fails. Unit test `a_view_splits_a_name_at_its_last_separator` covers the root-level word no fixture reaches.\n\nONE INVENTORY ROW MOVED AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE. `variables/automatic.diff` goes `unclassified ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation` → `narration ninja-progress+recipe-echo`. That is GNU Make's own automatic-variables test, and line 23 of it is `@echo '$$? = $?, $$(?D) = $(?D), $$(?F) = $(?F)'` against the expectation `$? = $dir/bar.y, $(?D) = $dir, $(?F) = bar.y` — this node's cell exactly. The `evaluation` residue is gone because the values now agree and only the progress line and the recipe echo differ. 1299 rows, 155 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1107 narration, compiler bucket still ZERO.\n\nSTILL DIVERGING, recorded rather than fixed: `$(filter-out ...)` over `$(?D)` sees the D reference as one token and cannot special-case it the way func.rs does for the base one, so the filter is applied to the reference. It was applied to the reference before this change too — that path is untouched — and no case reaches it.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 902 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 854 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements with the manifest byte-identical; upstream inventory 1299 rows, one row moved and audited above; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 19s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 32s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:28:24.109659346Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-scheduler-bound-recipe-is-expanded-though-its-target-is-current
                title "Leave a recipe naming the new inputs unexpanded when its target is up to date"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:00:00.301619656Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell, and pre-existing: unmoved by that node's fix and identical on a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4.\n\nGNU Make expands a recipe in `new_job`, immediately before running it and only for a target it decided to remake, so a target that is already up to date has none of the expansion's effects happen at all. Ronin keeps that for every recipe it can defer — the whole of make-recipe-expanded-at-launch — but a recipe naming `$?` is one of the four kinds `defers_recipe` must read while the graph is built, and reading it is expanding it.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, with `out` newer than `a`:\n\n    out: a\n    \\t@printf 'q=[%s]\\\\n' '$?' > witness $(error this should never be expanded)\n\n      GNU:   exit 0, `out` untouched, `witness` untouched, nothing said\n      Ronin: exit 2, the build abandoned on the `$(error)`\n\nThe same shape reaches a `$(shell)` that runs when GNU would never have started it, and a `$(wildcard)` answered against the files that existed before the build rather than after — everything make-recipe-expanded-at-launch is about. Take away the `$?` and the recipe defers and the cell agrees, which is what makes this a property of the `$?` binding rather than of the recipe.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY DEFERRED, measured rather than assumed. Removing the `$?` refusal from `defers_recipe` was tried first and is wrong: `NewInputsTiming::Launch` answers `$?` by stat'ing the target and its prerequisites, and that is a strictly weaker model than GNU's `d->changed`. Seven corpus cases fail under it — a prerequisite that does not exist counts as changed for GNU and is not newer than anything by stat, a prerequisite remade without its mtime moving (an empty recipe, a phony, a restat) counts and does not stat as newer, and an archive member's index date is not a file's. The scheduler's list is the accurate one and that is why the edge declares it.\n\nSo matching GNU here means keeping the scheduler's list while not expanding the recipe: the compiler would have to declare the edge's deferred freshness from the recipe's TEXT — which `references_new_inputs` already answers without expanding — and the launch-time expansion would have to be handed the list the scheduler computed. The hard part is the rest of the declaration, which is derived from the expansion rather than from the text: `deferred_new_inputs_filter_out` comes from a `$(filter-out ...,$?)` the expansion saw, and only an expansion can see it. Settle what happens to a filter-out that cannot be known before choosing.\n\nThe corpus case `tests/make/an-up-to-date-target-never-expands-its-new-inputs-recipe` is recorded from the oracle and carries a `divergence` file naming this node."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell
                title "Launch the three recipe shapes that still reach one shell line by line"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:00:18.900614017Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The residue make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell leaves, measured cell by cell against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make after its fix landed rather than predicted.\n\nThat node carried the per-line launches across the compiler boundary for an edge whose command was settled while the graph was built, which closed the `$?` kind and the grouped double-colon kind. Three shapes still hand a whole recipe to one shell, and the reproducer for each is the same one: a line whose shell syntax is left open, which GNU Make dies on and a joined script completes.\n\n    @printf '%s' 'x\n    y' > out\n\n  beside a recursive `$(MAKE)`   GNU refuses, writes nothing, exit 2\n                                 Ronin writes `x ) && (y` and runs the child\n  with a declared depfile        GNU refuses; Ronin writes `x ) && (y`\n  after a 120,000-byte line      GNU refuses; Ronin writes `x ) && (y`\n\nWHY EACH IS WITHHELD, and the withholding is deliberate rather than missed.\n\nA recipe composed into child graphs does not run the assembled script as one edge at all: `sink_node` splits it into a preceding segment per invocation and a residual segment, each assembled on its own and attached to a different edge. The per-line steps of the WHOLE recipe would be a different program from what that edge runs. Matching GNU means each segment carrying its own steps, which is `residual_segment` returning a step list beside its script and `SinkSubninja` growing a field for it.\n\nA depfile extraction REWRITES the script — `get_depfile` takes the depfile name out of the command text and hands back a changed `cmd_buf` — so steps made from the pre-rewrite lines would run text the edge no longer holds. Matching GNU means the extraction working over the lines rather than over the assembled script. Note this one is reachable only through kati's own `.KATI_DEPFILE` or `--detect_depfiles`, neither of which a GNU makefile writes.\n\nA line over the 100,000-byte response-file threshold cannot be launched on its own, because the response file is named per edge (`<output>.rsp`) and several long lines in one recipe would want one name. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` is that test and it refuses the WHOLE recipe when any one line is too long, so a recipe with one long line and ten short ones runs all eleven in one shell. Matching GNU means a response file named per step — `PreparedStep` carrying its own, written before that step spawns and removed after it — which is a change to the build engine's response-file lifetime and to `take_step`, and it has to leave every Ninja edge exactly as it is.\n\nNone is urgent and each is the old behaviour rather than a new defect. Whoever takes them should take the long line first: it is the one an ordinary GNU makefile can reach without a kati extension, and it degrades a whole recipe rather than one line of it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-plain-dash-word-becomes-a-goal
                title "Ignore a plain dash argument the way GNU Make does instead of making a goal of it"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:45:55.61168804Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-an-empty-argument-word-stops-the-build — the same GNU function guards both, so the probe that measured one measured this too. Left out of that node because the empty word is a build GNU RUNS and Ronin abandons, and this is a build both run with a different goal list.\n\n`handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c) opens with\n\n    if (arg[0] == '-' && arg[1] == '\\0')\n      /* Ignore plain '-' for compatibility.  */\n      return;\n\nso a lone `-` is neither a goal nor an assignment. Ronin's `parse_arguments` routes `argument == b\"-\"` to `classify_word` explicitly — the word does not start an option cluster — and `classify_word` has no `=` in it, so it becomes a goal.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make over `all: ; @printf 'goals=[%s]\\n' '$(MAKECMDGOALS)' > out`, GNU on the left:\n\n    make -        exit 0, `out` holds `goals=[]`   / `ronin: '-' missing and no known rule to make it`, nothing built\n    make -- -     the same, both tools the same way\n\nSo it reaches build intent in the same direction the empty word did: GNU builds the default goal, Ronin abandons with exit 2. It is narrower — a wrapper hands Make an empty word by accident far more often than a bare dash — which is why it is filed rather than folded in.\n\nThe fix is the same site and one more clause: `classify_word` returning early for `-` as it now does for the empty word, with the `--`-terminated path covered because both spellings reach the same function in GNU (main.c's second `while (optind < argc)` loop calls the same handler).\n\nWatch for one thing before writing it: `-` already means something to `-I` — make-include-directories records that \"a bare dash forgets accumulated directories\" — so the clause belongs where a word has already been ruled not to be a switch, not anywhere earlier.\n\nGate it on effects through the port harness: the default goal's file present and exit 0 with `-` among the arguments, and `MAKECMDGOALS` empty."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:49:46.058413361Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:59:35.663557778Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing needed no correction at all — including its warning, which was the part worth checking. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE CELLS, re-measured before writing anything, over `all: ; @printf 'built=[all] goals=[%s]' '$(MAKECMDGOALS)' > out` with a `second` rule beside it:\n\n    make -           exit 0, `out` holds `built=[all] goals=[]`  /  `ronin: '-' missing and no known rule to make it`, exit 2, nothing built\n    make -- -        the same, both tools the same way\n    make - second    exit 0, `built=[second] goals=[second]`     /  the same refusal\n    make second -    the same                                    /  the same refusal\n    make -I -        both tools build the default goal           /  agrees, and did before\n\nGNU's reason is the opening lines of `handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c), before any test of anything:\n\n    if (arg[0] == '-' && arg[1] == '\\0')\n      /* Ignore plain '-' for compatibility.  */\n      return;\n\nTHE FIX, one clause at the site the sibling node opened. `classify_word` in src/make/cli.rs returns on a lone `-` as it already returns on a zero-length word. `parse_arguments` routes `argument == b\"-\"` there explicitly — the word does not start an option cluster — and `--` only changes which words reach the same function, which is why the terminated spelling is covered by the same clause rather than a second one. GNU's own second `while (optind < argc)` loop calls the same handler, so the two tools cover it the same way for the same reason.\n\nTHE WARNING WAS RIGHT AND IS WHY THE CLAUSE SITS WHERE IT DOES. A bare `-` means something to `-I` — make-include-directories records that it forgets the directories accumulated before it — so a clause placed any earlier would have eaten the switch's argument. It sits where a word has already been ruled neither a switch nor a switch's argument, and `make -I one -I - -I two` still leaves `two` alone in the search path. Measured through the oracle as well as in the unit test: `-I -` builds the default goal in both tools, before and after.\n\nSEVEN SHAPES MEASURED, all now identical on exit code and on the file the build wrote: `-`, `-- -`, `- second`, `second -`, `- - second`, `-I -`, `-k -`. One more beside them, because a dash can arrive without an argv: `MAKEFLAGS=' -- -'` builds the default goal in both tools. GNU passes MAKEFLAGS words through the same handler, so the same clause covers it, and it was worth measuring rather than reasoning about.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-plain-dash-command-line-word-leaves-the-default-goal-standing` — args `-`, gating `built=[all] goals=[]` with a `second` target that must not run.\n* `tests/make/a-plain-dash-beside-a-goal-is-passed-over` — args `- second -`, the dash on both sides of a named goal, gating `built=[second] goals=[second]` and the default goal left alone.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the clause taken out and everything else in place, both fail with \\\"build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\\\" — Ronin refuses over `-` as a missing file at exit 2 and writes nothing. Unit test `a_plain_dash_is_passed_over` in src/make/cli.rs covers the alone, surrounded and post-`--` spellings at the parse, and the `-I` case in the same test is what stops a later reader moving the clause earlier.\n\nWHERE, src/make/cli.rs alone. No kati change, and no submodule pin moves for this node.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed — 903 plus the new unit test; make_port 4/4 over 871 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed, unchanged because the new test is behind the `make` feature."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:59:40.397987871Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-worked-out-names-value-is-its-own-name
                title "Hand back the live list for the value of a name Make works out, not the name itself"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:09.294936689Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, and left out of it because that node is about the origin ladder — where a binding stands and what may replace it — and this is about what `$(value)` hands back once it stands in the right place.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left:\n\n    $(firstword $(value .VARIABLES))    a real name from the table  /  `.VARIABLES`\n\n`$(value)` reads the variable's stored text without expanding it, and GNU Make's `.VARIABLES` is a SIMPLE variable whose value is the list `lookup_special_var` rebuilt — so `$(value .VARIABLES)` is the list, the same bytes `$(.VARIABLES)` gives, and it holds the `D`/`F` automatic forms exactly as the expansion does.\n\nRonin answers `.VARIABLES` — the name — because `Variable::string` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) maps `InnerVar::VariableNames { name, .. }` to `Cow::Borrowed(name)`. That is the branch `$(value)`, `$(origin)`'s neighbours and every unexpanded-text reader go through. `.KATI_SYMBOLS` answers its own name the same way.\n\nThe fix is to build the list there as `Variable::eval`'s `InnerVar::VariableNames` arm does, which means lifting that loop into a helper both call — it needs `&Session` for `global_var_names` and `peek_global_var`, and `string` already takes one. Watch the `all` flag: `.KATI_SYMBOLS` filters out the function-valued names and `.VARIABLES` does not, and the base automatic variables are excluded from both as of the sibling node.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(words $(value .VARIABLES))` agreeing with `$(words $(.VARIABLES))`, and a name defined in the makefile appearing in the `$(value)` answer. Do not gate the list verbatim — it holds the environment."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:20:02.438175848Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:31:17.335736073Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right on every point including the shape of the repair. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE CELL, re-measured before touching anything:\n\n    $(firstword $(value .VARIABLES))    a real name from the table  /  `.VARIABLES`\n\nand the three that say what the answer has to be, all now identical in both tools: a name the makefile defined appears in `$(value .VARIABLES)`; `$(words $(value .VARIABLES))` equals `$(words $(.VARIABLES))`; and neither of the two answers holds a word the other does not.\n\nWHY THE VALUE IS THE LIST, which is the part worth writing down. `$(value)` reads a variable's stored text without expanding it, and GNU Make's `.VARIABLES` is a SIMPLE variable whose stored text `lookup_special_var` rebuilds whenever the table has changed since it last did. So the text and the expansion are the same bytes BY CONSTRUCTION over there — there is no second code path that could disagree, and no other answer `$(value)` could give. Here they were two code paths, which is exactly how they came to disagree: the list was built in `Variable`'s `Evaluable::eval` arm and nowhere else, and `Variable::string` — the branch `$(value)` and every other unexpanded-text reader goes through — returned a name the `InnerVar::VariableNames` variant carried for that one purpose.\n\nTHE FIX is the filing's: lift the loop into `write_variable_names` and call it from both. `&Session` is all the loop ever needed — `global_var_names`, `peek_global_var` and `symtab` — and `string` already takes one, so nothing had to be threaded anywhere. The variant's `name` field went with it, because after the change nothing reads it and the two definition sites already say which name they are (`Symbol::VARIABLES` and `Symbol::KATI_SYMBOLS`).\n\nSharing the loop rather than copying it is the point: it is what stops the two answers drifting apart a second time, and it carries the two rules the filing warned about for free rather than by remembering them. `all` is the whole of what separates the two names — `.KATI_SYMBOLS` leaves out the function-valued bindings and `.VARIABLES` does not — and both leave out the BASE automatic variables, which is the split the sibling node established against GNU: `$@` and its siblings are bound in the FILE's own variable set by `set_file_variables`, and this walks the global table.\n\n`.KATI_SYMBOLS` is repaired by the same construction and is NOT gated by a fixture, because there is no oracle answer to compare against — GNU has no such name. It is kati's own extension and it stands, which is the judgement already recorded across the conformance corpus's kati-extension family. Measured by hand instead: `$(value .KATI_SYMBOLS)` and `$(.KATI_SYMBOLS)` now hold the same words.\n\nFIXTURE, one, oracle-recorded, branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-value-of-a-worked-out-name-is-the-list-it-answers` — `ifeq ($(filter MY_OWN_NAME,$(value .VARIABLES)),MY_OWN_NAME)` chooses the goal, gating `mine=[MY_OWN_NAME] words=[same] difference=[]`.\n\nEverything it gates is environment-independent although the list is not: each cell compares two answers the SAME tool gave in the SAME run, so what the environment holds cancels. The list is never gated verbatim, in either direction — the filing said so and it is right, and it is the reason the case is written as a comparison rather than as a transcript.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recording in place, the case fails by building the other target, and the file it leaves says exactly what the defect was — `value=[.VARIABLES]`.\n\nWHERE, kati ae4bd58c8ae2e672cc60ebd5850a71b98e76b411 on branch nplan/make-a-worked-out-names-value-is-its-own-name. No Ronin-side change beyond the one fixture.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 867 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:31:21.569690838Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing
                title "Define the four names GNU works out for itself that Ronin leaves undefined"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:30.229063049Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin. That node made the `.VARIABLES` list agree with GNU's about the automatic variables; what it did not do — and could not, because it is a different mechanism — is add the names Ronin never defines at all. They show up as the whole of one side of the list diff and each is also its own `$(origin)` divergence.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make in a clean environment, diffing `$(sort $(.VARIABLES))` at read time and again inside a recipe. Identical both times. GNU has and Ronin has not:\n\n    .INCLUDE_DIRS   origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    .LOADED         origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    MAKE_COMMAND    origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    MAKE_HOST       origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n\n`MAKE_HOST` is the configure triplet — `x86_64-pc-linux-gnu` on this host, and the oracle provenance already records it under that name, so what Ronin should answer is a decision to take rather than a value to copy. `MAKE_COMMAND` is the invoked path, which Ronin already works out for `$(MAKE)`: `make_named_invocation` in src/make/cli.rs produces exactly it, and GNU's `MAKE` is `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` with the switches appended. `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains. `.LOADED` is the `load`ed object list and is empty here; the oracle provenance records `default .LOADED` with an empty value.\n\nSo three of the four are values Ronin already has and does not publish, and the fourth is a value to decide. None of them is a graph question, which is why this is a small node and not a risky one.\n\nGate it on effects: a recipe writing `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` for all four, plus `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` being the same word `$(MAKE)` starts with, plus `.INCLUDE_DIRS` naming a directory a `-I` put there."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:34:10.998139354Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:49:02.600310554Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about three of the four and wrong about the fourth in a way that turned into its own node. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE re-measured and confirmed before anything was written — all four `default`/`simple` there and undefined here — and a fifth cell found in the same probe that the filing named without listing: `$(value MAKE)` is `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` there and was the path itself here. GNU's `MAKE` is a REFERENCE, not a copy, which is what makes a makefile's write to MAKE_COMMAND change the program `$(MAKE)` runs.\n\nTHREE OF THE FOUR were values this evaluator already had and did not publish, exactly as the filing said.\n\n* `.LOADED` — empty at default rank, and empty for good rather than for now: `load` is not a directive this parser knows, so no object can join the list. GNU's is empty on every makefile in the corpus for the same reason its own is empty on one that loads nothing.\n* `MAKE_COMMAND` — `session.flags.subkati_args`, which is what `make_named_invocation` in src/make/cli.rs already worked out and what the bootstrap's `MAKE?=` line already carried. The line now carries `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` instead, which is GNU's `define_variable_cname (\"MAKE\", \"$(MAKE_COMMAND)\", o_default, 1)` said the same way — and it takes the path out of makefile text, where a `#` or a `$` in it would have been read as syntax. That was a latent defect nothing had reached.\n* `.INCLUDE_DIRS` — the `-I` list, in the spelling `construct_include_path` (read.c) records rather than the spelling the switch was given: every directory stat'ed, the ones that are not there left out, trailing slashes discarded. Both rules measured against the oracle — `-I nosuchdir` leaves the list unchanged and `-I inc/` joins it as `inc`.\n\nMAKE_HOST WAS THE DECISION, and it is recorded on `make_host` in kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs rather than only here. GNU bakes its configure triple, so there is nothing to copy. What a makefile does with the name is `$(findstring mingw32,...)`, `cygwin`, `darwin`, `linux` — it is asking what platform it is ON. So Ronin answers the host the RUN is on, assembled from the target this build was made for, with the vendor field spelled the way `config.guess` spells it: `apple` on Apple systems, `pc` for the x86 family elsewhere, `unknown` otherwise. On this host that produces `x86_64-pc-linux-gnu`, which is the oracle's own answer character for character. It was not written to match it — the rule was taken from config.guess first and the agreement checked afterwards.\n\nTHE FILING'S ONE ERROR, and it became a node. It said `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is \\\"the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains\\\". GNU's list is the `-I` directories FOLLOWED BY a built-in default search path — `/usr/local/include /usr/include` on this host, from `default_include_directories` in read.c — and this evaluator has no such path and does not search there. So `.INCLUDE_DIRS` here holds what `-I` put there and no more, which is an honest answer about a search that really does stop at the `-I` list, and the gap is filed as make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing. It is a build question rather than a variable one: `include foo.mk` with the file only in `/usr/local/include` is one GNU finds and this does not. Every other `-I` cell agrees, including the two where `-I -` turns the list off, which was worth measuring because GNU's `-I -` disables the defaults permanently rather than merely restarting the list.\n\nNINE INVOCATIONS MEASURED: bare, `-R`, `-I inc`, `-I inc -I inc2`, `-I nosuchdir`, `-I inc/`, `-I -`, `-I inc -I -`, `-I - -I inc`. `-R` is in the list because it withholds the tool catalogue and these four are not in it — GNU keeps all four under `-R` and so does Ronin now.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-four-names-make-works-out-for-itself-are-there` — `ifeq ($(origin MAKE_COMMAND),undefined)` chooses the goal, gating `inc=[default,simple] loaded=[default,simple,] cmd=[default,simple] host=[default,simple] make=[default,recursive,$(MAKE_COMMAND)] same=[same]`. The two values that legitimately differ between the tools — and between any two hosts — are deliberately not compared; `same=[same]` compares `$(MAKE)` with `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` instead, which is what makes the reference a reference.\n* `tests/make/a-search-directory-joins-the-list-make-works-out` — args `-I inc -I nosuchdir -I inc2/`, branching on `$(filter inc,$(.INCLUDE_DIRS))`, gating `named=[inc] absent=[] stripped=[inc2] as-written=[]`. Every cell is a `filter` over the list rather than the list itself, so the case says the same thing after the default search path lands.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, both fail by building the OTHER target — `branch=[missing]` and `branch=[absent]`.\n\nINVENTORY MOVED AND AUDITED. Three rows, all in `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS`, from `unclassified`/evaluation to `narration`/ninja-progress: `.diff.1` is upstream's test #2 (`-I$dir` must be in the list), `.diff.4` is `-I somedir`, `.diff.5` is `-I - -I somedir`. All three now agree on everything but the progress line. `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS.diff` — test #1, whose whole content is `ifeq ($(.INCLUDE_DIRS),) $(warning .INCLUDE_DIRS is empty)` — is still unclassified, and it is precisely the default-search-path node's row. Counts moved 151→148 unclassified and 1111→1114 narration over the same 1299 rows; compiler stays ZERO. The tsv is re-recorded with `--update` because the movement is understood and is in the improving direction.\n\nWHERE, kati 8b096c5752756d330945f71034d10a242ef11caa on branch nplan/make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing. No Ronin-side change beyond the two fixtures and the inventory record.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 869 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, three rows audited above; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:49:08.529047756Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them
                title "Define MAKECMDGOALS and MAKEOVERRIDES only when GNU does, not always"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:52.267039419Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, and the remaining half of that node's list divergence once the automatic variables were taken out of it. It is a question about WHEN a name comes into existence rather than about where it stands in the ladder, which is why it is filed apart.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, three invocations of the same makefile:\n\n    make            MAKECMDGOALS undefined      / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined     / default, recursive\n    make all        MAKECMDGOALS default,simple / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined     / default, recursive\n    make FOO=1      MAKECMDGOALS undefined      / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES default,recursive, value `FOO=1` / default, recursive, value `FOO=1`\n\nGNU defines `MAKECMDGOALS` inside `handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c), on the branch that enters a goal target — so with no goal word there is no name. `MAKEOVERRIDES` is defined by `define_makeflags` only when `command_variables` is non-empty, and its value is `${-*-command-variables-*-}`, which is why it reads back `recursive` while `MAKECMDGOALS` reads back `simple`.\n\nIt reaches real makefiles: `ifdef MAKECMDGOALS` and `ifeq ($(origin MAKEOVERRIDES),undefined)` are both idioms for \"was I invoked bare\", and under Ronin both answer the same whatever the command line said.\n\nThe values already agree — this is only about the name existing and about the flavour it reads back with. Where: the two are defined unconditionally in Ronin's session setup (`record_invocation` / `compiler_flag_variables` in src/make/cli.rs feed them). Making them conditional means not defining them rather than defining them empty, since `$(origin)` distinguishes the two.\n\nBeware one interaction: `MAKEOVERRIDES` is also what a recursive child reads to inherit command-line variables, so withdrawing the name when there are none must not withdraw the mechanism. Probe a recursive child with and without a command-line variable before and after.\n\nGate it on effects through the port harness's `args` sidecar: three cases, or one case whose recipe writes `$(origin MAKECMDGOALS)` and `$(origin MAKEOVERRIDES)` with a goal and a variable among the arguments and one without."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T05:43:07.015470443Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:00:40.89578292Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and one thing the filing predicted is not true — which changes which idiom this repairs. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE FILING'S CELLS ALL RE-MEASURED AND ALL CONFIRMED, four invocations of one makefile:\n\n    make            MAKECMDGOALS  undefined / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined / default,recursive\n    make all        MAKECMDGOALS  default,simple / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined / default,recursive\n    make FOO=1      MAKECMDGOALS  undefined / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES default,recursive `FOO=1` / agrees\n    make all FOO=1  both defined, both tools\n\nand a third name in the same block, probed because GNU's guard covers it too: `-*-command-variables-*-` is `undefined` there and `automatic` here whenever nothing overrode anything. It is askable — `$(origin -*-command-variables-*-)` — and it is in `.VARIABLES`, so it is a cell like the other two.\n\nWHAT IS NOT TRUE. The filing says `ifdef MAKECMDGOALS` \"cannot work under Ronin\". It always worked. `ifdef` asks whether the value is NON-EMPTY, not whether the name exists — measured: `EMPTY :=` followed by `ifdef EMPTY` takes the false branch under GNU. Both names were defined EMPTY here, so `ifdef` answered exactly as GNU's does in every one of the four invocations. The guard that genuinely could not work is the other one the filing names: `ifeq ($(origin MAKEOVERRIDES),undefined)`, and its sibling over MAKECMDGOALS. `$(origin)` is the only question that can tell an empty binding from an absent one, which is why the fixtures branch the build on `$(origin)` and not on `ifdef` — an `ifdef` fixture would have had no teeth at all.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, both halves at their own site. `read_bootstrap_makefile` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) emitted `MAKECMDGOALS?=` unconditionally; it now emits the line only when the target list is non-empty, which is GNU defining the name from inside the goal branch of `handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c) rather than up front. `install_compiler_invocation_variables` defined `-*-command-variables-*-` and `MAKEOVERRIDES` unconditionally; the pair is now inside `if !make_overrides.is_empty()`, which is GNU's `if (command_variables != 0)` in `define_makeflags`.\n\nTHE INTERACTION THE FILING WARNED ABOUT, probed rather than reasoned about. Withdrawing the name must not withdraw the inheritance. It does not, and the reason is that the table Ronin gates on is filled the same way GNU's is: an assignment arriving in an inherited `MAKEFLAGS` reaches `invocation.variables` exactly as an argv one does, so `make_overrides` is non-empty in a child of `make FOO=1` and the pair is defined there. Measured over a recursive `$(MAKE) -C sub` in three invocations — bare, `FOO=1`, and a goal word — and the child agrees with GNU's child on all six cells including `MAKEFLAGS`. The env-only case is measured too and is where Ronin used to be wrong in the other direction: `MAKEOVERRIDES=BAR=2 make` with no MAKEFLAGS gives `-*-command-variables-*-` `undefined` under GNU and gave `automatic` here; it now agrees, and `MAKEFLAGS` still reads ` -- BAR=2` because the reference resolves to the environment's own binding.\n\nThe `?=` spelling is left alone deliberately: it is what makes an environment `MAKECMDGOALS` outrank the bootstrap, which is the same answer GNU's `o_default` define gives — `MAKECMDGOALS=zzz make all` reads back `environment`/`zzz` under both tools, GNU having computed `zzz all` and then been refused by origin precedence.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each branching the BUILD on the cell rather than only printing it, so the teeth are a different target running:\n\n* `tests/make/a-bare-invocation-works-out-neither-of-the-two-names` — no args; `ifeq ($(origin MAKECMDGOALS),undefined)` chooses the goal, gating `branch=[bare] goals=[undefined] over=[undefined] hidden=[undefined]`.\n* `tests/make/a-goal-word-works-out-the-goals-name-alone` — args `deliver`; branches on `$(origin MAKEOVERRIDES)`, gating `branch=[none] goals=[default] value=[deliver] hidden=[undefined]`.\n* `tests/make/a-command-line-write-works-out-the-overrides-name-alone` — args `FOO=1`; branches on `$(origin MAKECMDGOALS)`, gating `branch=[none] over=[default] value=[FOO=1] hidden=[automatic] table=[FOO=1] foo=[1]`.\n\nThe two crossing cases are the ones that matter: an implementation gating both names on \"any non-switch word arrived\" would pass a bare case and a both-given case and fail these.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, all three fail, and each fails by building the OTHER target — `branch=[goal]`, `branch=[some]`, `branch=[some]`.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT AND WHOSE IT IS. `$(flavor MAKECMDGOALS)` still reads `recursive` where GNU says `simple`, because the bootstrap's `?=` makes a recursive variable. That cell is make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour's and is deliberately not gated here — this node's fixtures ask `$(origin)` only, so neither recording moves when that node lands.\n\nWHERE, kati b2dde7d72e75349a4ecc20c90fd1d5c692c93f18 on branch nplan/make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them. No Ronin-side change beyond the three fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 864 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 32s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:00:45.454570974Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour
                title "Give the built-in names the flavour and origin GNU defines them with"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:39:15.399431148Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, which fixed `.VARIABLES` and `.KATI_SYMBOLS`. This is the same question asked of the names both tools DO define, and the answers were surveyed in the same pass rather than guessed.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with `make FOO=1 all`, GNU on the left, every built-in name that disagreed:\n\n    CURDIR          file, simple        / default, simple\n    MAKE_VERSION    default, simple     / default, recursive\n    MAKELEVEL       environment, simple / environment, recursive\n    MAKECMDGOALS    default, simple     / default, recursive\n    MAKEOVERRIDES   default, recursive  / default, recursive   (agrees)\n\nNames checked and already agreeing, so the survey is not open-ended: `.FEATURES`, `.RECIPEPREFIX`, `.DEFAULT_GOAL`, `MAKEFILE_LIST`, `MAKEFLAGS`, `MAKE`, `SUFFIXES`, `.SHELLSTATUS`, `MAKE_RESTARTS`, `MAKE_TERMOUT`, `MAKE_TERMERR`.\n\nTWO MECHANISMS. `CURDIR` is GNU's `o_file` — `define_variable_cname (\"CURDIR\", current_directory, o_file, 0)` in main.c, deliberately at file origin so a makefile's own `CURDIR = x` is a peer rather than an override. Ronin has it at `default`, so a makefile assignment behaves the same but `$(origin CURDIR)` — which is what a makefile checks before deciding to trust it — does not.\n\nThe flavour cells are one mechanism: Ronin defines these as recursive variables holding text, where GNU defines them simple. `$(flavor)` sees the difference and so does `$(value)`: a recursive `MAKE_VERSION` hands back its text, and for these names the text and the value are the same string, so nothing but `$(flavor)` and `$(value)` can tell. Both are makefile-visible.\n\n`MAKECMDGOALS`'s row here is about flavour only; whether it should exist at all is make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them.\n\nWhere: `GlobalVars::with_builtins` in kati/src-rs/var.rs for the catalogue, and src/make/cli.rs's `record_invocation` for the invocation-derived ones. Both build with `Variable::with_simple_string` or the recursive constructor, so this is choosing the right one per name.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` for the four names, plus a `CURDIR = x` line proving the assignment still lands the way it did."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:04:45.474891332Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:19:25.910510918Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Every cell in the filing's table re-measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and every one confirmed, and the survey was widened past the names the filing listed — which found one more.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE, re-probed with `make FOO=1 all`, GNU on the left, all four now agreeing:\n\n    CURDIR          file, simple        / was default, simple\n    MAKE_VERSION    default, simple     / was default, recursive\n    MAKELEVEL       environment, simple / was environment, recursive\n    MAKECMDGOALS    default, simple     / was default, recursive\n    MAKEOVERRIDES   default, recursive  / agrees, and still does\n\nONE MECHANISM, not two, and the filing was right that it was one: three of the four were bootstrap MAKEFILE LINES, and a makefile line cannot say what GNU says here. `?=` builds a recursive variable and every bootstrap assignment lands at default rank, so neither the flavour nor CURDIR's file rank was expressible. GNU says both in the same call — `define_variable_cname (name, value, origin, 0)`, and the trailing zero IS the recursive flag. So the fix is to stop writing them as makefile text: `install_worked_out_variables` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) now makes the calls, and `read_bootstrap_makefile` is three lines shorter and no longer takes the goal list.\n\nEach name goes through the path its own GNU call implies rather than through one shared path:\n\n* MAKE_VERSION and MAKECMDGOALS through `claim_at_default`, which is `crate::builtins::claimable` — the same test `define_variable_cname` applies with `o_default`: another default may be replaced, an environment or command-line or makefile binding may not.\n* CURDIR through the ordinary ladder, `Session::set_global_var` with `VarOrigin::File`. That is not a detail: kati's `assign` declines a File write over CommandLine, Override and EnvironmentOverride and allows it over Environment, which is exactly GNU's `o_file` rank, so `-e` and a command-line write stay above it without a special case.\n* MAKELEVEL simple as the environment is imported, which is GNU defining it a second time in `define_automatic_variables` once the environment is in scope.\n\nNINE INVOCATIONS MEASURED, not four, because the ladder either side of a rank change is where a rank change goes wrong. All nine now identical: clean; an environment CURDIR (overridden by both, value becomes the real cwd); an environment MAKE_VERSION (kept by both at `environment`/`recursive`); an environment MAKELEVEL (value kept, `environment`/`simple`); a makefile's own `CURDIR = x` and `MAKE_VERSION = 9.9` (both `file`/`recursive` in both tools); a command-line `CURDIR=cl` (`command line` in both); `-R`; `-e` over an environment CURDIR; `-e` over an environment MAKELEVEL.\n\nThat last one moved during the work and is worth recording. MAKELEVEL under `-e` is `environment override` in GNU and was `environment` here, and it is a THIRD mechanism rather than the promotion the other names need: `define_variable_in_set` lifts an INCOMING `o_env` define to `o_env_override` when `-e` is in force, and only a name Make defines over again is ever incoming. kati says that at the import — the origin the constructor is handed — rather than by defining twice. Both tools now agree, including when MAKELEVEL is absent from the environment entirely.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FOUND BY THE SAME WIDENED SURVEY AND FILED, not fixed, each because it is a whole-surface question rather than these four names':\n\n* make-the-e-rank-does-not-show-on-a-name-make-defines-over — `$(origin CC)` under `-e` with `CC` in the environment is `environment override` there and `environment` here, and so is `$(origin MAKE_VERSION)`. `claimable` reads the binding's rank without promoting it; GNU promotes the binding it is about to define over and THEN declines the write. Some fifty catalogue names have the same cell, so fixing two of them here would have left the surface inconsistent. An ordinary environment name is `environment` under `-e` in both tools — measured, and it is what makes the eager reading of `-e` wrong.\n* make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour — `$(origin SHELL)` is `file` there and `default` here. GNU's answer depends on whether the environment had a SHELL, because the o_default define is declined and a following block rewrites rank and value while leaving the import's recursive flag alone. Two answers from one site, plus the NoExport/`shell_var` interactions, is its own node.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both branching the BUILD on the cell rather than only printing it:\n\n* `tests/make/the-names-make-works-out-for-itself-are-simple` — args `deliver`; `ifeq ($(flavor MAKE_VERSION),simple)` chooses the goal, gating `version=[default,simple] level=[environment,simple] goals=[default,simple] curdir=[file,simple]`.\n* `tests/make/a-makefile-writing-the-directory-name-is-its-peer` — `ifeq ($(origin CURDIR),file)` chooses the goal, and the makefile then writes `CURDIR = written`, gating `before=[file] after=[file recursive written]` — the rank before, and the ladder cell the filing asked for, in one line.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, both fail, and each fails by building the OTHER target — `branch=[recursive]` and `branch=[elsewhere] before=[default]`.\n\nWHERE, kati 389234bb8178264fa0aa2d6107497bd1426d56dd on branch nplan/make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour. No Ronin-side change beyond the two fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 866 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:19:29.847920174Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-e-rank-does-not-show-on-a-name-make-defines-over
                title "Promote the environment's rank on a name Make defines over, as -e makes GNU do"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:18:28.038795453Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour, and left out of it because it is a whole-surface question rather than those four names': the same cell is wrong for every name in the `-R` catalogue.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, `CC=gcc PLAIN=p make -e`:\n\n    $(origin CC)            environment override / environment\n    $(origin MAKE_VERSION)  environment override / environment      (with MAKE_VERSION=9.9 in the environment)\n    $(origin PLAIN)         environment          / environment      (agrees — nothing ever defines over it)\n\nSo the rule is not \"`-e` promotes the environment\". It promotes exactly the names Make itself defines over, and it promotes them whether or not the definition then lands. GNU's `define_variable_in_set` (variable.c) does it in two steps as it is ABOUT to write:\n\n    if (env_overrides && v->origin == o_env)\n      v->origin = o_env_override;\n    if (v->origin <= origin) { ...write... }\n\nThe promotion happens first, the rank comparison then declines the write, and the promoted rank is what `$(origin)` answers for the rest of the run. A name nothing ever defines over — `PLAIN` above — is still `environment`, which is why an eager promotion of the whole environment would be wrong. This was measured rather than assumed, and it is the reason `note_environment_outranks_the_makefile` exists and is called from `Session::set_global_var` alone.\n\nWHERE. `crate::builtins::claimable` (kati/src-rs/builtins.rs) is the \"would `define_variable_cname` with `o_default` write here\" test, and it reads the binding's origin without promoting it. Its callers are `install_default_variables` — the whole tool catalogue, some fifty names — and `claim_at_default` in evaluate.rs, which is MAKE_VERSION and MAKECMDGOALS. Every one of them is a name GNU defines over, so every one should promote. `note_environment_outranks_the_makefile` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) is already the promotion and already does the right thing; it just is not reached from here.\n\nTwo cells to check before writing it, because `claimable` is called in a loop over a table and the promotion has to happen even for entries whose write is declined: an environment `CC` under `-e` keeps `gcc` AND reads `environment override`, and `.POSIX:`'s second pass over the same names must not double-promote into something else.\n\nMAKELEVEL is already right and is a different mechanism, recorded here so the next reader does not try to unify them: GNU lifts the INCOMING `o_env` define to `o_env_override` under `-e` (the first line of the same function), and kati says that at the import instead of defining twice.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin CC)` and `$(origin MAKE_VERSION)` with each name in the environment and `-e` among the arguments, beside an ordinary environment name that must still read `environment`."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour
                title "Stand SHELL where GNU Make stands it, which depends on whether the environment had one"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:18:51.929473019Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour, by widening that node's survey past the names its filing listed. SHELL was not among the eleven the earlier survey checked and agreed on, and it disagrees.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, with SHELL in the environment (which is the ordinary case, and the case the port harness runs in — it removes MAKEFLAGS and MAKELEVEL and nothing else):\n\n    $(origin SHELL)   file    / default\n    $(flavor SHELL)   recursive / recursive   (agrees)\n    $(SHELL)          /bin/sh / /bin/sh       (agrees)\n\nand under `-e` Ronin says `environment override`, which is a third answer neither tool's ladder would give for this name.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is why this is its own node rather than a line beside CURDIR's. GNU's answer is not a constant: it depends on whether the environment had a SHELL at all. `define_automatic_variables` (variable.c) does\n\n    v = define_variable_cname (\"SHELL\", default_shell, o_default, 0);\n    /* Don't let SHELL come from the environment.  */\n    if (*v->value == '\\0' || v->origin == o_env || v->origin == o_env_override)\n      { v->origin = o_file; v->value = xstrdup (default_shell); }\n\nso with no SHELL in the environment the first line lands and the name stands `default` and SIMPLE; with one, the first line is declined, the block rewrites the VALUE and the RANK and leaves the recursive flag the environment import set — so the name stands `file` and RECURSIVE. Two different pairs of answers from one call site. Predicted rather than measured for the no-SHELL case; probe `env -u SHELL make` before writing anything.\n\nRonin says `default` and recursive in both, from the bootstrap line `SHELL=/bin/sh` in `read_bootstrap_makefile` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs), which is a makefile assignment and therefore recursive at bootstrap rank whatever the environment held.\n\nTWO INTERACTIONS TO PROBE FIRST, because SHELL is not an ordinary name. The import already marks it `VarExport::NoExport` — POSIX says a makefile's SHELL must not change the one subprocesses are given — and `crate::eval` reads `shell_var` separately for what actually runs recipes. Changing the rank must not change either. GNU also consults MAKESHELL before any of this on platforms that have it; this host does not, and the branch is `#ifdef`ed out.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin SHELL)` and `$(flavor SHELL)` with SHELL in the environment and again with it removed, plus a recipe line proving the shell that ran it is still `/bin/sh`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:10:47.281457121Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:27:31.016465253Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about both halves of the mechanism — including the half it flagged as predicted rather than measured. `env -u SHELL` was probed before anything was written and GNU answers `default`/SIMPLE there, exactly as the filing predicted from the source.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE re-measured and confirmed, and the survey widened from the four cells it listed to TWENTY invocations, which found two more divergences the filing did not name — one of them a build failure.\n\n    with SHELL in the environment      file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was default, recursive\n    with no SHELL in the environment   default, simple, /bin/sh   / was default, RECURSIVE\n    -e over an environment SHELL       file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was `environment override`, AND THE ENVIRONMENT'S SHELL RAN THE RECIPE\n    -e over an environment SHELL,\n      makefile assigning SHELL         file, recursive, assigned  / was `environment override`, the makefile write declined\n    a command-line `SHELL=` (empty)    file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was `command line`, empty, and the build DIED 127\n\nThe two the filing did not name are the last two, and they are effects rather than cells. `SHELL=/bin/zsh make -e` executed `/bin/zsh -c` where GNU Make executes `/bin/sh -c` — the whole point of GNU's special case is that the environment's shell must not run a recipe, and `-e` was the one door it was still coming through here. `make SHELL=` ran `'' -c` and exited 127 where GNU Make builds.\n\nONE MECHANISM, one call site, two pairs of answers, and the filing quoted it correctly. `define_automatic_variables` (variable.c) offers the built-in shell at default rank and then refuses to let the environment's answer survive:\n\n    v = define_variable_cname (\"SHELL\", default_shell, o_default, 0);\n    /* Don't let SHELL come from the environment.  */\n    if (*v->value == '\\0' || v->origin == o_env || v->origin == o_env_override)\n      { v->origin = o_file; v->value = xstrdup (default_shell); }\n\nWith no SHELL in the environment the offer lands: `default`, and SIMPLE because the trailing zero is the recursive flag. With one, the offer is declined on rank and the block rewrites the RANK and the TEXT while leaving the import's recursive flavour alone: `file`, RECURSIVE, holding `/bin/sh`. Ronin said `default` and recursive in both because it said it as a bootstrap makefile line — `SHELL=/bin/sh` — and no makefile line can say either of those two things.\n\nWHY `-e` IS INVISIBLE HERE, which is the part worth writing down: the promotion `-e` performs on an environment binding is to `o_env_override`, and `o_env_override` is one of the two ranks this block overwrites. So the promotion happens and is then overwritten by `o_file` in the next statement. That is why SHELL is the one environment name `-e` does not lift — and why, at `file` rank, a makefile's own `SHELL =` is a peer that may replace it even under `-e`.\n\nTHE EMPTY TEST IS NOT ABOUT THE ENVIRONMENT AT ALL, which the filing did not say and the probe found: `*v->value == '\\0'` reads whatever binding won the slot, so a command-line `SHELL=` — which outranks everything — stands back at the built-in shell at `file` rank. That is what keeps a recipe running when an empty one arrives.\n\nWHERE. `stand_the_shell` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) is the call site, and `Variable::restate_at` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) is the in-place rewrite the second half needs — defining the name again would be declined on rank and would install the wrong flavour, which is exactly why GNU assigns `v->origin` and `v->value` directly rather than calling `define_variable` twice. The bootstrap line is gone.\n\nPLACED WHERE GNU PLACES IT, and the placement is load-bearing rather than tidy. `decode_switches` enters the command line's variables at main.c:1624 and `define_automatic_variables` runs at main.c:2032, so GNU stands SHELL after the command line and before the first makefile. Ronin applies command-line assignments after the bootstrap, so the call sits after that phase. Two things follow that a call earlier would have got wrong: a command-line `SHELL=` is the binding the empty test sees, and an expansion DURING the command line reads the environment's shell in both tools because neither has stood this one yet — measured, `make 'FOO:=$(shell echo $$BASH_VERSION)'` under `SHELL=/bin/bash` answers `5.2` in both and answered empty here before.\n\nTHE TWO INTERACTIONS THE FILING ASKED TO PROBE FIRST, both probed and both unchanged. The import's `VarExport::NoExport` on SHELL still stands, so a child is given the invocation's own SHELL and not the makefile's — measured with `printenv SHELL` inside the recipe rather than `$$SHELL`, which bash sets for itself and would have confounded the reading. An `export SHELL` directive still overrides that and hands the child `/bin/sh` in both tools. `crate::eval::get_shell` reads the same binding and now reads the right one, which is what took the `-e` recipe back off `/bin/zsh`. MAKESHELL is `#ifdef`ed out on this host and was not probed.\n\nA HARNESS CHANGE WAS NEEDED AND IS THE RONIN-SIDE DELIVERY. `tests/make_port.rs` cases could say their arguments and their setup but not their environment, and this node's answer turns on whether the environment had a name at all. Cases may now write an `env` sidecar — `NAME=value` per line, `-NAME` for a name the run must not carry. Both the recording and the replay go through the same code, so the oracle and Ronin are asked the same question rather than each inheriting the host's.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-shell-the-environment-gave-is-not-the-one-a-recipe-runs` — env `SHELL=./impostor`, args `-e`; `ifeq ($(origin SHELL),file)` chooses the goal, gating `origin=[file] flavor=[recursive] value=[/bin/sh]`. `impostor` is a checked-in executable that touches a file and then execs `/bin/sh \"$@\"`, so a build whose shell came from the environment still succeeds and is caught by what it left behind rather than by a failure that could have had any cause.\n* `tests/make/a-shell-the-environment-did-not-carry-is-simple` — env `-SHELL`; `ifeq ($(flavor SHELL),simple)` chooses the goal, gating `origin=[default] flavor=[simple] value=[/bin/sh]`.\n* `tests/make/an-empty-shell-stands-back-at-the-built-in-one` — args `SHELL=`, env `SHELL=/bin/sh` so the ambient environment cannot decide it; gating `origin=[file] value=[/bin/sh]`, and the recipe running at all is half the assertion.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the bootstrap line put back and `stand_the_shell` unreached, all three fail. Two fail by building the OTHER target — `branch=[recursive]` and `branch=[environment override]` — and the third fails on the outcome, `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`. The first also leaves `shell-came-from-the-environment` in the run directory, which is the impostor having run the recipe.\n\nWHERE, kati a490e27973e3c90623d79622d2b25f3bd8a73e9b on branch nplan/make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 874 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing
                title "Search the built-in include directories an -I did not name, as GNU Make does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:46:07.756828586Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing, and it CORRECTS that node's filing. The filing said `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is \\\"the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains\\\". It is not: GNU Make's list is the `-I` directories FOLLOWED BY a built-in default search path, and this evaluator has no such path at all.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, after that node landed:\n\n    make                 /usr/local/include /usr/include        / (empty)\n    make -I inc          inc /usr/local/include /usr/include    / inc\n    make -I -            (empty)                                / (empty)          agrees\n    make -I inc -I -     (empty)                                / (empty)          agrees\n    make -I - -I inc     inc                                    / inc              agrees\n\nSo the divergence is exactly the default path and nothing else — every `-I` cell already agrees, including the two that turn the defaults off.\n\nIt is a BUILD question and not only a variable one, which is why it was filed rather than folded in: `include foo.mk` with `foo.mk` in `/usr/local/include` and not in the working directory is a file GNU Make finds and this one does not. `at_include_dirs` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) searches `flags.include_dirs` and stops; with nothing in it, the name is returned unchanged and the include fails.\n\nWHAT THE PATH IS. `default_include_directories` (read.c:103) is the configure-time `INCLUDEDIR`, then `/usr/gnu/include`, `/usr/local/include`, `/usr/include`. `construct_include_path` stats each and keeps the ones that are there, which is why the oracle answers with two of the four on this host. Ronin has no configure step, so `INCLUDEDIR` is a decision like `MAKE_HOST` was — probably the same three GNU ships without it.\n\nTWO RULES TO CARRY, both already measured against the oracle and both currently invisible because the list is empty:\n\n* `-I -` does not merely restart the list, it DISABLES the defaults for the rest of the run — `disable = 1` in `construct_include_path`, never cleared. `make -I - -I inc` gives `inc` alone and not `inc` plus the defaults. Ronin's `include_dir` (src/make/cli.rs) clears the list on `-` and would grow the defaults back at the end unless the switch sets a flag of its own.\n* A `-I ~/x` is tilde-expanded before it is stat'ed (`tilde_expand`, read.c). Ronin's `path_of` does not expand it, which is a second small divergence at the same site and belongs here rather than in a node of its own.\n\nTHE INVENTORY ROW THAT PROVES IT. `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS.diff` is upstream's test #1 and its whole content is `ifeq ($(.INCLUDE_DIRS),) $(warning .INCLUDE_DIRS is empty)`. It is the one row of that case still unclassified after the sibling node moved the other three to narration, and it will move when this lands.\n\nGate it on effects, not on the variable: an `include` of a file that exists only under a default directory, reaching a rule the build then runs. `/usr/include` cannot be written to from a test, so the case needs the `INCLUDEDIR` decision to be something a fixture can create, or the effect gated through `-I` plus the `-I -` disable rule instead."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-08T03:10:23.934566535Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Five nodes worked in parallel in isolated worktrees on 2026-08-08 and merged as one wave: make-second-expansion-patterns, make-option-output-sync, make-option-touch-and-what-if, make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent, make-option-debug-and-trace + make-option-shuffle.\n\nThe merged classification, measured after the merge rather than summed from the lanes — each lane measured against the same 767-semantic baseline, so their movements overlap and adding them would be wrong:\n\n                 before   after\n  capability       126      54\n  option-refused    93      20\n  narration        439     522\n  semantic         767     772\n  counted        1,332   1,348\n\nSemantic is up five and that is the honest number, but it is not the reading. 72 capability refusals were removed, which makes previously uncountable cases countable and lands most of them in semantic; against that, second expansion moved 65 cases from semantic to narration. `no-rule-to-make` fell 105 → 45 and `unsupported-feature` to 35, both from second expansion.\n\nThe real-project gate on a quiet host: Ronin 21s against GNU Make's 20s on Ninja's own CMake-generated Makefile at -j8. It was 64.4s against 22.9s on 2026-08-07.\n\nThree lanes found and fixed defects outside their own node, which is the argument for running them concurrently rather than serially: `|` did not end the word it fell in, so `%.o: %.c|bar.h` silently fell through to the built-in cc rule; `-O` arriving in an inherited MAKEFLAGS was read letter by letter and silently turned on -e and -r; and `-t` was undone by the next build, because an output with no build-log entry is dirty here.\n\nThree lanes also found the recorded defect on their node incomplete or wrong and said so. make-second-expansion-patterns named one of five causes, and the largest — that a prerequisite list should be deferred only when it still holds a `$`, as GNU's read.c does — was not among them.\n\nThe cost was contention on src/make/cli.rs, which four lanes edited. It ended three functions over the density gate; the extraction into src/make/report.rs (ea942c5) cleared it. One wave of this size is about the limit for one shared file.\n\nHandoffs recorded by the lanes and not yet noded: `$(MAKEFLAGS)` cannot report this build's own budget, because the fifo does not exist until after evaluation; `announcing()` implements only the -C half of GNU's should_print_dir, missing `makelevel > 0`; the directory announcement carries no `[N]` level; kati marks a recipe recursive only for a leading `+` and not for `$(MAKE)`; `*** No targets.` fires when only pattern rules exist even with a goal named; `--debug=i`/`v` narrate the dependency search and need kati; and SinkEdge::loc is now read, which recipe-error-line (78 cases) wants next."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-exit-status-parity
        title "Exit with the status Ninja exits with when a command fails"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:25:12.172102097Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Opened from the dangling referral on ronin-command-launcher's 2026-08-04 note: Ronin exits 1 where Ninja propagates the failing command's exit status. The claim about Ninja's behaviour must be verified against real Ninja before matching it — the oracle is Ninja, not the note. The failure-surface conformance scenarios from ronin-failure-surface-parity are the natural place for exit-status assertions."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:09.775678397Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Exit-status parity: the referral was stale, and the real defect was the interrupt.\n\nThe provenance claim — ronin-command-launcher's \"Ronin exits 1 when a command fails where Ninja exits with the command's own status\" — is REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT. It was true when written on 2026-08-03 and fixed hours later by ronin-failure-surface-parity, whose own note opens its defect list with it. The referral was never retracted, so it outlived its subject by six days. Pinned Ninja b51a1e3 does propagate the status (subprocess-posix.cc ParseExitStatus returns WEXITSTATUS transparently; build_result.h carries it out through BuildResult::exit_status), and Ronin already did the same before this node: `exit 7` was 7 on both sides, and compare_build_case had been comparing rc all along.\n\nProbing 66 shapes found three real divergences, none of them the one referred. All three are fixed.\n\nThe first was predicted, in writing, by ronin-failure-surface-parity: \"Ninja returns immediately when it cannot finish a command, where Ronin counts it as a keep-going failure and carries on. I could not produce a case where the two differ — the obvious one, a missing depfile under -k 0, behaves identically in both — so I did not restructure the scheduler on a hypothesis.\" The case exists; it is an interrupt rather than a finish failure. That note's discipline is why this one found it.\n\nPROBE TABLE (ninja / ronin, both after the fix; \"was\" is what Ronin did before it)\n\n  Direct command status\n    exit 0 (control)                      0 / 0\n    exit 1                                1 / 1\n    exit 3                                3 / 3\n    exit 111                          111 / 111\n    exit 255                          255 / 255\n    exit 130                          130 / 130   was 130, but with a FAILED: line and no early stop\n    command not found                 127 / 127\n\n  Signal-killed command (raw wait status + 128, truncated to & 0xff on exit)\n    SIGSEGV                             11 / 11   (FAILED: [code=267])\n    SIGBUS                                7 / 7\n    SIGQUIT                               3 / 3   (FAILED: [code=259])\n    SIGKILL                           137 / 137\n    SIGINT                            130 / 130\n    SIGTERM                           130 / 130\n    SIGHUP                            130 / 130\n\n  Multiple failures\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 0              5 / 5   (last non-zero wins)\n    exit 5 then exit 3, -k 0              3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, no -k             3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 2              5 / 5\n    exit 9 then success, -k 0             9 / 9\n    SEGV then exit 5, -k 0                5 / 5\n    SEGV then success, -k 0             11 / 11\n    exit 5 then SIGTERM, -k 0         130 / 130\n    SIGTERM then exit 5, -k 0         130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGINT then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGHUP then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    exit 130 then exit 5, -k 0        130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n\n  Dry run\n    -n over a failing command             0 / 0\n    -n over a manifest error              1 / 1\n\n  Manifest and graph errors\n    syntax error, unknown rule            1 / 1\n    missing input                         1 / 1\n    self-referencing phony (warns)        0 / 0\n    phony cycle, real-rule cycle          1 / 1\n    unknown target on the command line    1 / 1\n    missing manifest, unknown flag, bad -j  1 / 1\n    failing generator regen (stale)       1 / 1\n    generator killed by SIGSEGV (stale)   1 / 1\n\n  Tools and arguments\n    -t query on an unknown target         1 / 1\n    -t unknown tool, -t browse            1 / 1\n    -t targets/list/clean/deps/missingdeps/recompact  0 / 0\n    --version                             0 / 0\n    -h, -d list, -d bad, -w bad           1 / 1\n\n  Post-command paths\n    success, no depfile written (no deps) 0 / 0\n    success, garbage depfile (no deps)    0 / 0\n    deps=gcc, depfile never written       0 / 0   was 1    <-- DEFECT 2\n    deps=gcc, depfile in a missing dir    0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    plain depfile in a missing dir        0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    failure plus a garbage depfile        6 / 6\n    rspfile in a missing directory        1 / 1   was 0    <-- DEFECT 3\n    restat with the output untouched      0 / 0\n\n  Quiet, console, verbose, subninja\n    --quiet and -v over exit 7            7 / 7\n    console pool, exit 7 / exit 111   7 / 7, 111 / 111\n    console pool, SIGSEGV               11 / 11\n    failure inside a subninja           21 / 21\n\n  The tool itself signalled mid-build\n    SIGINT to the tool                130 / 130\n    SIGTERM to the tool               130 / 130\n    SIGHUP to the tool                130 / 130\n\nDEFECT 1 — an interrupt ends the build, and 130 is how Ninja spells one.\nNinja's ExitStatus enum spends 130 on ExitInterrupted and then reads every finished command's status back through that same number: `result.interrupted() || result.exit_status() == ExitInterrupted`, checked before the completion is counted as a failure at all. Two consequences Ronin missed. A command that plainly exits 130 is an interrupt — no FAILED: line, build over — because Ninja has no way to tell it from one killed by SIGINT and does not try. And an interrupt leaves the loop whatever -k still allowed, so no later command's status can overwrite the 130 that says why the build stopped. Ronin classified only signal deaths as interrupts and, under -k, counted them as ordinary failures and carried on: the whole point of the status was lost precisely when a user had asked for the build to stop. Fixed in src/build.rs as `Builder::command_interrupted`, plus a break on BuildError::Interrupted in the scheduler loop.\n\nGated behind a new BuildOptions field, `command_status_interrupts`, which the Make front end turns off. Measured GNU Make 4.4.1: a recipe exiting 130 is `*** [Makefile:2: a] Error 130` and make exits 2, with no interrupt semantics anywhere. Ronin's Make mode wraps recipes in an outer shell, so a signalled recipe already arrives as 128+N rather than as a signal and the existing classification never fired there — but an ungated 130 rule would have changed Make's narration and its -k behaviour for a number Ninja's enum merely happens to have spent. Make conformance output is byte-identical before and after, verified by reverting to base, rebuilding and re-running: the same six recorded-difference drifts, which are pre-existing on main and not this node's.\n\nDEFECT 2 — a depfile that is not there is an empty one.\nNinja's ExtractDeps clears NotFound and returns success on empty content, so a compiler that emits no depfile for a translation unit with no includes keeps the status it exited with. Ronin raised DependencyFileMissing and turned a successful command into exit 1 — samurai's behaviour, not Ninja's. The deps-log record the fix produces is identical to the one an existing-but-empty depfile produces (`#deps 0 … (VALID)`), checked against Ninja through `-t deps` rather than assumed.\n\nDEFECT 3 — the depfile's directory is made and the response file's is not.\nNinja MakeDirs the outputs and the depfile, then WriteFile the rspfile with no MakeDirs. Ronin had these exactly the wrong way round, and so was wrong in both directions: a compiler writing into a depfile directory that did not exist failed (2 against Ninja's 0), and an rspfile pointed at a missing directory succeeded (0 against Ninja's 1). The asymmetry is not an oversight upstream — a compiler writes its own depfile and cannot be asked to create the directory first, while a response file the build tool writes itself has nowhere to go and should say so.\n\nCONFORMANCE\nBUILD_CASES went 32 to 44, and every case, build and invocation alike, now records the Ninja status it must leave with as a literal. Comparing the two tools to each other was never the gap; what was missing is a check that a case still exercises what it is named after. Three did not. \"A killed command reports the shell's status\", \"a terminated command reads as an interrupt\" and \"a quit command is an ordinary failure\" were written `kill -KILL $$`, and `$$` is the manifest escape for one `$` — so all three ran `kill -KILL $`, a usage error exiting 2 that both tools agreed on perfectly. Two of the three were the only coverage of the interrupt path this node then found broken. Fixed to `$$$$`, and the recorded statuses (137, 130, 3) now hold them to it. New shapes added: SIGSEGV, SIGHUP, exit 130, exit 111, a missing command, an interrupt outranking -k in both orders, keep-going past a signal, a dry run over a failure, a missing depfile, the depfile directory, and a console command's status.\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT — recorded rather than fixed, status matches and bytes do not\nAn rspfile that cannot be written reports differently. Ninja: stderr `ninja: error: WriteFile(nodir/x.rsp): Unable to create file. No such file or directory` plus stdout `ninja: build stopped: .`; Ronin: stdout `ronin: build stopped: No such file or directory.` Both exit 1, so the status contract holds, but a conformance case cannot be added until the bytes agree. Filed as ronin-response-file-write-diagnostic.\n\nUncovered incidentally, off this node's surface, by a typo in a case manifest: an unexpected indent following a top-level binding is located at a different line by each tool — Ninja build.ninja:4 with no source context, Ronin build.ninja:3 with context. Filed as ronin-indent-error-location.\n\nVERIFICATION\ncargo test --all-targets 475 passing plus the integration binaries, clippy clean, fmt --check clean, scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh green: 425 upstream tests, 10 CLI/tool cases, 44 build-outcome cases each on its recorded exit status, 3 invocation cases likewise, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. All 66 probed shapes match. Probes and gates run under scripts/sandboxed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:53.863809098Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T19:05:47.569973822Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-baseline-rerecord
        title "Re-record the performance baseline against the pinned Release Ninja"
        kind @chore
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:25:14.780156143Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Opened from the review of 2026-08-09: benchmarks/README.md pins the reference recipe to a Release Ninja (cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release, recorded by ronin-reference-in-checkout), but benchmarks/baseline-v1.csv has not been re-recorded since 553dc19 and still encodes the unoptimised Ninja that ronin-ninja-reference-unpinned identified — the gate compares against a stale ratio. Re-record on a quiet host; do not run alongside other work, per the wall-time discipline. Blocked on host quietness, not on any node."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-10T15:57:34.680166147Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Selected the first quiet-host Release-reference sweep as the new baseline. The pinned Ninja checkout remained at b51a1e37c2fb89bbefa600bd155e1ce13983f09d and was rebuilt with CMake Release flags (-O3 -DNDEBUG, -flto=auto), shrinking the stale 1,464,216-byte unoptimised binary to 430,528 bytes. Live preflight intervals showed 99% CPU idle, no blocked tasks, and no material I/O wait. The primary run used clean Ronin revision 3e6b9479835c5b55514b0de70aebc0c8a4f97a4d with two warmups and 15 interleaved samples; an immediate equal-size confirmation kept every tool median within 7.1% and each Ronin/Ninja ratio within 8.6%. Ronin is faster than Release Ninja and C samurai on seven of eight workloads; scheduler-barrier is 1.016x Ninja and 1.079x C, while every Ronin/Ninja peak-RSS ratio is below 1. baseline-v1.csv now exactly matches the primary raw output, with gate thresholds unchanged. Evidence: benchmarks/performance-validation-2026-08-10.{md,csv}; the 1-warmup/7-sample release gate passed, the baseline validation unit test passed, and the forced workspace check passed. The first forced workspace test run had one transient manifest-reload failure; that exact test passed three isolated reruns and the full forced suite passed on repetition."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-10T15:57:40.249129095Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
