    {
        id make-conformance-oracle-identity
        title "Record which Make the vendored-corpus run measures against"
        kind @test
        deps (make-oracle-identity-record make-conformance-corpus)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-12T13:29:30.331398533Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The 387-run vendored-corpus gate (examples/make_conformance.rs, scripts/check-make-conformance.sh) resolves its oracle as `make` on PATH with a `--make` override, and admits it on the same evidence the build-intent corpus used to: the first line of `--version` equalling \"GNU Make 4.4.1\". Debian's, Fedora's, Arch's and the released source all pass that check, so tests/make_corpus_inventory.tsv records 387 live comparisons against whichever one the host had — Debian's, here. Pointing it at reference/make-oracle and giving it the same provenance record the build-intent corpus now carries would reclassify the inventory, so it is deliberately not folded into the pass that established the pin. The upstream-suite gate needs nothing: its oracle is GNU Make's own test suite, pinned by git commit d66a65a, and it runs Ronin rather than any Make binary."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-14T06:18:40.95176801Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-14T06:49:17.843217277Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The 387-run vendored-corpus gate now names the Make it measures against, checks it, and the inventory is re-recorded from the pristine build.\n\nHOW THE ORACLE IS RESOLVED. Shared, not reimplemented: examples/make_conformance.rs includes tests/support/oracle.rs with `#[path]`, so the probe, the record format and the difference report are the build-intent recorder's own code, and both gates read one record — tests/make/oracle.provenance. Including a module rather than importing a crate costs two allowances, scoped to the include and argued there: `dead_code`, because this harness reads the record and never writes one, and `unreachable_pub`, because the module's items are `pub` for the recorder while the example denies that. A parallel implementation would have been a second answer to the question the record exists to settle. oracle.rs itself gains only a paragraph saying it is shared, `pub fn version` for a legible pre-probe check, and a per-process probe scratch directory so two harnesses cannot delete each other's.\n\nThe default oracle is reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, and scripts/check-make-conformance.sh runs scripts/build-make-oracle.sh first, so the gate builds its own oracle rather than borrowing the host's. Enforcement is the full probe, not the version string: version, MAKE_HOST, .FEATURES, every default-origin variable and every value .POSIX: changes. Verified — `--make /usr/bin/make` now refuses with `under .POSIX: ARFLAGS is \"-rvU\", and the record has no such default`, which is Debian's patch and nothing else. `--make` still names another copy of the recorded build. `--other-make` is the deliberate second build: its departures print as a header, the run proceeds as a report, and `--update` refuses so no other build can rewrite the classification. The oracle is also absolutised before it is spawned, by path if it has one and through PATH the way execvp would otherwise, because a case runs in a scratch directory where a relative path names nothing.\n\nTHE DELTA, AND WHERE IT CAME FROM. Debian → pristine moved nothing. The two runs are identical in every count and in every listed problem, because the corpus's only `.POSIX:` case is posix_var.mk, which declares it to watch what `override SHELL := echo` does to `$(shell ...)` and never mentions ARFLAGS, ar or an archive member. The one behavioural departure between the builds had nowhere to show.\n\nTen rows moved anyway, all for the oracle's NAME rather than its build, because the corpus reads the name it is handed:\n\n  Seven scripts test `grep -q \"^make\"` and print a canned expectation *instead of* running the tool when it matches — final_global, final_rule, final_rule2, readonly_global, readonly_global_missing, readonly_rule, readonly_rule_missing. Under the old default their oracle side was a sentence the corpus wrote about Make; it is now GNU Make running. Demonstrated on final_rule.sh: handed `make` the script prints `Makefile:3: *** cannot assign to readonly variable: FOO` and exits 0 having started nothing; handed a path, GNU Make runs and says `Nothing to be done for 'all'`, which is what it really does with kati's `$=` syntax. The front-end side is byte-identical in both — rkati's path never matched `^make` — so only the digests moved. Class stays `extension`, family stays `kati-extension`, and the family's description gained the second mechanism it now covers.\n\n  Three makefiles hold `ifeq ($(MAKE)$(MAKEVER),make4)` and answer `$(error test skipped)` — err_export_override.mk, err_override_export.mk, wildcard_cache.mk. `$(MAKE)` is the invoked name, so GNU Make skipped itself in all three and kati never did; what had been recorded as a difference was Make's skip diagnostic against kati's real run. Named by a path, GNU Make runs them and agrees byte for byte, so the rows are gone. wildcard_cache.mk's own corpus comment — \"GNU make 4 agrees with ckati\" — is what the run now shows.\n\nNothing moved away from agreement, and no row changed class or family. 73 → 70 differing, 314 → 317 identical of 387; defect 36 → 34, recorded 5 → 4, extension 31, artefact 1. Counts re-pinned in tests/make_corpus_inventory.rs with the reason written into the running history there.\n\nTHE THIRD CONSUMER. scripts/check-make-upstream.sh runs no Make: cargo builds, then `perl run_make_tests.pl -make <scratch>/bin/make` where that link is Ronin, then the classifier, which spawns nothing at all. The driver's own `$make_path = \"make\"` fallback at run_make_tests.pl:399 is unreachable because -make is always passed, and its other bare `make` is VMS-only. Confirmed rather than assumed.\n\nRESIDUE, DOCUMENTED. Twelve corpus makefiles still compute MAKEVER from `$(shell make --version)`, which is the host's Make whatever --make names. It is symmetric — both tools evaluate the same $(shell ...) and take the same branch — so it moves the corpus's own gates together rather than tilting the comparison, and docs/make-oracle-divergences.md says so. Filed as make-corpus-host-make-shellout.\n\nGATES. fmt clean. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean apart from two pre-existing warnings (kati/src-rs/command.rs:883 unused import, examples/make_upstream.rs:771 unused mut). Workspace tests 769 passed / 0 failed, which carries the build-intent gate's 540 cases (550 recorded case directories less the 10 discovery-only) untouched. Clippy lib+bins pedantic+nursery --no-deps clean; `cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings` still fails on that one pre-existing unused import, red at base and left alone rather than dragging a submodule pin into a harness change — filed as make-release-clippy-runnable along with the --no-deps defect in scripts/check-release.sh. Conformance gate green against the new default: 387 runs, 317 identical, 70 differing. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Upstream 1287 rows, unclassified 215 / compiler 50 / interface 45 / narration 977, inventory file unchanged. No flapper fired.\n\nNo repin: nothing in kati or Ronin's runtime changed. This is harness, classification and prose."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-14T06:49:21.596588034Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id make-prerequisite-free-rule-currency
        title "Keep prerequisite-free rules current when their target exists"
        priority 0
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-13T13:04:11.686178419Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "priority pinned to 0 — operator override: Operator explicitly directed this session to fix the sole pcre2 failure from the monitored Necessary OS world-build run 1786539432179-211696 now."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-13T13:04:16.671681798Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-13T13:04:30.278259931Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Observed in Necessary OS world-build run 1786539432179-211696 (81 built / 1 failed / 64 skipped). The sole failure is pcre2 at edge [32/46]: Ronin scheduled Automake's `$(srcdir)/Makefile.in` maintainer regeneration rule, which invoked automake-1.16 through the `missing` shim and exited 127 because automake is absent from the build sandbox. GNU Make 4.4.1 does not run that rule at all. Root cause is NOT equal-mtime tie-breaking: in the extracted pcre2-10.47 tarball Makefile.in (1760960108) is strictly newer than every prerequisite (aclocal.m4 1760960106, configure.ac and m4/*.m4 1760960102/105). The rule text is `$(srcdir)/Makefile.in: # $(srcdir)/Makefile.am $(am__configure_deps)` — configure substituted @MAINTAINER_MODE_TRUE@ to `#` because maintainer mode is off, so Make's comment stripping leaves the rule with ZERO prerequisites. Kati strips that comment correctly. The defect is in Ronin's deferred-freshness path: src/graph/deferred.rs recompute_deferred_freshness forced `timestamp_dirty` whenever `edge_data.non_order_only_inputs().is_empty()`, applying GNU's double-colon \"no prerequisites means always run\" rule to every deferred edge. Deferred freshness is selected precisely when a recipe references `$?` (lowered to KATI_NEW_INPUTS at the scheduler boundary), so any single-colon rule with no prerequisites whose recipe mentions `$?` ran on every invocation even with its target present and `$?` empty — exactly the Automake maintainer rule. The clause entered in 0bbb6d4 (make-grouped-double-colon-actions) where it was correct for `::` rules only. Kati already distinguishes the two: node.unconditional_double_colon is `is_double_colon && has commands && no inputs && no order-only inputs`, and reaches the edge as `always_dirty` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs:807)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-13T13:29:45.197319745Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Fixed in src/graph/deferred.rs: the empty-ordinary-prerequisite clause in recompute_deferred_freshness is now gated on edge_data.always_dirty, which kati already derives as `node.is_phony || node.unconditional_double_colon`. The double-colon \"no prerequisites means always run\" semantics are preserved; single-colon prerequisite-free rules now fall through to the timestamp comparison. No kati change was needed — the signal already reached the edge; the graph was ignoring it.\n\nFive build-intent cases recorded from the pristine GNU Make 4.4.1 oracle: target-prerequisite-free-rule-is-up-to-date, target-commented-prerequisites-leave-no-inputs (Automake shape, with `dep` deliberately newer than `out`), target-double-colon-prerequisite-free-in-question, target-question-rule-runs-for-a-newer-prerequisite, target-equal-timestamps-keep-a-question-rule. Against the previous binary the first two fail (\"'ran' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file\") and the other three pass, so the pair isolates the defect and the trio guards the semantics that must not move.\n\nReplay of the retained PCRE2 tree (/data/pkg-build/work/pcre2--x86-64-x86-64-, overlay lower + upper merged with mtimes and the original .ninja_log): `make -n all-am` goes from 46 scheduled edges to 44. The failing automake-1.16 regeneration edge and its cascaded `config.status Makefile` remake are both gone; GNU Make 4.4.1 on the same tree schedules neither (0 automake mentions, 0 config.status-remake mentions). Remaining 44 edges are the compile/link recipes, which need the sandbox's /host/usr/bin/sh and /build/tools/bin/target-clang and are outside Make's scope.\n\nGates: cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; cargo test --workspace --no-fail-fast 713 passed / 0 failed; ronin pedantic+nursery Clippy reports zero findings in Ronin's own code (all 612 are kati, which is untouched and green under its own `cargo clippy -p kati --all-targets -- -D warnings`); Make build-intent oracle 339 recorded cases pass; Make conformance 387 runs, 301 normalised identical / 86 differing, all classified, unchanged from baseline; direct-vs-manifest equivalence 297 makefiles, 276 graphs, 0 disagreements; Ninja conformance all 425 upstream tests plus 10 differential, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log-content cases.\n\nUpstream GNU Make inventory moved exactly one row of 1362, with class counts unchanged (unclassified 576, compiler 39, interface 175, narration 572): variables/EXTRA_PREREQS.diff.1 from ninja-progress+evaluation to no-work-line+evaluation. Audited to ground: the case is `a%: ; @echo .../$?/...` with `.EXTRA_PREREQS = tick tack` and goal `all`. Run in isolation the artifacts are byte-identical before and after, so the move comes from the suite's shared working directory, which the harness documents as leaving targets behind. With `all`, `tick` and `tack` all present as files, GNU Make 4.4.1 prints `make: 'all' is up to date.`, the previous binary printed a [1/1] progress line and ran the recipe, and the fixed binary prints `ronin: no work to do.` The row moved because Ronin stopped rerunning a prerequisite-free target whose file exists — the defect this node fixes — so the movement is the correction surfacing, and both families are narration class."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-13T13:30:14.727080472Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }
)
